1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Source:Hegder Binder C-441, 442, C-441, Wp - There is some incredible history regarding Elisha Thomas Denny that is given in the third book of Denny genealogy by Margaret Collins Denny Dixon and Elizabeth Chapman Denny Vann: When 
2 CONC just past two years old, elisha' s parents moved  by wagon train to Mercer Co., Kentucky, and he grew to manhood on the frontier. In the "year of his marriage," moved by a dislike of rearing his prospective family under a culture 
2 CONC of slavery, Elisha went on horseback across Ohio to Indiana Territory, and selected a quarter section, (160a) of forest land for his future home. He camped his first night at Royse's Lick. A salt lick first used by forest animals,
2 CONC  and then by Indians, lay on the bank of a fork of the Blue River, several miles southeast of the present town of Salem. Mr. Royse had made salt there since about 1803 and had a thriving trade with the Indians. The salt sold for $
2 CONC 12/ bu. Royse's Lick was the land where Elisha camped in 1810. He returned to Kentucky via Jeffersonville to pay for the land he had selected and it was entered in the County Tract Book on the 28 of July 1813.  After the birth of 
2 CONC his first child he returned to his property in Indiana leaving Mary, who was nicknamed "Polly" behind in Woodford Co., Kentucky.  He charred a small patch in the dense forest and with the help of some neighbors, built a cabin of u
2 CONC nhewn logs with a chimney of stick and mortar. The roof of hewn clapboards was held down by weight poles. He returned to Kentucky for his family and they arrived at Fleenor's Fort On April 12 1812. At the fort there were seven cou
2 CONC ples
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
