2 TITL Flushing Remonstrance.txt
1 NOTE In his journal, John Bowne wrote "7 Jan. 1649, I entered Mr. Philip's service.  We came aboard the shipp Charles at Nantuckett, Mass., that day I parted wth my deare father at Boston, the 25th day my sister Dorothy came abord at N
2 CONC antasket to take her leave of me." 
2 CONT
2 CONT John went back to England but returned to Boston in May 1651.  In June of that year he visited Flushing, Long Island, in company with his brother-in-law, Edward Farrington, who had married his sister Dorothy, and moved there short
2 CONC ly thereafter.  On May 7, 1656, he married Hannah Feake (daughter of Lieutenant Robert Feake and Elizabeth Fones.  John Bowne was at this time in the 29th year of his life and his worth and personal attractions were such to elicit
2 CONC  the favorable notice and commendation of Captain John Underhill, then living in Southold, Long Island.
2 CONT
2 CONT In a note, dated April 12, 1656, written from Captain John Underhill to John Winthrop, Jr., he speaks of the impending marriage:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Sir.  I was latli at Flushing.  Hannah Feke is to be married to a verri jentiele young man of gud abiliti, of lovli fetture and gud behafiour."
2 CONT
2 CONT Hannah became acquainted with some members of the Society of Friends, which resulted in her joining it.  He husband afterwards attended one of their meetings from curiosity, and was so struck by the beauty and simplicity of their 
2 CONC worship that he also became a Friend, and invited the members to assemble for their devotions at his house. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Meetings had not long been held at John Bowne's house before complaints were made "by the Magistrates of Flushing, August 24, 1662, that many of the inhabitants are followers of the Quakers".  An ordinance of the West India Compan
2 CONC y (which controlled the Dutch possessions in America), of 1662 provided that "Besides the Reformed religion no conventicles should be holden in houses, barns, ships, woods or fields, under penalty of fifty guilders for the first o
2 CONC ffense, double for the second and arbitrary correction for every other."
2 CONT
2 CONT John Bowne was arrested, Ninth Month, 1st, 1662, charged with "harboring Quakers and permitting them to hold meetings in his house."  On the 14th of the same month judgment was rendered:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Because John Bowne, at present a prisoner, dwelling at Flushing, Long Island, has made no scruple in villipendation of the orders and mandates of the Director General and Council of the New Netherlands, we do in justice to the hi
2 CONC gh and mighty States of the United Province and the Administrators of the West India Company of the Chamber of Amsterdam, having heard of the demand of the substitutes, and the acknowledgment of the prisoner, have condemned and do
2 CONC  condemn the said John Bowne by those present, Boete 5 and 20 pounds Flemish, with the charges of the Justician, and with express admonition and interdict to abstain from all such forementioned meetings and conventicles, or else f
2 CONC or the second boete, he be condemned in a double boete, and for the third boete to be banished out of this province of New Netherland."
2 CONT
2 CONT John refused to pay the fine and was then confined in a dungeon and restricted to bread and water, no person whatever being allowed to talk with him.  As this did not change his steadfastness of purpose, he was sometimes afterward
2 CONC  taken to the Stadthous (courthouse) and put in the prison there and allowed to see his wife and other friends.  He was then notified that the Court had resolved that he must pay the fine or be sent out of the country, or that he 
2 CONC would be set free if he would promise to leave the country in three months.  On the 31st of October, he was put aboard a ship and sent as a prisoner to Holland along with this communication from the authorities of New Netherland t
2 CONC o the West India Company:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Honorable, Right Respectable Gentlemen.  We omitted in our general letter the trouble and difficulties which we and many of our good inhabitants have since sometimes met with, and daily are renewed by the sect called Quakers, chi
2 CONC efly in the county and principally in the English villages, establishing forbidden conventicles and frequenting those against our public placards, and disturbing in a manner the public peace, in so far that several of our magistra
2 CONC tes and well affected subjects remonstrated and complained to us form time to time of their insufferable obstinacy, unwilling to obey our orders or judgement.  Among others was one of their principal leaders named John Bowne, who 
2 CONC for his transgressions was, in conformity to the placards, condemned to and amends of 150 Guilders in suevant, who has been now under arrest more than three months for his unwillingness to pay, obstinately persisting in his refusa
2 CONC l, in which he still continues, so that we at last resolved, or were rather compelled, to transport him in this ship from this province in the hope that others by it will be discouraged.  If nevertheless by these means no more sal
2 CONC utary impression be made upon others we shall, thought our inclinations be compelled to prosecute such persons in a more severe manner, and which we previously solicit to be favored with your Honour's wise and foreseeing judgement
2 CONC .  With which after our cordial salutations we recommend your Honour's to God's protection, and remain, Honorable and Right Respectable Gentlemen, your Honour's faithful servants."
2 CONT
2 CONT The officials of the West India Company considered the case and drew up a paper for John Bowne to sign.  In reply, he sent to the Company, the following:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Friends, the paper drawn up for me to subscribe I have purused and weighed, and do find the same nor according to that engagement to me through one of your members, viz: that he or you would do herein to me as you would be done u
2 CONC nto, and not otherwise.  For which of you being taken from your wife and family without just cause, would be bound from returning to them unless upon terms to act contrary to your conscience, and deny your faith and religion, yet 
2 CONC this in effect do you require of me and not less.  
2 CONT
2 CONT But truly I cannot think that you did in sober earnest ever think I would subscribe to any such thing, it being the very thing for which I rather chose freely to suffer want of the company of my dear wife and children, imprisonmen
2 CONC t of my person, the ruin of my estate in my absence there, and the loss of my goods here, than to yield or consent to such an unreasonable thing as you hereby enjoin me unto.
2 CONT
2 CONT For which I'm persuaded you will not only be judged in the sight of God, but by good and godly men, rather to have mocked at the oppressions of the oppressed and added afflictions to the afflicted than herein to have done to me as
2 CONC  you in like case would be done unto, which the royal cause of our God requires.  I have with patience and moderation waited several weeks expecting justice from you, but behold an addition to my oppression in the measure I receiv
2 CONC e.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wherefore I have tis now to request for you, that the Lord will not lay this to your charge, but to give eyes to see and hearts to do justice, that you may find mercy with the Lord in the day of judgement.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Bowne
2 CONT
2 CONT The directors there of the West India Company, more tolerant than the Government of New Amsterdam, immediately released him, and in a dispatch dated April 16, 1663, reprimanded Governor Styvesant and the council for their arbitrar
2 CONC y conduct.
2 CONT
2 CONT "We, finally, did see from your last letter, that you had exiled and transported hither a certain Quaker named John Bowne, and although it is our cordial desire that similar and other sectarians might not be found there, yet as th
2 CONC e contrary seems to be the case, we doubt very much if rigorous proceedings against them ought not to be discontinued except you intend to destroy your population, which however, in the youth of your existence, ought rather to be 
2 CONC encouraged by all possible means.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wherefore, it is our opinion that come connivance would be useful that the conscience of men, at least, ought ever to remain free and unshackled.  Let every one be unmolested as long as he is modest, as long as he does not disturb
2 CONC  others or oppose the government.  This maxim of moderation has always been the guide of the magistrates of this city, and the consequence has been that from every land people have flocked to this asylum.  Tread thus in their step
2 CONC s and we doubt not you will be blessed.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Directors of the West India Company
2 CONT Amsterdam Department
2 CONT Abraham Wilmandonk
2 CONT David Von Baerle"
2 CONT
2 CONT This document has particular historic interest because it was one of the first official proclamations of religious liberty for any part of America.  With this decree, the persecution of Friends on Long Island ceased.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Bowne left Amsterdam in April 1663 and after a visit to England and a journey to the island of Barbados, returned to his home at Flushing, where he arrived on January 30, 1664.
2 CONT
2 CONT George Fox stayed at the Bownes house during his visit to America in 1672 where he preached to a large meeting under the oak tree opposite the house.
