1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === Hannah Bowne
2 CONT *Born 10 Apr 1665 in Flushing, Long Island, New York
2 CONT * Died 30 Dec 1707 in Flushing, Long Island, New York
2 CONT *Parents: John Bowne Sr. and Hannah Feake
2 CONT *Spouse: Benjamin Field (1663–1732) Marriage: 30 Nov 1691, Flushing, New York
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT # Benjamin Field (1692 - ) married Sarah Tayler [NO]
2 CONT # John Field (1693–1773) married Elizabeth Woolsey 
2 CONT #Samuel Field (1696–1783) Married Mary Palmer 
2 CONT # Anthony Field (1698–1777) Married Hannah Burling 
2 CONT #Hannah (Field)  (1700–1721) Married Thomas Haviland
2 CONT # Joseph Field (1702–1793) married Molly Denton 
2 CONT # Sarah Field (1704–1724) married James Clements 
2 CONT #Robert Field (1707–1784) married Rebecca Burling and Abigail Sutton
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT Field Genealogy: Being the Record of All the Field Family in ..., Volume 1 By Frederick Clifton Pierce.  Page 132.  [https://books.google.com/books?id=ejAfAAAAMAAJ&lpg=PA569&dq=William%20Hazard%20Field%201833%201888&pg=PA132#v=one
2 CONC page&q&f=false GoogleBooks] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Among other papers preserved at the old Bowne house is the draft of the following letter from Hannah Bowne to her parents. It bears no date, but was no doubt written in 1690, for in that year her father lost his second wife, Hanna
2 CONC h Pick- erstaffe, and did not marry his third, Mary Cock, till 1693: "And dear father and mother, I may also acquaint you that one Benjamin Field, the youngest son of my friend Susannah Field, has tenderd his love to mi-—the quest
2 CONC ion he has indeed proposed as concerning marriage the which as yet I have not at present rejected nor given much way to, nor do I intend to proceed nor let out my affection too much towards him till I have well considered the thin
2 CONC g and have yours and friends' advice and consent concerning it."
2 CONT
2 CONT The writer of this letter was Hannah, daughter of John Bowne, and his first wife Hannah, daughter of Robert Feaks, or Feeks, as it was sometimes spelt. This Feaks married Elizabeth Fones, granddaughter of Adam Winthrop of Groton, 
2 CONC and widow of her cousin Henry, son of John Winthrop, first governor of Massachusetts. Hannah Bowne was born in 1665, according to the Friends' register, and her marriage entry in it occurs the year after the supposed date of her l
2 CONC etter. It reads: "Benj. Field, son of Anthony Field of Long Island, deceased, and Hannah Bowne, daughter of John Bowne of Long Island, aforesaid married 3oth, qth, 1691, at John Bowne's in Flushing."
2 CONT
2 CONT At the old Bowne house is a deed of land by Benjamin Field to Samuel Bowne, dated 9th, 12th month, 1696-97. In a list of the inhabitants of Flushing in 1698 is the following: "Benj. Feild and Hannah his wife, children Benji, John,
2 CONC  Anthony, and Sam'l, negroes Jo and Betty."
2 CONT
2 CONT Hannah Field died shortly after the birth of the last child, as shown by this entry: "Hannah Field, wife of Benjamin Field, of Flushing, died 3oth day, 10th mo., 1707."
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband married again a lady who must have been a near relative of his first wife and her mother. This marriage is thus entered in the register: "Benjamin Field and Elizabeth Feaks, daughter of John Peaks, of Matinecock, marri
2 CONC ed the 3rd day of I2th mo., 1709-10, at Jericho." Her death is recorded as follows: "Elizabeth Field, wife of Benjamin Field of Flushing, died 1724." 
2 CONT
2 CONT As far as the writer can learn, she left no children, nor does he know the date of her husband's death.
