1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===William Burton===
2 CONT * Born 1630 in Providence, Providence, Rhode Island
2 CONT * Died 20 Feb 1714 in Providence, Providence, Rhode Islandmap
2 CONT * Son of [father unknown] and [mother unknown]
2 CONT * [sibling(s) unknown]
2 CONT * Husband of Hannah (Wickes) Burton — married 1654 (to 1701) in Rhode Island
2 CONT * Husband of Isabel (Potter) Burton — married 1701 [location unknown]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===notes===
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://archiver.rootsweb.ancestry.com/th/read/BURTON/2010-07/1279682558
2 CONT
2 CONT This information was submitted by Dwayne Burton on April 30, 2001:
2 CONT
2 CONT William Burton and Hannah Wickes
2 CONT
2 CONT The LDS records say that William Burton was born around 1630, but if he was the son of John and Elizabeth Burton of London, he was probably born between 1619 and 1622. When and where William Burton first arrived in the Colonies re
2 CONC mains a mystery. The Thayer Book says that he arrived in Providence, but the early town records of Providence do not bear that out. His arrival is more likely linked to the one known error in John Burton's biography in the Thayer 
2 CONC Book: i.e., William Burton was not the first Burton to inhabit Rhode Island. The first Burton to arrive in Rhode Island was actually Thomas Burton. ....
2 CONT
2 CONT The NE Dictionary says that William Burton was from Warwick, Rhode Island. The first evidence of William being in Warwick is found in the early records of the Town of Warwick (the "Warwick Records"). A partially destroyed entry, w
2 CONC hich appears to be the Town's later denounced declaration of allegiance to Oliver Cromwell (who had gained control of England), was signed by William Burton and others on January 23rd, 1648. So, even if William had first arrived i
2 CONC n Portsmouth, he moved on to Warwick about the time that Warwick was settled by Samuel Gorton, John Wickes, Robert Potter and a handful of other Gortonites.
2 CONT
2 CONT The NE Dictionary and the RI Dictionary report that William Burton was married first to Hannah Wickes. Although those publications occasionally equivocate as to whether her given name was actually Hannah or Ann and as to whether h
2 CONC er surname was spelled, Wicks, Wickes, Weeks or Weekes, they are both definite in identifying her as the daughter of John and Mary Wickes (however spelled) of Warwick. Nothing tells us when or where William and Hannah were married
2 CONC , but the RI Dictionary gives 1674 as the date of their eldest child Elizabeth's marriage. That would probably put Elizabeth's birth during the early or middle 1650's, and William and Hannah's marriage around the early 1650's in W
2 CONC arwick, where they then resided. ....
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 8th, 1657, the Warwick Town Council "Ordered that William Burton have his vote in Towne affairs" and granted him a Town lot along with an exemption from the Town's building requirement if he built a dwelling elsewhere in 
2 CONC Warwick. (According to The History of Warwick, Rhode Island [Oliver P. Fuller, 1875], William Burton's lot was probably located between the lots of Samuel Gorton and John Wickes. It also appears from the Warwick Records that Willi
2 CONC am Burton may have acquired John Wickes' Town lot around that same time.) Upon being given his vote, William Burton became a "freeman." Evidence of that status if found later in the Warwick Records where he is occasionally referre
2 CONC d to as "goodman Burton" (a term identifying him as a freeman and yeoman, just below the rank of gentry).
2 CONT The Warwick Records of October 3rd, 1659, contain the following order of the Town Council:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Ordered by the Towne that John Greene and William Burton beinge chosen by the Towne have liberty granted to sell wine and liquors in their houses by retale to either Townsmen or strangers, and they are at their liberty to keepe e
2 CONC ntertainment for strangers if they please, and all other persons are prohibited to sell by retail any wine or liquors, that is to say except they sell a gallon to be carried away at once . . . ."
2 CONT
2 CONT William was frequently appointed as a juryman, and in March of 1660, he participated in an inquest that ruled that an Indian had died from being beaten to death. On April 1st, 1661, William was selected as the Town "cunstable." In
2 CONC  August of 1663, he bought 18 acres from Mathias Harvey, and in October of 1663, he sold 20 acres to John Lippitt. We know something more of William and Hannah's lives from their association with the first Roger Burlingame of Rhod
2 CONC e Island. Nelson H. Burlingame, one of the authorities on the Burlingame Family in North America, has written:
2 CONT
2 CONT ". . . Thomas Ralph and Roger Burlingame are both said to have fled from the Connecticut Colony because of their religious beliefs. They were Quakers. On June 6, 1662 Thomas Ralph, Roger Burlingame and John Harrud purchased 4,000 
2 CONC acres of land from the Coweeweette Indians at a place then called Mashantatack and called by some Paquabuck, which is now [the western portion of] Cranston, RI. According to Sidney's 'History of Rhode Island Lands,' these three me
2 CONC n were the first English settlers in this section of the country. . . ." 
2 CONT
2 CONT That account is probably largely accurate. In addition, History of Kent County says that on June 17, 1662, the Rhode Island General Assembly granted permission to William Burton, Thomas Ralph and several others to purchase up to f
2 CONC our thousand acres of land from the Indians.
2 CONT
2 CONT The puchase of those lands in the Mashantatack area, just north of Warwick across the Pawtuxet River, gave rise to a boundary dispute between those Warwick men and several of the original proprietors of The Providence Plantations.
2 CONT  disputes involving the Mashantatack area were not completely settled for almost another 50 years, in 1714, but that did not discourage Willian and Hannah. In February of 1668, they sold their home and an orchard in Warwick then i
2 CONC n their posession to John Gorton (who was a son of Thomas Burton's colleague from Portsmouth, Samuel Gorton). They most likely sold that home in connection with their move across the Pawtuxet River to the Mashantatack tract. There
2 CONC after, the Mashantatack areas served as the "headquarters" for the Rhode Island Burton Family until the early 1800's. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mashantatack is located on the cusp between the Narragansett Lowlands and the New England Uplands. According to the Thayer Book, the William Burton homestead was situated on Applehouse Hill and adjoining meadows near today's inter
2 CONC section of Phenix Avenue and Interstate Highway 295, just over a mile north of the confluence of Furnace Hill Creek (formerly called Herrod Creek in honor of John Herrod) and Mashantatack Brook.  It might be assumed that Hannah's 
2 CONC father was living in Mashantatack with the Burtons when he was killed in 1675 during King Phillip's War. The History of Warwick, Rhode Island indicates otherwise. It reveals that John Wickes Senior, who did not flee Warwick with m
2 CONC ost of the Town's other residents, was convinced that the Indians would not do him harm, and that he left the safety of the garrison house in Warwick to search for his cattle. The next day, friends found "his head set upon a pole 
2 CONC near his dwelling." The History of Warwick, Rhode Island further states that John Wickes' dwelling was in Warwick "on the corner leading to Rocky Point, nearly opposite the Old Quaker Meeting House." That would probably mean that 
2 CONC the John Wickes involved in the Mashantatack boundary dispute was actually Hannah's brother John Wickes Jr..
2 CONT
2 CONT William and Hannah, along with their children, almost certainly were among those who fled to the island towns during King Philip's War. There is no doubt that their home was destroyed by the Indians, as the only structure to survi
2 CONC ve in the Mashantatack area was a building known as the "Long House." Whatever else may have happened, though, it did not keep William and Hannah from Mashantatack for long. We know that they returned to Mashantatack after the War
2 CONC  because according to notes from the Louise Prosser Bates Collection (the "Bates" Notes") maintained by the Rhode Island Historical Society, Harris instructed his attorney after King Philip's War had ended to take possession of th
2 CONC e lands in Mashantatack where William Burton and others had built and to seize their cattle. In addition, according to the Bates Notes, John Rice testified in his deposition in 1678 that William Burton and Edward Searles had fence
2 CONC d in the Mashantatack Meadow "in some years past" and had since mowed it together with Roger Burlingame.
2 CONT
2 CONT William and Hannah acquired additional land in that area in 1680 when Hannah's brother John Wickes Jr. deeded a parcel of his land in Mashantatack to William and Hannah for life, with their son John to receive the land upon the de
2 CONC ath of the last of them. The land given over by John Wickes Jr.. might have been a part of his land just south of the William Burton homestead, which was acquired by the younger Wickes in the apportionment of the "Natick Lands" to
2 CONC  Warwick inhabitants in 1673, but it was more likely the Chestnut Hill property.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Williams's will, William and Hannah had seven children: Elizabeth, Hannah, Rose, Ethalannah, Susanna, John and a daughter whose name is not known. John, who was apparently their seventh and last child, was their only 
2 CONC son. He was born May 2, 1667, which means he almost certainly was born in Warwick. (Warwick began keeping records of births, marriages and deaths in early 1663, but the earliest of those records have apparently been lost or destro
2 CONC yed.)
2 CONT
2 CONT William Burton is called the "first William of Providence." As to that reference to Providence, the area called Mashantatack where William lived after 1668 was between the town of Providence and Warwick. Warwick petitioned the Gen
2 CONC eral Assembly in 1671 to have the inhabitants and lands of Mashantatack added to Warwick, but given the on-going boundary dispute between Providence and Warwick, that petition was apparently never acted upon. Although it is not en
2 CONC tirely clear, Mashantatack was apparently thereafter identified with Providence until Mashantatack became a part of Cranston, when Cranston became a separate town in 1754. In any event, the first mention of William Burton in the P
2 CONC rovidence Records as an inhabitant of Providence was in 1787, when he was taxed four shillings. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hannah died sometime around 1700.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT * [https://books.google.com/books?id=LA7ntaS11ocC&lpg=PA266&ots=ZsgyptxoTd&dq=William%20Burton%201630%20hannah%20wickes&pg=PA266#v=onepage&q=William%20Burton%201630%20hannah%20wickes&f=false page 266] of The Genealogical Dictionar
2 CONC y of Rhode Island: Comprising Three Generations of ...By John Osborne Austin
