1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Note about parents===
2 CONT
2 CONT Rebecca S Haines is described on Martha A Dill's death certificate as her mother, but heretofore nobody apparently knew where she came from.  Census information clearly indicated she was born around 1799-1800, and her grave marker
2 CONC  exists in Mount Holly Cemetery, in Mount Holly, NJ.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, it is noteworthy that she was not buried in a Quaker cemetery, despite the fact that the Haines family have been Quakers for time out of mind.
2 CONT
2 CONT So I set about looking at the history of other non-Quakers buried in that cemetery.  Several Haines burials there have known origins, and I pursued each of these to see about feasibility.
2 CONT
2 CONT An almost perfect match was found for the Quaker family of Samuel Haines and Mary Stevenson.  At least one grandson who left Quakerdom was buried in the non-Friends cemetery.  The "Stevenson" was used as a middle name for other ch
2 CONC ildren of this couple, and the dates were right too.  So I think it is likely that Rebecca Stevenson Haines married William Inskeep Wright, a Methodist, and thus violated the most important rule of Quakerdom: never marry outside t
2 CONC he faith.  That is why she is buried where she is.
