2 CONT Chapter 1
2 CONT
2 CONT Mayflower Lineage History
2 CONT
2 CONT Laura’s family history and ancestry begins is story with Stephen Hopkins,  Laura’s Greatx10 Grandfather, in 1609. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1947:            Laura Von Luckey 1919:            Charles W. Luckey m. Margaret C. Franklin 1889:            Laurel B. Faulconer m. Charles L. Luckey 1859:            Elizabeth B. Swinford m. George W. Faulconer 1829:            
2 CONC John P. Swinford m. Sarah E. Terry 1798:            William Swinford m. Juliann Noland 1760:            Thankful Doane m. Elisha Swinford  1737:             Hezehiah Doane III m. Sarah Bland * 1712:             Hezehiah Doane Jr. 
2 CONC m. Thankful Bickford 1670:             Hannah Snow m. Hezehiah Doane Sr. 1638:             John Snow. m. Mary Smalley 1607:             Constance Hopkins (Mayflower) m. Nicolas Snow 1580:             Stephen Hopkins (Jamestown & M
2 CONC ayflower) m. Mary? 
2 CONT
2 CONT *THIS IS A PROVEN LINEAGE BY THE MAYFLOWER SOCIETY
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHICAL SUMMARY:  Stephen Hopkins Stephen Hopkins was one of only a few passengers on the Mayflower to have made  a prior trip to America. He came in 1609 on the Sea Venture headed for  Jamestown, Virginia. But instead, they 
2 CONC were marooned on an island following a  hurricane, and the 150 passengers were stranded for nine months. Hopkins led an  uprising, challenging the governor's authority, and was sentenced to death. But  he begged and moaned about t
2 CONC he ruin of his wife and children, and so was  pardoned out of sympathy. The company eventually managed to build a ship, and  escaped the island. After spending several years in Jamestown, Hopkins returned  to England sometime betw
2 CONC een 1613 and 1617.  Stephen Hopkins brought with him on the Mayflower his wife Elizabeth, children  Giles and Constance by his first marriage, and Damaris by his second marriage.  A son Oceanus was born while the Mayflower was at 
2 CONC sea. Stephen participated in  the early exploring missions and was an "ambassador" along with Myles Standish  for early Indian relation. Constance Hopkins was a Mayflower passenger. She journeyed with her father and  stepmother, S
2 CONC tephen and Elizabeth Hopkins, her brother Giles, her half-sister  Damaris and her half-brother Oceanus who was born during the voyage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime before 1627, Constance Hopkins married Nicholas Snow. Nicholas Snow  had arrived in Plymouth on the Anne in 1623.
2 CONT
2 CONT The inventory of Nicholas’ estate, taken at the time of his death, includes  carpenter’s tools. This may have been his trade. His inventory also included  books, so he was probably literate. Nicholas held various minor positions i
2 CONC n  Plymouth, such as highway surveyor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Snows moved from Plymouth to Nauset, on Cape Cod in the 1640s. On the Cape,  Nicholas served as surveyor, constable and selectman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance and Nicholas had 12 children. Nicholas died in November of 1676,  Constance a year later in October of 1677. Before his death, Nicholas Snow  wrote a will.   The Pilgrim Society displays one artifact that is attributed t
2 CONC o Constance  Hopkins. It is a beaver hat; made in England, c.1615-1640 Steeple crowned hats,  usually with a decorative band, was popular for both men and women in the early  17th century. Beaver fur, imported into England from th
2 CONC e colonies, was  processed into felt to make hats.    Descendants of Nicholas1 and Constance (Hopkins) Snow 		
2 CONT   1 Nicholas Snow b: 18 Jan 1598/99 in England d: 15 Nov 1676 in Plymouth, MA.
2 CONT      +Constance Hopkins b: About. 1607 in England m: 22 May 1627 in Plymouth,  Plymouth Colony, MA d: Oct 1677 in Eastham, Barnstable, MA. They are Laura’s  Greatx9 Grandparents.
