1 NOTE Robert Feake was said to have come with the Winthrop Fleet of 1630 in the work by Colonel Banks.  Stated in the FTM LI Families, Vol I,
2 CONT p. 407, it states if he was not with that fleet he must have arrived
2 CONT shortly thereafter for he petitioned to be made a freeman on 19 Oct
2 CONT 1630 and was accepted (as such) on 18 May 1631.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married the governor's daughter-in-law, Elizabeth Fones.
2 CONT
2 CONT If interested in more generations, contact CalOwen@attbi.com and I'll send you what you want.
1 NOTE Robert Feake was apprenticed to his father for a term of eight years on September 21, 1615. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert came to America in 1630 in the Winthrop fleet and settled that year in Watertown, MA.   The earliest notice of him occurs in the journal of the Governor, detailing the particulars and incidents of a prospecting expedition m
2 CONC ade by him (the Gov.)  "and some company with him" on January 27, 1631, when they "went up by Charles River about eight miles above Watertown, and named the first brook on the north side of the river (being a fair stream and comin
2 CONC g from a pond a mile from the river), Beaver Brook, because the beavers had shorn down divers great trees there, and made divers dams across the brook.  Thense they went to a great rock, upon which stood a high stone cleft in sund
2 CONC er that four men might go through which they called Adam's Chair, because the youngest of their company was Adam Winthrop.  They came to another brook, greater than the former, which they called Master's Brook, because the eldest 
2 CONC of the company was one John Masters.  Thense they came to another high pointed rock having fair ascent on the west side, which they called Mount Feake, from one Robert Feake who had married the Governor's daughter-in-law.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Feake was admitted freeman, May 1631, appointed Lieutenant to Captain Patrick in 1632 until 1636.  He had his "homestall" in Watertown and was a grantee of a number of plots in the same place; was selectman several years (1
2 CONC 634-6); and was a representative to General Court from Watertown.  He moved to Connecticut with Captain Patrick in 1638-40.  In July 1640, they purchased land from the Indians, which would now be located in Greenwich, CT.  The Dut
2 CONC ch claimed the land, but they continued to hold it for two more years, but because of the strife between the English and the Dutch, and threatening troubles with the Indians, they put themselves under the protection of the Dutch, 
2 CONC April 9, 1642, the submission being signed by Patrick alone as Feake was sick and had commissioned his wife to act in his stead.  Patrick was killed by a Dutch soldier in 1644.  This was a serious loss to Robert and may have been 
2 CONC the cause of his loss of reason later.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1647 Robert Feake sailed to England and entrusted his lands and property at Greenwich to his wife and William Hallett. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Whether his family believed he was lost at sea, or whether the Dutch authorities granted a divorce, by May of 1647, on grounds of Mrs. Feake's alledged adultery with William Hallett, it is not precisely clear.  It is possible that
2 CONC  Lt. Feake was imprisoned in England for a Robert Feake was granted a pardon by the House of Commons on March 4, 1649.   He returned to Greenwich prior to September 18, 1649.  In February 1648/49 his (former) wife mentioned settle
2 CONC ment of his estate in a letter to John Winthrop, Jr.
2 CONT
2 CONT By April 14, 1648 his wife Elizabeth was pregnant, and claimed she was William Hallett's wife. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The suceeding years of his life were to him, a blank.  He died at the house of Samuel Thatcher of Watertown, MA.  He probably did not live with his wife after his return from England because he had lost his mind and had to be take
2 CONC n care of.
