2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p14/c2/16/bc/f5/5344486220a4ea13/spared-from-the-wrecking-ball-elizabeth-feake-house-was-a-hidden-treasure-under-our-noses-65886_original.png?hash=3f8778c7854eab62a1bc14367aae105b73437b3d9c320e8a86ddad0a1f6045ed.1795075199
2 FORM png
2 TITL https://greenwichfreepress.com/around-town/spared-from-the-wrecking-ball-elizabeth-feake-house-was-a-hidden-treasure-under-our-noses-65886/
1 NOTE Elizabeth Fones was a niece of Governor John Winthrop, and also became his daughter-in-law when she married his son, her first cousin, Henry Winthrop.  Following his death on 2 Jul 1630, she married Robert Feake.  She and Robert s
2 CONC eparated about 1647, and she then married a third time.  Her third marriage was to William Thorne.
1 NOTE
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Fones Feake was the subject of a historical novel by Anya Seton, :"The Winthrop Woman"  The facts are documented thru family letters that still survive.  Her mother was the elder sister of John Winthrop, future Gov of th
2 CONC e Mass Bay Colony, her father was a London apothecary.  Elizabeth is said to have been in love with her cousin John Winthrop, Jr but permitted herself to be seduced by his younger brother Henry Winthrop who was a family black shee
2 CONC p who had been sent to Barbados to start a tobacco plantation to help revive the family fortunes.  He played instead and returned to England in disfavor.  Henry confessed his sins and a marriage followed.  Henry accompanied his fa
2 CONC ther to Mass while Elizabeth waited behind (according to the book because she was pregnant, but  many researchers attribute the paternity of her eldest child, Johanna, to her marriage to Feake)  When they reached Boston Harbor, He
2 CONC nry Winthrop jumped off the boat and decided to show-off a skill that he had learned in Barbados, swimming, and promptly drowned.
2 CONT Elizabeth, now a widow, had no real choice  but to go to Mass as orginally planned.  her parents were dead and she was under the guardianship of her uncle-father-in-law.  When she reached Boston her uncle had a marriage lined up w
2 CONC ith her to Robert Feake.  Robert Feake was a London goldsmith with no useful skills for living in the new colony.  He was also strange and vague and effeminate.  They had 4 children and lived in Boston  and then Watertown.  Elizab
2 CONC eth got in trouble with her uncle by supporting Anne Hutchinson, and later, according to the book, they had to leave atertown in a hurry because of the possibilty of witchcraft charges against their Indian servant woman.
2 CONT  They moved to what is now New Haven, Conn, but was then under the political control of the Dutch in New York.  Robert Feake was a religious fanatic and became insane.  When he had a lucid period he decided he must return to Engla
2 CONC nd ( it is believed that he thought that he had murdered his apprentice in London, went to England, gave himself up and was imprisoned for a year before the apprentice emerged alive and well, Robert Feake returned to Boston, went 
2 CONC completley and harmlessly mad and lived the rest of his life there).  Before he left for England Robert Feake legally put his land and family under the care of a neighbor, William Hallet.  Once Feake was gone, Elizabeth and Willia
2 CONC m Hallet decided that they wanted to marry and so went to the Dutch Governor seeking a divorce for her on thegrounds of abandonment.  The Gov. granted the divorce, but then refused to marry them.  They returned to New Haven preten
2 CONC ding to be married.  (William Hallet was better than 12 years younger than she).   Conn. then fell under the control of England and the Puritans and many people suspected that they weren't really married.  They fled first to RI th
2 CONC en to Long Island, NY.  It is uncertain when Elizabeth died, she was perhaps killed by Indians, William Hallet (by whom she had had 2 more children)  outlived her for many years and remarried.  Her Feake children became Quakers.
1 NOTE Robert Feake's wife, Elizabeth, first married Henry Winthrop, her first cousin and son of John Winthrop, in April of 1629.  Henry went to America with his father a year later and drowned, leaving Elizabeth a young widow.  She left
2 CONC  England and sailed to America on the ship Lyon, arriving on November 2, 1631.  Shortly thereafter,  she married Robert Feake.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth, during her husband's "sickness", began an affair with William Hallett, who was married and had children.  She moved with him to New London and then to Flushing, NY.  On March 9, 1648, prior to Robert's return from Engla
2 CONC nd, there was a court order issued which enjoined Elizabeth from selling any part of her husband's property; placing her children under the care of curators at Greenwich, where she is permitted to reside on the condition she quit 
2 CONC the bed and board of William Hallett, her paramour, who is sentenced to be banished for adultery.
2 CONT
2 CONT In a letter dated Stamford April 14, 1648, Thomas Lyon related to his "loving grandfather" John Winthrop the history of Mr. Feake and Elizabeth (Fones) Winthrop:
2 CONT
2 CONT "...when I married first, I lived in the house with her because my father being distracted I might be a help to her.  Whereupon seeing several carriages between the fellow she now hath to be her husband and she the people also too
2 CONC k notcie of it which was to her disgrace which grieved me very much ... and seeing what condition she were in I spake to her about it privately and after I discovered my dislike I see her carriage alter toward me ... Father concer
2 CONC ning the condition she is in and the children and estate my father Feike going away suddenly, having taken no course about the children and estate only desired a friend of his and I in case we see them about making away the estate
2 CONC  and to remove we should stay it ... She hath also confessed since she came there openly she is married to him is with child by him and she hath been at New Haven but could have no compfort nor hopes for present to live in the jur
2 CONC isdiction and what will become of her I know not."
2 CONT
2 CONT The story of Elizabeth Fones was told in 1958 in an historical novel called The Winthrop Women by Anya Seton.
1 NOTE Robert Feake's wife, Elizabeth, first married Henry Winthrop, her first cousin and son of John Winthrop, in April of 1629.  Henry went to America with his father a year later and drowned, leaving Elizabeth a young widow.  She left
2 CONC  England and sailed to America on the ship Lyon, arriving on November 2, 1631.  Shortly thereafter,  she married Robert Feake.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth, during her husband's "sickness", began an affair with William Hallett, who was married and had children.  She moved with him to New London and then to Flushing, NY.  On March 9, 1648, prior to Robert's return from Engla
2 CONC nd, there was a court order issued which enjoined Elizabeth from selling any part of her husband's property; placing her children under the care of curators at Greenwich, where she is permitted to reside on the condition she quit 
2 CONC the bed and board of William Hallett, her paramour, who is sentenced to be banished for adultery.
2 CONT
2 CONT In a letter dated Stamford April 14, 1648, Thomas Lyon related to his "loving grandfather" John Winthrop the history of Mr. Feake and Elizabeth (Fones) Winthrop:
2 CONT
2 CONT "...when I married first, I lived in the house with her because my father being distracted I might be a help to her.  Whereupon seeing several carriages between the fellow she now hath to be her husband and she the people also too
2 CONC k notcie of it which was to her disgrace which grieved me very much ... and seeing what condition she were in I spake to her about it privately and after I discovered my dislike I see her carriage alter toward me ... Father concer
2 CONC ning the condition she is in and the children and estate my father Feike going away suddenly, having taken no course about the children and estate only desired a friend of his and I in case we see them about making away the estate
2 CONC  and to remove we should stay it ... She hath also confessed since she came there openly she is married to him is with child by him and she hath been at New Haven but could have no compfort nor hopes for present to live in the jur
2 CONC isdiction and what will become of her I know not."
2 CONT
2 CONT The story of Elizabeth Fones was told in 1958 in an historical novel called The Winthrop Women by Anya Seton.
