1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Dorothy Bowne''', daughter of Thomas Bowne, was born on 14 Aug 1631 in Matlock, Derbyshire. Dorothy died in Flushing, New York, on 24 Jun 1678; she was 46.
2 CONT
2 CONT About 1651 she married Edward Farrington. Born ca 1615.14 Edward died in Flushing, New York bef 1 Jul 1676, when the administration of his estate was granted to his widow, Dorothy. He was the son of Elizabeth Newhall and Edmund Fa
2 CONC rrington.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From < [https://www.bownehouse.org/profiles-of-the-flushing-charter-signers/edward-farrington bownehouse.org] > (October 3, 2020) accessed 8 December 2025
2 CONT
2 CONT … Edward [Farrington] lived in Flushing for the remainder of his life. He appears on the 1666 Patent for Flushing issued by Governor Nicolls after the English takeover. In 1667, his father Edmund wrote a will leaving him and his b
2 CONC rother-in-law Robert Terry each twenty shillings. (The bulk of the paternal estate went to the siblings who had remained in Lynn.) Edward raised four sons with Dorothy, named after himself and his three brothers: John, Edward, Tho
2 CONC mas, and Mathew. (The fact that he named his firstborn John, rather than Edward Jr., may hint at his great regard for his brother-in-law, John Bowne.) Genealogies also record three daughters: Dorothy, Mary, and Margaret, although 
2 CONC little is known of them. Edward seems to have prospered in Flushing and become a substantial landowner before his death around age 60. His will was probated on July 1, 1675, when his widow was granted letters of administration. Mo
2 CONC st of his estate went to John. Younger sons Edward and Thomas received 50 acres each plus a share of meadow, while Mathew received 20 acres and three meadows. His daughters either predeceased him or were omitted from the inheritan
2 CONC ce.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following year John Bowne wrote to his wife, then traveling in England: “I must acquaint you to thy grief, as it has been to mine, that my sister did take a husband yesterday. It was the man who went and came with L[ewis] Morr
2 CONC is to the New Countries with us.” (It is not clear from this description who the new husband was; Lewis Morris was a Quaker based in the English colony of Barbados who undertook religious visits to New England, while the “New Coun
2 CONC tries” may have referred to New Jersey or Pennsylvania.) According to family historian Jacob Titus Bowne, Dorothy was censured and expelled from the Flushing Quaker Meeting for “outmarriage,” or marrying a non-Quaker. However, thi
2 CONC s second marriage may have ended before her death, because on June 24, 1678, Letters of Administration were granted to her son John for “Dorothy Farrington, widow & relict of Edward Farrington.” The matter poses a bit of a mystery
2 CONC . Regardless, the Farringtons remained an influential family in both the Quaker Meeting and the town of Flushing well into the 19th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT #Administration of Estate for Dorothy Farrington (1678) ([https://www.geni.com/photo/view?album_type=photos_of_me&id=6000000002591622210&photo_id=6000000109893500118&position=0 image attached])
2 CONT # http://aleph0.clarku.edu/~djoyce/gen/report/rr03/rr03_500.html#P40238
2 CONT #Find a Grave, database and images (https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/164211621/edward-farrington: accessed December 8, 2025), memorial page for Edward Farrington (1615–1675), Find a Grave Memorial ID 164211621, citing Old Bowne
2 CONC  Family Burial Ground, Flushing, Queens County, New York, USA; Maintained by C Boetsch (contributor 48409474).
2 CONT # Find a Grave, database and images (https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/164211534/dorothy-farrington: accessed December 8, 2025), memorial page for Dorothy Bowne Farrington (1631–1678), Find a Grave Memorial ID 164211534, citing 
2 CONC Old Bowne Family Burial Ground, Flushing, Queens County, New York, USA; Maintained by C Boetsch (contributor 48409474).
2 CONT # https://www.bownehouse.org/profiles-of-the-flushing-charter-signers/edward-farrington
2 CONT # https://flushingfriends.org/history/historic-graveyard/ The Graveyard at Flushing Meeting House. In 1676, before the Meeting House was built, John Bowne donated land and arranged for a burial ground on Northern Boulevard. The Me
2 CONC eting House was built on land purchased adjacent to the graveyard in 1694. …
2 CONT # https://maryandersondupont.wordpress.com/2023/03/24/dorothy-bowne/
