1 NOTE Benjamin Field is called youngest son in 1691.  Named among the inhabitants of Flushing in 1698.  At the old Bowne house, is a deed of land by Benjamin Field to Samuel Bowne, dated 9th, 12th Mo., 1696-7.  In a list of the inhabita
2 CONC nts of Flushing in 1693 is the following:  "Benj Feils and Hannah his wife, children Banj, John, Anthony and Sam'l, negroes Jo and Betty."  At the same place, there are two or three letters preserved, dated at Chesterfield in 1700
2 CONC  and 1701, signed B. Field, and addressed to Samuel Bowne.  They relate to purchases of land in that neighborhood, in which they were both interested.  One of them speaks of "another purchase of land to the quanity of 1,000 to 1,5
2 CONC 00 acres" which "lyes above the falls of Delaware, about 10 or 11 miles from Salem. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the name of God, Amen, I, WILLIAM PIDGEON, of Flushing, in Queens County, being sick. My body to be buried in good and decent order, and I appoint Captain William Lawrence administrator of my "outward estate." I leave to Benjam
2 CONC in Field, son of Anthony Field, my two lots lying in Flushing containing about 14 acres. I leave to James Clement my half lot of 25 acres lying by the Bay side with all privileges. I leave to Anthony Field the young swine. The res
2 CONC t of estate is left to the administrator to pay debts, and the balance "for ye relief of ye Poor of ye Towne." Dated January 12, 1677/8. Witnesses, Richard Stockton, James Clement, "Clerk.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Richard Field, who was born about 1790, has a large number of family papers, reaching back to Benjamin Field, son of Anthony, and grandson of the first Robert Field.  He finds amoung these papers one which was given him by his
2 CONC  grandmother, and is no doubt over 100 years old.  This statement is as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Benjamin Field was born in Flushing in the year 1663, was the son of Anthony and Susanna Field.  He had a brother, John, a few years older than himself, who removed to the Jerseys and settled there.  His father, Anthony Field, wa
2 CONC s born in England in 1638, and came out with his father, Robert Field, to Boston in 1644, and came to Flushing in 1645, together with his brother Robert, who was born in 1636, and Benjamin, born in 1640.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert,. father of Anthony, was born at Ardsley, in England in 1610.  He had a brother James, and two sisters, Anne and Judith.  James Field, father of Robert, was born at Ardsley in 1587.  He was the son of Matthew Field, and had
2 CONC  a brother Robert younger than himself.  Mathew Field, father of James, was born in Ardsley in 1563.  He had seven brothers, whose names were Richard, older than himself, and Christopher, John, Willima, Thomas, James and Martin, a
2 CONC nd a sister Anne, who were younger. John Field, the father of Mathew, was born about 1525.  He lived in London, where it was believed he was born, until about 1560, when he married Jane Amyas, daughter of John Amayas, and removed 
2 CONC to Ardsley, where he resided til his death, in 1587.  While he resided in London, he was engaged in publishing astronomical tables, by which he gained a very high reputation as an astronomer."
