1 NOTE Anthony Field was among the earliest settlers of  Harrison's Purchase when he moved there in 1725. Rye was in regular communication with Long Island, in 1739; a ferry was established between it and Oyster Bay.  This ferry continue
2 CONC d in use til the later part of the century, encouraging many Quakers to migrate from Long Island to Westchester.  As one of the first Quaker families to settle in this area, Anthony donated the ground, adjoining his property, for 
2 CONC the building of the first Society of Friends Meeting House.
2 CONT
2 CONT This area seemed to be a favorite settlement of the Friends.  They were shamefully prosecuted in Connecticut and Massachusetts; from there driven to Rhode Island, from whense they had to fly to Long Island.  Even there, they could
2 CONC  find no rest, for the Governor of New York issued an order forbidding them to worship even in a barn.  In Harrison, they were hemmed in by their old enemies;  the Dutch on the Hudson River, and the unforgiving and intolerant Puri
2 CONC tans on the East.  They thus extended up this narrow strip of country, and the family names of the first settlers can be traced for over 100 miles north.
2 CONT
2 CONT First owners of the Grandfather Clock that is now in the possession of David Stephenson, Sr., Denver, CO.  This family gave the name to "Burling Slip", New York having a grant of land in the vicinity.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hi will named wife Hannah and sons Benjamin and John as executors; sons Thomas, Samuel, Anthony, John, Benjamin, William and Moses and daughter Sarah Field.  Witnesses Thomas Vail, Mary Vail, Jr., Benedict Carpenter.  Hannah (Burl
2 CONC ing) Field was named as heiress of her Aunt Grace (Burling) Bowne, sister of her father William Burling, in Grace's will dated Feb 27, 1759.
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Will and Testament
2 CONT Page 189.--"The Last Will and Testament of me, ANTHONY FIELD, of Harrisons Purchase, in Westchester County, to dispose of the earthly estate which the Lord has blessed me with. I leave to my wife Hannah a good bed and furniture an
2 CONC d all household goods sufficient to set out a room, and a good horse, saddle, and bridle, and my Great Bible and Sewell's History, and an equal share of my farm when it is sold and divided between her and my children; Also the use
2 CONC  of the house where I now live so long as she remains my widow. I order my lands to be sold that lie on the north side of the road that leads from Kings Street to the White Plains, and all debts paid. I leave to my son Thomas œ40.
2 CONC  To my son Samuel œ30. To my son Anthony fiveshillings. To my son John œ60, which will make my sons, Samuel, Anthony, and John, equal with what my son Benjamin hath already had, which is œ80. I leave to my daughter, Sarah Field, œ
2 CONC 40. To my son William œ40. And when my widow pleases to sell the farm where I now live on the east side of the road that leads from the Purchase Meeting House to Rye, I order œ80 to be paid to my son Moses, and to each of my child
2 CONC ren enough to make up œ80, except Anthony, who has had his full share allready. I leave my land in Hampshire to my sons, William and Moses. I make my wife Hannah and my sons, Benjamin and John, executors."
