1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  Samuel Thorn was born on 12 Jul 1696 in Flushing, Long Island, NY. Samuel Thorn/e's year of birth can be found in the records of the Flushing Monthly Meeting for the Religous Society of Friends. He died in 1759 in
2 CONC  Rumbout Precinct, Dutchess Co., NY. the death in 1759 of Samuel Thorne was noted in the Flushing Friend's Records. Had Samuel & Father Joseph not remained good Quakers this note would have never occured. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He owned land in New Hackensack & died there in 1759. He is no doubt buried there. It's interesting that most of his kids from 2nd marriage ended up in Dutchess Co. & they all appear to have dropped the E from Thorne as He did. Ho
2 CONC wever in the first marriage not all of the sons dropped the E. I suspect most of the ones who followed the Quaker religion, perhaps retained it. He was Quaker. Samuel was no doubt named after his mother's nearest brother in age, S
2 CONC amuel Bowne. 
2 CONT
2 CONT All of Samuel's sons, except Thomas, who was Quaker, by both marriages, were Loyalists. Samuel sat out the Revolutionary War in White Plains, NY. He clearly sided with the crown privately. Samuel built a famous old structure in Du
2 CONC tchess Co., known as the OLD HOMESTEAD (located in New Hackensack, Dutchess Co., NY). This property was left by him, for his son by his second marriage Dr. Stephen Thorne. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Thorne was a large land speculator & on one land transactions, He and his partners bought 5000 acres of land in New Jersey. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It was many of Samuel's offspring that were to drop the "E" of the name Thorne.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married to Ann Stevenson (daughter of Thomas Stevenson Jr. and Anne Field) on 9 Dec 1715 in Newtown, Nassau, NY. SAMUEL THORNE & ANNE STEVENSON WERE MARRIED IN THE HOME OF BENJAMIN FIELD IN NEWTOWN THIS COULD A BROTHER OF H
2 CONC ER MOTHER ANNE OR HER GRANDAD'S BROTHER BENJAMIN FIELD. Ann Stevenson was born about 1696 in Newtown, Nassau, NY. She died on 19 Mar 1724 in Flushing, Long Island, NY. She was Quaker. Samuel Thorn and Ann Stevenson had the followi
2 CONC ng children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT +404 i. Joseph Thorn. 
2 CONT
2 CONT +405 ii. Thomas Thorn. 
2 CONT
2 CONT +406 iii. Nathaniel Thorn. 
2 CONT
2 CONT +407 iv. Samuel Thorn.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married to Esther Hunt about 1724 in Westchester Co. or Dutchess Co., NY. Samuel Thorn and Esther Hunt had the following children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT +408 i. Jonathan Thorn. 
2 CONT
2 CONT +409 ii. John Thorn. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 410 iii. James Thorn was born about 1731. 
2 CONT
2 CONT +411 iv. Robert Thorn. 
2 CONT
2 CONT +412 v. Stephen Thorn Dr..
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT 159. Samuel Thorn was born on 12 Jul 1696 in Flushing, Long Island, NY. Samuel Thorn/e's year of birth can be found in the records of the Flushing Monthly Meeting for the Religous Society of Friends. He died in 1759 in Rumbout Pre
2 CONC cinct, Dutchess Co., NY. the death in 1759 of Samuel Thorne was noted in the Flushing Friend's Records. Had Samuel & Father Joseph not remained good Quakers this note would have never occured. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He owned land in New Hackensack & died there in 1759. He is no doubt buried there. It's interesting that most of his kids from 2nd marriage ended up in Dutchess Co. & they all appear to have dropped the E from Thorne as He did. Ho
2 CONC wever in the first marriage not all of the sons dropped the E. I suspect most of the ones who followed the Quaker religion, perhaps retained it. He was Quaker. Samuel was no doubt named after his mother's nearest brother in age, S
2 CONC amuel Bowne. 
2 CONT
2 CONT All of Samuel's sons, except Thomas, who was Quaker, by both marriages, were Loyalists. Samuel sat out the Revolutionary War in White Plains, NY. He clearly sided with the crown privately. Samuel built a famous old structure in Du
2 CONC tchess Co., known as the OLD HOMESTEAD (located in New Hackensack, Dutchess Co., NY). This property was left by him, for his son by his second marriage Dr. Stephen Thorne. 
