2 CONT Samuel Thorn is the son of Joseph Thorne and Martha J Bowne. Death noted in Flushing Friends Records (quaker) Samuel Thorn's year of birth can be found in the records of the Flushing Monthly Meeting for the Religous Society of Fri
2 CONC ends. His death was noted in the Flushing Friend's Records. Had Samuel and Father Joseph not remained good Quakers, this note would have never occured. He was married to Ann Stevenson in the home of Benjamin Field in Newton.9 Dec 
2 CONC 1715. They had children Joseph, Thomas, Nathaniel and Samuel. This could be a brother of her mother Ann or her Grandad's brother Benjamin Field. He married 1724 second to Esther Hunt and had children Jonathan, John, James, Robert,
2 CONC  Steven. He owned land in Hackensack and died there. He is no doubt buried there. It's interesting that most of his kids from his 2nd marriage ended up in Dutchess County and they all appear to have dropped the E from Thorne as he
2 CONC  did. However, in the first marriage not all of the sons dropped the E. It is suspected most of the ones who followed the Quaker religion, perhaps retained it. He was a Quaker. Samuel was no doubt named after his mother's nearest 
2 CONC brother in age, Samuel Bowne. All of Samuel's sons, except Thomas, who was Quaker, by both marriages, were Loyalitsts. Samuel sat out the Revolutionary War in White Plains, New York. He clearly sided with the crown privately. Samu
2 CONC el built a famous old structure in Dutchess County known as the OLD HOMESTEAD (located in New Hackensack, Dutchess, New York). This property was left by him, for his son by his second marriage, Dr. Stephen Thorne. Samuel Thorn was
2 CONC  a large land speculator and on one land transaction, he and hi partners bought 5000 acres of land in New Jersey.
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 1, 1728, John Stevenson and John Feris, executors of the will of "Daniel Clark, late of ye Burrough twon of Westchester, Gent.," conveyed to "Samuel Thorne, of Flushing, blacksmith" for 145 lbs "All the lot of land whereo
2 CONC n the sd. Daniel Clark did dwell, containing near nine acres," adjoining Abner Hunt and Underhill Barnes, including dwelling house, barn, fruit trees and fences, together with 19 a. of woodland, all in the town of Westchester.. "a
2 CONC lso a 25 pound priviledge of commonage in ye sheep pasture." (Westchester Co. Deeds F:205). On Sept. 4, 1728 Ithamar Pelton of Marmaroneck, lawyer, mortgaged for 37/16 lbs to John Cromwell, samuel Thorne, and William Oakly of the 
2 CONC Borough Town of Westchester certain lands "together with ye Saw Mill & dwelling house thereon" (Westchester Co. Deeds G:33). On Dec. 17, 1728 "Samuel Thorne of ye Burrough & town of Westchester, Blacksmith by & with ye advice & co
2 CONC nsent of Esther, his wife,"conveyed to Jeremiah Fowler for 125 lbs the property he had purchased earlier in the year "whereon he now lives," but without mention of the 19 a. of Woodland or rights in the sheep pasture. The deed was
2 CONC  signed "Saml. Thorn" and "Esther Thorn" (Ibid:G:133). 
