2 CONT Source: S984 Abbreviation: The Fones Family, Art Reierson Title: The Fones Family, Art Reierson, reprinted by Higginson Book Company, Salem MA. TMPLT TID 0 FIELD Name: Footnote VALUE The Fones Family, Art Reierson, reprinted by Hi
2 CONC gginson Book Company, Salem MA. FIELD Name: ShortFootnote FIELD Name: Bibliography Repository: #R48 Call Number: 929.273 F732r Page: 4 TMPLT FIELD Name: Page VALUE 4 Data: Text: 10. Elizabeth Fones was born January 21, 1610 in Gro
2 CONC ton Suffolk England, and died 1669 in New York. She married (1) William Hallett, Sr. She married (2) Henry Winthrop April 25, 1629 in St. Sepulchre New London, London, England. She married (3) Robert Feake December 02, 1631 in Wat
2 CONC ertown Middlesex Massachusetts.
2 CONT Repository: R48 Name: Family History Library Address: 35 N West Temple Street Salt Lake City, Utah 84150 Address 1: 35 N West Temple Street City: Salt Lake City State: Utah Postal Code: 84150
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT From an unidentified source: "In 1655, William Hallett was one of the colonists who purchased a tract of land which became the Town of Jamaica. Jamaica, Newtown and Flushing Townships comprise modern Queens. In 1664, William Halle
2 CONC tt visited Astoria and bought 2,200 acres there, comprising all of modern Astoria and Steinway. In 1680, William Hallett was appointed overseer by Newtown Township. He died in 1706, age 90, after a long and prosperous life"
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT William Hallett, a son of Richard Hallett, was born about 1616 in Bridgeport, Dorchester, England and died August 17, 1729 in Newtown, Queen County, NY.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He married:
2 CONT # Rebecca Bailey. She was born about 1629 in England.  
2 CONT # Elizabeth "Bess" (Fones) [Winthrop] in August 1649.  Elizabeth, a daughter of Thomas and Anne (Winthrop) Fones, was born January 21, 1609/10 in Groton Manor, Suffolk, England. She married to Henry Winthrop April 25, 1629 in Engl
2 CONC and, and one year later Henry set out for America aboard the "Talbot".  The pregnant Elizabeth was to follow later with her step-mother-in-law, the governor's third wife (who was also pregnant at the time). On July 2, 1630, the ve
2 CONC ry day of his arrival in the New World at Salem, MS, Henry went for a swim at a nearby Indian village. While making for a boat across the bay he suffered a severe cramp and drowned in full sight of his friends.  Elizabeth gave bir
2 CONC th on May 9, 1630 to a daughter named Martha Johanna. On  November 2, 1631, she arrived in Massachusetts aboard the Lyon with  her daughter and without a husband.The situation did not last for long. Within three months of her land
2 CONC fall she had met and married Robert Feake (c.1602-c.1662), a landowner of some means who had arrived with the Winthrop fleet the prior year. Their union produced five children, including Hannah Feake, who married John Bowne. Rober
2 CONC t Feake subsequently went insane as a result of personal, domestic, and financial troubles. Elizabeth left him for his business manager, William Hallett. They were eventually recognized as husband and wife and settled in the New N
2 CONC etherlands colony in what became Halletts Cove, Long Island. Elizabeth (Fones) [Winthrop] {Feake} Hallett died about 1656 and William Hallett married
2 CONT #  Susannah Thorne in April 1674. She was born in New Town, Long Island, NY.
2 CONT
2 CONT William arrived in Massachusetts Bay Colony in 1631 aboard the "Lyon." He was a business manager for Robert Feake.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Henry and Elizabeth (Fones) Winthrop.(Step child of William Hallett)
2 CONT
2 CONT 12.  (1). Martha Johanna          b.May  9 1630
2 CONT
2 CONT Known children of Robert and Elizabeth (Fones) [Winthrop] Feake. (Step Children of William Hallett.)
2 CONT
2 CONT 13. (1). Hannah    m. John Bowne
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of William and Elizabeth (Fones) [Winthrop] {Feake} Hallett.
2 CONT
2 CONT 14.  (1). William                 b.c.    1648    d. Aug 18 1729       m. Sarah Wollsey Dec  1 1669       m. Mary <nowiki>-----</nowiki>       m. Ruth <nowiki>------</nowiki> Feb 1 1692/93
2 CONT
2 CONT 15.  (2). Samuel                 b.c.    1650    d.  1727 m. Bridget Blackwell
2 CONT
2 CONT NEHG Register 48:216 (1894) contained this query: (...) Early in 1647 Robert Feke (Feake) went to England, leaving his lands in charge of William Hallett (...) and Mrs. Feke. In August 1647, we have a hint at an improper intimacy 
2 CONC between Hallett and Mrs. Feke; this is again mentioned in March, 1648; in April 1648 Mrs. Hallett is with child, and in July of the same year there is again some mention as to the validity of her marriage with Hallett. (a list of 
2 CONC references appears here) Any information that might lead to the date of this marriage would be very welcome (...).
2 CONT
2 CONT Feake returned from England sometime before September 1649 to find his wife had taken up with his former friend, and that she had sold off all his lands to Robert Husted and the Dutch Governor. No doubt these events contributed in
2 CONC  large measure to his subsequent "loss of reason".
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Genealogical record of the Saint Nicholas Society: Advanced sheets, first series By Saint Nicholas Society of the City of New York
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT William Hallett was born about 1615 in Bridport, Dorsetshire. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Dorchester legend, William spent 6 years (from age 8-14) as page in the household of Earl of Bristol at Sherborne Castle. He played with the young Digbys and was tutored by their tutor. For a time, he was befriended b
2 CONC y the heir Lord George.
2 CONT
2 CONT At 14, he was apprenticed to a joiner in Dorchester, England. A joiner does similar work to a carpenter but fit joints rather than use nails. As an apprentice, William was treated well. He was reportedly beaten daily and given lit
2 CONC tle to eat- just bowls of slop. After a year, he ran away to his brother who gave him 40 pounds and told him to be off quickly before his master caught him.
2 CONT
2 CONT So, William paid for his voyage to the US in 1631…on the same ship that carried over Bess Fones and her mother-in-law Mary Winthrop. The sailing was the second voyage of the Lyon to Plymouth/Salem Massachusetts.
2 CONT
2 CONT William worked in Virginia for 2 years and then ran a trading post for a wealthy Connecticut man (William Whiting). This trading post was on the Delaware River where the Swedes were trying to establish a colony. The trading post w
2 CONC as a business failure.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the trading post venture failed, William returned to England to fight with his boyhood friend, George Digby (2nd Earl of Bristol and a Roman Catholic) who sometimes fought for the King and sometimes for the opposition. After
2 CONC  that, he traveled to the West Indies- Nevis, St, Christopher, Antigua.
2 CONT
2 CONT St. Christopher, St. Kitts and Nevis share a long history of British colonization. St. Christopher was the first English colony in the Caribbean. A small group of Britons established themselves at Sandy Bay in 1623. Nevis was colo
2 CONC nized in 1628 by an English party dispatched from St.Kitts. Anthony Hilton was accompanied by 80 other settlers, soon to be boosted by a further 100 settlers from London who had originally hoped to settle Barbuda. Hilton became th
2 CONC e first Governor of Nevis. Between 1675 and 1730, the island was the headquarter for the slave trade for the Leeward Islands, with approximately 6,000-7,000 enslaved West Africans passing through on route to other islands each yea
2 CONC r. The Royal African Company brought all its ships through Nevis.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT William left the islands years before the slave trade started. He returned to New York and reconnected with Bess Fones . He married her in 1649 in New Amsterdam, New York when he was about 34. William Hallett was her third husband
2 CONC . They had two sons – William and Samuel who are both our direct ancestors as you will discover as their stories unfold.
2 CONT
2 CONT  “On Dec. 1st, 1652, William Hallett obtained a brief for 161 acres of land, which had previously been in possession of Jacques Bentyu, one of Director Van Twiller's council. It was described as "a plot of ground at Hellegat upon 
2 CONC Long Island, called Jacques' farm, and beginning at a great rock that lies in the meadow, goes upward southeast to the end of a very small swamp, two hundred and ten rods; from thence northeast two hundred and thirty rods; on the 
2 CONC north it goes up to a running water, two hundred and ten rods; containing, in the whole, eighty morgen and three hundred rods." This tract is now included within the village of Astoria.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Helle-gat or Hell gate was a narrow straight on the Sound about 6 miles north of New York. It was dangerous to shipping because of numerous rocks, shelves and whirlpools. The name appears in a map in Vander Donck's history publish
2 CONC ed in 1656 - by Olgivie's history of America 1671.
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1653, Bess wrote a letter to her cousin John Winthrop Jr. “ Our habitation is by the whirlpool which the Dutchmen call the Hellgate where we have purchased a very good farm through the governor's means ... we live very 
2 CONC comfortably according to our rank. In the spring the Indian killed four Dutchmen near to our house which made us think to have removed ... yet now the Indian are quiet and we think not yet to remove.”
2 CONT
2 CONT In the fall of 1655, the Indians destroyed their house and plantation at Hallett's Cove, which induced them to take up his residence at Flushing. Here, William was appointed "Schout" or chief official of Flushing in 1656. That sam
2 CONC e year he was deposed by Governor Stuyvesant who had him fined and imprisoned for entertaining the Rev. William Wickenden, a Quaker, from Rhode Island, allowing him to preach at his house and receiving the sacrament of the Lord's 
2 CONC Supper from his hands. Disgusted at this treatment, William Hallett warmly advocated the claims of Connecticut during the revolt of Long Island from the Dutch. He was sent as a delegate to the general court of that colony and appo
2 CONC inted a commissioner or justice of the peace for Flushing.
2 CONT
2 CONT “Another purchase, not less interesting, was that effected August 1st, 1664, by " William Hallett, Senior of the town of Flushing," of a large tract of land, near Hallett's Cove, from Shawestcont and Erramohar, Indians residing at
2 CONC  Shawcopshee, upon Staten Island, by authority of Mattano, their sagamore, and in the presence of two Indians, Warchan and Kethcaneparan, and Eandell Hewitt, John Coe, Jonathan Rite, and Edward Fisher. It is described as " beginni
2 CONC ng at the first creek called Sunswick; westward below Hellgate, upon Long Island, and from the mouth of the aforesaid creek, south to a markt tree fast by a great rock, and from that said markt tree southward, fifteen score rods, 
2 CONC to another markt tree, which stands from another little rock a little westward, and from that markt tree east, right to the point of an island which belongs to the poor's bouwery, and from the point of the island belonging to the 
2 CONC poor's bouwery round by the river through Hellgate” This tract, called by the Indians " Sintsiuck," and embracing nearlythe whole of " Hellgate Neck," was afterwards confirmed to Hallett by the English governors NicoU and Dongan, 
2 CONC or "so much of the aforesaid Indian deed or purchase, as had not before been disposed of to others by ground brief or patent."
2 CONT
2 CONT “Those who sold Hellgate Neck to William Hallett, were of the Canarsee tribe, a clan of reputed power, whose jurisdiction extended over the whole of King's county, the islands in Hellgate, and says Ocallaghan, some part of Newtown
2 CONC . The extinction of the Indian title to the soil forms an interesting epoch in the history of the town. The red man was no longer able to withstand the advance of civilization; the country began to wear marks of human thrift that 
2 CONC made it uncongenial with his ideas of wild solitude and savage life his hunting-grounds invaded, the deer and the beaver driven from their haunts, he must needs seek for himself a new home in the unbroken forests. It is probable t
2 CONC hat the most of them vacated the town at about the period of their last sale to the whites, though there is evidence that scattering ones remained for a number of years later, some of whom had their wigwams at Mespat Kills.”
2 CONT
2 CONT After their land purchase, William and Bess moved back to Hellgate. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bess died in about 1672. William was a town overseer from 1679-81. He did marry again- Rebecca Baylies He divided his property in Hellgate Neck between his two sons William- and Samuel in 1688. William was Commissioner of Town Cou
2 CONC rt from 1688-89.
2 CONT
2 CONT William died prosperous at the age of 94. His will is dated Newtown, Queens Co, 17 Apr 1706 
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT * Reference: [http://www.public.iastate.edu/~chart/OpenTree/ElizabethFones.html Open Tree]
