2 CONT Rose/Rosamond Thorne was born about 1650.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT She and Nathaniel Soule were married about 1680, most probably in Duxbury. Their first child, also Nathaniel, was born 12 January 1681.[2][1]
2 CONT
2 CONT "Rose Soule died in her 104th year." The following is quoted from the Ridlon Book about the Soule family, quoting Wesson Soule.[3]"Wesson Soule. His Book of Accounts 1792. "I was born the 8 day of January in the year of our Lord 1
2 CONC 735. My great-grand-father George Soule was one of the 42 that first landed in New England. You may read of him in the New England Memorial. His wife's name was Rosamond Thorn. She died in her 104 year. I remember hi[e]r well. My 
2 CONC fathers name was Nathaniel." NOTE: "This sounds like George the immigrants wife, was Rose Thorn, but since we know George was married to a Mary, and son Nathaniel was married to a Rose, we have to assume that Wesson was rambling."
2 CONT
2 CONT If she was born about 1650 that would make her death in 1754, when her grandson Wesson Soule was 20 years old, as Wesson related in 1792: "I remember hir well."
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: Before May 1708, Dartmouth, Bristol, Massachusetts[2] which is before the birth of her grandson Wesson Soule in 1735---so is not chronologically possible. Death: After 15 May 1702, Dartmouth, Bristol, Massachusetts. This is
2 CONC  the date of the division of husband Nathaniel's estate, in which the widow received her thirds.[1] Death: after 19 April 1726, when she was a witness to the Quaker marriage of John Tibbetts and Sarah Soule
2 CONT
2 CONT Note
2 CONT
2 CONT In Section "The Swansea Branch of Soules" of [4]:
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathaniel Soule, son of George and Mary Beckett Soule, married Rose Thorn, by whom there were four sons (and probably daughters whose names are unknown).
