1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==from https://www.thornfamily.net/williamthorne1/d4408.htm#P4408 ==
2 CONT
2 CONT 273. Stephen Thorne was born about 1750 in Hempstead, Long Island, NY. He was christened on 29 Jul 1756 in Flushing, Long Island, NY. He died on 24 Oct 1814 in New York. Stephen Thorne is believed to have remained in New York, aft
2 CONC er his father & brother removed to Nova Scotia.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married to Sarah Platt '''''††''''' on 14 Oct 1773 in New York. Stephen Thorne and Sarah Platt had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT child526 i. Stephen Thorne was born on 2 Jul 1774. He died on 25 May 1828 in New York City, NY.
2 CONT child527 ii. William K. Thorne.
2 CONT child+528 iii. Sallie Thorne.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married to Sarah Kippen on 23 Feb 1779 in New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''††''''': Was it Platt or Kippen ? See: https://www.geni.com/documents/view/marriage_event-6000000218751383845?doc_id=6000000218834064833&event_id=6000000218751383845
2 CONT He may have had two wives, both named Sarah...
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT see: https://www.geni.com/list/descendants/6000000029594766036#6
2 CONT
2 CONT ~• Although part of a Loyalist family which was deported to Canada at the end of the Revolution, this Stephen did not go to Nova Scotia while his mother, father, and siblings did.  See [https://www.geni.com/documents/view/marriage
2 CONC _event-6000000218751383845?doc_id=6000000218834064833&event_id=6000000218751383845 source]
2 CONT When the British forces arrives on Long Island Stephen was already in his 30s. 
2 CONT His father then chose to become a Captain in a British Regiment.
2 CONT It is unclear as to how our Stephen ''Jr'', negotiated those War years while Howe's forces were posted throughout the region.
2 CONT After the war he remained in Lower Manhattan where he seems to have prospered as a merchant, Ties remained strong with his parents who were buried in Canada.
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/path/Leffert-Lefferts+is+related+to+Stephen-Thorne-of-the-South-St-Seaport?from=6000000029964382256&path_type=inlaw&to=6000000206211653201 Note the relationship] of the deceased Stephen,Jr. with one of the ex
2 CONC ecutors. Leffert Lefferts was a successful judge and his mother's nephew. Some Lefferts family were noted 'Patriots'.
2 CONT > His last residence was John St. by the South St. seaport, Lower Manhattan
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Research==
2 CONT As John St is near South and Beekman, the following clipping may have to do with this family:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT > may have also ran a Lottery: https://www.newspapers.com/image/37450110/?match=1&terms=%22Stephen%20thorne%22
2 CONT The Evening Post
2 CONT Wed, Jun 23, 1813 ·Page 4
2 CONT
2 CONT > aka Stephen Thorne, Jr. whose estate held on to property at  [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000218868738828 '''''417 Broome St.'''''] after his death. See: The Evening Post [https://www.newspapers.com/image/4088
2 CONC 8646/?match=1&clipping_id=new Tue, Jan 27, 1829 ·Page 3]
2 CONT
2 CONT >Seems the sale was not to occur for a while as we read in The Evening Post
2 CONT New York, New York · Monday, January 09, 1832  that it was still for sale.
2 CONT
