1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ANNALS of OUR COLONIAL ANCESTORS AND THEIR DESCENDANTS
2 CONT
2 CONT A. M. Shotwell
2 CONT
2 CONT pg. 96
2 CONT
2 CONT "Benjamin Shotwell married Bathsheba pound in the old Plainfield Friends Meeting House.On March 11, 1783, he was elected by the town of Woodbridge, New Jersey as one of the surveyors of the roads for the coming year.  On August 15
2 CONC , 1787 he and Bathsheba received a certificate from Rahway and Plainfield Monthly Meeting to Kingwood Monthly Meeting of Friends. Sometime after 1810 they removed from Warren Co., New Jersey to Seneca Co., New York.  According to 
2 CONC Shotwell (page 93) he was a farmer and a consistent Friend. His nephew William Shotwell was heard to say that when Benjamin visited his father's house he ate his dinner without removing his hat. He was deaf for many years and in h
2 CONC er later years Bathsheba went blind."
