1 NAME John /Scarborough/ III
2 GIVN John
2 SURN Scarborough
2 NSFX III
2 _MARNM Scarborough
2 NICK John Balderston Scarborough
1 SEX M
1 OCCU unknown
2 PLAC Farmer
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE BET 10 FEB 1667 AND 26 DEC 1667
2 ADDR
3 CITY London
3 STAE England
1 BURI
2 DATE 27 APR 1727
2 PLAC Buckingham Friends Burial Ground
2 ADDR
3 CITY Lahaska
3 STAE Pennsylvania
1 DEAT
2 DATE 27 APR 1727
2 ADDR
3 CITY Buckingham
3 STAE Province of Pennsylvania
1 OCCU farmer and a minister., blacksmith
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 1667-John Scarborough Jr. was born in London.  He also had a sister Sarah 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1682 On July 4 John Sr, his father, bought 250 acres of land in Pennsylvania from William Penn.  He then left for America in late October with his son John Jr.  His wife who was not Quaker remained in England.  The land that he ha
2 CONC d bought was in Middletown Township, near the present site of Langhorne, PA.  He was one of the first settlers and remained there two years and built a rude home.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1684- John Sr. returned to England to bring his wife back to PA.  But they never returned.  After John Sr. arrived in London, the Quaker persecutions had let up some and so he stayed there.  Also it is said that his wife was afrai
2 CONC d of the ocean.  John Jr. had been  left with neighbors that he did not like and went away to live among the Indians. ''' He married an Indian girl named Mary Pearson/Pierson'''.  They are believed to have had eight children ''{•~
2 CONC no sourcing for this allegation as yet} ~ MMvB vol. curator''. ''There are two other alternate genealogies that do not include a Native American element''
2 CONT
2 CONT 1691- Their first child William was born.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1700- John Jr. sold his land near Middletown and moved to 500 acres near {Solebury}Township, Bucks Co. PA.  John Jr. was a Quaker minister as well as a farmer.  His son John III became a quite noted minister and at his death his f
2 CONC uneral had the largest attendance of any funeral that had been in the county up to that time.<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT 1969- In the twentieth century part of John Scarborough's Sloebury land became protected under a National Historic Landmark District. It is known as [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honey_Hollow_Watershed '''''Honey Hollow Watershed
2 CONC ''''']
2 CONT
2 CONT John came to America with his father and when his father returned to England, he stayed here. It is said that he ran away with the Indians for five years and later served as William Penn’s interpreter. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1700 he sold a farm in Middletown Township, Bucks County, PA, and bought 510 acres in Solebury, Township, Bucks County, PA, and moved there. In 1705 he traded for 520 acres adjoining it near Lahaska Station. In 1709 he traded h
2 CONC is farm for 820 acres with Jacob Holcombe. He was thought to be the first white settler in the Buckingham-Solebury valley. Part of his farm was later owned by a third great grandson, [https://www.geni.com/people/Isaac-Scarborough/
2 CONC 6000000089273444886 '''ISSAC PEARSON SCARBOROUGH''']. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John was a farmer and a minister. He and Mary were members of the Middletown and Falls MM. On 3 Sep 1795 ''(?1695?)''John was condemned by the Falls MM for too much celebrating of so called Christmas the previous year. On 10-2-172
2 CONC 4 he was recorded a minister. John and Mary were charter members of the Buckingham MM, Bucks County, PA where he was memorialized: "in his youth somewhat airy… a man of remarkable self-denial and endowed with much wildness.. with 
2 CONC loving and kind deportment". (from scarbeary.com) ''{note: this source '''does not''' maintain that John married an Indian}''
2 CONT
2 CONT Another account:
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in London, England in 1667 came to Pennsylvania at age of 15. His father returned to England, and John lived with Indians for 10 years & learned their language. The will of John Scarborough Jr, on October 2, 1727 (See Early S
2 CONC ettlers of Solebury Township, Bucks Co. PA) 
2 CONT
2 CONT "To my son Wm. Scarborough 60 acres with the little meadow therein wch he has now in his possesion.." " to my loving wife [https://www.geni.com/people/Mary-Scarborough/6000000000006700426 '''''Mary Scarborough'''''] my plantation 
2 CONC Conty 200 acres of land having the lake meadow therein for and during her natural life and after his Decease I will yt to my son Wm. enjoy the seme for and During his natural life and after his Decease to ye Male Heirs of his Body
2 CONC  lawfully Begotten and if there be none such at the Death of my son Wm. Then my will is thet the Said Plantation get to the next male heir at Law of the Scarborough's" 
2 CONT
2 CONT Also he he left some building lots in Philadelphia to be divided among his sons and the following: "To my son Wm. my Bible and my brass mortar. To my son Robert my Silver dram cup and my Pewter Salt Seller to my daughter Sarah Haw
2 CONC orth my brass Warming pan To my daughter Mary Oichring my great Iron Pottage Putt to mydaughter [https://www.geni.com/people/Elizabeth-Fisher/6000000000912711598 Elizth Fisher] my Silver Spoon and a Pinquishin with a drawer for it
2 CONC  and a Pewter Still to my daughter Hannah one Brass Candlestick and a Pair of Iron Snuffers and a Joint Stool" 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT John Scarborough was born in London in 1667. As a young man, he came
2 CONT
2 CONT with his father to Pennsylvania in 1682. He was to remain here for the rest of his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT John spent some ten years in the then wilds of Bucks County living with the Indians before he
2 CONT
2 CONT became married.
2 CONT
2 CONT He and his wife Mary moved to Solebury on 1700 and bought land there in 1705, built several cabins on their land and gave shelter to poorer members of the family when they needed it. Being great workers themselves, they had no use
2 CONC  for loafers. They were members of the Buckingham MM,  (Quakers) John is memorialized in meeting records. "In his youth somewhat airy........a man of remarkable self-denial and endured with much mildness......with loving and kind 
2 CONC deportment and may faithful services, impressed our minds with a deep sense of his worth and our great loss."
2 CONT
2 CONT INFO FROM ANOTHER SITE:
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHN SCARBOROUGH, b 1667 London, England, d 27 Apr 1727 Solebury Township, Bucks County, PA at 60 years old. He was buried, Buckingham FBG, Bucks County, PA.
2 CONT
2 CONT John married c1690, Middletown MM, Bucks County, PA, MARY (PIERSON)?. No proof has been found concerning Mary’s maiden name. Mary was born c1676, Midletown, Middletown Township, Bucks County, PA, died 1-23-1751 at nearly 85 years 
2 CONC old. Mary married 2] PHILIP TORREY.
2 CONT
2 CONT John came to America with his father and when his father returned to England, he stayed here. It is said that he ran away with the Indians for five years and later served as William Penn’s interpreter.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1700 he sold a farm in Middletown Township, Bucks County, PA, and bought 510 acres in Solebury, Township, Bucks County, PA, and moved there. In 1705 he traded for 520 acres adjoining it near Lahaska Station. In 1709 he traded h
2 CONC is farm for 820 acres with Jacob Holcombe. He was thought to be the first white settler in the Buckingham-Solebury valley. Part of his farm was later owned by a third great grandson, ISSAC PEARSON SCARBOROUGH.
2 CONT
2 CONT John was a farmer and a minister. He and Mary were members of the Middletown and Falls MM. On 3 Sep 1795 John was condemned by the Falls MM for too much celebrating of so called Christmas the previous year. On 10-2-1724 he was rec
2 CONC orded a minister. John and Mary were charter members of the Buckingham MM, Bucks County, PA where he was memorialized: "in his youth somewhat airy… a man of remarkable self-denial and endowed with much wildness.. with loving and k
2 CONC ind deportment".
2 CONT
2 CONT His will was dated 13 March 1726/7 and was confirmed 2 Oct 1727 by the original witnesses at Doylestown, Bucks
2 CONT --------------------
