2 DATE 18 APR 1720
2 ADDR
3 CITY Newtown
3 STAE Pennsylvania
3 CTRY British Colonial America
1 OCCU Became large landowner, prominent, influntial citizen
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} "The 500 acres of Thomas Rowland, extending from Newtown creek to Neshaminy, probably included the ground the Presbyterian church stands upon. It was owned by Henry Baker in 1691, who conveyed 248 acres to Job Bunt
2 CONC ing in June 1692, and in October 1697 the remainder, 252 acres, to Stephen Wilson . In 1695 Bunting conveyed his acres to Stephen Twining , and in 1698 Wilson did the same, and Twining now owned Thomas Rowland's whole tract. In 17
2 CONC 57 part or the whole of this land was in the possession of Benjamin Twining. In 1702 Stephen Twining owned 690 acres in Newtown, which John Cutler surveyed March 10th. " from ~• http://files.usgwarchives.net/pa/bucks/history/local
2 CONC /davis/davis15.txt 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Bucks County Will Abstract
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.48.  STEPHEN TWINING of Newtown.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 12th mo., 20th day, 1719/20.  Proved May 19, 1720.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sons John and Stephen, Plantation at Newtown bought of John Ward adj. land formerly Michael Hough's. 
2 CONT * Son John, land left me by Government of New England. 
2 CONT * Sons Eleazer and Nathaniel, land adj. Wm. Buckman, where Jos. Lupton lived, 230 acres.  
2 CONT * To daus. Rachel Twining and Mercy Lupton, grandchildren each 5 shillings 
2 CONT * Sons Stephen and Nathaniel exrs.
2 CONT * Wit: James and Agnes Yeates and Abrm. Chapman.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===modern newspaper article===
2 CONT "It was David Twining's grandfather, Stephen, who according to a 1703 survey, had bought up 690 acres of land establishing the family in Newtown. That acreage included a 500-acre tract of fertile farmland bordered by Swamp Road, t
2 CONC he Newtown Creek and the Neshaminy Creek.
2 CONT
2 CONT David Twining was a farmer, a businessman and a two-term member of the Pennsylvania General Assembly. He also served as a librarian and founding member of the Newtown Library Company. In its early days, the library's books were ke
2 CONC pt at Twining's house.
2 CONT
2 CONT Newtown historian Edward R. Barnsley likened Twining to Benjamin Franklin when it came to libraries." ~• from: https://patch.com/pennsylvania/newtown-pa/twining-farm-home-to-one-of-the-most-intelligent-farm09fa4e185b 
