3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE BET 1724 AND 1753
2 ADDR
3 CITY Newtown
3 STAE PA
3 POST 18940
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me}     Nathaniel appears to have been a man of more than ordinary note andstrong
2 CONT
2 CONT     individuality. He lived and died on the 300 acres inherited from his
2 CONT
2 CONT     father in Newtown. He and wife were disowned from Friends after 1730. On
2 CONT
2 CONT     the "Sixth day of May, 1742," he ordains his "loving brother JohnTwining,
2 CONT
2 CONT     yeoman, my lawful attorney, to demand, recover and receive dues andother
2 CONT
2 CONT     demands in the Province of New England, or any other adjacentprovinces."
2 CONT
2 CONT     This nicely executed and well preserved writing is in possession ofhis
2 CONT
2 CONT     gr.-gr.-gr.-grandson, the author of these records. December 1, 1744,he
2 CONT
2 CONT     and wife Sarah deed "in trust" one acre of land "for a meeting houseand
2 CONT
2 CONT     burial place," to the trustees of the Newtown Presbyterian church. The
2 CONT
2 CONT     said house of worship was erected 1769; is standing as seen in the
2 CONT
2 CONT     accompanying cut. It is probably they became identified with this
2 CONT
2 CONT     society, if any. He married 1) Joan Penquite (John and Agnus), 1719;born
2 CONT
2 CONT     1696; died 1720; married 2) 23 Jul 1723 Sarah Kirk; born 1698. The
2 CONT
2 CONT     Probate records show that he died intestate 1753, his widow the sameyear.
2 CONT
2 CONT     The 300 acres of his estate were divided about equally between hissons:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Samuel, Benjamin, Nathaniel and daughter Sarah.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Abbrev: Imported GEDCOM file
2 CONT
