1 NAME William O. /Lupton/
2 GIVN William O.
2 _MARNM Lupton
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1840
2 ADDR
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY United States
1 BURI
2 DATE 1862
2 PLAC Old City Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Lynchburg
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 26 APR 1862
2 ADDR
3 CITY Lynchburg
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU
1 TITL Lieutenant (CSA),
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Civil War Veteran'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Affiliation: Confederacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Rank: 2nd Lt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Co.: G
2 CONT
2 CONT Regt.:114th Va.
2 CONT
2 CONT Branch:  Militia 
2 CONT
2 CONT Additional Service: Ashby's 7th Regt., Calvary; volunteered March 1, 1862.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT 7th Virginia Cavalry
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT History
2 CONT
2 CONT The regiment was initially assigned to guarding the upper Potomac and was attached to the command of Thomas "Stonewall" Jackson in the Valley. In the spring of 1862 the regiment took part in Jackson's Valley Campaign, where the ex
2 CONC ploits of the unit and its commander, Turner Ashby, became famous on both sides of the war. Near the conclusion of the campaign, Ashby was mortally wounded and Col. Richard Henry Dulany took command of the regiment, which had swel
2 CONC led to 29 companies. The regiment was reorganized at the end of the campaign, with the original 10 companies remaining and the excess 19 forming the 12th Regiment and 17th Battalion of Virginia Cavalry. Together with these two reg
2 CONC iments, the 7th would become the nucleus of the famed Laurel Brigade.
2 CONT
2 CONT As part of the brigade, the 7th saw major action during the Gettysburg Campaign in 1863 and was at the famed cavalry Battle of Brandy Station that same year. They took part in Jubal A. Early's ill-fated Valley Campaigns of 1864 an
2 CONC d were at Appomattox Courthouse, though much of the unit escaped through federal lines and returned home to disband rather than taking parole with the rest of the Army of Northern Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Link''':https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/7th_Virginia_Cavalry
2 CONT
2 CONT Co B 7th Va Cav CSA
2 CONT
2 CONT (Last name is difficult to read in records.  Appears to be spelled as Olupton.  Member LJH has been gracious enough to clear up the confusion.  This soldier's last name was Lupton.)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1850 US Census shows him living with his father Jesse and mother Martha along with his brothers Samuel, James, Jonathan and Jesse Jr in Hampshire County, Virginia.  1860 Census had him living not far away in Clarke County.  He was
2 CONC  assigned to the 114th Va Militia after volunteering for service.  Then volunteered for Col Turner Ashby's 7th Cavalry which was a part of Stonewall Jackson's Valley campaign.
2 CONT
