2 CONT
2 CONT Granddaughter Evelyn says he was a civil war veteran, but perhaps she meant his father - William Ashley Anderson was only a child at the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT =================Obit=================
2 CONT
2 CONT W.A. Anderson, 91, Dies Quietly In His Own Home Monday
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT Beloved Ord Man, Resident 75 Years, Suffered Several Heart Attacks, Died
2 CONT
2 CONT --
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services will be held at the Frazier chapel at 1:30 pm Friday for W.A. Anderson, beloved resident of Ord, who died quietly at his home Monday afternoon after a series of heart attacks.  He had been a resident of Valley Cou
2 CONC nty 75 years, and had lived in Ord since 1884, during which period he was one of this city's most widely respected citizens.  Several years ago Mr. Anderson gave to the city the North Loup river island park which bears his name.  
2 CONC His body will be laid to rest in the Ord cemetery beside his second wife, who passed away in 1937.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Ashley Anderson, son of William I. and Hester Hillman Anderson was born at Bankston, Dubuque Co. Ia. on April 14, 1853, and departed this life July 3, 1944, aged 91 years, 2 months, and 19 days.  He was of a family of 12 c
2 CONC hildren, only one of whom, Mrs. Minnie Campbell, survives him.
2 CONT
2 CONT He attended grade school and later the Epworth Seminary at Epworth, Ia.  After teaching a number of terms in his home couty he also taught near Red Oak, Ia. and later in several schools in Valley county.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Sept.5, 1876, he was married to Elizabeth Anne Hather, at John's Creek, Ia.  To this union were born five children, the three older dying in infancy.  Surviving are Mrs. Mabel Abernethy and Roy W. Anderson.
2 CONT
2 CONT In March, 1879, he and his family moved by covered wagon from Western Iowa, near Red Oak, to Valley county, where he took up a homestead and a tree claim, in the present Plain Valley community.  The family endured all the hardship
2 CONC s of the early pioneers - prairie fires, drought, grasshoppers, etc.  He dug the first well on the Sand Flats, stopping when he found stratas of sand, and went to the canyons near Burwell to cut cottonwood logs, to be hauled to th
2 CONC e mill and sawed into curbing for his well.  To one of the present generation, this would seem to be an endless task.  He also did freighting from Grand Island to the village of Ord.  He was an ardent hunter of all wild game at th
2 CONC is time and being a very good marksman, kept his family supplied with meat in this way.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1884 after "proving up" on his government land, he moved to Ord and resided in the same house until the time of his death, with the exception of a short period spent in California, where his first wife died and is buried.  
2 CONT
2 CONT On moving to Ord, Mr. Anderson had engaged in the photographic business and many of his photographs of early citizens and residences have at different times been published in the Quiz.  On July 25, 1893, he was married to Sarah O.
2 CONC  Snodgrass, of Farley, Ia, who was his faithful companion and mother to his two children until she departed this life Jan. 9, 1937.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Anderson was a writer of no mean ability, a number of his articles on pioneer life, hunting etc., were not only published in the Quiz, but also in numerous state publications.  He was a great reader and was well posted on almo
2 CONC st any subject one might bring up, and retained his interest in politics, wold news, science, and inventions to the last.
2 CONT
2 CONT He helped to organize the Unitarian church in Ord and was instrumental in establishing the first public library.  A number of years ago he donated Anderson Island to the city of Ord to be used as a public park, was a member of the
2 CONC  park board, of the Ord school board and Ord township boards for many years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since his second wife's death Mrs. Emma Hanson has been his housekeeper, until seven weeks ago when she was called away by the death of a sister.  For five weeks, he then boarded with Mrs. Mina Jensen next door to him.  After suff
2 CONC ering several heart attacks, he evidently felt the premonition of death and desired to be in his own old home for the event.  Mrs. Clyde Kelley assisted by Mr.s Mina Jensen and his daughter, Mrs. Jason Abernethy, gave him every at
2 CONC tention until his passing Monday morning.
2 CONT
2 CONT He leaves to mourn his loss one daughter, Mrs. Jason Abernehty, his son, Roy W. Anderson having passed on in 1932.  Also four granddaughters and one grandson, ten great-grandchildren and his sister, Mrs. Minnie Campbell.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Anderson had the ability to make and keep friends and many happy hours were spent visiting with old and new friends.  Of late years, he had been greatly handicapped by increasing deafness.  He had the satisfaction of seeing Or
2 CONC d grow from a small frontier town to it's [sic] prsent size and add his support to it's [sic] betterment.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT end of obituary
2 CONT
2 CONT ------
2 CONT
2 CONT William Ashley Anderson was born in Iowa, 1853, and married Elizabeth Hather there, later coming to Nebraska to homestead near Ord. Some of his writings about the early days are attached to this narration. Both Mabel and Ray were 
2 CONC born on the homestead. When Grandpa Anderson had "proved up" on this farm, he sold it and, in 1888, the family sought the "West" for opportunity. Grandpa came by immigrant train to San Francirco, where Grandpa had a brother. Grand
2 CONC pa invested in a community land project in Tulare County and travelled there. While Elizabeth and the two children were on the way to join him, Elizabeth became ill and died. Abject with grief, Grandpa returned to Nebraska, and ta
2 CONC ught school and travelled as a salesman. Three years later he married an Iowa friend, Sarah Snodgrass, and returned to Ord to make his home with her and the two children. "Grandma Anderson" was a pleasant, cheerful stepmother and 
2 CONC grandmother, always welcoming and hospitable, a wonderful cook, and noted for her sugar cookies. Grandpa Anderson built a house and, also, had other land holdings. There in Ord. Mabel and Roy grew up and finished Ord High, Roy pin
2 CONC g back to Iowa to become a business man. and Mabel, to teach school, and eventually marry Jason. Grandpa Anderson died in 1944 in Ord. Mabel had, also, worked for a time as a "Hello Girl" for the phone company. She taught school s
2 CONC ix years, the last three in the Joint neighborhood.\
2 CONT
2 CONT The following  was typed onto paper from a notebook of W.A. Anderson by Evelyn Howard, and retyped into Geni by Kristina Pereyra
2 CONT
2 CONT ===================
2 CONT
2 CONT Sketch of Life of W. A. Anderson
2 CONT
2 CONT This is something of the story of my life as I look back on it after 66 years and is only slight details.  I was born at what was called Bankston Prairie In Dubuque County Iowa on April 14, 1853.  my earliest recollections are of 
2 CONC the old home in a large brick house and with a beautiful grove of trees on the north and in front of house the public road and across the road, a very large barn.
2 CONT
2 CONT The road passed through my father's large farm and residence on one side road and farm buildings on the other.  Of the beauty of the grove of trees and the delightful and pleasna timtes I and my brothers and sisters had in our chi
2 CONC ldhood days, words would fail me in telling.  it would seem, as I recall, that all kinds of wild fruits grew there in the season such as plums, grapes, crabapples, several kinds of the wild haw and hzelnuts in the autumn and only 
2 CONC a short distance away were walnuts and hickory nuts in abundance.  Such times we had after wild strawberries in June in the meadows and playing in the old barn on rainy days with our cousins who lived a mile or so away, are among 
2 CONC my most pleasant recollections.    But when I was 7 years old my mother passed to the other life, leaving a baby sister, Lillian, only a week old.
2 CONT
2 CONT Our home was broken up but my father married again very soon and the wife he brought home should never have been chosen for a stepmother.  From there on there was discord, and my older sisters soon left home and the whole life of 
2 CONC the family was changed.  My father sold the farm and bought another about 10 miles away near Peosta, and near the railroad station.  We removed there in the spring of 1868.  The new farm was not as good soil nor in any way as valu
2 CONC able as the farm at Bankston but in some ways it was our advantage as we were near the railroad and socially  much better, as the people were Protestant people and generally American whereas at Bankston they were almost entirely I
2 CONC rish and Catholic of the most bigoted variety.  Here I went to school and Centralia & Peosta as well as in the country.  My father was a surveyor and as a result was mostly away  from home and consequently the farm was run by the 
2 CONC boys or a hired man and never paid expenses.  My father was always in debt after this marriage.  Not only that, but he had the habit of endorsing notes for friends and these notes he had to pay, and thus he lost money that should 
2 CONC have been used for the family.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The war of rebellion or Civil War had closed  about this time and many soldiers were returning to their families.  At the time there was much bitterness in evidence among neighbors as some were in sympathy with the South and other
2 CONC s were entirely partisan.  Some were in favor of hanging anyone who seemed in any way disloyal.  i recall being at church on a Sunday morning in April soon after the assassination of President Lincoln and one man made some remarks
2 CONC  unfavorable and a crowd at once got ropes from the horses tied among the trees and would have hung him at once had he not recanted his words.  Party feeling ran high and only a word brought on a fight and people were shot dead al
2 CONC most without excuse.
2 CONT
2 CONT My father had come from Kentucky and was a states rights man, though not a sympathizer with slavery as the family left Kentucky owing to their antipathy to slavery and its damnable conditions.  During these strenuous times my fath
2 CONC er always carried a revolver and often when at work in the woods he would suddenly draw out his revolver and begin shooting at a tree and was thus always in practice, and i recall he seldom failed to mark the tree at every shot.
2 CONT
2 CONT After and during the war were hard times for poor people.  Prices were very high on almost everything, and no gold or silver were in circulation but only paper money which we called "shin plasters".  Men were out of work and the r
2 CONC eturned soldiers increased the cheap labor.  There were all kinds of cheap goods on the market that were called "shoddy".  the garment would look fairly well for a few days but in a week or two looked like it had been worn for yea
2 CONC rs.
2 CONT
2 CONT My father had a large family to care for and I never had any underclothes of any kind until past 18 years of age and only wore a "hickory" shirt and denim overalls - winter and summer.  Why I did not freeze to death is now a myste
2 CONC ry to me but I guess a vigorous constitution is what saved me.  When about 18 years of age I attended the seminary at Epworth, Iowa for a term.  At this time my two older brothers passed to the other life.  After leaving the farm 
2 CONC I taught school for several terms in Dubuque County.  Once not far from Farley, Iowa and again north of Farley and once at Spring Valley, not far from Cascade.
2 CONT
2 CONT These were pleasant terms and I enjoyed them and life was passing pleasantly.  In spring of 1870 I went to Strawberry Point about 50 miles away, and remained there 8 months working for my uncle, who died while I was there.  I retu
2 CONC rned home in the autumn and at this period attended seminary.  In the spring of 1872 I went to southwest Iowa in Montgomery county where I spent two years.  I worked as a farmhand for various parties in and around the city of Red 
2 CONC Oak at an average wage of $18 per month.  In the autumn of 1874 I was taken with chills and in fact the genuiune "ague".  After a few weeks of chills I decided to get away from there and returned to Dubuque coutny again.  Of cours
2 CONC e very many changes had occurred in my absences.  IU went to school during the winter and the next spring engaged to work for Andrew Rae about amild from Farley.  At about this time I was in the habit of going to church and one Su
2 CONC nday, while at church, saw a young lady come in and as she passed down the aisle, I said to myself that I should try to win that girl for my wife although I had never met her and did not even know her name.  During the following w
2 CONC eek I met my cousin and he told me who he believed she was and where she lived.  I wrote a note to her and asked if I might call and be introduced to her.  She assented and I called as per an appointment on the next Sunday.  But o
2 CONC n being introduced to her I found she was not the girl I had noticed at church.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, we took a walk and became acquainted and so she seemed very nice and we kept company for several months and in July we were engaged to be married tho' no date was set.  The girl lived near Furnace, about 5 miles from Dubu
2 CONC que, that is her home was there though she was at this time living in Farley and doing housework for L.W. Carpenter.  I was in the habit of taking her to her home near Dubuque every two weeks and generally on Sunday.  Some time in
2 CONC  August I took her to her home and left her there for a visit with her folks.  i wrote to her during the week and the next week got a letter from her saying I had deceived her and she would have no further use for me.  I wrote and
2 CONC  asked for an explanation but she gave me none.  And then I demanded return of my letters and she sent them back.  I never got any explanation of why I was given the grand bounce but have never found out why I was turned down.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time I felt bad and grieved about it but my friend Mr. Rae told me it might be the best thing after all and later on I found he was right and if ever good fortune came my way it was when Alice Fern turned me down.  We can n
2 CONC ot always see the working of the Divine Providence we call "God" but I shall always believe that in this instance some unknown intelligence worked in my favor.
2 CONT
2 CONT That winter I taught school and in later part of the winter rented the farm of my grandfather, John Hillman, north of Farley.  I had saved up some money from my school work though salary was only $30 per month, at that time.  In t
2 CONC he spring I bought a team, harness, and wagon and began farming.  i got my board for help around the place when not doing work on my own.  I was to give one third of crop which was mostly corn and hay.  While not busy at my own wo
2 CONC rk I worked for others and so made some money to keep up ends.  
2 CONT
2 CONT One day while at Farley, I met the girl, face to face, whom I had seen at church that eventful Sunday.   This time I said there will be no mistake and after she passed I noted where she went and never stopped until I had her name 
2 CONC and where she lived.  There is a Divinity that shapes our ends and in this part of my life I have ever and always believed that I had His aid.  Of course I knew nothing of this young lady, only her looks and appearance and so far 
2 CONC as I knew she might have been engaged to some other man.  however, to me she was the one and only person on earth for my first impression at church when I first saw her come back only with ten times the force.  One thing I was res
2 CONC olved and that was that this time, there would be no mistake and she and only she would be my future wife.  Why I had so decided without even knowing her I can not tell, but her face at the time I first saw her seemed a photo of h
2 CONC er character and it really was,I found out later and to me from then on it was the only one and none other has ever been the same to me.
2 CONT
2 CONT I went home that night, perhaps the most thoughtful man I had ever been.  of course I was silent with my own deep thoughts and my grandmother said to me "What ails thee Willie" but, of course, I could give only an evasive answer. 
2 CONC  That night I sat down and wrote the girl a plain straightforward letter telling her who I was and where to inquire of me as to my character and referred her to various people and also told her of my hopes and ambitions in life an
2 CONC d told her where I had seen her and that I would like to make her acquaintance.  The next day I went to town and again met her while I had my letter to her in my pocket.  I posted the letter, the die was cast, and I went home with
2 CONC  all hopes and aspirations about my future life, all depending on the answer to my letter.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Not a word did I get in answer to my request that I might make her acquaintance.  She wrote that arrangements would be made so we could meet and the next Sunday her father invited me to go home to dinner with the family.  As they 
2 CONC lived 4 miles in the country, I accepted and during the ride to their home got more or less acquainted with the family.  At dinner I was introduced to the one and only girl.  Can I ever forget that day or that meeting?  Not while 
2 CONC life and memory endure.  After dinner I asked her to walk with me and we went to the nearby woods.  Thus a courtship began that ended in marriage.  In August we talked of marriage but I was so poor that it looked impossible.  We d
2 CONC iscussed the matter form every point and finally decided to risk marrying.  I don't think I had over $80 to cover all expenses for starting housekeeping.  I bought a table, some chairs and dishes and my grandfather gave me a stove
2 CONC  and my wife had bedding of her own, and also a cow.  So our new life began with less in a financial way of more of a spirit of true love I have never met anyone with  more of a spirit of deep love.
2 CONT
2 CONT I engaged a school for the winter and we were married September 5th, 1876.  I went on the farm work for the fall and began school in November.  A two mile walk to and from school gave me exercise as snow in the woods was sometimes
2 CONC  2 feet deep.  During the winter we decided we would go west in the spring and so made all our plans accordingly.  In March my term of school was out and we moved for a week to her folks while preparing for our trip west.  About M
2 CONC arch 30th we started west, headed for Red Oak, IA.  The snow was deep and the first day my brother-in-law put on a team ahead and went about 30 miles with us.  After that the roads got worse and in places seemed as tho' there was 
2 CONC no bottom.  in the morning it would be frozen and awfully rought and by noon hubcap in mud.  I think I never saw worse roads anywhere.  However, we reached the end of our trip sometime in April.  I rented some land and put up a ho
2 CONC use 8x10 ft on it and we were at home in our own house for the first time.  The summer passed and in August our first baby was born but alas it died at birth and we were desolate (1877).
2 CONT
2 CONT During the season of 1877 I grew over 1000 bushels of corn on 30 acres of rented land.  That winter I taught school again and my wife would be in the field when i retunred from school and we would get a load of corn out before dar
2 CONC k.  That way we got the corn husked and on Saturday I husked all day, and with her help we got 100 bushels in a day.  In March I hauled much of the corn to Red Oak to market and got 14 cents a bushel for it.  Many people burned co
2 CONC rn as fuel as a poor grade of coal cost $5 per son and the trouble of hauling it home added.  In the spring of 1878 I moved to another farm and tried raising flax but the crop was a failure and I really lost most of my season's wo
2 CONC rk.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pioneer Period
2 CONT
2 CONT In the fall of 1878 my wife and I concluded we had better go West into Kansas.  I had that idea in view to go out and look over the country (1878) and perhaps file a claim and then move in the spring.  I made such arrangements but
2 CONC  the more I neared the point of leaving my wife the more I did not like leaving her alone for nearly a month with the stock to care for as we now had the cow and her 2 calves, a yearling and a 2 year old.  I had intended to drive 
2 CONC the team on the western trip and to look over Kansas.  However, finally my wife said "We are going anyway in the spring, why not wait and only make the one trip".  So we put in the winter getting ready for the trip west.  I hauled
2 CONC  off and sold my corn at the 14 cents per bushel and my hogs at $3.65 and late in Feb. had our wagon all in shape to move in and start.
2 CONT
2 CONT I am not sure of the date we started, but about March 1st we drove north to Walnut, IA and visited with my Uncle Thomas Hillman and then we headed for the setting sun.  We followed the line of C.R.I & P Railroad to Council Bluffs.
2 CONC   After the first few days the roads were good and we made about 25 to 30 miles daily.  We camped out at night sleeping in our wagon.  I had our vow and the 2 heifers and at the start tried to lead them but it was a lot of trouble
2 CONC , so I put them in the road ahead of the team and they learned that this was their place and gave very little trouble and the cow, none, as she could see they were ahead and so followed behind the wagon.  Council Bluff was reached
2 CONC  and about 3 pm one day we drove into a big car team and wagon, cattle and all, and in a few minutes were across the Missouri River and in Omaha.  The main street was about hub deep in mud, and Farnam Street was awful.  We drove t
2 CONC o the top of the bluff just west of where the present Courthouse stands, and camped the night on some vacant land.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===================
2 CONT
2 CONT Evelyn writes:  "This was so poignant a story that I felt I must share it with the others who knew Grandpa.  In his writings in the Ord Quiz you will find more about his travels and filing on a homestead in Valley County, where th
2 CONC ey settled and my mother Mabel Anderson Abernethy, was born 12/2/1880 and Uncle Roy Wilbur Anderson was born 10/24/1883.  Elizabeth Hather Anderson died in California 4/7/1888.  In another page Grandpa writes of the marriage of El
2 CONC izabeth Hather Anderson and he being married at Johns Creek, IA, near Farley on 9/5/1876, ceremony by Reverend Agnew, Presbyterian minister.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another item was that Grandpa Anderson's Uncle George Hillman was also homesteading in Nebraska - near Scotia and perhaps that was why he ended up in that area.  George Hillman was a brother of Hester Hillman Anderson - Grandpa's 
2 CONC mother.  he as also a minister and started the ME church in the Scotia area.
2 CONT
2 CONT =========== book article about Nebraska pioneers.  Page 95-96==============
2 CONT
2 CONT William Ashley Anderson was born April 14, 1853 in what was called Bankston Prairie in Dubuque County, Iowa.  His father came from Kentucky, but left that state because of his antipathy to slavery.  Although the family beginnings 
2 CONC were pleasant and well-to-do, people became so poor after the Civil War that W. A. Anderson told of never having any underclothes until past 18 years of age, and of wearing only a hickory shirt and denim overalls winter and summer
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Anderson attended school at Centralia, Iowa, and the Seminary at Epworth, Iowa.  He taught school and farmed in Iowa.  On September 5, 1876, he married Elizabeth A. Hather.  Their first child died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early in 1879, the Andersons started west.  They sold their corn at 14 cents a bushel and their hogs for $3.65.  With a cow and two heifers and a covered wagon, they followed the CRI and P Railroad to Council Bluffs and Omaha.  Th
2 CONC e covered wagon became their home for several weeks.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Andersons reached the Loup Valley of Nebraska in late February.  An uncle and cousins lived near Scotia.  There were all kinds of claims for land for sale at $50 to $500 for a quarter section.  The Andersons filed a claim on t
2 CONC he northeast quarter of section 222 in Township 20, Range 13 West in Valley County, March 25, 1879.  It cost $14 to file.  On March 30, 1885, Mr. Anderson received Homestead certificate No. 5461, signed by President Grover Clevela
2 CONC nd.
2 CONT
2 CONT Until a well could be dug, water had to be hauled five miles from Wallace creek.  Mr. Anderson dug the well and built a soddy.  he shot game for food.  While on this claim, Mabel Wilma Anderson was born December 2, 1880, and Roy W
2 CONC ilbur Anderson was born October 4, 1883.
2 CONT
2 CONT November 21, 1883, Sylvester S. Haskell and wife deeded to Elizabeth A. Anderson for the sum of $75 Lot 8 block 35 in Haskell's Addition to the Village of Ord.  (We think this is the place where Mr. Anderson bulit a frame house he
2 CONC re, and lived in the house all the rest of his life).
2 CONT
2 CONT About 1887 or 1888, Mr. Anderson went to California with his wife and two children.  Here, on April 7, 1888, Elizabeth A. Anderson died.  Mr. Anderson returned with his two children to Ord.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Anderson was the official weather observer for Valley County most of his life in Ord.  He enjoyed making predictions and organized a Horoscope club.  He was instrumental in establishing the Ord Public Library.  He sold lightni
2 CONC ng rods, and had a photography studio.  he wrote extensively about pioneer times in the Ord Quiz and Nebraska State Journal.  he was, also, a teacher.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Anderson died in Ord in July, 1944.  His son preceded him in death January 17, 1932, leaving two daughters and a son surviving him.  His daughter, Mabel, who married Jason L. Abernethy October 4, 1905, was a telephone operator
2 CONC  and teacher.  After the death of her husband, a farmer in Noble Township and Valley county Assessor, Mrs. Abernethy became a bookkeeper for the Ord Creamery, from which she retired at age 82, and moved to California.  By his daug
2 CONC hter Mr. Anderson had a grandson, Ray, and two granddaughters, Wilma and Evelyn, and nine grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===end of page 95-96 of book article  (which continues about George Abernethy family) ===
