1 TITL Rev.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Rev William Neverson Chaffee
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth 10 SEP 1837 • Bennington, Genesee, New York, United States
2 CONT
2 CONT Death 21 NOV 1912 • Des Moines, Polk, Iowa, United States
2 CONT
2 CONT Iowa Methodism Loses Another Pioneer Minister __The funeral of Rev. W. N. Chaffee was held this afternoon at one-thirty from the residence of Dr. C. T. Brown, 116 North Seventeenth street, Rev. Dr. T. E. Fleming, district superint
2 CONC endent of the Marshalltown district, conducting the service, assisted by other members of the conference. The music was furnished by a male quartet and interment was in Linwood. __William Neverson Chaffee, born in Cataraugus count
2 CONC y, N.Y., September 10, 1837, Died at Des Moines, Iowa, November 21, 1912. __One of the pioneers of Iowa Methodism is gone. It is forty years since William Neverson Chaffee, then a young farmer at Elwood, Iowa, was licensed to prea
2 CONC ch. Since that time, to the day of his death he has been a member of the Upper Iowa conference, and in the regular pastorate, with the exception of one year as conference evangelist, and two years as field agent of Cornell college
2 CONC : serving charges, all in Iowa, at Johnson, Miles, Inland, Clarence, Mechanichsville, West Union, Dysart, Springville, Alburnett, Geneseo, Dumont, Urbana, Center Point, and Laurel, with but a few exceptions remaining the full conf
2 CONC erence limit in each place. __In 1861 he enlisted in the war for the Union as member of Company G. First Regimental Illinois Cavalry, serving fifteen months, when on account of serious illness he received honorable discharge. __On
2 CONC  November 9, 1863, he married Emily McClure at Cameron, Ill., who lived until May 9, 1908. To them were born a son who died in infancy and three sons and two daughters who still survive; Franklin and William of Chicago, Orlyn of W
2 CONC averly, Iowa, and Mrs. Ella D. Paul and Mrs. Irene Brown of Cedar Rapids. On Oct. 14, 1909, deceased married Lura Harrington of Miles, Iowa, who also survives him. __W. N. Chaffee, or as he is well known to thousands of friends an
2 CONC d former parishioners, "Brother Chaffee" was a man of strong personality. In every community in which he lived, his influence began immediately to be felt and became a factor in the community life. He was positive in his convictio
2 CONC ns, uncompromising in his principles, and yet of rare sympathy, and his leadership was that of a captain who is followed because he has won the hearts of his soldiers. __Few men have ever lived who could more truly say; "Write me 
2 CONC as one who loves his fellow men." His visiting from home, as pastor, never was perfunctory. He assumed that people wanted him to come and his calls and visits were on a basis that was so personal that they seemed like the coming o
2 CONC f a special friend or one of the family. __[Photo w/caption, "Rev. W. N. Chaffee."] __He understood men. Few ministers had a large number of men and of young men than he always had in his congregation: and not only members of his 
2 CONC own church, but those of other denominations and of no denomination were glad of his friendship. __In his business relations he showed the strong practical side of his character. He had made a success as farmer before entering the
2 CONC  ministery and all his life not only handled his own finacial affairs successfully but always left the charge he served in fine finacial condition. __It is worthy of note that few men have kept so well as he the spirit of youthful
2 CONC ness. Many remarked that his sermons grew better every year. He kept up his study and did more and more general reading as the years went by: still keeping in touch with all classes and conditions of men, never "caring more for bo
2 CONC oks than for folks." __In addition to these, and many other admirable qualities, no man was ever more devoted to his family, and always, from the time he joined the Methodist church as a boy, through his early manhood, and his for
2 CONC ty years in the ministry, his was the utmost loyalty to his religion and to the church of his choice. It was his dearest pride to have been a lifelong soldier of the cross.
