1 NOTE {geni:about_me} --------------------
2 CONT JAMES FEAKE (1567-1625) married Judith Thomas of St. Dunstan, Stepney. He continued in the family’s goldsmith business.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Retrieved from WikiTree contributors, "James Feake (abt.1567-bef.1625)," WikiTree: The Free Family Tree, (https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Feake-8 : accessed 24 July 2025) and then edited
2 CONT
2 CONT flag of the Jamestowne Society
2 CONT James Feake is a Qualifying Ancestor of the Jamestowne Society
2 CONT City of London (historic flag)
2 CONT
2 CONT James Feake was born in the City of London, England.
2 CONT James Feake[1] was born about 1567 based upon his own testimony stating his age as 34 on 1 Jul 1601 at the High Court of Administrative Examinations.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT James Feake was a member of the Goldsmith's Guild. He was married at St. Nicholas Acons Church in London on January 29, 1592,[3] to Judith Thomas, daughter of Robert Thomas and his first wife Judith Fisher.[2][4][5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children (surname FEAKE):[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT # Robert - b. c1602; d. 1 Feb 1669/1; m. Elizabeth FONES Winthrop Feake Hallett
2 CONT # James - d. aft 1618; m. sis/o Tobias DIXON
2 CONT # John
2 CONT # Elizabeth
2 CONT # Hannah
2 CONT # Sarah
2 CONT # Alice - m. Tobias DIXON
2 CONT
2 CONT James Feake was executor of his mother's will in 1619 but was dead by 20 May 1625 when his widow Judith was appointed administrix of his father's will.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Visitation of Surrey (1620), the goldsmiths of Wighton, Norfolk, bore the following arms: Sable, a fesse dancette or, in chief three fleur-de-lis argent. Crest: Out of a coronet or, demi-ostrich with wings spread 
2 CONC argent holding in the beak a horse's shoe.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Mentioned in the Will of Robert Thomas, of St. Nicholas Acons, dated 14 Feb. 1609/10, proved 18 Oct. 1610:
2 CONT
2 CONT >To my daughter Judith Feake £1000 in full satisfaction of her marriage money and Child's part. To her four children James, Robert, Alice and Judith £100 apiece.<BR>
2 CONT >James Feake; my son-in-law 'hathe given out threatening wordes that he would go to law with me for his portion.' If the said James refuses to release my exors. from all Actions and demands, within a quarter of a year after my dec
2 CONC ease, then this my said, legacy to his wife with the legacies to his children are cancelled, and I leave him to be relieved by the law.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Children include:
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. This individual mentioned in the following article about Robert Feake per "The Great Migration Begins: Immigrants to New England 1620-1633," Volumes I-III:
2 CONT "Robert Feake 
2 CONT Occupation: Goldsmith. He served an apprenticeship with his father, James Feake, for eight years beginning 21 September 1615, but probably never practiced his craft in the New World [NYGBR 86:212]...
2 CONT Birth: About 1602, son of James and Judith (Thomas) Feake [NYGBR 86:144-45]..."
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT # https://myfamilysearch.net/getperson.php?personID=I4784&tree=2005217a
