1 OCCU apothecary
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Apprenticed to a London grocer in 1595. Freeman of the Company of Grocers in 1602. An apothecary and man of learning.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alternate birthplace:  Bromsgrove, Worcestershire.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Thomas Ffones was born on 24 Mar 1573 in London, England. He married his first wife Anna Wynthropp on 25 Feb 1605. Three of their children survived infancy including their eldest Elizabeth who was our ancestor. Anna died in 1618 a
2 CONC nd Thomas remarried in 1621 when his daughter Bess was 12 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas ran an apothecary at the sign of the Three Fawns on Old Bailey Street. In the apothecary, he prepared herbal preparations to help people heal from, deal with or prevent illnesses. His daughter Elizabeth and an apprentice wo
2 CONC rked with him and learned the art.
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition to his pharmacy responsibilities, Thomas offered general medical advice and a range of services such as surgery and midwifery. He also operated a retail shop selling ingredients for medicines, tobacco and patent medici
2 CONC nes.
2 CONT
2 CONT By the 15th century, his profession had gained the status of skilled practitioner. Such work marked the beginnings of chemistry and pharmacology.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was probably because he was a Puritan that he refused a knighthood to the King who had married a Roman Catholic. “Bromsgrove gentlemen fined for refusing a knighthood: The following is a list of gentlemen belonging to Bromsgrov
2 CONC e who were fined for not taking the order of Knighthood on the coronation of Charles 1st (1626) Roger Lowe, John Crabbe, Walter Brace, Thomas Fownes – 10 pounds sterling each. Nicholas Lilley 9.6.8 pounds sterling. John Westwood a
2 CONC nd Richard Burford 12 pounds sterling each.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas and his family lived in the cramped town house above his apothecary shop in the Old Bailey area of London. He was well educated and relatively prosperous. The household had two servants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unfortunately, he was a sickly man in mid-life. He died 15 April 1629 in Groton, Suffolk, England at the age of 56. His second wife Priscilla later married the Rev. Henry Painter of Exeter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Ffones’ will: 
2 CONT
2 CONT “Thomas Ffones citizen and apothecary of London 14th April 1629. Having already in acts executed in my life time disposed of the greater part of my personal estate to and among my children and to the use and benefit of my wife I d
2 CONC o hereby commit the tuition, education, care and tutelage of my son Samuel Ffones during his minority unto his uncle John Winthrop of Groton in the County of Suffolk Esqur., John White of the Middle Temple London Esqr., and James 
2 CONC Thurlby citizen and grocer of London, and do earnestly desire these my loving friends to have a special care that he be brought up in learning and in the fear of God and knowledge of his ways; and do charge and require my son, upo
2 CONC n my blessing, to subject himself unto them and to be ruled by them in all things. And the tuition and education of my daughters Elizabeth and Martha I do commit unto my said loving brother John Wynthropp until they shall be marri
2 CONC ed or attain their full age of one and twenty years. The tuition of my youngest daughter Mary I commit to my loving wife her mother. My loving wife Pricella Ffones and my loving brother John Wynthropp to be executors, etc. Witness
2 CONC es: John Smith, Ri. Ffitch, Tho: Smith” Probate 29 April 1629 (P.C.C.28 Ridley).
2 CONT
2 CONT Profile of Thomas Ffones 
2 CONT
2 CONT • Quickly grasped essentials Strong need for independence. Competitive. Radiated forceful, confident vitality. • had a solid and accurate business sense and an excellent capacity for bargaining and shrewd negotiating. He instincti
2 CONC vely knew how to accomplish things and succeed n business. For the sake of reaching an important goal, he could sacrifice without complaint. He was willing to work hard and postpone relaxation. • A resourceful and determined worke
2 CONC r. Great shrewdness. Resourcefulness strong. Great ability to love greatly or hate bitterly. Strong lust for power • poetical, psychic and visionary type of mind. Extremely sensitive. Loved music, gentleness & innate refinement. E
2 CONC asily hurt. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Thomas Fones Memorial
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: 	Mar. 24, 1573, England Death: 	Apr. 15, 1629 Groton Suffolk, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Fones was at an early age apprenticed to a grocer in London. He was freed from the apprenticeship in 1602. However he did not pursue the grocery trade becoming an apothacary.
2 CONT
2 CONT His first wife of Ann Winthrop with which the following children were born:
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Elizabeth Martha  Mary
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Ann in 1618 he married the widow of Bezalell Sherman, named Priscilla. They had no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT He wrote his will two days prior to his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not known for certain, but he is most likely buried with Ann at St. Sepulachre Church in London. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Family links: 
2 CONT  Spouse:
2 CONT   Anne Winthrop Fones (1585 - 1618)*
2 CONT
2 CONT  Children:
2 CONT   Elizabeth Fones Winthrop Feake Hallett (1610 - 1673)*
2 CONT
2 CONT *Calculated relationship
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Maintained by: charlott jones Originally Created by: P Fazzini Record added: Jan 06, 2011  Find A Grave Memorial# 63814377
