1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Winthrop-10
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne Winthrop, the second child of Adam Winthrop and Anne Browne, was born 16 Jan 1585-6 at Groton, Suffolk, England.[1][2] Anne married Thomas Fones 25 Feb 1604 in London, England.[1]. Her father mentions her birth in his diary: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >''1585. Anne the second daughter of Adam Winthrop and Anne his wife was born in Edwardston on Sunday aboute 10 of the clocke in the evenynge beinge the 16 daye of January anno 1585 in the 28 yere of the reigne of Qu: Eliz: and in
2 CONC  the 37 yere of my age.'' [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 22 Jul 1587, Anne returned to her father's from a visit to her Uncle Mildmay’s. On 10 May 1602, she had a new gown brought from London. On 23 Feb 1604-6, Thomas Fones came to her father’s house. They were married 25 Feb 1604. T
2 CONC wo days later, they departed towards London. On 2 Sep 1604, Anne was at Ipswich attending the marriage of the maid of her father's cousin Sparrow. On 8 May 1605, she visited her father and departed from home May 23, being there at
2 CONC  the marriage feast of her brother John May 9th. On 1 Aug 1605, John visited his father at Groton. Thomas Fones, was citizen and apothecary of London, England, at the Three Fawns, Old Bailey, in 1628.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Their second daughter and child was Elizabeth, born 21 Jan 1609-10, who for her second husband married Robert Feake.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Anne Winthrop and Thomas Fones
2 CONT
2 CONT # Dorothy Fones
2 CONT # John Fones
2 CONT # Elizabeth (Fones) Hallett
2 CONT # Martha (Fones) Winthrop
2 CONT # Ann Fones
2 CONT # Samuel Fones
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne died 16 May 1618,[1] or 20 May 1619 at Groton, Suffolk, England at the age of 33. Thomas died on 15 Apr 1629 in Groton, Suffolk, England at the age of 56. She was buried at St. Sepulchre without Newgate Churchyard; London, Ci
2 CONC ty of London, Greater London, England.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Other information
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne was christened and married in St. Peter upon Cornhill, London. An inscription in the churchyard claims that St Peter upon Cornhill is the earliest Christianised site in Britain, founded in 187. Its website states, “it is wide
2 CONC ly held that there has been a Christian place of worship on the site of St Peter-upon-Cornhill since 179AD when Lucius, the first Christian King of Britain, founded it as the first church in London and the chief church of his king
2 CONC dom.”[4] The church is situated on the corner of Cornhill and Gracechurch Streets.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne was buried at St Sepulchre-without-Newgate, located on Holborn Viaduct, almost opposite the Old Bailey. In medieval times, it stood just outside ("without") the now-demolished old city wall, near the Newgate. The original Sax
2 CONC on church on the site was dedicated to St Edmund the King and Martyr. During the Crusades in the 12th century the church was renamed St Edmund and the Holy Sepulchre, in reference to the Church of the Holy Sepulchre in Jerusalem. 
2 CONC The name eventually became contracted to St Sepulchre. The church is today the largest parish church in the City. It was completely rebuilt in the 15th century but was gutted by the Great Fire of London in 1666. St Sepulchre is on
2 CONC e of the "Cockney bells" of London, named in the nursery rhyme Oranges and Lemons as the "bells of Old Bailey". Traditionally, the great bell would be rung to mark the execution of a prisoner at the nearby gallows at Newgate.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne was honored to have Cape Ann named for her.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Research notes
2 CONT
2 CONT In his diary Adam Winthrop mentions Edwardstone as Anne's birthplace rather than nearby Groton.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Thomas Fones 
2 CONT
2 CONT ''The Frost Genealogy : Descendants of William Frost of Oyster Bay, New York; showing connections never before published with the Winthrop, Underhill, Feke Bowne and Wickes families''. New England Historic Genealogical Society. Th
2 CONC e New England Historical and Genealogical Register. Boston: The New England Historic Genealogical Society. pg. 350 < [https://archive.org/details/frostgenealogyde00fros/page/349/mode/2up Archive.Org] >
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Seventh Generation.
2 CONT
2 CONT THOMAS FONES, son and heir of John.
2 CONT
2 CONT THOMAS, citizen and apothecary of London, England, at the Three Fawns, Old Bailey, in 1628, died April 16, 1629. Married Feb. 25, 1604- .5, Anne, daughter of Adam and Anne (Browne) Winthrop, of Groton, and sister of our Puritan go
2 CONC vernor, John Winthrop. She died May 16, 1618, and he m., 2nd, Aug. 28, 1621, Priscilla, daughter of John Burgis, D.D.and widow of Bezalicll Shearman.
2 CONT
