2 TITL North American Histories Capt John Underhill Father Sir John Underhill mother Mary Moseley.pdf
1 NOTE Captain John Underhill was a soldier and served under Maurice of Nassau, Prince of Orange, in the Low Countries.  Family tradition associates John with the Earl of Leicester, as the bearer of dispatches from Queen Elizabeth to the
2 CONC  Earl, then commanding the English forces in the Low Countries.  Upon the Earl's death, John attached himself to the fortunes of the Earl of Essex.  He accompanied him in his successful attack upon Cadiz, and shared his ill fortun
2 CONC e in his fruitless expedition against Tyrme, the rebel chief of the revolted clans of Ireland.  The Earl of Essex was beheaded and Edward was obliged to seek safety in Holland until the assession of King James , when he applied fo
2 CONC r pardon and leave to return to England; but he could not procure it.  When the Rev. Mr. Robinson, with a number of other separatists, fled from England to Holland, John dwelt and communed with them for a number of years.  He met 
2 CONC his first wife in Holland.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Underhill was strongly solicited to go with Captain Carver, Elder William Brewster and other worthies, part of the Rev. John Robinson's church, to the settlement of Plymouth, and had partly engaged with them as their chief mi
2 CONC litary officer; but Capt. Miles Standish, his brave fellow-soldier in the Low Countries, undertaking the business, in 1620, he declined.   How he joined Governor Winthrop, does not appear, but he came over to New England with him,
2 CONC  as captain of the militia force, in 1630.  He brought with him his first wife, Helena Kruger.  
2 CONT
2 CONT His reputation was so good that Governor John Winthrop secured him to serve as commander of the military company that his Massachusetts Bay Colony would need, when it commenced its settlement at Boston, in 1630.  John Underhill is
2 CONC  listed at the 57th name on the list of members of the First Church in Boston.  He was sworn freeman on May 18, 1630.  He was appointed one of the first deputies from Boston to the General Court in 1634 and in 1637, one of the ear
2 CONC liest officers of the Ancient and Honorable Artillery Company.  In 1637, he was sent as commander of the colony troops to Saybrook, Connecticut.  The same year, he was disfranchised and eventually banished from the jurisdiction of
2 CONC  Massachusetts - his ideas of religious toleration being more liberal than those around him.  In 1638, he returned to England, but was banished thence for certain religious and moral delinquencies.
2 CONT
2 CONT While he was in England, he published a work called "Nevves From America, or a New and Experimental Discoverie of New England."  The book is filled with religious cant, for he was an ardent hypocrite.  He appears equally fond of s
2 CONC inning and repenting.  It is amusing to read in these pages of Governor Winthrop, how ingeniously he managed through several years, to delude the Puritans by his professions of sanctity, while he led in their midst, the most disso
2 CONC lute of lives.  The book gives a good account of the Pequot War, he related;
2 CONT
2 CONT "Myself received an arrow through my coat sleeve, a second against my helmet, on the forehead; so as if God, in His providence, had not moved the heart of my wife to persuade me to carry it along with me (which I was unwilling to 
2 CONC do), I had been slain.  Give me leave to observe two things from hence - first, when the hour of death is not yet come, you see God useth weak means to keep his purpose inviolated; secondly, let no man despise advice and counsel o
2 CONC f his wife, though she be a woman."
2 CONT
2 CONT John Underhill went to Dover, NH with Rev. Hanserd Knollys, to establish the First Church, in November 1638.  That year, he was also chosen as Governor.  The poet Whittier, in his poem "John Underhill", makes it appear that the ca
2 CONC ptain came to Dover on horseback and begins his story as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT A score of years had come and gone
2 CONT Since the Pilgrims landed on Plymouth stone,
2 CONT When Captain Underhill, bearing scars
2 CONT From Indian ambush and Flemish wars,
2 CONT Left three-hilled Boston and wandered down
2 CONT East by north to Cochecho town.
2 CONT
2 CONT He cheered his heart as he rode along
2 CONT With screed of Scripture and holy song,
2 CONT Or thought how he rode with his lancers free
2 CONT By the lower Rhine and the Zuyder Zee,
2 CONT Till his wood-path grew to a trodden road,
2 CONT And Hilton Point in the distance showed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Goodly and stately and grave to see,
2 CONT Into the clearing's space rode he,
2 CONT With the sun on the hilt of his sword in sheath,
2 CONT And his silver buckles and spurs beneath;
2 CONT And the settlers welcomed him, one and all,
2 CONT From swift Quamphegan to Gonic fall.
2 CONT
2 CONT He moved to Stamford, CT in 1642 where Governor Kieft gave him command of one hundred and twenty men in their war with the natives.  In February 1644, he and his soldiers encountered the Indians on what is now called Indian Hill, 
2 CONC in the town of Bedford, leaving 500 of their enemy dead on the field.  He totally extinguished the Pequots.  Besides this, Underhill is said to have killed 150 Indians on Long Island.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a representative from Stamford to the General Court of Connecticut in 1643. He also held the position of Assistant Justice.   In 1644, he came with Rev. Mr. Denton and others of his church to Long Island and soon became a r
2 CONC esident of Flushing.  He moved to Oyster Bay in or about the year 1655 and in that year he was a delegate from that town to the meeting at Hempstead and was made high sheriff of the North Riding on Long Island.  In 1667, the Matin
2 CONC cock Indians conveyed to him a large tract of their land, a part of which, called Kenilworth or Killingworth, a portion which remained in the family for over 200 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonial Families of the United States of America: Volume 6
2 CONT Underhill 
2 CONT
2 CONT CAPT. JOHN UNDERHILL, was b. in Bagington, Warwickshire, England, on 7th October, 1597; d. in Matincock, New York, 21st July, 1672; came to
2 CONT Boston with WINTHROP'S fleet as Captain of any military force that might be employed. Speedily joined the Church. Previously served under the great
2 CONT Dutch Prince in the war of the Netherlands. Sworn in as Freeman; Officer of Ancient and Honorable Artillery Company, Boston; Deputy to first General
2 CONT Court, Massachusetts (Legislature); appointed Selectman; appointed by General Court with Daniel PATRICK and Robert FEAKE to fix upon a site for a fort
2 CONT on Castle Island in the Bay; ordered by the Magistrates, with a shallop, to bring Roger WILLIAMS from Salem to Boston; on account of murder of Captain
2 CONT OLDHAM by Block Island Indians, Governor VANE and Council, ordered sent thither ninety men, distributed to four commanders, Capt. John
2 CONT UNDERHILL, with commission to put to death the men, but spare the women. Commander with Captain MASON in Pequot War; Colonial Governor of
2 CONT Dover and Exeter, New Hampshire; Representative from Stamford, Connecticut, in General Court. Led the Dutch against the Simaroy Indians; appointed
2 CONT member of Council of New Netherland; commanded by the Director to attack and subdue certain hostile Indians on Long Island, which was done; elected one
2 CONT of the eight men of New Amsterdam to adopt measures against the Indians. Governor KIEFT grants to Capt. John UNDERHILL, Meutalers (Bergens)
2 CONT Island. Sheriff of the North Riding of Long Island; Magistrate at Flushing; command by Rhode Island, Newport and Providence Plantations to Privateers to
2 CONT go against the Dutch, Capt. John UNDERHILL made Commander in Chief of the land forces. Appointed Deputy by Governor NICHOLLS, with sober and
2 CONT discreet powers, etc. Mrs. Schuyler VAN RENSSELAER in her history of the City of New York says: "The most conspicuous Englishmen in New
2 CONT Netherland in the time of Governor KIEFT were Isaac ALLERTON and John UNDERHILL." See above History, Volume 1, page 215. "That he had served
2 CONT with credit in the army in the low Countries, Ireland and Spain." Same History and Volume, p. 216. Sherman WILLIAMS in his book called "New York's
2 CONT Partin History" at page 124, referring to the Indian Wars of New Netherland and particularly to the Battle of Stricklands Plain where the Colonists were led
2 CONT by Capt. John UNDERHILL says: "The Colony was saved from utter destruction chiefly through the efforts of John UNDERHILL." He led several
2 CONT expeditions against the Indians and the last one compelled a peace. "At Stricklands Plain not far from Stamford a decisive battle was fought." "It was a
2 CONT stunning blow for the Indians and it ended the war and saved the Colony." "KIEFT proclaimed a Public Thanksgiving for the result of UNDERHILL'S
2 CONT Expedition." Capt. John UNDERHILL m. (secondly) 1658-1659 probably at Flushing, Elizabeth FEAKE, b. in Watertown, Massachusetts, 1633, d. in
2 CONT Killingworth on Matincock, in 1674-1675, dau. of Lieut. Robert FEAKE and Elizabeth (FONES) WINTHROP, his wife; their son,
