1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Edward Farrington''', son of  Elizabeth Newhall & Edmund Farrington, was born about 1615, probably in Sherrington, Buckinghamshire, England.  Edward died in Flushing, New York bef 1 Jul 1676, when the administration of his esta
2 CONC te was granted to his widow, Dorothy.  He was buried in thr Old Bowne Family Burial Ground, Flushing, Queens County,  New York, USA Note: No marker.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/164211621/edward-farrington and then edited. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward Farrington was born circa 1615.  In 1622, he removed with his family to Olney, Buckinghamshire, England.  He was one of the two elder sons of Edmund Farrington and Elizabeth Newhall who emigrated to New England separately f
2 CONC rom the rest of the family, which departed from London in 1635 on the ''Hopewell''. In 1639, he was a resident of Lynn, Massachusetts when he embarked for Long Island in search of a location for a new settlement.
2 CONT
2 CONT Resident of Southampton by 1645 and of Vlissingen (Flushing) by 1651. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was appointed magistrate of Vlissingen, New Netherland (present-day Flushing, Queens, New York) in 1655 and 1656. Signer of the Flushing Remonstrance in 1657, for which he was arrested in 1658. Patentee of Flushing in 1666.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward married Dorothy Bowne, daughter of Thomas Bowne, by 1651.   Born on 14 Aug 1631 in Matlock, Derbyshire. Dorothy died in Flushing, New York, on 24 Jun 1678; she was 46.
2 CONT
2 CONT His will mentioned his wife Dorothy and his sons: 
2 CONT *John, 
2 CONT *Edward, 
2 CONT *Thomas and 
2 CONT *Matthew.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in Flushing before 1 July 1675, when the administration of his estate was granted to his widow, Dorothy.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === Edward Farrington: Flushing's Honorary Town Father
2 CONT
2 CONT From < [https://www.bownehouse.org/profiles-of-the-flushing-charter-signers/edward-farrington bownehouse.org] > (October 3, 2020) accessed 8 December 2025
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000222665711839&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward Farrington, while not himself a Flushing Charter signer like his brother Thomas, was a prominent member of the community who amply deserves the title of honorary founder. Although his early life remains the most enigmatic o
2 CONC f all the Farringtons, it was Edward’s later career in Flushing that established the family alongside the Bownes as one of the town’s important Quaker lines for generations to come. …
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family notes
2 CONT
2 CONT .… Edward lived in Flushing for the remainder of his life. He appears on the 1666 Patent for Flushing issued by Governor Nicolls after the English takeover. In 1667, his father Edmund wrote a will leaving him and his brother-in-la
2 CONC w Robert Terry each twenty shillings. (The bulk of the paternal estate went to the siblings who had remained in Lynn.) Edward raised four sons with Dorothy, named after himself and his three brothers: John, Edward, Thomas, and Mat
2 CONC hew. (The fact that he named his firstborn John, rather than Edward Jr., may hint at his great regard for his brother-in-law, John Bowne.) Genealogies also record three daughters: Dorothy, Mary, and Margaret, although little is kn
2 CONC own of them. Edward seems to have prospered in Flushing and become a substantial landowner before his death around age 60. His will was probated on July 1, 1675, when his widow was granted letters of administration. Most of his es
2 CONC tate went to John. Younger sons Edward and Thomas received 50 acres each plus a share of meadow, while Mathew received 20 acres and three meadows. His daughters either predeceased him or were omitted from the inheritance.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following year John Bowne wrote to his wife, then traveling in England: “I must acquaint you to thy grief, as it has been to mine, that my sister did take a husband yesterday. It was the man who went and came with L[ewis] Morr
2 CONC is to the New Countries with us.” (It is not clear from this description who the new husband was; Lewis Morris was a Quaker based in the English colony of Barbados who undertook religious visits to New England, while the “New Coun
2 CONC tries” may have referred to New Jersey or Pennsylvania.) According to family historian Jacob Titus Bowne, Dorothy was censured and expelled from the Flushing Quaker Meeting for “outmarriage,” or marrying a non-Quaker. However, thi
2 CONC s second marriage may have ended before her death, because on June 24, 1678, Letters of Administration were granted to her son John for “Dorothy Farrington, widow & relict of Edward Farrington.” The matter poses a bit of a mystery
2 CONC . Regardless, the Farringtons remained an influential family in both the Quaker Meeting and the town of Flushing well into the 19th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT From < [http://aleph0.clarku.edu/~djoyce/gen/report/rr02/rr02_231.html#P18567 Pane-Joyce Genealogy] > accessed 8 December 2025
2 CONT
2 CONT On 29 Nov 1613 Elizabeth married Edmund Farrington in Sherington, Buckinghamshire.229 Born ca 1588.14 Edmund died in Lynn, MA 20 : 11 [Jan] : 1670[/1].228 Occupation: felmonger. Edmund (Edward) of “Oney” aged 47, with wife Elizabe
2 CONC th 49, and children Sarra 14, Mathew 12, John 11, and Elizabeth 8, emigrated with others of the Olney Cluster on the Hopewell in 1635, and settled in Lynn.229
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT See Dorothy Farrington Parker, ''The Farringtons, Colonists and Patriots'', pp. 67-157, 1976. Also ''NEHGR'' 118:216. These correct ''NEHGR'' 50:301-2.229
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT from ''History of Lynn, Essex County, Mass,'' "Annals of Lynn," page 153, by Lewis & Newhall, pub John Shorey, Boston, 1865 --
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Edmund Farrington -- embarked in the ''Hopewell'', of London, 1 April 1635 with his wife and four children. (Record in Westminster Hall, London.) He was a native of Oney, in Buckinghamshire and born 1588. He was a farmer and had
2 CONC  200 acres of land, part of which was on the western side of Federal Street, where he lived and part on the western side of Myrtle street. In 1655 he built a corn mill on Water Hill, where a pond was dug and a water coarse opened 
2 CONC for half a mile. He died in 1670, age 82. The name of his wife was Elizabeth, and she was born 1586. His children were, Sarah, b. 1621; Martha, b. 1623; John, b. 1624; Elizabeth, b. 1627, and married John Fuller, in 1646. He also 
2 CONC had a son, Matthew to whom he gave half his corn mill, "except the tole of my son [in law] ffuller's grists, which is well and duly to be ground tole free, during the life of my daughter, Elizabeth".
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
