2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob Soule, son of Nathaniel Soule and Rose (Thorn) Soule, was born about 1687. His parents lived in Dartmouth.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Rebeckah Gifford Jan 22, 179 [page torn 1709?/10], in Dartmouth.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The lands of Nathaniel Soule and descendants were on the east side of Main Road in present Westport, which was set off from Dartmouth in 1787, and ran down to the east branch of the Accaxet or Westport River.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Although not a Quaker, he triggered controversy in the Quaker community in 1730.[4] He appeared at the Dartmouth monthly meeting and complained that the local Quaker George Soule had gotten his daughter pregnant and then left town
2 CONC .[4] A committee was formed to investigate and conduct hearings with those involved.[4] After several months of being unable to reach a decision, the committee took the unusual step of deciding by majority, rather than consensus.[
2 CONC 4] It decided that George's father Nathan Soule was to blame for being instrumental and encouraging in his son's absence.[4] Nathan refused to acknowledge any wrongdoing.[4] He was subsequently disowned by the monthly meeting.[4] 
2 CONC The identity of the daughter or child are not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob died between August 1747 (will dated) 3 May 1748 (will proved) at Dartmouth.[1][5][6]
2 CONT
2 CONT His will, dated Dartmouth 12 August 1747, proved 3 May 1748, named sons Stephen, Joseph, Nathaniel and Benjamin, daughters Elizabeth Tripp and Rosamond Potter, and grandchildren Phebe and Jeremiah Wait. He gave son Stephen the wes
2 CONC t end of his homestead farm, son Joseph land on the south side of the farm and all buildings, son Nathaniel the middlemost lot that butts on the river, etc., son Benjamin land on the north side of that given to Nathaniel, being al
2 CONC l the remaining part. To the daughters the indoor moveables. Son Joseph was named executor, witnesses were Silvanus Soule, James Soule and Mary Soule.[7][1][1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The inventory was presented 2 Mar 1747/8.[1] His wife was not named implying her previous decease.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Children born in Dartmouth:[1]
2 CONT Joseph Soule b. 16 Feb 1710/11.
2 CONT Elizabeth Soule b. 14 Nov 1712.
2 CONT Oliver Soule b. 7 Sep 1714; d. 4 Jan 1714/5.
2 CONT Rebecca Soule b. 18 Dec 1715.
2 CONT Nathaniel Soule b. 23 Jan 1717/18.
2 CONT Benjamin Soule b. 18 Nov 1719.
2 CONT Rosamond Soule b. 28 July 1723.
2 CONT Stephen Soule b. 1 Jan 1726/7.
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
