1 NAME Richard /Cornell/
2 GIVN Richard
2 SURN Cornell
2 _MARNM Cornell
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 26 JAN 1678
2 PLAC Scarsdale, Westchester, NY
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 15 APR 1757
2 PLAC Scarsdale, Westchester, NY
2 ADDR
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} History of Westchester County edited by John Thomas Scharf
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Cornell, grandson of Thomas Cornell, of 
2 CONT
2 CONT Cornell's Neck, and eldest son of John and Mary (
2 CONT
2 CONT Russell) Cornell, of Cowneck, in Hempstead, was born 
2 CONT
2 CONT about 1670, and died at Scarsdale in 1757. He married
2 CONT
2 CONT in 1701, Hannah Thorne, of Flushing, and brought her
2 CONT
2 CONT and their 10 children to Scarsdale in 1727.
2 CONT
2 CONT children
2 CONT
2 CONT I. Mary, born at Cowneck, 1703, died 1782 ; married Rev. Henry 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sands. 
2 CONT
2 CONT II. Deborah, born at Cowneck, 1705, died 1772 ; married Matthew 
2 CONT
2 CONT Franklin, a Quaker preacher. 
2 CONT
2 CONT III. Richard Cornell, Jr., born 1708 ; married Mary Ferris.
2 CONT
2 CONT IV. Joseph, born in Cowneck, 1708, died 1770 ; married, 1734, Phebe 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferris, daughter of Peter Ferris.
2 CONT
2 CONT V. Hannah, born 1711 ; married Joshua Quimby.
2 CONT
2 CONT VI. Phebe, born 1715 ; married Ebenezer Haviland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT VII. John, born 1717 ; died 1781, without Issue. 
2 CONT
2 CONT VIII. Rebecca, born 1718 ; married Edward Burling. 
2 CONT
2 CONT IX. Elisabeth, born 1720, died 1795; married 1st, Aaron Palmer; married 2d, Aaron Quimby. 
2 CONT
2 CONT X. Benjamin, born 1723, died 1771 ; married 17th of 9th month, 1742, Abigail Stephenson, daughter of Stephen Stephenson, of Rye, and Jane Clement, of Flushing, his wife
1 FAMS @F6000000003648168011@
1 RFN geni:6000000003648993032
1 SUBM @S5427310443420075357@
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://www.geni.com/images/documents/doc.png
2 FORM txt
2 TITL Geni World Family Tree
1 NOTE In 1702, Richard Cornell and his brothers John and Joshua but from Thomas Willett the farm that John, Richard and Caleb lived on, and where they had their shipyards.  On March 10, 1705, these three and their wives convey to their 
2 CONC brother, Caleb, half of this tract with buildings and orchards.  On February 29, 1703, Richard was foreman of the grand jury which ignored the bill against Bownas the Quaker.  On August 12, 1705, he buys of his brother John, land 
2 CONC on the west side of Cow Neck.  About 1713, he bought 500 acres in Scarsdale from the heirs of Samuel Palmer.  It was probably through the Richbills and Motts that Richard became interested in Westchester County.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1719, it was voted to build a Friends Meeting House at Cow Neck, the place and dimenstions to be left to Richard Cornell, William Mott and others.  From 1721 to 1726, he was annually elected a fence viewer in Hempstead.  He pro
2 CONC bably removed to Westchester in 1725 but he owned his Cow Neck farm until 1730 when he sold it to his son-in-law, Edward Sands.  On October 7, 1730, he joined the Purchase Meeting, Westchester County, with a certificate from Westb
2 CONC ury, NY.
2 CONT
2 CONT It appears he also owned land in Goshen, Orange County, NY.  On December 16, 1733, he and other Quakers "inhabitants and freeholders of Westchester County", petition against Nicholas Cooper the sheriff, for refusing to allow them 
2 CONC to vote.
2 CONT
2 CONT Will dated,3 October 1756, leaves to sons, Richard,Joseph,John and Benjamin all lands in New Rochelle,Scarsdale and Mamaroneck,not disposed of by deed.To son Joseph, my silver watch, to son Benjamin, my clock and cart. to daughter
2 CONC  Mary,widow of Edward Sands, one Pistole over and above what I have already given her. Legacies to daughter Elizabeth, wife of Aaron Quimby, Deborah,wife of Mathew Franklin,Hannah,widow of Josiah Quimby, Rebecca,wife of Edward Bur
2 CONC ling. and to Hannah Griffin and Elizabeth Haviland,daughters of my daughter Phebe,late wife of John Williams. The executors to Richard's will were his son Richard, Edward Burling and John Burling.
2 CONT
2 CONT Farm in Scarsdale about 2 miles west of Mamaroneck village on Quaker Ridge, probably a few rods south of the Quaker Church.  The towns of Scarsdale, New Rochelle and Mamaroneck join at that point.  His farm was in the three towns.
1 NOTE In 1702, Richard Cornell and his brothers John and Joshua but from Thomas Willett the farm that John, Richard and Caleb lived on, and where they had their shipyards.  On March 10, 1705, these three and their wives convey to their 
2 CONC brother, Caleb, half of this tract with buildings and orchards.  On February 29, 1703, Richard was foreman of the grand jury which ignored the bill against Bownas the Quaker.  On August 12, 1705, he buys of his brother John, land 
2 CONC on the west side of Cow Neck.  About 1713, he bought 500 acres in Scarsdale from the heirs of Samuel Palmer.  It was probably through the Richbills and Motts that Richard became interested in Westchester County.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1719, it was voted to build a Friends Meeting House at Cow Neck, the place and dimenstions to be left to Richard Cornell, William Mott and others.  From 1721 to 1726, he was annually elected a fence viewer in Hempstead.  He pro
2 CONC bably removed to Westchester in 1725 but he owned his Cow Neck farm until 1730 when he sold it to his son-in-law, Edward Sands.  On October 7, 1730, he joined the Purchase Meeting, Westchester County, with a certificate from Westb
2 CONC ury, NY.
2 CONT
2 CONT It appears he also owned land in Goshen, Orange County, NY.  On December 16, 1733, he and other Quakers "inhabitants and freeholders of Westchester County", petition against Nicholas Cooper the sheriff, for refusing to allow them 
2 CONC to vote.
2 CONT
2 CONT Will dated,3 October 1756, leaves to sons, Richard,Joseph,John and Benjamin all lands in New Rochelle,Scarsdale and Mamaroneck,not disposed of by deed.To son Joseph, my silver watch, to son Benjamin, my clock and cart. to daughter
2 CONC  Mary,widow of Edward Sands, one Pistole over and above what I have already given her. Legacies to daughter Elizabeth, wife of Aaron Quimby, Deborah,wife of Mathew Franklin,Hannah,widow of Josiah Quimby, Rebecca,wife of Edward Bur
2 CONC ling. and to Hannah Griffin and Elizabeth Haviland,daughters of my daughter Phebe,late wife of John Williams. The executors to Richard's will were his son Richard, Edward Burling and John Burling.
2 CONT
2 CONT Farm in Scarsdale about 2 miles west of Mamaroneck village on Quaker Ridge, probably a few rods south of the Quaker Church.  The towns of Scarsdale, New Rochelle and Mamaroneck join at that point.  His farm was in the three towns.
