1 NOTE 267     THE RAPELYE FAMILY
2 CONT
2 CONT      This numerous and reputable familY ia descended from that of de  Rapalie, which, as early as the eleventh century possed large estates  in Bretagne, and ranked among the arriere-ban of the French nobility.  Some of its member
2 CONC s were distinguished as military leades in the  crusades, others were celebrated far political eminence and
2 CONT professional talent.  But in the religious wars of the sixteenth  century, being known as Protestants, they became the victims of Papal  animosity and were scattered and expelled from France. The family  subsequently gained promin
2 CONC ence in Switzerland and Belgium, where they  acquired large possessions and continue to the present time.  Their  ancient coat of arms, above given, are intended to represent the noble  birth and origin of the family, and their re
2 CONC putation for firmness and  fidelity.
2 CONT      1. Joris Jansen de Rapalie, one of this proscribed Huguenot race,  "from Rochelle in France," was the common ancestor of all the American  families of this name.*  He came to this country with other colonists  in 1623, in the
2 CONC  Unity, a ship of the West India Company, and settled  at Fort Orange, now Albany, where he continued three years.  In 1626  he removed to New Amsterdam, and resided there till after the birth of  his youngest child.  On June, 16,
2 CONC  1637, he bought from the Indians
2 CONT
2 CONT      *The practice which afterwards obtained, of writing the final  syllable of this name with a j, was a Dutch perversion of the original  orthography.
2 CONT      Some assert that Joris Jansen de Rapalie, and Antony Jansen Van  Salen, of Gravesend, were brothers, and their family name, jansen.   Our early records do not corroborate this statement, but go very far  to disprove it.  The 
2 CONC whole seems to be a mere assumption from the  occurrance of Jansen .
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 268                     ANNALS OF NEWTON
2 CONT
2 CONT a tract of land computed at 335 acres, called Rennegaconck, now  included within the town of Brooklyn, and comprehending the lands  occupied by the U. S. Marine Hospital Here Mr. Rapalie finally  located, and spent the remainder o
2 CONC f his life. He was a leading  citizen, acted a prominent part in the public affairs of the colony,  and served in the magistracy of Brooklyn.  He died soon after the  close of the Dutch administration, his widow, Catalyntie, dau. 
2 CONC of  Joris Trico, surviving him many years. She was born in Paris, and died  Sep. 11, 1689, a. 84. Their ch. as taken from the original family  record preserved in the library of the New York Hitoricol Society,  were as follow, to 
2 CONC wit, Sara. b. June 9, 1625, m. successively to Hans  Hansse Bergan and Teunis Gysberta Bogart;* Marritie, b. Mar. 11, 1627,  m. Michael Vandervoort; Jannetie, b. Aug. 18, 1629, m. Rem  Vanderbeeck; Judith, b. July 5, 1635, m. Piet
2 CONC er Van Nest; Jan, b. Aug.  26, 1637, m. but died in 1662 without surviving issue; Jacob, b. May  28, 1639, who was killed by the Indians; Catalyntie, b. Mar. 28, 1641,  m. Jeremis Westerhout;
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT In their names; Which, however, proves not a family identity, but  simply indicates that the father of each had borne the common name of  Jan or John. It has also been set down as veritable history, that said  Antony Jansen Van Sa
2 CONC les was the progenitor of a large family of  Johnsons upon Long Island and elsewhere, of which is Hon. Jeremiah  Johnson of Brooklyn.  This is an error.  Gen. Johnson, as clearly  appears from existing records, is a descendant, in
2 CONC  the fourth  generation, of Jan Barentsen van Driest, who came in 1657 from Zuiphen  in Gueiderland, and settled at Gravesend.  See an article on Antony  Jansen van Sales in the Brooklyn Daily Eagle of Feb. 20, 1851.  The  valuabl
2 CONC e work on American Genealogy, by Holgate contains very serious  mistakes respecting the johnson and Rapalye families, which only those  aquainted with the difficulties of genealogical investigation will  know how to excuse.
2 CONT
2 CONT      *This lady, say our early records, was "the first-borne Christian  daughter in New Netherland."  In honor of this, the Dutch authorities  presented her a tract of land at the Wallabout.  This circumstance has  probably given 
2 CONC rise to the belief that she was born at the latter  place, but the statement in the text (based upon the N.Y. Documentary  Hist. iii. 50, and other records,) shows that her parents were living  at Albany at the time of her birth/ 
2 CONC nor did they settle at the  Wallabout till more than twenty five-years after.  Sara early became a  church member in New York, but united with the church at Brooklyn by  certificate in XXXX???????????? . She d. a. about XX??????. 
2 CONC  Her  ????????????? decendants ????????????????????????????????.
2 CONT
2 CONT 269                    ANNALS OF NEWTOWN
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeronimus,2 b. June 27, 1643; Annetie, b. Feb. 8, 1646, m. Marten  Reverse and Joost Fransz: Elizabet, b. Mar. 28, 1648,. m. Dirck  Hooglandt; and Daniel,10 b. Dec. 29, 1650.
2 CONT
2 CONT      2. Jeronimus Rapalje became a man of some prominence, a justice  of ihe peace and a deacon of the Brooklyn church. He m. Anna, dau. of  Teunis Denys, and had ch. Jons, b. Nov. 5, 1668; Teunis,3 b. May 5,  1671; Jan,4 b. Dec. 
2 CONC 14, 1673; Femmetie b. Oct 5, 1676, m. Jan Bennet;  Jacob, b. June 25, 1679; Jeronimus, b. Mar. 31, 1682; Catalina, b.  Mar. 25, 1685, m. Peter Dumond, of Rantan, N. J.; Sarah, b. Nov. 4,  1687, m. Hans Bergen; and Cornelius, b. Oc
2 CONC t. 21, 1690. Of these Joris  m. July 27, 1694, Neily, dau. of Jan Couwenhoven, and d. at  Cripplebush in 1697; issue Antie. b. 1696, who m. Johannes Lott. Jacob  m. Sarah, dau. of Abm. Brinckerhoff settled at Raritan, N. J. and ha
2 CONC d  sons. Abraham, George, Jacob, and perhaps others.  Cornelius, m.  Joanna, dau. of the Rev. Vincentius Antonides, resided in New York,  and left only female issue.  His dau. Titie, m. Hendrick Smith, and  Catalina m. John De Gra
2 CONC w.  Jeronimus m. Hilletie, dau. of Hendrick Van  Vechten, and served twenty-five years as a trustee of the town of  Brooklyn; occupying the ancestral farm at the Wnllabout, which he sold  to his son-in-law, Martin Schenck, in 1755
2 CONC . He d. Feb. 8, 1762, and  his widow, a. 91 yrs. July 10, 1775. They had ch. Antie, m. Martin  Schenck, Catalina m. Johannes Alstyne, Jannetie, m. Aris Remsen, ————  m. Rem Remsen, and Jeromus, who married Jane, daughter of Jacobu
2 CONC s  Lefferts and d. 1754, having issue Jeromus, who d. childless, and  Jane, who Married Thorn.
2 CONT
2 CONT      3. Teunis Rapalje, son of Jeronimus,2 m. Sarah Van Vechten, and  lived upon the farm in the town of Brooklyn, now owned by the heirs of  Folkert Rapelye. He was a deacon of the Brooklyn church. and d. in  1723, a. 52. He had 
2 CONC ch. Jeromus, George,4 Derick, Teunis, Folkert,5  Jane m. Remsen, and Sarah, who m. Jacobus Van Nuyse. Teunis was a  blacksmith at Bushwick, and d. in 1734, leaving issue, Sarah, Ann, and  Jane. Jeromus m. in 1719 Aeltie, dau. of C
2 CONC ornelius Van Arsdalen, and  with his brother Derick, or Richard, settled near New Brunswick, N. J.  Jeromus d. there in 1775. His ch. were Cornelius, Teunis, and Sarah.  Richard d. during the Revolution, having by his wife Antie, 
2 CONC sons  George, and Jeromus.
2 CONT
2 CONT "The Annals of Hewtown,
2 CONT  Queens County, N.Y.," by
2 CONT James Riker, Jr., 1852
1 NOTE 267     THE RAPELYE FAMILY
2 CONT
2 CONT      This numerous and reputable familY ia descended from that of de  Rapalie, which, as early as the eleventh century possed large estates  in Bretagne, and ranked among the arriere-ban of the French nobility.  Some of its member
2 CONC s were distinguished as military leades in the  crusades, others were celebrated far political eminence and
2 CONT professional talent.  But in the religious wars of the sixteenth  century, being known as Protestants, they became the victims of Papal  animosity and were scattered and expelled from France. The family  subsequently gained promin
2 CONC ence in Switzerland and Belgium, where they  acquired large possessions and continue to the present time.  Their  ancient coat of arms, above given, are intended to represent the noble  birth and origin of the family, and their re
2 CONC putation for firmness and  fidelity.
2 CONT      1. Joris Jansen de Rapalie, one of this proscribed Huguenot race,  "from Rochelle in France," was the common ancestor of all the American  families of this name.*  He came to this country with other colonists  in 1623, in the
2 CONC  Unity, a ship of the West India Company, and settled  at Fort Orange, now Albany, where he continued three years.  In 1626  he removed to New Amsterdam, and resided there till after the birth of  his youngest child.  On June, 16,
2 CONC  1637, he bought from the Indians
2 CONT
2 CONT      *The practice which afterwards obtained, of writing the final  syllable of this name with a j, was a Dutch perversion of the original  orthography.
2 CONT      Some assert that Joris Jansen de Rapalie, and Antony Jansen Van  Salen, of Gravesend, were brothers, and their family name, jansen.   Our early records do not corroborate this statement, but go very far  to disprove it.  The 
2 CONC whole seems to be a mere assumption from the  occurrance of Jansen .
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 268                     ANNALS OF NEWTON
2 CONT
2 CONT a tract of land computed at 335 acres, called Rennegaconck, now  included within the town of Brooklyn, and comprehending the lands  occupied by the U. S. Marine Hospital Here Mr. Rapalie finally  located, and spent the remainder o
2 CONC f his life. He was a leading  citizen, acted a prominent part in the public affairs of the colony,  and served in the magistracy of Brooklyn.  He died soon after the  close of the Dutch administration, his widow, Catalyntie, dau. 
2 CONC of  Joris Trico, surviving him many years. She was born in Paris, and died  Sep. 11, 1689, a. 84. Their ch. as taken from the original family  record preserved in the library of the New York Hitoricol Society,  were as follow, to 
2 CONC wit, Sara. b. June 9, 1625, m. successively to Hans  Hansse Bergan and Teunis Gysberta Bogart;* Marritie, b. Mar. 11, 1627,  m. Michael Vandervoort; Jannetie, b. Aug. 18, 1629, m. Rem  Vanderbeeck; Judith, b. July 5, 1635, m. Piet
2 CONC er Van Nest; Jan, b. Aug.  26, 1637, m. but died in 1662 without surviving issue; Jacob, b. May  28, 1639, who was killed by the Indians; Catalyntie, b. Mar. 28, 1641,  m. Jeremis Westerhout;
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT In their names; Which, however, proves not a family identity, but  simply indicates that the father of each had borne the common name of  Jan or John. It has also been set down as veritable history, that said  Antony Jansen Van Sa
2 CONC les was the progenitor of a large family of  Johnsons upon Long Island and elsewhere, of which is Hon. Jeremiah  Johnson of Brooklyn.  This is an error.  Gen. Johnson, as clearly  appears from existing records, is a descendant, in
2 CONC  the fourth  generation, of Jan Barentsen van Driest, who came in 1657 from Zuiphen  in Gueiderland, and settled at Gravesend.  See an article on Antony  Jansen van Sales in the Brooklyn Daily Eagle of Feb. 20, 1851.  The  valuabl
2 CONC e work on American Genealogy, by Holgate contains very serious  mistakes respecting the johnson and Rapalye families, which only those  aquainted with the difficulties of genealogical investigation will  know how to excuse.
2 CONT
2 CONT      *This lady, say our early records, was "the first-borne Christian  daughter in New Netherland."  In honor of this, the Dutch authorities  presented her a tract of land at the Wallabout.  This circumstance has  probably given 
2 CONC rise to the belief that she was born at the latter  place, but the statement in the text (based upon the N.Y. Documentary  Hist. iii. 50, and other records,) shows that her parents were living  at Albany at the time of her birth/ 
2 CONC nor did they settle at the  Wallabout till more than twenty five-years after.  Sara early became a  church member in New York, but united with the church at Brooklyn by  certificate in XXXX???????????? . She d. a. about XX??????. 
2 CONC  Her  ????????????? decendants ????????????????????????????????.
2 CONT
2 CONT 269                    ANNALS OF NEWTOWN
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeronimus,2 b. June 27, 1643; Annetie, b. Feb. 8, 1646, m. Marten  Reverse and Joost Fransz: Elizabet, b. Mar. 28, 1648,. m. Dirck  Hooglandt; and Daniel,10 b. Dec. 29, 1650.
2 CONT
2 CONT      2. Jeronimus Rapalje became a man of some prominence, a justice  of ihe peace and a deacon of the Brooklyn church. He m. Anna, dau. of  Teunis Denys, and had ch. Jons, b. Nov. 5, 1668; Teunis,3 b. May 5,  1671; Jan,4 b. Dec. 
2 CONC 14, 1673; Femmetie b. Oct 5, 1676, m. Jan Bennet;  Jacob, b. June 25, 1679; Jeronimus, b. Mar. 31, 1682; Catalina, b.  Mar. 25, 1685, m. Peter Dumond, of Rantan, N. J.; Sarah, b. Nov. 4,  1687, m. Hans Bergen; and Cornelius, b. Oc
2 CONC t. 21, 1690. Of these Joris  m. July 27, 1694, Neily, dau. of Jan Couwenhoven, and d. at  Cripplebush in 1697; issue Antie. b. 1696, who m. Johannes Lott. Jacob  m. Sarah, dau. of Abm. Brinckerhoff settled at Raritan, N. J. and ha
2 CONC d  sons. Abraham, George, Jacob, and perhaps others.  Cornelius, m.  Joanna, dau. of the Rev. Vincentius Antonides, resided in New York,  and left only female issue.  His dau. Titie, m. Hendrick Smith, and  Catalina m. John De Gra
2 CONC w.  Jeronimus m. Hilletie, dau. of Hendrick Van  Vechten, and served twenty-five years as a trustee of the town of  Brooklyn; occupying the ancestral farm at the Wnllabout, which he sold  to his son-in-law, Martin Schenck, in 1755
2 CONC . He d. Feb. 8, 1762, and  his widow, a. 91 yrs. July 10, 1775. They had ch. Antie, m. Martin  Schenck, Catalina m. Johannes Alstyne, Jannetie, m. Aris Remsen, ————  m. Rem Remsen, and Jeromus, who married Jane, daughter of Jacobu
2 CONC s  Lefferts and d. 1754, having issue Jeromus, who d. childless, and  Jane, who Married Thorn.
2 CONT
2 CONT      3. Teunis Rapalje, son of Jeronimus,2 m. Sarah Van Vechten, and  lived upon the farm in the town of Brooklyn, now owned by the heirs of  Folkert Rapelye. He was a deacon of the Brooklyn church. and d. in  1723, a. 52. He had 
2 CONC ch. Jeromus, George,4 Derick, Teunis, Folkert,5  Jane m. Remsen, and Sarah, who m. Jacobus Van Nuyse. Teunis was a  blacksmith at Bushwick, and d. in 1734, leaving issue, Sarah, Ann, and  Jane. Jeromus m. in 1719 Aeltie, dau. of C
2 CONC ornelius Van Arsdalen, and  with his brother Derick, or Richard, settled near New Brunswick, N. J.  Jeromus d. there in 1775. His ch. were Cornelius, Teunis, and Sarah.  Richard d. during the Revolution, having by his wife Antie, 
2 CONC sons  George, and Jeromus.
2 CONT
2 CONT "The Annals of Hewtown,
2 CONT  Queens County, N.Y.," by
2 CONT James Riker, Jr., 1852
