1 NAME Hannah Dudley /Thorne/
2 GIVN Hannah Dudley
2 SURN Thorne
2 _MARNM Thorne
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 13 OCT 1821
2 PLAC Chester Twp, Burlington Co, NJ
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 18 MAY 1901
2 PLAC Moorestown, Burlington, New Jersey, United States
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Their only child inherited the property a year after her marriage, Nov 17 1842, to Asa R Lippincott. Her mother and maiden aunt, Mary Thorne, known as Aunt Polly, continued to live in the homestead until the time o
2 CONC f their deaths.
2 CONT
2 CONT The farm, known as the 'Thornedale Farm' remained in the family until it was sold in 1922 to the neighboring farmer, J. Heulings Coles.  Now (1960) its fields are covered with dwellings, the old homestead had been demolished durin
2 CONC g the previous year, and the development is known as Cherry Valley and Peppermill farms.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is interesting to note that the original King's Highway  from Salem to Burlington crossed the farm.  From Haddonfield to Moorestown it passsed in front of the old St. Mary's Church in the Colestown Cemetery (whose site is indic
2 CONC ated by a marker), down the hill across the garden to the east of the farmhouse, on down past the Matlack sawill it crossed by bridge the southern branch of the Pennsauken Creek, up to the old blacksmith shop still standing on the
2 CONC  Lindley Gardiner farm, on across the present Route 73, in front of the old Matlack homestead occupied by "Libby" Matlack Haines and family to the Chester Brick school house (destroyed by fire), and on across the northern branch o
2 CONC f the Pennsauken creek near the present bridge on the Lenola Road.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hannah D, as she was generally known, attneded the Chester Brick School and also a baording school, kept by Lydia Sharp in Mount Holly.  
2 CONT
2 CONT A few years after their marriage, Thornedale farmhouse was extensively altered to about twice its former size, much better suited to the growing family.  Three months after the youngest of the six daughters was born, the eldest wa
2 CONC s married and during the next three years there were two more weddings.  When the older son was married, the parents and the younger children moved to Moorestown at 219 East Main Street.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hannah D, showed an exceptional interest in her relatives through visits and correspondence, keeping the bonds so closely with her Indiana cousins that later generations have interchanged visits.  During the eight years that she s
2 CONC urvived her husband, she was a welcome visitor in the families of her children.  The compiler has happy memories of her annual visit to Thornedale for the month of August.  
2 CONT
2 CONT src: Descendants of Asa R. and Hannah D. Lippincott. c. 1960 Elizabeth R. Lippincott
