1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Jonathan Thorne (1801-1884) was a leatherworker and tannery manager and "proprietor of one of the largest establishments of trade in New York," according to Prominent Families of New York (1898).
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-...
2 CONT
2 CONT Jonothan Thorne: Jonathan Thorne was born on 20 Apr 1801 in Nine Partners, Westchester Co, NY. He was born in Father's store building He died on 11 Oct 1884 in New York. He was buried in Woodlawn Cemetery, Jerome Ave, Bronx, NY. H
2 CONC e was born in the original Thorne house & general store close, to the brick Nine Partners Meeting House. In 1820 he went to New York to work in the dry goods business. There he met & married Lydia Ann Corse. After returning to Nin
2 CONC e Partners, he decided in 1830 that his future was in NY. He went into his father-in-law's leather business, with his brother-in-Law, Barney Corse. Their offices were at Jacob & Ferry St. Two years later He bought out Barney's int
2 CONC erest. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They first lived on Cliff Street, then 3 Washington Square, which was laid out in 1826, then Orchard Street until they made a final move up town to 524 5th Avenue. Orchard Street, one of the finest in NYC near the Battery was near
2 CONC  the Tannery on Pea Street. In the 1830's only 1/6 of Manhattan was filled with houses & shops. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a steadfast Friend with a reputation for honorable dealings & for extending a helping hand to all good causes. His Tannery prospered, & men in the market said they would rather buy seconds from Thorne than firsts from other
2 CONC  leather merchants. He bought large tracts of hemlock land in Pennsylvania, where he established tanneries. His interests broadened to include banks, insurance, gas for lighting, the 6th Avenue Railroad, coal mining, distribution 
2 CONC of food supplies, & other tanneries in New York & Pennsylvania, as well as woodlands, which supplied the required tannic acid. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was attracted to progress & very early showed showed a strong interest in transportation, as a means of progress. The first clipper ship was not launched until 1843. His kids doubtless rode the first horse drawn railroad est. i
2 CONC n 1832 between Price & 14th Streets. The first steam powered British ship arrived 1838. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At the death of his Father in 1849, He inherited the family homestead Thornedale in Dutchess County. He made substantial improvements, to convert from a farm, to a summer residence. In effort to teach the locals a better sense of 
2 CONC enterprise, He decided to begin rearing blood cattle. He ran the operation until 1854 when Samuel took it over. Samuel & F. Rotch, an adviser, left for the U.K. with the order to buy the best animal they could find at any price. T
2 CONC hey selected Grand Duke, for a thousand guineas, the highest price ever paid for a single animal. He also bought 3 Dutchesses abroad. Over time the Thorndale herd took its stand as one of the best & most valuable in the world. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As he grew up the Quaker debate between the conservatives & the reformists was raging. He & the other Thorne's sided with the Liberal Hicksites who retained the Nine Partners Meeting House. The conservatives took over the Boarding
2 CONC  School. In 1835 He obtained a removal certificate to transfer him to the Hester Street Meeting in New York, at the corner of Elizabeth Street. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He & his daughter Phebe Anna Thorne founded The Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children. He had 11 children but only 7 lived to maturity. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He moved to New York & worked with Father-in-Law in his hide & fur business amassing a fortune. Phoebe Anna Thorne the well known Quakeress, was his 3rd child 1828 - 1909.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married to Lydia Ann Corse (daughter of Israel Corse and Lydia Troth) on 9 Jul 1823 in New York City. Lydia Ann Corse was born on 27 Dec 1805. She died on 14 Oct 1872 in London, Essex, England. She died while traveling abro
2 CONC ad with husband & son Samuel. She was 1 of 11 children.  
2 CONT
