1 NAME Christian /Parker/
2 GIVN Christian
2 SURN Parker
2 _MARNM Parker
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 11 SEP 1807
2 PLAC Twin Creek
2 ADDR
3 STAE Ohio
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 24 AUG 1888
1 OCCU 5 children
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Birth 	11 Sep 1807 Death 	24 Aug 1888 (aged 80) Burial 	 Parker Cemetery Fort Wayne, Allen County, Indiana, USA Memorial ID 	55174007 · View Source
2 CONT
2 CONT Among the early settlers of St. Joseph township a conspicuous figure was Christian Parker, who was elected justice in 1834, county commissioner in 1839, and in 1844 as the whig candidate, representative in the state legislature, t
2 CONC o which he was sent for four successive terms. He was born in Preble County, Ohio, September 11, 1807, of English ancestors. His grandfather, Amariah Parker, fled from Cornwall to escape impressment into the British army, and sett
2 CONC led near Boston in 1761. He and his three brothers assisted in the revolutionary struggle, one of them falling at Bunker Hill, as a lieutenant-colonel. After the war, Amariah removed to New Jersey and married Tamar Munson, and los
2 CONC t all his fortune by the depreciation of continental money.
2 CONT
2 CONT His son, Jacob Parker, was left an orphan at thirteen, without home, and he was bound out to a blacksmith at New York. Escaping from harsh treatment he reached Fort Washington, Ohio, and enlisted under General Anthony Wayne, and f
2 CONC ought at the battle of the Maumee, August 20, 1794, receiving a wound there. After his discharge he settled near Middleton, Ohio, married Mary Loy, and settled in 1801 in Preble County, Ohio. Christian Parker was the son of this s
2 CONC oldier under Wayne. He cut his way from Fort Wayne into the St. Joseph Township forests in October, 1833, and before the following February had a cabin built on section 20.
2 CONT
2 CONT He brought with him his wife, Rachel, daughter of Henry Cassell, of Preble county, born August 1, 1807, in Virginia. They were married June 18, 1829, and had the following children: Samuel C, born April 24, 1830; Francis A. C, Dec
2 CONC ember 21, 1831; Harriet (deceased), August 22, 1833; Julia A. (died May 8,1868), August 7, 1835; Jacob H. (deceased), July 17, 1837; Allen H. (deceased), April 9, 1839; Caroline, May 10, 1841; Henry C. (deceased April 20, 1875), F
2 CONC ebruary 4, 1844; Maria (deceased), January 14, 1846; Oliver P., October 13, 1848; Mary E. (deceased), January 21, 1851; Winfield S. (deceased), July 19, 1853. Mrs. Parker died February 4, 1879, and Christian Parker passed away Aug
2 CONC ust 24, 1888.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their son, Oliver P., was born on the St. Joseph township homestead, and there raised, receiving the common school education of those days. He now owns 160 acres of the old farm, a beautiful and productive tract of land. In 1868 M
2 CONC r. Parker was married to Fanny Fike, born in 1844, by whom he had three children, two of whom are living, Ulysses Grant and Estella H. On December 24, 1876, Mr. Parker was married to Kitty Lischy, a native of Kosciusko County, bor
2 CONC n April 18, 1857, and they have two children, Mabel F. R. and Pansy D.
2 CONT
2 CONT Valley of the Upper Maumee River: With Historical Account of Allen County and the City of Fort Wayne, Indiana, Volume 1, pub. by Brant & Fuller, 1889.
