1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Children of Richard Townsend and Elizabeth Wicks:
2 CONT
2 CONT IV Richard Townsend, b. 8/11 mo. (Jan.) 1670/1; d. Cedar Swamp, L. I., about 1739; m. Ruth Marvin, b. 20/10 mo. (Dec.) 1687, dau. of John and Hannah (Smith) Marvin, of Hempstead. (Mentioned In Settlement of estate).
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT W.A. Townsend, publisher: A Memorial of John, Henry, and Richard Townsend, and Their Descendants; New York: W.A. Townsend, 1865, pp. 220-221.
2 CONT
2 CONT CHAPTER XVII.
2 CONT
2 CONT RICHARD, SON OF RICHARD.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the settlement of his father's estate, we could find no mention of the twelve-weeks-old Richard, except that in 1691 he sold land in Rhode Island to Thomas Townsend, and we had despaired of tracing him, when Chancellor McCou
2 CONC n lent us some papers belonging to John D. Townsend, of Astoria, by which we found that in 1693 he was living in Hempstead, where he married the daughter of John Smith, whose wife was a daughter of Richard Gildersleeve, a magistra
2 CONC te and leading man in the early days of Hempstead. Among the papers is the following engagement with his father-in-law:
2 CONT
2 CONT "This writing witnesseth, that I, Richard Townsend, of Hempstead, on Long Island, otherwise called the Island of Nassau, in Queen's County, in requital, or satisfaction, to my father-in-law, John Smith, for his kindness to me, bot
2 CONC h in land and meadow which he has been pleased to bestow on me, I do bind myself in the sum of forty pounds, current money of this province, to keep in his employ, and to carry on his business as heretofore I have done, as far as 
2 CONC the Lord shall enable me; and if that hereafter I shall see cause to change my condition, yet I do hereby own and engage myself to be an assistant and helpful to the carrying on and managing my father's business with my own, durin
2 CONC g his and my mother's life, so far as I am able to do. In confirmation hereof I have set my hand and seal, this 18th day of November, in the year 1693.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Townsend."
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married twice, but the name of his second wife is not known. In 1717 we find, by the Town Record, that he bought a farm at Cedar Swamp. He died about 1739. It is very pleasant, after having so long lost sight of the fatherl
2 CONC ess infant, to find him prosperous, as these papers prove him to have been.
2 CONT
2 CONT --Issue, by the first wife, Richard; by the second, Mary, John, Timothy, and Silvanus.
2 CONT ________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT ~• Richard appears to have spent time in southern New Jersey for the Quaker minister  https://www.geni.com/people/Thomas-Chalkley/6000000004969779943 visits him, as recorded in Chalkley's journal (page 230-231). Richard had been a
2 CONC ssociated with Chalkley by virtue of the fact that Chalkley's wife, Martha Spicer, had been a member of the same Quaker meeting in or near Oyster Bay, Long Island.
2 CONT
2 CONT Chalkley's journal is free and online.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * '''Residence''': Hempstead,Queens Co,Cedar Swamp,Queens Co, NY
