2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} DAR Ancestor #: A022262
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Revolutionary War Military Service.:==
2 CONT
2 CONT "...I Eleazar Clark of Gloucester, in the county of Providence, in the state of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations, a revolutionary pensioner of the United States, being in the eighty first year of my age, and being solemnly 
2 CONC engaged according to the law, do testify and say, That in the month of May 1775 I enlisted into Capt. Jeremiah Olney's company in  Col. Daniel Hitchcock's regiment of Rhode Island state troops and served eight months in said compa
2 CONC ny and Nehemiah Field of Cranston was Ensign. We marched from Providence to Jamaica Plains in Roxbury in the neighborhood of Boston Massachusettes and remained there until a short time after the battle of Bunker Hill when we march
2 CONC ed to Prospect Hill, and there remained until we were discharged on the last of December or the first of January..."
2 CONT
2 CONT >''SOURCE'': www.fold3.com/image/#16181312
2 CONT
2 CONT "...I Eleazar Clark aforsaid do not know when I was born from any Records - As I have never had any - but I have always understood that I was born in 1756  I was born in Gloucester - Thence removed to Cranston at 7 years of age - 
2 CONC Thence to Providence for six months - Thence to Cranston where I was when I enlisted - After the war I went to Gloucester when I have lived ever since.
2 CONT
2 CONT I listed for Eight months in May 1775 in Jeremiah Olney's Company and Col Hitchcock's regiment and did actual service during the whole time - I joined the company from Providence - from thence I 
2 CONT was marched to Jamaica Plains and staid there until about the first of July - Thence I was marched to Prospect Hill - and staid there until the last of Dec when my enlistment expired.
2 CONT
2 CONT After this - immediately after, I enlisted for one year in the same company under the same Captain and Colonel - and after remaining at Prospect Hill till the last of March 1776 I was marched to New York - I stayed a few days at P
2 CONC rovidence - A few days at Norwith - a few days at New London and took shipping and went to New York - and stayed in New York three weeks, I then went to Long Island and was in the Fort during the engagement.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then we retreated into New York - and after staying there a week or ten days I went to Haarlem - and after a stay of about a fortnight there, I went to Haarlem Heights thence I went into Jersey, among the sick - and was there abou
2 CONC t 3 weeks - Upon recovering, I went to a place called English Neighborhood- and then stayed till the first of January - at which time my enlistment expired. I served one month at Peekskill guarding Prisoners - and provisions - and
2 CONC  floating wood across North River - This ended my enlisted service - making in all twenty one months
2 CONT
2 CONT In June '77 I was drafted to go to Warwick under Capt. Frederick Williams of Cranston and stayed about 16 days - In the same year I was drafted to go to Providence under Capt. Keene and stayed about 16 days - 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1778 I went as a substitute for Sion Arnold of warwick, under Capt Lippot..."
2 CONT
2 CONT >''SOURCE'': Revolutionary War Pensions; Rhode Island; http://www.fold3.com/image/#1|12195175
