2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT [[Category:Philadelphia Yearly_Meeting]] [[Category:Darby Monthly_Meeting,_Pennsylvania]]
2 CONT {{Quakers Sticker}}
2 CONT {{Pennsylvania Settlers}}
2 CONT == Biography == [Please note: this biography and sources are being upgraded to the standards required of the William Penn Pennsylvania Settlers project. Please be patient. If you have documented sources to add please use inline ci
2 CONC tations. We are aware the save command prompts a Warning at this time. This will be rectified as correct birth dates of proven children are researched and entered. ''Please do not link additional children nor add any below unless 
2 CONC there is a primary source document showing date, place and parentage.'' Thank you.] 
2 CONT Edward Pearson is believed to be the eldest child of Lawrence Pearson and Elizabeth Janney born between 1648 and 1651 in Pownall Fee or Wilmslow, Cheshire, England. While the Quaker birth records of his youngerbrothers Thomas and 
2 CONC John have been located, Edward's birth predates the minutes book in which his younger siblings records are found. The prior minutes book has not yet been located. 
2 CONT The marriage record between Edward and Sarah Burgis (which is the spelling on documents of the period) is dated 6 July 1670 in the Monthly Meeting of Cheshire records.<ref>Society of Friends' Registers, Notes and Certificates of B
2 CONC irths, Marriages and Burials. Records of the General Register Office, Government Social Survey Department, and Office ofPopulation Censuses and Surveys, RG 6. The National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey, England.  Piece 1036: Mon
2 CONC thly Meeting of Cheshire, formerly Morley (1655-1741), Frame 19</ref> It is sometimes stated that the marriage occured in Thomas Pott's Home, Pownall Fee, Cheshire, England but a citation for this has not yet been found.  Five of 
2 CONC the six documented children of Edward and Sarah were born in England and the sixth after their arrival in Pennsylvania. :[[Pearson-389 | Deborah]] 25 Oct 1675, Pownall Fee, Cheshire<ref name="CheshireBirth">Society of Friends' Reg
2 CONC isters, Notes and Certificatesof Births, Marriages and Burials. Records of the General Register Office, Government Social Survey Department, and Office of Population Censuses and Surveys, RG 6. The National Archives, Kew, Richmond
2 CONC , Surrey,England. Piece 1398: Monthly Meeting of Cheshire, formerly Morley (1654-1776), Frame 29</ref> :[[Pearson-390 | Lawrence]] 2 Sep 1677, Pownall Fee, Cheshire<ref name="CheshireBirth"  /> :[[Pearson-387 | Sarah]] 27 Aug 1680
2 CONC , Pownall Fee, Cheshire<ref name="CheshireBirth" /> :[[Pearson-357 | Enoch]] 27 Mar 1683, Pownall Fee, Cheshire<ref>Society of Friends' Registers, Notes and Certificates of Births, Marriages and Burials. Records of the General Reg
2 CONC ister Office, Government Social Survey Department, and Office of Population Censuses and Surveys, RG 6. The National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey, England. Piece 1398: Monthly Meeting of Cheshire, formerly Morley (1654-1776), F
2 CONC rame 33</ref> :[[Pearson-391 | Phebe]]  22 Apr 1685 (died 1686), Pownall Fee, Cheshire<ref>Society of Friends' Registers, Notes and Certificates of Births, Marriages and Burials. Records of the General Register Office, Government 
2 CONC Social Survey Department, and Office of Population Censuses and Surveys, RG 6. The National Archives, Kew, Richmond, Surrey, England. Piece 1035: Quarterly Meeting of Cheshire and Staffordshire: Cheshire (1631-1799), Frame 28</ref
2 CONC > :[[Pearson-388 | Martha]] 8 De 1687, Delaware, Pennsylvania<ref>Swarthmore College; Swarthmore, Pennsylvania; Births and Burials, 1682-1835;Collection: Quaker Meeting Records; Call Number: MR-PH-139</ref> Edward's brothers Thoma
2 CONC s and John are documented arriving in Pennsylvania in 1683. From various records it is concluded that Edward arrived 1687. One source does list his arrival as 1682<ref>TEPPER, MICHAEL, editor. New World Immigrants: A Consolidation
2 CONC  of Ship Passenger Lists and Associated Data from Periodical Literature. Baltimore: Genealogical Publishing Co., 1979. 568p. and 602p. Repr. 1980. Vol. 1.</ref> and another with sometimes uncertain verification lists 1687<ref>DILL
2 CONC ER, CORINNE HANNA. "Immigrant Ancestors of Miami Valley, Ohio, Quaker Families." In Miami Valley Genealogical Society: Genealogical Aids Bulletin, vol. 13:3 (Winter 1984), pp. 64-67.</ref> Hinshaw's unpublished index contains a tr
2 CONC anscription of the acceptanceof Edward's Society of Friends transfer from England which was accepted 1 Apr 1687 at the Monthly Meeting held at Ralph Brockes (Brookes?) in Bramhall, Chester County.<ref>Hinshaw, William Wade. Willia
2 CONC m Wade Hinshaw's Index to Unpublished Quaker Records. Swarthmore, Pennsylvania: Friends Historical Library, Swarthmore College. Darby Monthly Meeting (Me-Z)</ref> Further, the birth locations and dates of his children corroborate 
2 CONC arrival in 1687.  Various references to Edward refer to him as a mason and builder, a trade in common with at least one of his siblings.  The Quaker death record of Edward has not been found as yet. He appears in Falls Monthly Mee
2 CONC ting minutes in 1695 and 1696. Various family histories read that he passed away in 1697 and it is said he was buried at Fallsington, Bucks, Pennsylvania. The area today is Levittown. The original meeting house was replaced in 178
2 CONC 9 and was last reported as being used as a Friends Day Care Center.  Some believe that an October 1698 intention to marry<ref>Swarthmore College; Swarthmore, Pennsylvania; Minutes, 1664-1807; Collection: Philadelphia Yearly Meetin
2 CONC g Minutes; Call Number: MR-Ph 305</ref> between Martin Wildman and Sarah Pearson refers to the widow of Edward. The record does not indicate she is the widow of Edward and this requires further research to prove or disprove this i
2 CONC s the widow of Edward. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Children Incorrectly Associated with this Family==
2 CONT
2 CONT A number of children are sometimes wrongly attached to this family:
2 CONT [[Pearson-450 | Simon Pearson]] born 1665 cannot be a member of this family. His date and place of birth (and other records in his profile) prove that he is not a member of this family as does the date of birthitself which predate
2 CONC s the proven marriage by five years. [[Pearson-430 | Mary Pearson]] born 1665 cannot be a member of this family. Her date of birth is five years before the proven marriage of the parents and the location in the Colonies predates t
2 CONC heir arrival in the colonies by 20 years. [[Pierson-13 | Mary Pierson Scarborough]] born 1673. While the date ofbirth is after the marriage of Edward and Sarah they had not yet arrived in the Colonies. There does not appear to be 
2 CONC any birth record in England or Pennsylvania for a daughter Mary born to Edward and Sarah.  There is a Thomas who would have been the youngest child sometimes associated with this family. There is not yet enough information to be c
2 CONC ertain if he is or is not a part of the family but he does not seem to appear in Pennsylvania records as part of this family.  Other children sometimes seen with this family for which there is no evidence other than internet genea
2 CONC logy include: Joseph, Priscilla, William, and Elizabeth. There were a number of Pearson families in this area at this time and they become confused with some regularity. 
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT <references />
2 CONT * Hawthorne, Enid Pearson. ''The Pearson Family Tree: From England to America''. 1988. pg.3 This profile was re-sourced and rewritten by [[Stanton-3574 | T Stanton]] Sep 2018.
