1 NOTE <p>In Colonial Virginia/Maryland, the name Simon Pearson has documented history in two states, in a region known as the Northern Neck of Virginia, and the adjacent region across the Potomac River in Baltimore County, Maryland. On 
2 CONC the surface, it appears that there were two separate men, both of similar age, and sharing the same friends, etc.</p><p>     Earlier genealogist have proposed that both men were in fact, the same man. There is significant evidence
2 CONC  to support this theory, yet nothing found to absolutely prove it, or to refute it.      </p><p>     The genealogy of Simon Pearson of Stafford County Virginia is well documented in Virginia history books. Simon was the son of Tho
2 CONC mas Pearson and  the daughter of John Peake, Sr.  Simon had 4 children from an unknown wife. In ca. 1726, he married Hannah Ball, half sister to the mother of George Washington. Simon's Last Will and Testament was probated in Staf
2 CONC ford Virginia in 1733. </p><p>     The second Simon Pearson is first documented in Baltimore County, Maryland in the late 1690's acquiring land. His first wife was named Emm. Simon had substantial land holdings in Southern Marylan
2 CONC d and his name is found numerous times in the early colonial deed transactions. In ca. 1715, he married Sarah Thurston Shaw (a widow), daughter of Thomas Thurston, an infamous Quaker of his time. Moses Pearson was born in May of 1
2 CONC 716 and his birth is found record in Parish records. </p><p>     The last child born to Simon and Sarah was named Samuel, who was born in 1723. What happened next, or why, we may never know, but we find Sarah deserting Simon. We f
2 CONC ind documents in the Maryland archives in Simon's handwriting declaring her desertion, and disavowing responsibility for her actions and debts... a document which was posted on the courthouse door...</p><p>     Nothing else has be
2 CONC en found of Sarah Pearson. We know that their daughter, and sister to Moses and Samuel, Comfort Pearson married in Maryland and her name can be found numerous times in the Baltimore County records.  </p><p>    Researchers of Samue
2 CONC l Pearson have theorized that Sarah took some of the children with her, including Moses and moved eventually to North Carolina. We find early records of Samuel in colonial Craven County.... part of which became Johnston County... 
2 CONC and the next the part that became Wake County..</p><p>    Moses Pearson's name is found in early deed records of Johnston County, as a reference to property boundaries to his brother Samuel and other neighbors. From the Johnston C
2 CONC ounty archives, etc... it appears that Moses Pearson only stayed in the area for a short period and was probably gone by 1750-1755.</p><p>     There was no Will record for Simon Pearson of Maryland. His name literally disappears f
2 CONC rom all Maryland records prior to 1733. There are notations associated with his last land transactions in Maryland as being the last of his holdings in Maryland.</p><p>    The riddle to resolve for genealogist is whether or not bo
2 CONC th Simon Pearson's were the same man. We find a strong friendship with the Harrison Family in both states.. The same Harrison family held land in both states (counties). We find John Harrison as a resident of Stafford County, Va. 
2 CONC in the latter 1730's. Another close friend of Simon was Thomas Preston. The name Preston  is strongly associated with the region, inclusive of both sides of the Potomac.  </p><p>    We find the name Preston appearing numerous time
2 CONC s in the lineage of Moses Pearson and Samuel Pearson as well.</p><p>     If both Simon Pearsons were not the same, we strongly believe they were contemporaries and closely related. Hopefully later research will define the relation
2 CONC ship.       </p><p>     In his book, J. Kelly Pearson assumed that Moses Pearson and Simon Pearson were brothers. This assumption today we believe to be totally false. If we look at the evidence purely from the perspective of Simo
2 CONC n Pearson of Virginia, we can find no reference to Moses Pearson... in a family that has substantial documentation.</p>
1 NOTE <p>RESIDENCE: In 1692, "Symon Parson" was taxable at the Widow Long's in the Northside of Patapsco Hundred.</p><p>RESIDENCE: In 1694, "Simeon Parson" was taxable in the Southside of Gunpowder Hundred.</p><p>RESIDENCE: Symon Pearso
2 CONC n and wife, Emma, of Baltimore County, weaver, purchased 100 acres at the head of Bush River and the north side of Bynum's Run, March 9, 1704.</p>DEATH: In November 1742, Elizabeth Matthews, widow, petitioned the court regarding "
2 CONC Pearson's Park," originally surveyed for Simon Pearson, now deceased.
