1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From the website: http://www.dickensoncounty.net/clinchhist.html  "The History of Clinchco"
2 CONT
2 CONT Clinchco was incorporated in 1990, but the town's history began nearly a century and a half before with the sale of the Warder land holdings in Virginia in 1850.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1788, twenty-two years after the Revolutionary War, Virginia entered the Union as the 10th state. It was customary in those days to grant large boundaries of land to individuals for settlement. Richard Smith received land paten
2 CONC ts for some 300,000 acres of land in this area from the State of Virginia in the 1780's and 1790's.
2 CONT
2 CONT Smith was a wealthy merchant and absentee land owner. In 1806 he conveyed 348,724 acres of land to a group of Philadelphia businessmen, consisting of John Warder, Jeremiah Warder, and John Head Warder for a sum of $12,000. The bou
2 CONC ndary of the land ran from the Clinch River in Russell County to the headwaters of Contrary Creek in Buchanan County, encompassing most of what is now Dickenson County.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sale of the Warder lands began in 1850, and it was in that year that Johnathan W. "John or Shaun" Wright (note by Bobby, this is our Johnathan Wright) secured a deed from the Warders for 360 acres of land, which included and surro
2 CONC unded all of the present town of Clinchco. He built a one-room log home in the area of Clinchco now known as "back row". The Old House Fork of Mill Creek was named for Wright's house.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1879 Noah Sykes purchased 853 acres of land from John Wright's son, William B.Wright (note by Bobby, this is our William Bailey Wright, father of William Jasper Wright). Sykes moved his family to Clinchco from Russell County, w
2 CONC here they added rooms to the old log "Wright" house, and raised their family there. Noah, and his wife, Susan J. (Arrington) Sykes are both buried in the Graveyard Hill cemetery in Clinchco on the hill which stood behind their hom
2 CONC e.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sykes brought the first industry to Clinchco in the form of a water powered grist mill. The Sykes Mill stood in the area of town known now as the Brick Flats.
2 CONT
2 CONT Clinchco remained a sparsely settled place until 1915 when the Clinchfield Railroad was built through the area from Spartanburg, South Carolina to Elkhorn City, Kentucky. As late as 1913 there were only 18 residences in the vicini
2 CONC ty of Clinchco. The early pioneer families simply called the settlement "The Mouth of Mill Creek".
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, the Clinchfield Railroad located a flagstop at Clinchco which was called Moss after one of the railroad contractors who worked in the area. And so Clinchco was known as the town of "Moss' for sometime. Eventually a post off
2 CONC ice was established in the growing town in 1917. The Post Office was named Clinchco, in honor of the Clinchfield Coal Corporation, which had begun mining operations in the town, and had made their first shipments of coal from Clin
2 CONC chco in that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Solomon H and Rachel Pickering Wright
2 CONT Married Mary Jane (Polly) Bailey Before 1820 in Burke,Yancey County,North Carolina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Research Notes:
2 CONT Jonathan was one of the first settlers in the Clincho,Virginia area.
2 CONT The following is an excerpt from"The History of Clinchco:
2 CONT In 1778.twenty-two years after the Revolutionary War,Virginia entered the Union as the 10th state.It was customary in those days to grant large bountaries of land to individuals for settlement.Richard Smith recieved land patents f
2 CONC or some 300,000 acres of land in this area from the state of Virginia in the 1780's and 1790's.
2 CONT Smith was a wealthy merchant and absentee land owner.In 1806 he conveyed 348,724 acres of land to a group Philadelphia businessmen,consisting of John Warder,Jeremiah Warder,and John Head Warder for a sum of $12,000.The boundary of
2 CONC  the land ran from the Clinch River in RussellCounty to the headwters of Contrary Creek in Buchanan County,encompassing most of what is now Dickenson County.
2 CONT Sale of the warder lands began in 1850,and it was that year that Jonthan W. "John or Shaun" Wright secured deed from the Warders for 360 acres of land,which included and surrounded ll of the present town of Clinchco.
2 CONT He built a one room log home in the area of Clincho now known as "Back Row".The Old house fork of Mill Creek was named for Wright's house.
2 CONT In 1879 Noah Sykes purchase 853 acres of land from John Wright's son,William B Wright.Sykes moved his family to Chincho from Russell County,where they added rooms to the old log "Wright" house,and raised their family there.Noah,an
2 CONC d his wife Susan J (Arrington) Sykes are both buried in the Graveyard Hill Cemetery in Clincho on the hill which stood behide their home.Sykes brought the first industry to Clincho in the form of a water powered grist mill.The Syk
2 CONC es mill stood in the area of town known as the brick Flats.
2 CONT Clincho remained a sparsely settled place until 1915 when the Clincho Railroad was built through the area from Spartanburg,South Carolina to Elkhorn City,Kentucky.As late as 1913 there was only 18 residences in the vicinity of Cli
2 CONC ncho.The early pioneer families simply called the settlement "The Mouth of Mill Creek"
2 CONT Later,the Clinchfield Railroad located a flagstop at Clincho which as called Moss after one of the railroad contractors who worked in the area.And so Clincho was known as the town of "Moss" for sometime.Eventually  post office was
2 CONC  established in the growing town in 1917.The Post Office was named Clincho,in honor of the Clinchfield Coal Corporation,which had begun mining operations in the town,and had made their first shipment of coal from Clincho in that y
2 CONC ear.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jonathan and Polly are listed in the 1820 Census of Burke County,North Carolina living next door to his father Solomon Wright.They moved to Virginia around 1820 stopped one year at Guest Station(Coeburn MM) around 1836,then moved 
2 CONC to below Clintwood.In 1850 he purchased several thousand acres in the Clincho area making him the first settlers there.His land holding showed a value of $4000.
2 CONT Jonathan W. and Polly appear on the 1850 Census of Russell County,Virginia living next door to their son Aaron.Their following children are also listed:
2 CONT John B 19yrs,
2 CONT Solomon H 17yrs,
2 CONT Thomas J 14yrs,
2 CONT Jasper 12yrs,
2 CONT Waitsel 10 yrs.
2 CONT All are listed as born in North Carolina
2 CONT
2 CONT 1860 United State Federal Virginia Buchanan
2 CONT Jonathan Wright 59 yrs old
2 CONT Wife Mary 60 yrs old
2 CONT Son Thomas 23 yrs old
2 CONT
2 CONT 1870 United State Federal Virginia Buchanan Sand Lick
2 CONT Wright J.W. 69 yrs old
2 CONT Wife Mary 70 yrs old
2 CONT
2 CONT Jonathan married Mary Jane (Polly) Bailey,daughter of William "Billy" Bailey and Elizabeth McMullen,before 1820 in Burke,Yancey County,North Carolina.
2 CONT Mary was born in 1798 in Yancey County,North Carolina,died on 15 Nov 1883 in Dickenson County,Virginia at age 85,and was buried in John Kerr Cem.,Dickenson County,Virginia.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/32113510
