1 NOTE William TWINING (b. ca 1625 in Eastham MA; d. 4 Nov 1703 in NewtownPennsylvania.)
2 CONT In Eastham he was a deacon in the Eastham Congregational Church. Around
2 CONT 1650, while still in Massachusetts, he married
2 CONT Elizabeth Deane, and they had numerous children: Elizabeth, {m. John
2 CONT Rogers 19 Aug 1669}, Anne {m. 3 Oct 1672 (1) Thomas Bills and (2) David
2 CONT Kelley}, Susanna (b. 25 Jan 1654; d. young), William {m. Ruth
2 CONT Cole}, Mehitable (d. bef 1726) {m. Daniel Doane [Richard Milhouse Nixon
2 CONT descends from this line]}, Joanna (b. 30 May 1657) and Stephen. The
2 CONT Deanes: The Connection to the Puritans on the Fortune
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Author: Twining, Thomas Jefferson, 1851- Title: Genealogy of the
2 CONT Twining family, descendants of William Twining, sr. Who came from Wales
2 CONT or England, and died at Eastham, Massachusetts, 1659. With information
2 CONT of other Twinings in Great Britain and America. By Thos. J. Twining ...
2 CONT . Chicago,
2 CONT Pub. for the author, 1890. Description: 172, ix p. ports. 23 cm.
2 CONT --------------------------
2 CONT --------------------------
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT William TWINING was active in the community. In 1659 Eastham granted him
2 CONT three and one-half acres for a house lot adjoining William TWINING Sr. In
2 CONT 1662 he was admitted and sworn to the Grand
2 CONT Jury at Eastham, and again three times in 1671. He owned and sold land at
2 CONT  Bound Brook, Namakassett, Poche, Billingate, etc. As late as 1681 he was
2 CONT "Deacon TWINING." Sometime thereafter, he, his wife, and
2 CONT his son Stephen changed from the Congregational Church to the Society of
2 CONT Friends. From 1670 to 1700 there was a large migration from different
2 CONT parts of New England to Long Island, East NJ, and later Pennsylvania, onaccount of
2 CONT persecution, especially of Quakers, but also some other "heritics". In
2 CONT 1695 he is on the astham records for the last time as a voter, and the
2 CONT same year he and his wife removed to Newtown Pennsylvania., accompaniedby his son
2 CONT Stephen, and his family. Their names apper in the Middletown Meeting
2 CONT records in 1699. His son William (the third) remained in Eastham and
2 CONT Congregational.
2 CONT --------------------------
2 CONT --------------------------
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT William "Deacon" Twining II, Born: October 25, 1619 in Gloucester,
2 CONT England, Died: November 04, 1703 in Newtown, Bucks County, Pennsylvania.
2 CONT Both William and his father William were involved in land and civil
2 CONT transactions on Cape Cod. William Jr. was probably the one admitted to
2 CONT the grand jury at
2 CONT Plymouth in 1752 and three times more by 1671.  His father was granted
2 CONT land next to him at the head of Great Namskaket Bay on May 13, 1654.  He
2 CONT was a deacon in the Eastham Congregational Church.     In 1659, he
2 CONT was granted a house and 3.5 acres next to his father at Poche, on the
2 CONT east side of Town Cove.  In 1662, he was sworn to the Grand Jury at
2 CONT Eastham.  He received 10 acres from his step-father-in-law Josiah Cooke
2 CONT in 1664. There were other lands also received at Billingate(Wellfleet)
2 CONT and in Barnstable County. He deeded land at Bound Brook in Yarmouth to
2 CONT Peter Warden in 1669 and also 100 acres to Thomas Doggett in 1671, for 28
2 CONT pounds.  He served on the Grand Jury three times in 1671.  In 1674, he
2 CONT deeded land at Princes Neck by River Sparrow, near Eastham, to John
2 CONT Yates, for 20 pounds. On May 5, 1693, he granted land at Poche to
2 CONT son-in-law Joseph Young. He was also deacon in the church in 1677 and
2 CONT 1681.  The family moved to Newtown, Pennsylvania about 1695, after he
2 CONT appeared on the voter rolls of Eastham for the last time. Son Stephen had
2 CONT purchased 500 acres
2 CONT near Newtown, and become a Quaker. His will was made out in 1697 and
2 CONT proved in April of 1705. It refers to his children and land still held on
2 CONT Cape Cod.  William converted from being a Congregationalist to being a
2 CONT Quaker. He moved to Pennsylvania to live in the Quaker community. He left
2 CONT his Massachusetts
2 CONT property to his son William.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:  Mark S. & Mariah Lawson GEDCOM  midgard@icx.net>
