1 NAME James Allen /Starbuck/
2 GIVN James Allen
2 SURN Starbuck
2 _MARNM Starbuck
1 NAME James Allen Starbuck (U.S.A.)
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Twin Hill Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Pennville
3 STAE Indiana
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 20 DEC 1838
2 ADDR
3 STAE Ohio
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 9 JUN 1914
2 ADDR
3 CITY Pennville
3 STAE Indiana
3 CTRY United States
1 TITL Lt.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Veteran of Civil War'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Affiliation: Union
2 CONT
2 CONT Rank:1st Lt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Division: Infantry
2 CONT
2 CONT Company: C &G
2 CONT
2 CONT Regiment: 75th and 12th  Indiana
2 CONT
2 CONT Branch: Infantry
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Recorded as written:
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Starbuck is a native of Logan county. Ohio and was born on the 20th of December, 1838. He was the eldest child in a of four children born to Joseph and  Rebecca (Lupton) Starbuck. Three of the family are now living. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Our subject was but five years old when he was doubly orphaned within less than one year. From that day to the present he has been largely dependent upon his own resources. He lived with his grandfather Lupton until death again re
2 CONC ndered him homeless, after which he lived in the family of his uncle William Lupton. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Returning from his militaryservice, Mr. Starbuck went in August 1863 to Linn county, Iowa where he lived for fourteen years. While there he was engaged as a contractor of brick work and was also interested in agricultural work Whi
2 CONC le a resident of Iowa, Mr. Starbuck was married April 4, 1869 to Miss Ann Eliza Crew. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT '''War Record'''
2 CONT
2 CONT While a member of that family, the Civil war overshadowed the country with darkness and gloom and he was among the first to offer services in defense of the flag. Responding to the president's call for three months men, he was enr
2 CONC olled as a member of Company G, Twelfth Indiana Volunteer Infantry on the 19th of April 1861. Their temporary rendezvous was at Fort Wayne, going from there to Indianapolis for drill and equipments, where they were assigned duty i
2 CONC n the Army of the Potomac and hurried on to the defense of Washington, The regiment arrived on the field just after  the disastrous defeat at Bull Run on the of July I86I. Our subject was at Washington D.C. some time after the exp
2 CONC iration of his term of enlistment  and returned home for a brief period only. The call for three hundred thousand men for "three years or during the war" was a stimulus which prompted many a young to leave the domestic fireside, f
2 CONC or it now evident that the suppression of the rebellion was no" before breakfast job" as at first designated. Young Starbuck promptly responded to this call and was commissioned first lieutenant in the Seventy fifth Indiana Infant
2 CONC ry. His regiment became a part of the Fourteenth Army Corps under command of General H. Thomas and General Rosecrans was in command of the Army of the Cumberland. The division commander was General J.J. Reynolds. Our subject parti
2 CONC cipated in battle of Hoover's Gap, which for a beginning was a rather rough initiation into realities of war. He was on the ready for action at the battle of Stone River but did not participate.
2 CONT
2 CONT The regiment was employed in the chase after the raider General Morgan and assisted in the capture of that brave and fearless Confederate. Our subject participated in all the general engagements in which his command took part incl
2 CONC uding Chickamauga, Missionary Ridge, Nashville, the famous march to the sea and battles en route viz: Atlanta, where his regiment was under fire for one hundred and five days during the siege and final capture. Hartsville, Milton,
2 CONC  Hoover's Gap, Tullahoma Chickamauga, Chattanooga, Missionary Ridge, Graysville, Ringgold, Tunnel Hill, Rocky Face Ridge, Dalton. Resaca, Adairsville, Ackworth, Cassville, Pickett's Mills, Culp's Farm, New Hope Church, Big Shanty,
2 CONC  Lost Mountain, Pine Mountain, Marietta, Kennesaw Mountain, Smyrna, Peach Tree Creek, Chattahoochee River, Atlanta, Ezra Church, Utoy Creek, Jonesboro, Allatoona, and the march to the sea, Savannah, Ebenezer Church, Sister's Ferry
2 CONC , Barnwell Court  House through the Carolinas, Fayetteville, Averysboro, Bentonville and Smithfield. At the battle of Jonesboro, his regiment led the attacking column and government statistics show that every third man in the brig
2 CONC ade was killed or wounded. In the battle of Buzzards Roost, Lieutenant Starbuck's company was sent on a recoinoitering expedition to determine the position of the enemy. They were hotly engaged and closely pressed but escaped with
2 CONC out capture. The famous march to the sea was made on parallel roads, sweeping a strip of country sixty miles wide. The army lived on the product of the foragers who were regularly detailed from each company to bring in whatever fo
2 CONC od could be found. Sometimes they were successful but not always; hence it was a "feast or a famine" with the boys according as the foragers reported "plentiful or scanty supplies". The March to the Sea was completed in thirty fou
2 CONC r days of most exhausting marching. The "flanks" who protected the main army from surprise or attacks from ambush were in constant contact with the enemy. The flankers were detailed from the companies and the ranks were so deplete
2 CONC d in number that an individual was usually placed on this detail every other morning. Their line of march was through fields or woodland, across streams or rugged precipices, always maintaining about the same distance between them
2 CONC selves and the main marching columns. If attacked by a force superior in numbers they held their ground long enough to permit the main column to form in line of battle and then fall back to their comrades in the the ranks until th
2 CONC e contest was decided and then they resumed their former position if they escaped the carnage of battle. The command of our subject arrived at Savannah Georgia on the 20th of December, 1864 and entered the city on the 2nd of Febru
2 CONC ary, 1865. Then began the march through the Carolinas after the unconquered Rebel Cheiftan, General J.E. Johnston. While confronting the enemy in battle very near Smithville, North Carolina, the Union soldiers were apprised of the
2 CONC  surrender of General Lee's army in Virginia. All thoughtful soldiers knew that this was the beginning of the end. The surrender of Johnston's army followed in a few days and but for the sorrow occasioned by the brutal assassinati
2 CONC on of President Lincoln, the soldier boys would have felt jubilant and happy. The march was continued on through the Carolinas and Virginia to Washington. There our subject took part in the grand review of the victorious armies an
2 CONC d says that was the proudest moment of his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT  During his army service, Lieutenant Starbuck was twice wounded and was often in positions from which he had no hope of  escaping without injury. His clothes were pierced by bullets or pieces of shells, but his four years of conti
2 CONC nuous service at front was performed without serious injury. Very few soldiers of the war can show a better military record. Lieutenant Starbuck of this sketch is a proud heritage to his children and posterity which will be treasu
2 CONC red as a heirloom long years after the valiant shall have passed to his final rest. Our subject was finally discharged from service at Washington, D.C. on the of  21st of June, 1865. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source: Bioigraphical Memoirs of Jay County Indiana; Chicago;: B.F. Bowen Company Publishers; 1901; Page 352'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Link:https://books.google.com/books?id=Rk80AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA352&lpg=PA352&dq=joseph+starbuck+married+rebecca+lupton&source'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Link''':http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=19962731
2 CONT
2 CONT Information compiled by D. Glenn
