1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Civil War Vetern'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Affiliation: Union
2 CONT
2 CONT Enlisted: Sept. 13, 1861
2 CONT
2 CONT Re-enlisted: Jan. 10, 1864
2 CONT
2 CONT Rank: Pvt. and Sgt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Company: C
2 CONT
2 CONT Regiment: 2nd Indiana
2 CONT
2 CONT Branch: Calvary
2 CONT
2 CONT Mustered out: July 22, 1865 at Nashville Tn..
2 CONT
2 CONT ___________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT  Joseph Ambrose is a resident of Iowa. By profession he is an artist and designer, in which he is very talented and skillful. He was also a soldier in Civil war where he distinguished himself on many a bloody field. His services c
2 CONC overed almost the entire period of the war, he having re- enlisted in the field. He was twice wounded.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: " Biographical Memoirs of Jay County Indiana; Chicago; B.F. Bowen Company Publishers; 1901"
2 CONT
2 CONT Link:https://books.google.com/books?id=Rk80AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA352&lpg=PA352&dq#v=onepage&q&f=false
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT 2nd Regiment Indiana Cavalry From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia 2nd Regiment Indiana Volunteer Cavalry (41st Regiment Indiana Infantry)
2 CONT
2 CONT Engagements	Battle of Shiloh Siege of Corinth Battle of Chickamauga The 2nd Regiment Indiana Cavalry, also designated the 41st Regiment Indiana Infantry or the 41st Regiment Indiana Volunteers, was the first complete cavalry regim
2 CONC ent raised in the U.S. state of Indiana to fight in the American Civil War. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Service
2 CONT
2 CONT Organized at Indianapolis, Indiana, on September 20, 1861, with John A. Bridgeland as colonel, the regiment moved to Kentucky in December as part of the Union Army of the Ohio First action in a skirmish at Bowling Green Occupation
2 CONC  of Nashville, Tennessee Battle of Shiloh Siege of Corinth Battle of Perryville Battle of Stones River Battle of Chickamauga Defense of the Nashville and Chattanooga Railroad Skirmish with a Confederate force on October 2, 1863, i
2 CONC n defense of Union communications, brought the 2nd Indiana fame when a sketch of the action appeared in Harper's Weekly for October 31 The regiment saw further action through the end of the war Mustered out of service in Tennessee
2 CONC  in July 1865 According to Frederick H. Dyer (see references) the 2nd Indiana's total service fatalities were four officers and 38 enlistees killed and mortally wounded, and three officers and 211 enlistees dead of disease.
2 CONT
2 CONT Link:http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2nd_Regiment_Indiana_Cavalry
2 CONT
2 CONT Information compiled by D. Glenn
