2 CONT
2 CONT Recorded as Written:
2 CONT
2 CONT Obituary Ruth A. Lupton Burr 1845-1919
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruth A. Lupton, daughter of Joseph and Abigail Green Lupton, was born in Pennville, Jay County, Indiana, Nov. 10, 1845, and died in Leoti, Wichita County, Kansas, July 29th, 1919, being seventy three years, eight months and ninete
2 CONC en days old at the time of her death. On October 27th, 1864 she was married to Joseph Burr and in April 1874, the family moved to Smith County, Kansas, and in February 1888, to Wichita County where she has since resided. She is su
2 CONC rvived by her husband and four children, W.A. Burr and Mrs. Wm. Brock of Leoti, Mrs. Wm. Mathes of Smith Center, and H.A. Burr of Sunnyside. One child, Adelma, died in infancy. She joined the Friends church in her youth and was a 
2 CONC member of that body at her death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The funeral services were conducted by the Rev. T.J. Davis at the Stone church north of Leoti, Kansas. The respect and love of her neighbors was evidenced by the number who attended the last services and spoke of her help and serv
2 CONC ice to them. From a life of devotion to her children and of helpfulness to her friends, and loyalty to her religious convictions she has gone to her final rest.  ______________ Ruth Ann's family sent a train carload of food to Smi
2 CONC th County after the grasshopper plague had devastated the farming community. Many survived who could not have done so without their generosity. Ruth Ann was a midwife and a life long Quaker whose lineage can be traced to William a
2 CONC nd Mary Durante Ring.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Ring (1584-1631) was a member of the Leiden (Leyden) Separatist (Quaker or Friends) community. Mary and her husband William had been aboard the Speedwell, sister ship to the Mayflower, intending to voyage across the Atlantic 
2 CONC in 1620. The William Ring family was, however, among the passengers who could not fit aboard the Mayflower when the Speedwell was deemed un-seaworthy. He returned to Leiden and died there sometime between 1620 and 1629. After his 
2 CONC death, Mary Ring took her children and set off for the New World. They arrived in Plymouth in 1629 or 1630 on the second Mayflower, which sailed from Gravesend in March, and landed at Salem Mass. on 15 May 1629, bringing 35 passen
2 CONC gers. (Mayflower Families, Hopkins, pg. 12)
2 CONT
