1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From History of Butler and Bremer Counties, Iowa:
2 CONT
2 CONT * J T Barrick was the next settler. He came here with his family in 1849, and was the founder of the village of Janesville.  John T. Barrick, a native Kentucky, but when a mere child was taken to Ohio and reared by an aunt. Upon r
2 CONC eaching his majority, John returned to the State of his birth and was there given by his father a plantation and 10 slaves. The gift he refused because of his opposition to slavery, which was decldedly marked in consequence of his
2 CONC  having been reared by a Quaker family. Returning to his foster State, he followed the trade of a carpenter and joiner. In 1840, he removed to Muscatine county, Iowa, and while there erected a saw mill. In 1845 he located in Cedar
2 CONC  Falls, Black Hawk county, and was the first person to utilize the water power at place building its first mil.l In 1849 came to Bremer county and founded the town of Janesville, naming it for his wife Jane (McPherson) Barrick. He
2 CONC re he built and operated the first mill in the county( at the same time carrying on his farm) and also erected the first frame house in the town of Janesville. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *  John T. Barrick made himself at home on section 35, township 91, range 14 where he subsequently erected a saw mill and platted the town of Janesville, naming it in honor of his wife, Jane. Here he remained until late in the six
2 CONC ties when he removed to Kansas, where he still lives. His son Isaac T. Barrick _still lives near Janesville. Mr. Barrick was a genial and pleasant old gentleman and made many warm friends among the old settlers.
2 CONT
2 CONT * (taken from a story on a supposed Indian attack on Janesville. I had to add this because it made me laugh): Some families below Janesville and some at Waterloo went down to Cedar Rapids, and some on the road moving into the coun
2 CONC try turned around and went back to Independence and stopped there until the scare was over. While the excitement was up to fever heat, John T. Barrick started out with his team and a load of men, guns, and ammunition to meet the f
2 CONC oe. Captain Eads started out with a squad of men from Cedar Rapids to the seat of war. They went up to Clear Lake or near there and finding no enemy to fight, turned the opportunity into a jolification and a social dance at Mr. He
2 CONC witt's. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Isaac and Jane Barrick:
2 CONT
2 CONT # John BARRICK b: 1841 in Ohio
2 CONT
2 CONT # Isaac BARRICK b: 25 JAN 1834 in Highland County, Ohio
2 CONT
2 CONT # Mary E. BARRICK b: 1847 in Iowa
2 CONT
2 CONT # Lydia J. BARRICK b: 1847 in Iowa
2 CONT
2 CONT # Elton A. BARRICK b: 1850 in Iowa
2 CONT
2 CONT # L. Dempsey BARRICK b: 1852 in Iowa
2 CONT
2 CONT # Samuel W. BARRICK b: 1832
2 CONT
2 CONT # Ruth BARRICK b: JUL 1856
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sources''':
2 CONT
2 CONT History of Butler and Bremer Counties, Iowa; Union Publishing Co. Springfield, Ill.; 1883
2 CONT
