1 NAME Samuel /Pike/
2 GIVN Samuel
2 _MARNM Pike
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Friends Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Largo
3 STAE Indiana
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 6 APR 1804
2 ADDR
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 11 DEC 1874
2 ADDR
3 CITY Leesburg
3 STAE Ohio
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Editor, Publisher and owner of  newspapers/Justice/auctioneer/postmaster/painter
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Written as recorded:
2 CONT
2 CONT After the HEARLD and TRIBUNE ceased to live, several other papers were started none,however, of more than ephemeral existence. Judge Lewis started one, and George Swap, another; but as neither bloomed out into full life and useful
2 CONC ness, we pass them by, not forgetting the ENQUIRER, published for about six months in 1874, by Colonel Samuel Pike, the veteran newspaper man, who had founded, and for a time successfully edited and published over 30 Democratic pa
2 CONC pers, alternating between four states. He advocated in the columns of the EQUIRER the claims of George N. Brown against W.C. Ireland for judge. the ENQUIRER was the grand old veteran's last newspaper venture. Like a wagon long in 
2 CONC use, he went down, all at once. He returned to his home in Ohio to engage in mercantile pursuits; but instead, the messenger of death suddenly summoned him from earth, and he ceased to work and live at once. He was a strong and bi
2 CONC tter partisan writer, but politics aside, no man had a kindier heart or more lovable nature than Samuel Pike.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Link''': http://files.usgwarchives.net/ky/boyd/newspapers/news1887.txt
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT From History of Franklin and Pickaway Counties, Ohio; pg. 167:
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Pike, after establishing more newspapers in four or five different States, more than any man in the country, died only a few years since in Highland county, Ohio. As an editorial writer, he occupied a high position. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Link''':https://books.google.com/books?id=hZk_AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA167&lpg=PA167&dq=samuel+pike+newspaper+editor&source=bl&ots=STKIvmnmgl&sig=hBfv4riJ6PTjwIem5y9bNID-Q-4&hl=en&sa=X&ved
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT "The Chillocothe Advertiser, owner 1840, John Hough. In 1865, owner, Hon. James Emmitt, with Sam Pike, Editor. "In Jan 1862, the office of Hillsborough Gazette" was purchased by Samuel Pike. He at once changed the tone of the pape
2 CONC r in regard to war, and advocated what was then known as t he "Peace Police." At one time, threats of mobbing the office were made by soldiers in camp at Camp Mitchell, near town.
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. Pike was a bitter partisan, and from the fact of his having published papers in the South, had a warm feeling for her people. His course seemed to be endorsed by the result of the election in Ohio that autumn, for the Democra
2 CONC ts carried the state."
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel sold the Gazette in 1865 to W. H. Hummell and return ed to Hillsboro and published the weekly paper called the " Highland Democrat," named later was changed to Hillsboro Gazette," until 1867.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was probably the most widely known editor of Highland County. He was b. April 6, 1804, in Grayson County, Virginia , but spent his early life on a farm in Penn township, and when grown to manhood removed to Leesbourgh and engag
2 CONC ed i n mercantile business.
2 CONT
2 CONT His first appearance as an editor and publisher was in Leesbourgh in 1832. To advocate construction of Belpre and Cincinnati Railroad, Joel Wright bought a small newspaper called the Highland County Democrat and R. R. Advocate. Da
2 CONC nd installed Pike as editor. Later Pike began a newspaper in Peru, Indiana; then to Bloomington, Illinois, to Piketon, Ohio, Covington, Paducah and Maysville, Kentucky; returned to Leesbourgh in 1841, Hillsborough from 1862-1869; 
2 CONC then back to Leesburgh in August 1871.
2 CONT
2 CONT As an editor he had considerable ability, but was always a bitter partisan. In private life he was a social, genial and high toned gentleman. He died in Leesburgh December 15, 1874, aged 75.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Link''':http://files.usgwarchives.net/ky/boyd/newspapers/news1887.txt
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Taken from account of destruction of William Baily's newspaper in Kentucky: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Coincidentally—later on the morning of October 6—Samuel Pike, editor of the pro-slavery Daily Maysville Kentucky Flag, appeared in Newport, offering $1,000 to bring his press from Maysville and build himself upon Bailey’s ruins.  
2 CONC Bailey, of course, refused Pike’s offer.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Link''':http://history.ky.gov/landmark/william-s-bailey-abolitionist-editor-in-the-slave-state-of-kentucky/
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sources''': 
2 CONT
2 CONT Newspapers and Periodicals of Illinois, 1814-1879; pg. 158
2 CONT
2 CONT History of Pickaway Co., Ohio and Representative Citizens; Pg. 341
2 CONT
2 CONT Collins Historical Sketches of Kentucky; History of Kentucky, Volume 2
2 CONT
