2 CONT From: <Muglich@aol.com>  Subject: [COX] Highland Co., OH Cox  Date: Thu, 26 Aug 1999 17:50:35 EDT
2 CONT
2 CONT The following msg came through on my Highland Co. newsgroup and I thought it  might be of interest to someone:
2 CONT
2 CONT Subj: [OHHIGHLA-L] Part II - Paint Township families Date: 08/26/1999 10:56:45 AM Eastern Daylight Time From: raey@email.msn.com (raey) To: OHHIGHLA-L@rootsweb.com
2 CONT
2 CONT > The following information comes from a small pamphlet compiled and,
2 CONT > "prepared by a Committee appointed by the Social Civics Club of New
2 CONT > Petersburg, Ohio - August 9th, 1967."
2 CONT >
2 CONT > My grandmother, Hazel Hiatt (Shimp) MARSH (1904 - 1999), received it when
2 CONT > she attended a reunion at McClain High School in Greenfield, Highland
2 CONT > County, Ohio.
2 CONT >
2 CONT > You can see that I have copied the names of the families described in this
2 CONT > pamphlet. I will add details taken from this pamphlet as time permits and
2 CONT > will email this document again and again until it is complete. Please be
2 CONT > patient with me. Thank you and I hope some of you may find this information useful.
2 CONT >
2 CONT > regards, Roberta
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT > Isaac OATES arrived in Highland County as a young boy of fifteen from Hampshire County, Virginia, with his parents Peter and Rachel (LUPTON) OATES. He had seven brothers and one sister with him. His father was a blacksmith by tr
2 CONC ade and died at the age of forty-eight. Isaac was married on October 24, 1861 to Catherine M., daughter of Henry and Rebecca (Parker) RHODES. He lived three years in Liberty Township before moving to Washington and later to Paint 
2 CONC Township. He then lived in Ross County until 1891 at which time he purchased a farm consisting of 355 acres lying on the Petersburg Pike eight miles from Hillsboro. Mr. and Mrs. OATES had five children : William ; Annie, wife of A
2 CONC nderson McKINNEY ; Laura, wife of Marion WILLIAMS ; Gertie, wife of William TOMPKINS ; and Philip. Marion WILLIAMS taught school in Petersburg for many years and was the town's notary public until his death,. (Wm Schubert farm)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Link''':http://news.rootsweb.com/th/read/COX/1999-08/0935704235
