1 NAME Mary Adeline /Combs/
2 GIVN Mary Adeline
2 SURN Combs
2 _MARNM Shouse
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1828
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1910
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The Courier-Journal Louisville, Kentucky Apr. 23, 1895 Page 4
2 CONT
2 CONT Wedded Half Century ……. Golden Anniversary of the Marriage of  Mr. and Mrs. D. J. Shouse ……
2 CONT
2 CONT Sulphur, Ky., April 22 – (Special) – Today witnessed the passing of a very pleasant social event in the celebration of a golden wedding by Mr. and Mrs. D. J. Shouse, of this place. All their children and quite a number of their gr
2 CONC andchildren were present, together with all their sons-in-laws and daughters-in-law, except the wife of the Rev. E. G. Shouse, of Terre Haute, Ind. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Their sons are William T. Shouse, of Henry County; J. D. Shouse, of Louisville; Rev. E. G. Shouse, pastor of the Tabernacle Baptist church, Terre Haute, Ind., and N. C. Shouse, Sheriff of Henry county, Ky. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Their daughters are; Mrs. S. H. Wright, Shelbyville, Illinois; Mrs. W. G. Morris, and Mrs. J. M. Morris of Louisville, Ky., and Mrs. L. E. Cowherd, Eminence, Ky. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Their grandchildren and great grandchildren number fifty. There were also present their life-long friends and neighbors, Mr. and Mrs. David Morris, who celebrated their golden wedding on February 23, 1893, and a number of other fr
2 CONC iends, who passed a very enjoyable day.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their former pastor, the Rev. J. T. Sampson, LaGrange, Ky., after some appropriate remarks and prayer, on behalf of the donors, presented Mr. and Mrs. Shouse with a number of beautiful and costly gifts, all of which were received 
2 CONC with due appreciation by them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Shouse was born May 30, 1819, and is therefore, nearly seventy-six years of age, his wife being his junior by a few years. He taught school for fifteen years, and spent twenty-five years in the United States Revenue service. H
2 CONC e is the only ex-revenue officer serving so long in this State. He was the son of Thomas Shouse, of Woodford county, who served under Gen. Harrison, and was wounded at the battle of Tippecanoe.
