2 CONT
2 CONT Brig. General Josiah E. Lockhart
2 CONT
2 CONT War of 1812 veteran
2 CONT
2 CONT Josiah was born in Back Creek, Frederick County, Virginia on July 17 1782. Son of Major Robert Lockhart who served in the Revolution and Margery Denny Wilson Lockhart, widow of James Wilson who was killed in the Revolutionary War.
2 CONT
2 CONT Josiah married Nancy O'Dell (b c1785 in Berkeley County, Virginia D about 1836 in Frederick County, Virginia), daughter of Rev. Thomas and Grace (Austin) O'Dell in 1803. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1805, his father Robert Lockhart received 1000 acres bounty in Adams County, Ohio, from his service in the Revolutionary War. While young, Josiah went to his father's land in Adams County, Ohio, near Bentonville. In 1810, Josia
2 CONC h received power of attorney over his father's land in Ohio, and in 1813, 500 acres were deeded to Josiah.
2 CONT
2 CONT Josiah served in the War of 1812. April 28 1812, Josiah was commissioned captain of 2nd Battalian of W. Arthur's regiment by Ohio Gov. Return Jonathan Meigs. Josiah was involved in the surrender at Detroit, Michigan by Brigadier G
2 CONC eneral William Hull. Josiah then received his commission of Brigadier General from Gov. Return Jonathan Meigs on March 1 1814, just weeks before Gov. Meigs resignation so Meigs could assume the position of Post Master General. Jos
2 CONC iah also served with General William Harrison (9th President of the US) during this conflict.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1817, Josiah and Nancy along with their first six children moved back to Frederick County, Virginia to take care of his late father's estate, of who he was one of three appointed as executor. Josiah posted $40,000 bond along wi
2 CONC th his brother-in-law, John Wright posting the same.
2 CONT
2 CONT Josiah and Nancy built a tavern and inn at Gore, Virginia which was known as the Lockhart Tavern. Josiah became the first post master of Back Creek on February 1 1821. Josiah was also Justice of the Peace in Frederick county, and 
2 CONC you can see many documents prepared by Josiah regarding applications for pensions of Revolutionary War veterans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nancy and Josiah had six more children according to my research, after they moved back to Frederick County, Virginia. Around 1836, Nancy O'dell died. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Josiah's second marriage was to Elizabeth Triplett (born c1803 and was still alive in 1860) daughter of Hesiciah Triplett. The date of marriage has not been documented, but their first child that Josiah acknowledged in his will wa
2 CONC s born 1826. Josiah and Elizabeth had nine children together that were acknowledged in Josiah's will written in 1849. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At some point, Josiah retired from the Lockhart Tavern and removed to Timber Ridge Farm. Josiah's son, Robert Volney Lockhart took over the Lockhart Tavern and Inn as well as the local Lockhart home in Gore when Josiah retired to 
2 CONC the property known as Timber Ridge Farm.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1847, Josiah and Elizabeth completed their two story home at the Timber Ridge Farm. In 1849, Josiah wrote his will, which was probated September 5 1853.
2 CONT
2 CONT Josiah was buried in the family cemetery at Timber Ridge Farm, for lack of a better name, the 'Josiah Lockhart Cemetery.' I believe that this is now lost from time, as is the cemetery where his father Major Robert Lockhart, and mo
2 CONC ther, Margery Denny Wilson Lockhart are buried, which was across from the Lockhart Tavern in Gore. I have not been able to find a current record of either in Frederick County at this time.
