2 CONT GENERAL JOSIAH LOCKHART
2 CONT "Josiah fought in the War of 1812 was Captain of his own company from Ohio. Robert's son Josiah moved to Adams Co, Ohio about 1803 and served in the war of 1812 - entering as a Captain and leaving as a General. Shortly before or a
2 CONC fter the war he became a member of the Ohio legislature. About 1817 he returned to VA (where the family had moved after leaving PA) upon the death of his father. This information was provided to LDS Church Archives by Mrs Shelton 
2 CONC R. Jackson, 6233 So. 340 East, Murray, Utah - Family representative Margarey Nail Jackson. verified by Frederick County Court records Frederick County 1870 Census records and the will of Josiah Lockhart. Per the 1850 census Genera
2 CONC l Lockhart lived with his wife Elizabeth and numerous children, Josiah was 67 at the time and listed as a Farmer, born in Virginia who owned land valued at $16,000. Josiah and his first wife Nancy O'Dell settled on his father's 10
2 CONC 00 acres of Revolutionary War Patent property in Adams County Ohio. In 1810, Josiah received a power of attorney from his father, and in 1813 was deeded 500 acres of his father's land. In the War of 1812 he was first commissioned 
2 CONC a Captain of his own Company, serving from May 4 to July 4, 1812. Governor Return Jonathan Meigs commissioned him Brigadier General, March 1, 1814. A Robert Lockhart is also listed in the company as a Private. In 1817 he and his f
2 CONC amily returned to Frederick County, Virginia. His father Robert Lockhart had died May 13, 1817 so he probably came back to help his mother settle his father's large estate. Josiah and his family with Nancy O'Dell settled in the Fr
2 CONC ederick County where they built and ran a Tavern. On February 1, 1821, Josiah became the first Postmaster of Back Creek. Note: Josiah and Nancy O'Dell Lockhart's children and their families and descendants may be found in Denny Ge
2 CONC nealogy, Third Book. According to the Lockhart Family in America, Josiah died at his Timber Ridge home and is believed buried in the Lockhart plot on the Timber Ridge Farm. The Last Will and Testament of General Josiah Lockhart, M
2 CONC ade 11th of June, 1849: In the name of God, Amen. I, Josiah Lockhart of the county of Frederick, State of Virginia, being of sound mind and disposing memory and calling to mind the uncertainty of life, and wishing dispose of such 
2 CONC worldly estate as I am possessed of, do make this my Last Will and Testament in the manner and form as follows: First, I will that all my just debts and funeral expenses be paid first, and the residue of my Estate, both real and p
2 CONC ersonal, I will to my wife one third part during her life. But if she should die before my youngest child by her arrives at the age of twenty one years, it is my will, in that case, that such part of my Estate as may be in her han
2 CONC ds at the time of her death, remain among such of my children, by her, as may be with her and unmarried till my youngest child by her arrives at the age of twenty one years. And in as much as the one third part of my Estate, after
2 CONC  all my debts are paid will amount to the considerable sum, and will as a matter of course be employed by my wife to the advantage of herself and her children, I will only to my children by her as follows, viz: Isabelle, James, Ju
2 CONC lia, Betsy, Ellen, Henry, and Aljournan, the sum of $l00.00 to each of them; and to Josiah the sum of $200.00: and to Beverly, $l.00. All of such sum I require to be paid as follows, that is to say, those who are of age and the ti
2 CONC me of my death are to be paid within two years of my death. And, those who may not be of age at the time of my death, are to be paid as they arrive of age. Secondly, To my children by my first wife, I will as follows, viz: to Sanu
2 CONC el S. Lockhart, $200.00; to my Son Thomas J. Lockhart, $l00.00; to my daughter, Margaret Lockhart, $500.00; to my daughter Emaline Lovett, $500.00; to my daughter Sally D. Addison, $300.00; to my son Robert V. Lockhart, I will my 
2 CONC servant Dave; to my grandson, Jonah Phillips, I will $500.00. My daughters, Margery D. Gore, Rebecca Campbell and Mary J. McDonald, having each received a servant, namely, Hannah, Kitty and Sally, I will them nothing, except herei
2 CONC nafter mentioned. The above legacies to my children by my first wife, I desire to be paid as soon as expedient regard to the interest of my Estate will allow. Third, It is my will that such part of my Estate as may be remaining af
2 CONC ter paying off such sums as I have herein before directed to be paid, an such other parts as may be remaining after the encumbrances which I have imposed upon it cease to exist, be divided among the following children of my first 
2 CONC wife as follows, viz: My Son Samuel S. Lockhart to have one full share of the same. My son, Thomas J. Lockhart, one full share; my daughter Margaret Lockhart, to have one full share; my daughter Sally D. Addison, to have one full 
2 CONC share; my daughter Emaline Lovett, one full share; my daughter Rebecca Campbell, one full share, my daughter Mary J. McDonald, on full share; my son John O. Lockhart and my daughter Margery D. Gore, to have one full share equally 
2 CONC divided between them. Fourth, It is my will and I so direct that such part or parts of my whole estate may fall to the shares of my son, Samuel S. Lockhart, Salley D. Addison and Margery D. Gore, be invested in land or some safe a
2 CONC nd profitable stocks such as my trustee hereinafter appointed for that purpose shall think best, and that Samuel S. Lockhart, Sally D. Addison, and Margery D. Gore, shall only receive the annual profits of such investments. My obj
2 CONC ect being to make it a permanent fund upon the following conditions; that is to say, if either or both of my said daughters shall survive their husbands, it is my will in such case, that they be the receives of the same with full 
2 CONC power and authority to dispose of the same as they may see proper, and if either or both of my said daughters shall die before their husbands, it is my will in such case that their children as the case may be, have the share or sh
2 CONC ares I have willed to Margery D. Gore and Sally D. Addison. Should my son, Samuel S. Lockhart, die without legal issue, it is my will that his part of my estate be divided among my children by my first wife, in such pro rata share
2 CONC s as I have herein before made to such children to carry into effect this part of my Will. I hereby appoint my son, Robert V. Lockhart, and my two sons-in-law, Robert M. Campbell and David C. Lovett, trustees, anyone of whom may a
2 CONC ct, but I desire that all three act. But if neither of them act, it is my will in that case that the Court appoint three trustees in their place. My son-in-law, Robert M. Campbell is indebted to me in quite a large sum by note, an
2 CONC d if he chooses to do so he may refuse to avail himself of the benefits of my Will as herein before expressed in relation to my daughter Rebecca Campbell his wife. And if he so refuses, it is my will that he be discharged and exon
2 CONC erated from the payment of his indebtedness to my Estate. My intention being that his interest in my Estate shall not be less than his indebtedness to my Estate. Lastly, I do hereby appoint my son-in-law, Robert M. Campbell and my
2 CONC  friends William Wood and James Cather, executors of this my Last Will and Testaments be me made. In witness thereof, I have hereunto set my hand and seal on this the 11th day of June, 1849. (Signed) Josiah Lockhart (Seal) Signed 
2 CONC and published in our presence as for the Last Will and Testament of Josiah Lockhart. (Signed) Isaac Steer Meredith Darlington Robert J. McKee Jacob Nichlin (Will was admitted to probate on the 5th day of September, 1853.} Referenc
2 CONC es: National Archives, Washington D. C. Denny Genealogy, Third Book by Margaret Collins Denny Dixon and Elizabeth Chapman Denny Vann Shenandoah Valley Pioneers and Their Descendants, by Thomas K. Cartmell Roster of Ohio Soldiers i
2 CONC n the War of 1812 Frederick County Court House, Winchester, Virginia Family Records Will Text - The Lockhart Family in America Gregath, Cook & Cook" _________________________________________________________________________
2 CONT Frederick VA Rootsweb Board:
2 CONT "Hi I have a copy of the book A History of Upper Back Creek Valley. The inside cover states that copies can be ordered for $27. from Dr. Wilmer L. Kerns 4715 North 38th Place Arlington, Virginia 22207  Nathaniel B Triplett had sev
2 CONC eral wives Elizabeth Smith his 2nd wife, 1st wife unknown their children John K. born 4/6/1810 died 3/6/1884 Mildred born 1815 died 6/19/1884 (married my 3X great grandfather Ruben Elliott) Elizabeth Carolyn b. 3/29/1803  (married
2 CONC  Josiah Lockhart on 2/15/1848) Mary J. b.?   Nathaniel B and 3rd wife Mary Henry or Hendry? had 6 children Edwin S. b. 7/29/1819 my 3Xgreat grandfather (married Elizabeth Poole on Oct. 28, 1840) Bushrod b. 5/26/1820 Isabelle Amand
2 CONC a b. 1827 James H. b. 1830 Martin Jones b. 1831 Mathias A. b. 1837"
2 CONT REPLY: "The Lockharts that I could find mentioned in the book are Josiah Lockhart who bought 3200 acres of property for the heir of Peter Babb for the sum of $2000. He sold 1115 acres to Nathaniel B Triplett and William Elliott fo
2 CONC r $1 and acre.  Josiah married the the daughter of Nathaniel B and Elizabeth (Smith) Triplett. Her name is Elizabeth C. Triplett (locally called Eliza this may be why there is come confusion between an Elizabeth and an Eliza) and 
2 CONC married on Feb. 15, 1848. Elizabeth was born 3/29/1803 and died 1846. Edwin S Triplett brother of Elizabeth bought in partnership with his half brother John K. Triplett 175 acres from Josiah Lockhart in 1840.  And a Robert Volney 
2 CONC Lockhart born 1809 in Adams County Ohio, son of Josiah Lockhart and his first wife Nancy O'Dell. Josiah Lockhart was a general in the war of 1812. Major Robert Lockhart was shot to death by Union soldiers August 23rd 1863 in his o
2 CONC wn bed. His son Zachary died the next day of a snake bite from a rattle snake. They were buried beside one another.  General Josiah Lockhart was the first postmaster of Back Creek Valley. The post office was established Feb. 1, 18
2 CONC 21.  John O'Dell Lockhart son of Josiah took over as post master on 6/10/1837 until his brother Samuel Socrates Lockhart was appointed May 24, 1845. Robert Volney Lockhart was in charge on 7/21/1845"
2 CONT ANOTHER REPLY: "He may have had a son named Richard. He had four wives that I know of and one of them is nameless. In Richard Tripletts book A History of Upper Back Creek Valley he states that Nathaniel (locally called Old Natty) 
2 CONC had half a dozen wifes and mistresses although this was hear say. Do you have anymore information on wives and children? I know of Elizabeth Smith, Mary Henry (or Hendry) and Mary Steele."   
2 CONT
