2 CONC an, Zodoc Odell, John W. . Parker, Hiram . Page, David Pitty, John . Pucket, Redmond . Robinson, Richard Redmond, William . Reed,, John . Robinson, William Soma, Nehemiah . Segert, Thomas . Stephenson, James Stivers, Samuel H. . S
2 CONC harp, Hugh . Stephenson, William Stanbury, John . Spiers, William . Thomas, William Underwood, Benjamin . Washburn, John . Williams, Benjamin Wright, John . Ellis, Samuel, Drummer
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Shenandoah Valley Pioneers and Their Descendants
2 CONT By Thomas Kemp Cartmell, pg 478
2 CONT The Lockhart Family
2 CONT This was once a large family in Gainsboro District; but the name no longer appears in that part of the county. The old homestead of this family as known by the writer, was where two very old buildings now stand on the West side of
2 CONC  the N. W. Turnpike near Gore; the store of John W. Parish being the next building on the same side of the road. This old group of buildings was the celebrated Lockhart Tavern. Here the writer saw Genl. Josiah Lockhart. In later y
2 CONC ears after the marriage of his children and death of his wife, he turned over the tavern and farm to his son Robert, and removed his home to another farm Southward on Timber Ridge, now the home of Thomas E. Morrison. Two of his so
2 CONC ns John and Thomas went to Missouri; Samuel to Western Virginia. Two daughters married and moved to Ohio. One was Emeline who married Mr. Lovett; the other, Margery, married Mr. Gore, an engineer who assisted in the survey of the 
2 CONC N. W. Turnpike. As already stated, Rebecca A. married R. M. Campbell; and the youngest Margaret B. married Joshua S. Lupton. Mary Jane married Sydnor McDonald.
2 CONT Robert V. Lockhart continued the tavern business until his death. He was a man of remarkable traits; was intelligent, generous, and wielded a large influence in the neighborhood. He was true to principles and friends; but having g
2 CONC eneral intercourse with all classes, he occasionally incurred the displeasure of some evil-minded men who never dared confront him on equal footing. Two such bided their time, and securing the services of a gang of Yankee soldiers
2 CONC  and scouts, hunted him down during the Civil War and shot him to death in his ice house, where they found him secreted. These two men whom he had helped in many ways, were strongly condemned by their neighbors for the dastardly a
2 CONC ct; and after the war sought other places to end their days. Robert V. married Mary dau. of Col. James B. Hall, and was the father of a large family, that are too scattered to attempt further mention. Genl. Lock- hart was a tall, 
2 CONC muscular man, of dignified style and manners. He was at one time General of the Militia Brigade of this District; and was an officer of the War of 1812, as was also his brother Samuel Lockhart, who was commissioned Captain May 22,
2 CONC  1811. They were the sons of Robert, Sr., who had a grant for large tracts.
2 CONT Turner Scrivener, who lives in Back Creek Dist. married a daughter of Bev. Lockhart, Esq.
