2 CONC on. Peter Cantrell, son of a different wife, sued the estate for his portion and named all children fathered by Rev. Cantrell. Reference Find A Grave Memorial 31250529.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is very important that we correct our family trees and any information online so we do not perpetuate misinformation. Please strike all middle initials and names for the first three generations. Richard Cantrill (his spelling) 
2 CONC was not a Jr.: the identity of his parents has never been established. No middle name has been documented. Dorothy was simply Dorothy Jones. Her mother was Jane Evans Jones (Reference Find A Grave Memorial# 109974443.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT . Joseph C. CANTRIL (Richard CANTRIL , Richard CANTRIL , Henry CANTRIL , William , Jr. CANTRILL , William , , , , , ) was born 1695 in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. He died about 1738 in New Castle county, Delaware.
2 CONT
2 CONT From material prepared by Eddy and Glenda Harrel - Reference attributed to "Early Families of the North Carolina Counties of Rockingham and Stokes with Revolutionary Service", compiled and published by members of James Hunter Chap
2 CONC ter, National Society, Daughter of American Revolution of Madison, North Carolina, published 1977:
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph spent his early boyhood in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. Joseph and Catharina lived in New Castle County, Pennsylvania on or near the site of the present city of Wilmington, Delaware, and were attendants at Holy Trinity (Old 
2 CONC Swedes) Church before 1720. Their first three children were baptised at the old historical church, known as Old Swedes, which is one of the oldest and quaintest churches in this country.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph was probably a farmer in these lower counties of Pennsylvania, which later became part of Delaware. A published work of the wills in the county of New Castle fails to reveal a will of Joseph or Catharina. It is possible tha
2 CONC t Joseph accompanied some of the sons on the move south to North Carolina. In 1752 and 1753, son John was taxed for two white polls in Orange County, North Carolina. One may have been his father. A complete list of the children of
2 CONC  John and Catharina has never been found. The nine children listed were compiled from family records and the court and county records of Orange County North Carolina. The sixth child (a female, name unknown) was born about 1722.
2 CONT
2 CONT Christina Parish was mostly situated on both sides of Christina Creek, partly on both sides of Brandywine Creek in New Castle County and in the Hundreds of New Castle, Christina and Brandywine. It stretched two Swedish miles in le
2 CONC ngth, and one in breadth. The most remote families of the parish were not more than six and a half English miles distance from the church. The city of Wilmington is build on the Swedish "church land" and the charter for the town w
2 CONC as granted on 1735. At the time Joseph Cantril went there, it was known as Christina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Many of the earliest records of this locality have been lost, or destroyed, and at best are very incomplete. There is evidence, however, from the scattered records in New Castle County today, that descendents of Richard Cantril li
2 CONC ved there from before 1720 until 1797, though the majority moved south during this period.
2 CONT
2 CONT A published work of the wills and probates of New Castle County during the period of Joseph's residence there fails to reveal his will or probate. As he was a man in his 50s when his sons moved south to Carolina, it can be assumed
2 CONC  that he made the move with his sons. In the tax list submitted to the Orange County Court, North Carolina for the year 1754, his son John's household listed two taxable white males. As John's sons were under 16 years of age, it c
2 CONC an be assumed that one of these was his father, Joseph.
2 CONT
2 CONT *******Virginia Young*********** JOSEPH CANTRELL WAS BORN ABOUT 1695 IN PHILADELPHIA, PA. HE MARRIED CATHERINE HEATH ABOUT 1718. A COMPLETE LIST OF THE CHILDREN OF JOSEPH AND CATHERINE HAS NEVER BEEN LOCATED. ONE DAUGHTER MAY HAVE
2 CONC  BEEN SARAH, WHO WAS THE WIFE OF LAWRENCE BANKSTON. FOR OVER 60 YEARS THE CANTRELL AND BANKSTON FAMILIES MIGRATED AND SETTLED TOGETHER. LAWRENCE BANKSTON STATED IN A COURT CASE IN 1807, THAT HE HAD KNOWN ISAAC CANTRELL FOR OVER 50
2 CONC  YEARS. THE FOLLOWING LIST IS COMPILED FROM THE BAPTISMAL LIST, THE LIST PROVIDED BY THE FAMILY GENEALOGIST IN 1907 AND FROM THE COUNTY RECORDS IN ORANGE COUNTY, NC.:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. HANNAH CANTRELL-BORN 20 MARCH 1720 IN PA. AND BAPTIZED 25, APRIL 1720 2. DAUGHTER BORN ABT. 1722. 3. JOHN CANTRELL 4. JOSEPH CANTRELL 5. ZEBULON CANTRELL 6. ISAAC CANTRELL 7. JAMES CANTRELL 8. BENJAMIN CANTRELL-BORN ABT. 1733 I
2 CONC N ORANGE CO. 9. STEPHEN CANTRELL-BORN ABT. 1735 IN ORANGE CO. VA.
2 CONT ***********Judia Kemper Terry****************** Joseph Cantrill was born about 1695 in Philadelphia, Montgomery Co., Pennsylvania. He died in New Castle, Pennsylvania. He was buried in possibly Orange County, North Carolina. "Jose
2 CONC ph Cantrill was born about 1695 in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, and spent his early boyhood in that city. Married Catharina _______. They were living in New Castle County, Pennsylvania, on or near the site of the present city of Wi
2 CONC lmington, Delaware, and were attendants at Holy Trinity (Old Swedes) Church before 1720. It is probable that Catharina was a daughter of one of the Old Swedes, first settlers of this section, but we could not verify this. Their fi
2 CONC rst three children were baptized at the old historic church still used and known as Old Swedes, which is one of the oldest and quaintest churches in this country.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Christina parish was mostly situated on both sides of Christina Creek, partly on both sides of Brandywine Creek in New Castle County, and in the Hundreds of New Castle, Christina and Brandywine. It stretched two Swedish miles dis
2 CONC tant from the church. The city of Willmington is built on the Swedish "church land" and the charter for the town was granted in 1735. At the time Joseph Cantrill went there it was known as Christina.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Many of the earliest records of this locality have been lost, or destroyed, and at best are very incomplete. There is evidence, however, from the scattered records in New Castle County today, that descendants of Richard Cantrill 
2 CONC lived there from before 1720 until 1797, though the majority moved south during this period. Children: Hannah, born March 20, 1720; baptised April 25, 1720, at Old Swedes; John; Joseph; Zebulon; Isaac; a daughter (name unknown." (
2 CONC THE CANTRILL - CANTRELL GENEALOGY, 1908, by Susan Cantrill Christie, page 7.)
2 CONT
2 CONT "Joseph Cantrill, son of Richard, was born about 1695 in Philadelphia, PA., where he probably spent his boyhood years and moved south to the lower counties of PA. after reaching manhood. He married Catherine Heath about 1718. She 
2 CONC was a daughter of John and Hannah Haines Heath. John Heath was a grandson of Thomas Heath who came to America from England in 1635 on the ship, SAFETY, landing in VA. and who later moved north to the lower counties, which later be
2 CONC came part of Delaware. Joseph was probably a farmer in New Castle county. We know that they were attending Holy Trinity (Old Swedes) Church in Wilmington as three of their children were baptized at this oldest and quaintest church
2 CONC  in the nation. Christina Parish was mostly situated on both sides of Christina Creek and partly on both sides of Brandywine Creek in New Castle county. The most remote families were not more than six and one half miles from the c
2 CONC hurch. Wilmington was first known as Christina and was built on Swedish "Churchland", and the charter was granted in 1735. Most of the records of the area have been lost, but we do know that descendants of Richard were living in t
2 CONC he area from 1720-1787, though the majority had moved south to Virginia and the Carolinas. A published work of the wills and probates in New Castle county, does not contain any record of Joseph or Catherine. It can be assumed that
2 CONC  they had made the trip south. In the tax list submitted to the Orange County Court for the year of 1754, son, John was listed as having two taxable white males. As John's sons were under 10 years of age, the possibility exists th
2 CONC at this other was for his father. A complete list of the children has never been found, but the following was compiled from the family genealogist and from county court records of Orange County, North Carolina." (Carolyn Sue Mitch
2 CONC ell Bouska, 15001 Quail Drive, Balch Springs, Texas 75180-2447; tele: 214-557-5532, 1993 - 1995, as per "The Cantrill-Cantrell Genealogy, A record of the descendants of Richard Cantrill, who was a resident of Philadelphia prior to
2 CONC  1689, and of earlier Cantrills in England and America," by Susan Cantrill Christie, later revised and published again by J. R. and Jackie Cantrell, further revised and published in 1973 by Joseph Caten.)
2 CONT
2 CONT "Joseph Cantrell was born abaout 1695 in Phildelphia, PA.. He spent his early childhood in that city. His parents may have moved to the lower counties of PA. soon after 1700. He married Catherine Heath ca 1718. According to one ge
2 CONC nealogist, she was a daughter of John and Hannah Heath. So far, research has not been located to substantiate. We do know that her christian name was Catherine from the baptismal records at the Holy Trinity (Old Swedes) church at 
2 CONC Wilmnington, Delaware. One researcher has stated that she was a great granddaughter of Thomas Heath who came to America in 1635 on the ship "SAFETY" landing in VA. and later moving north and finally settling near New Castle, PA. (
2 CONC later Delaware). From the Holy Trinity records of 1697 to 1773, which were translated from the Swedish to English in 1890, we find that Joseph and Catherine had three children baptized there in the 1720's. They were Hannah, John a
2 CONC nd Joseph. Christina Parish was mostly situated on both sides of Christina Creek, partly on both sides of Brandywine Creek in New Castle County, Pa. and in the Hundreds of New Castle, Christina and Brandywine. It stretched two Swe
2 CONC dish miles in length and one in breadth. The most remote families of the parish were not more than six and one half English miles distant from the church. The city of Wilmington is built on the Swedish "churchland" and the charter
2 CONC  for the town was granted in 1735. At the time, Joseph lived there it was known as Christina. Most of the earliest records of this locality have been lost or destroyed and at the best are very incomplete. There is evidence, howeve
2 CONC r, from some of the scattered records in New Castle Co., that possible descendants of Richard Cantrill lived in the area from 1720 to 1787, although the majority had moved south during this period. There is a published work of the
2 CONC  wills and probates of New Castle Co., during the period of Joseph's residence, but it fails to reveal evidence of Richard or Dorothy's death. From the records of the western part of VA. we know that in 1738, Joseph and his brothe
2 CONC r, Zebulon were in Orange County, VA.. A Rev. William William, an early Presbyterian minister filed suit for libel against dozens of the settlers in the Valley. The list of defendents encluded Zebulon Cantrel and Joseph Cantrel. W
2 CONC e know that son, John was in Orange County, NC. in 1754, when the tax list for that year was submitted by the sheriff, and that the list had two taxable white males. John's oldest son was too young to have been taxed so the other 
2 CONC one may have been for Joseph. A complete list of the children of Joseph and Catherine has never been located. One daughter may have been Sarah, who was the wife of Lawrence Bankston. For over 60 years the Cantrell and Bankston fam
2 CONC ilies migrated and settled together. Lawrence Bankston stated in a court case in 1807 that he had known Isaac Cantrell for over 50 years. The following list is compiled from the baptismal list, the list provided by the family gene
2 CONC alogist in 1907 and from the county records in Orange County, North Carolina." (Carolyn Sue Mitchell Bouska, 15001 Quail Drive, Balch Springs, Texas 75180-2447; tele: 214-557-5532, 1993 - 1995, as per Warren G. Cantrell, 1913 Will
2 CONC owbend, Killeen, Texas 76543.) He was married to Catherina Heath about 1718 in Holy Trinity Ch., New Castle, Dl.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph married Catherine HEATH, daughter of John HEATH and Hannah HANES, on 1718 in New Castle county, Delaware. Catherine was born about 1697 in Haverhill, Essex county, Massachusetts. She died BET. 1734 - 1792 in New Castle coun
2 CONC ty, Delaware.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is probable that Catherine was the daughter of one of the Old Swedes, first settlers of the section. Her first three children were baptised in the Old Swedes Chruch. At this time the old location was called Christiana. {Warren 
2 CONC G. Cantrell STRONGLY DISAGREES that Catherine is a daughter of John & Hannah. There were five Heath families in the Philadelphia area. Why would he marry a girl from MA which was so far away....9/2/1995/DAH}
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph and Catherine had the following children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT   16 F i Hannah CANTRIL was born Mar 20 1719/1720 in Wilmington, New Castle county, Pennsylvania. She died in New Castle county, Delaware.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hannah Cantrell died as an infant. Baptised 4-25-1720 Hannah Cantrell was born on 20 Mar 1720 in Pennsylvania. She was christened on 25 Apr 1720 in Holy Trinity, New Castle Co., Pennsylvania, (now Delaware). !BIRTH: Warren Cantrel
2 CONC l; Genealogical History of the Cantrell Clan; sent to Travis Morris, Cleburne, TX; 1660-1793; Copy in poss of Reta Evans, Ft. Worth, TX. Hannah was baptised 25 Apr 1720 in Old Swedes Church 
2 CONT   17 F ii Unknown Daughter Of Joseph CANTRIL was born 1722 in Wilmington, New Castle county, Pennsylvania. She died in New Castle county, Delaware.
2 CONT
2 CONT This child died as an infant.  + 18 M iii John , Sr. CANTRIL  + 19 M iv Joseph CANTRELL 
2 CONT   20 M v Zebulon CANTRIL was born about 1728 in Wilmington, New Castle county, Pennsylvania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Zebulon Cantrell was born about 1728 in New Castle County, Pennsylvania (now Delaware). "Zebulon Cantrell married Mary Montgomery. He died when his children were young and his widow married (2) David Henly, of Virginia, a Baptist 
2 CONC minister." (THE CANTRILL - CANTRELL GENEALOGY, 1908, by Susan Cantrill Christie, page 124.) 
2 CONT         Zebulon married Mary MONTGOMERY.  + 21 M vi Isaac CANTRIL 
2 CONT   22 F vii Alice CANTRIL was born 1 1730. 
2 CONT   23 M viii James CANTRIL was born about 1719 in Wilmington, New Castle county, Pennsylvania. 
2 CONT   24 M ix Benjamin CANTRIL was born 1733 in Wilmington, New Castle county, Pennsylvania. He died before 1823.
2 CONT
2 CONT *****From Virginia Young******** BENJAMIN IS FOUND LISTED IN THE COURT RECORDS OF ORANGE COUNTY, NC. THE COURT OF SEPTEMBER 1759, BENJAMIN CANTRIL PROVED AN ACCOUNT OF ONE POUND, FIFTEEN SHILLINGS AGAINST THE ESTATE OF ANDREW CULB
2 CONC ERTSON, DECEASED FOR FUNERAL CHARGES AND ATTENDANCE UPON THE SAME.
2 CONT ******Judia kemper Terry********* Benjamin Cantrell was born about 1733 in Orange County, Virginia. "Benjamin was born ca 1733 in Orange Co., VA.. We find him listed in the Court Records of Orange County, NC.. The court of Septemb
2 CONC er 1759, Benjamin Cantril proved an account of one pound, fifteen shillings against the estate of Andrew Culbertson, deceased, for funeral charges and attendence upon the same. No later record of Benjamin." (Carolyn Sue Mitchell B
2 CONC ouska, 15001 Quail Drive, Balch Springs, Texas 75180-2447; tele: 214-557-5532, 1993 - 1995, as per Warren G. Cantrell, 1913 Willowbend, Killeen, Texas 76543.)
2 CONT
2 CONT BIRTH: Warren Cantrell; Genealogical History of the Cantrell Clan; sent to Travis Morris, Cleburne, TX; 1660-1793; Copy in poss of Reta Evans, Ft. Worth, TX. NOTE: Benjamin was listed in the court records of Orange Co., NC, Sept. 
2 CONC 1759 where he proved an account of one pound, fifteen shillings against the estate of Andrew Culbertson, deceased for funeral charges and attendence upon the same. 
2 CONT   25 M x Stephen CANTRIL was born 1735 in Wilmington, New Castle county, Pennsylvania. He died 1783 in Wilkes county, Georgia.
2 CONT
2 CONT ****From Virginia Young********* STEPHEN IS FOUND LISTED IN THE COURT OF ORANGE COUNTY, NC IN MAY 1764, WHEN THOMAS HART SUED HIM ON A DEBT. IN THE RECORDS OF WILKES COUNTY, GA. 1777-1787, STEPHEN CANTRELL DIED INTESTATE AND GEORG
2 CONC E LUMPKIN, A CARPENTER, CREDITOR OF SAID DECEASED APPOINTED ADMINISTRATOR 25 OCTOBER 1783.  http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~pattiejo/cantrell/pafg11.htm#18017
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Name: Joseph Cantrell
2 CONT
2 CONT Sex: M
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: 1695 in Philadelphia, PA
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: ABT 1753 in Orange County, NC
2 CONT
2 CONT Note:
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph spent his early boyhood in Philadelphia, PA. After he married Catharina Heath, they lived in New Castle Co., DE, on or near the site of the present city of Wilmington, Delaware, and were attendants at Holy Trinity (Old Swed
2 CONC es) Church before 1720. It is probable that Catharina was a daughter of one of the Old Swedes, first settlers of this section, but this could not be verified. Their first three children were baptized at the old historic church sti
2 CONC ll used and known as Old Swedes, which is one of the oldest and quaintest churches in this country.
2 CONT
2 CONT The city of Wilmington is built on the Swedish 'church land' and the charter for the town was granted in 1735. At the time Joseph Cantrill went there it was known as Christina. There is evidence from scattered records in New Castl
2 CONC e County, that descendants of Richard Cantrill lived there from before 1720 until 1797, though the majority moved south during this period. (from The Cantrill-Cantrell Genealogy)
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Richard Cantrill 2 b: 13 MAY 1660 in Derbyshire County, England 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Dorothy Jane Jones b: 1672 in Wales
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 1 Catharine b: 1697 in PA
2 CONT
2 CONT Married: 1718 in New Castle County, PA now DE
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT  Hannah Cantrell b: 20 MAR 1719/20 in New Castle County, PA
2 CONT
2 CONT  Mary Cantrell b: 1722 in New Castle, PA
2 CONT
2 CONT  John Cantrell b: 6 OCT 1724 in New Castle, PA now Delaware
2 CONT
2 CONT  Joseph Cantrell b: JUN 1726 in New Castle, PA
2 CONT
2 CONT  Zebulon Cantrell b: 1728 in New Castle County, PA
2 CONT
2 CONT  Alice Cantrell b: ABT 1730 in New Castle, PA
2 CONT
2 CONT  James Cantrell b: 1731 in New Castle County, PA
2 CONT
2 CONT  Benjamin Cantrell b: 1733 in Orange County, VA
2 CONT
2 CONT  Stephen Cantrell b: 1735 in Orange County, VA
2 CONT
2 CONT  Isaac Cantrell b: 1738 in Orange County, VA
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Joseph Cantrell was born 1696 in Philadelphia; He married Catharina Heath about 1718 at Holy Trinity Church in New Castle Co., PA. Joseph and Catharina lived in New Castle County in or near the site of the present city of Wilmingt
2 CONC on, Delaware, and were attendants at Holy Trinity (Old Swedes) Church. Their first three children were baptized at this Church, which is one of the oldest Churches in this country. The majority of Richard Cantrills descendants mov
2 CONC ed south. It is thought that Joseph might have joined some of his sons and moved to North Carolina in either 1752 and 1753.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Joseph Cantrell was born about 1695 in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, and after reaching manhood moved to the “lower counties of Pennsylvania” that later became the state of Delaware. He married Catharina (surname unknown) about 1718
2 CONC , and they were living in New Castle County, Pennsylvania, near the present city of Wilmington, Delaware, and before 1720, were attendants at Holy Trinity (Old Swedes) Church. Catharina was probably the daughter of one of the orig
2 CONC inal Old Swedes families who first settled on the Swedish “church land.” From the Holy Trinity records of 1697 to 1773, which were translated from Swedish to English in 1890, we find that Joseph and Catharina had three children ba
2 CONC ptized there in the 1720s. They were Hannah, John and Joseph.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time Joseph Cantrell settled there, the town was known as Christina, which was mostly situated on both sides of Christina Creek, partly on both sides of Brandywine Creek in New Castle County, and in the Hundreds of New Cast
2 CONC le, Christina and Brandywine. The most remote families of the parish were not more than six and a half miles from the church. The city of Wilmington, Delaware was built on Swedish “church land.” The charter for the town was grante
2 CONC d in 1735.
2 CONT
2 CONT There aren’t any further records of Joseph or Catharina in the remaining records of Delaware, but from the records of Jefferson County, West Virginia, we know that in 1738, Joseph and his brother, Zebulon, were involved in a lawsu
2 CONC it in Orange County, Virginia. The libel suit brought against 54 defendants for “signing a scandalous paper reflecting on the Complaintiff” by Rev. William Williams (Presbyterian) occurred on July 27, 1738. It was filed at the Ora
2 CONC nge County, Virginia courthouse.  Both Joseph and Zebulon were listed as "other defendants." The events that were described in the suit probably happened somewhere between Winchester, Virginia and what is now Martinsburg, West Vir
2 CONC ginia, which is where the Rev. Williams lived.
2 CONT
2 CONT While living in New Castle County, Joseph's son, Isaac Cantrell, became associated with the Welsh Tract Baptist Church located at the foot of Iron Hill, in Pencader Hundred. It is the oldest Primitive Baptist church in America, an
2 CONC d Isaac was probably licensed to preach by this group. As the Church was composed of Welsh People, the preaching for about one hundred years was in the Welsh language.  Isaac’s mother, Catharina, was more than likely of Swedish pa
2 CONC rentage, but we know for certain that his grandmother, Dorothy Jones, was born in Wales. The Welsh language is not just a dialect of English; it is a language with an older pedigree, and a distinct one. Isaac Cantrell would have t
2 CONC o have been fluent in the Welsh language to have been a member of this church in the 1700s.
2 CONT
2 CONT A record of Isaac’s brother Zebulon being a witness to the will of Thomas Edmond on July 21, 1758, is among the Welsh Tract Baptist Church records, and lends further evidence that the Cantrells were early members of this church.
2 CONT
2 CONT About 1747,  many of the Cantrells as well as allied families joined a large caravan in the movement southward. They moved along the Great Wagon Road down through the Great Shenandoah Valley of Virginia. Within view to the west of
2 CONC  the Valley rose the Alleghenies and to the east were the Blue Ridge Mountains. From Roanoke, the Wagon Road went through the Staunton Gap and on south to the Piedmont Plateau of the Carolinas. They settled in the "Land of Eden" G
2 CONC ranville County, North Carolina, which became Orange County in September 1752. Later, in 1785, this part of Orange County became Rockingham County. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the tax list submitted to the Orange County Court for the year of 1754, John Cantrell was listed as having two taxable white males. It is possible that one of the males may have been his father Joseph. Isaac received a land gra
2 CONC nt for 202 acres of land in Orange County on November 13, 1756. Isaac sold this land to his brother John on 13 Mar 1759.
2 CONT
2 CONT I have created this memorial in honor and memory of Joseph and his descendants at Old Swedes Church where he was a member and several of his children were baptized. He was my husband's 5th great grandfather.
2 CONT
2 CONT CHILDREN:
2 CONT *James Cantrell (b. 1719 New Castle Co., DE; d. TN. James Cantrell settled in Southern Tennessee and owned the farm where the Battle of Shiloh was fought during the Civil War.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Hannah Cantrell (b. 20 Mar 1720 Christina, New Castle, DE)
2 CONT
2 CONT * John Cantrell (b. 6 Oct 1724 New Castle, DE; d. 11 Feb 1803 Spartanburg Co., SC) m. abt 1743 in Newcastle, DE, Rachel Brittain (b. 1725 New Castle Co., DE; d. abt 1769 Rockingham Co., NC) They had 17 children. John married secon
2 CONC dly, Jane ___, and they had 6 children.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Joseph Cantrell (b. Jun 1726 Wilmington, New Castle Co., DE; d. Jan 1804 Caswell Co., NC) m. Jemima Mitchell.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Zebulon Cantrell (b. abt 1728 New Castle Co., DE; d. 1765 Orange Co., NC) m. Mary Montgomery.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Isaac Cantrell (b. abt. 1729 New Castle Co., DE; d. 23 Aug 1805 Spartanburg Co., SC) m. Talitha Cloud about 1750 in Rockingham Co., NC, Elizabeth ___ about 1769 in Rockingham Co., NC, and  Mary Linder about 1773 in Rockingham Co.
2 CONC , NC.
