1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From The Seattle Star of Wednesday, May 14, 1919
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Hold funeral for C. L. Denny: Son of pioneer was born in log cabin'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services for Charles L. Denny, son of Mr. and Mrs. Arthur A. Denny, pioneer family, who died of heart failure Tuesday at his home, 1632 - 40th Ave. N., were scheduled to be held at the family residence at 2 p.m. Wednesday,
2 CONC  interment following at Lakeview Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT C. L. Denny, who is survived by a widow, two sons, a sister, and two brothers, was born in Seattle in 1861 in a log cabin on the site of the present Hotel Stevens. He attended the territorial university, of which his father laid t
2 CONC he cornerstone, and later entered the Dexter Horton bank. He married Viretta Chambers of Olympia, daughter of another pioneer, in 1887. He was associated with civic movements and was a member of a number of improvement organizatio
2 CONC ns.
2 CONT
2 CONT --
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Seattle Star of Thursday, May 15, 1919:
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services for Charles Latimer Denny were held at the family residence, 1622 - 40th Ave. N., at 2 p.m., Wednesday. A large number of Master Masons and lifelong friends attended the funeral. Interment was made at Lakeview Cem
2 CONC etery.
