1 OCCU Seattle's Boren Avenue is named in his honor; 1st King County Sheriff; Early founder of Seattle
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carson_Boren
2 CONT
2 CONT Carson Dobbins Boren (December 12, 1824, Nashville, Tennessee-August 19, 1912, Woodinville, Washington), was an early founder of Seattle, Washington (see Denny Party http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denny_Party). His sister Mary Ann w
2 CONC as married to Arthur Denny, and his sister Louisa to David Denny. Boren was the first King County Sheriff. Seattle's Boren Avenue is named in his honor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Carson Boren married Mary Ann Kays (November 6, 1830-June 21, 1905) on February 18, 1849 at Abingdon, Knox County, Illinois. Their first daughter Sarah E. was born December 17, 1849 but died 17 days later and is buried in the Cher
2 CONC ry Grove Cemetery in Knox County. A second daughter, Gertrude Livonia, (December 12, 1850-June 3, 1912) was carried as an infant on the cross-country trek in 1851 with the Denny Party.
2 CONT
2 CONT After erecting the first home in Seattle, the couple participated in the early life of Seattle and had two more children, William Richard (October 4, 1854-January 19, 1899) and Mary Louise (May 3, 1857-January 1, 1926). However, t
2 CONC he couple argued over Carson's constant hunting trips and giveaways, and by 1860 they separated. They were divorced by territorial legislative act December 17, 1861. Their children were raised by the Dennys. Mary Ann went south to
2 CONC  The Dalles, Oregon, and established a dressmaking business. There she had another daughter, Lydia Dell Blakeney (January 18, 1869-October 6, 1921) with John William Blakeney, a divorced man; they married in November 1872, but sep
2 CONC arated about 1880 and divorced in 1884. Mary Ann and Lydia moved back to Seattle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Ann Boren apparently kept in touch with her children by Carson Boren and lived with her youngest daughter and son-in-law in what is now the International District of Seattle. When she died in 1905, she was buried in an unmark
2 CONC ed grave in Lake View Cemetery, down the hill from where Carson Boren and their unmarried daughter (called Livonia as an adult) are buried in style with the Denny Clan.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Carson Dobbins Boren (December 12, 1824 – August 19, 1912) was an early founder of Seattle, Washington (see Denny Party). His sister Mary Ann was married to Arthur Denny, and his sister Louisa to David Denny. Boren was the first K
2 CONC ing County Sheriff.[1] Seattle's Boren Avenue is named in his honor.[2] Although he was an important figure in the early years of Seattle, historian Junius Rochester writes that "The pioneering contributions of Carson Dobbins Bore
2 CONC n to the founding of Alki (in future West Seattle) and Seattle began and ended within a short period of six years."[1]
