1 NOTE {geni:about_me} See also William Ashley Anderson, who wrote of their courtship in his journal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Evelyn writes:  Grandpa writes of the marriage of Elizabeth Hather Anderson and he being married at Johns Creek, IA, near Farley on 9/5/1876, ceremony by Reverend Agnew, Presbyterian minister.
2 CONT
2 CONT "George T. Hather and his wife Sarah Jane carter were also born in England and came to the US in Mid 1800's."
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Curtis
2 CONT
2 CONT b. 5/1/1808 Gloucester, Engl
2 CONT
2 CONT came to US 1836
2 CONT
2 CONT m. Joseph Carter
2 CONT
2 CONT    Sarah Jane Carter
2 CONT
2 CONT m. George Hather
2 CONT
2 CONT d. 11/18/1918
2 CONT
2 CONT   Elizabeth Ann Hather
2 CONT
2 CONT b. 9/21/1856 1st of 10  children
2 CONT
2 CONT m. William Ashley Anderson
2 CONT
2 CONT  9/5/1876
2 CONT
2 CONT d. 4/7/1888
2 CONT
2 CONT ==========================
2 CONT
2 CONT In the following document:
2 CONT
2 CONT me=Evelyn Abernethy Howard
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandpa=William A. Anderson 4/14/1853-7/6/1944
2 CONT
2 CONT his wife=Elizabeth Hather Anderson 9/21/56-4/7/1888
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandma Anderson=Sarah Snodgrass Anderson 11/26/1852-1/9/1937
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother=Mabel Anderson Abernethy 12/2/1880-12/23/1968
2 CONT
2 CONT Uncle Roy = Roy W. Anderson 10/24/1883-1/17/1932
2 CONT
2 CONT This is what I remember hearing from Mother regarding the Andersons California stay:  Wm. A. and Elizabeth Anne were married 9/5/1876 and lived in farms rented in Iowa until 1879 when they went to Valley County, Nebraska and homst
2 CONC eaded.  Both Mabel and Roy were born there.  After five years the land was "proved up" and Grandpa sold it and moved to Ord.  Apparently at that time he built the house he lived in for many years.  Since there was not much of a wa
2 CONC y to earn a living in Ord in 1887, they decided to move to California.  My mother had said that one of Grandpa's brothers was in San Francisco and they headed there.  In going over the list of Grandpa's brothers and sisters I can 
2 CONC not find any that were alive in that year except his brother Frank.  And if this were the brother, he must have moved back to the midwest, he was married in 1888.  I remember seeing him in Ord when I was a small child.  The trip t
2 CONC o California was made in late 1887 on an "emigrant train" where each family had a compartment in which they ate, cooked, and slept.  I have a photo of the family on the stps of a house on what mother said was 12th Street in San Fr
2 CONC ancisco.  Grandpa bought a share in a cooperative which was to farm and harvest trees in Tulare County.  It was called the Kaweah Colony.  He had gone down there and Mother and Uncle Roy and thir mother were following on the train
2 CONC  when Elizabeth Anne died suddenly on 4/7/1888.  She is buried in a cemetery in Kingsburg (deed dated 4/30/1888).  Grandpa purchased three burial sites, a fact which I have just found out when I had an opportunity to visit the cem
2 CONC etery.  However, Mother said that he returned to SF with the children and shortly thereafter they went back to Nebraska.
2 CONT
2 CONT I have several letters written by Grandpa to my Mother.  Apparently he boarded my Mother with a Mr. McMurphy in Ord, and took Roy and went to Iowa to find a way to make a living.  I remember that Mother said that he had taught sch
2 CONC ool and travelled around the country selling lightning rods for buildings.  In the letters he seems very sad to have to be separated from Mother.  In one letter he tells Mother to have "Grandma" go to the attic and get some of his
2 CONC  winter underwear to "make over" for Mother.  This "Grandma" was Grandma Hather, who was Elizabeth's mother and lived in the house next to Grandpa Anderson's.  Why Grandpa boarded Mother with another family and not with her own gr
2 CONC andmother, I don't know.  That question, like so many others, are things I would like to ask my Mother now.  But then all these letters and papers were only read by us after her death.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is also a small discrepancy - Mother hadthis doll named "Lillian".  She had a wooden head, cloth body, leather hands and shoes and was dressed in the style of the 1880's.  She was named Lillian because she was given to Mothe
2 CONC r by her Aunt Lillian, when they were to start their journey to California, sometime prior to her birthday 12/2.  But mother always said that she received Lillian on her 8th birthday but she must have been mistaken about which bir
2 CONC thday because Elizabeth died in April 1888 and Mothe was not 8 until 12/2/1999.  Lillian was given to Wilma when she was 8, to me when I was 8, and to my duaghter, Julie when she was 8 and is one of her treasured possessions.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 7/25/1893 in Iowa Grandpa married Sarah Snodgrass and she was our "Grandma Anderson".  On their marriage they went directky to Ord and made their home, and that is where Mother and uncle Roy were raised.  Grandpa and Grandma li
2 CONC ved in that same house, with some additions, until their deaths.
2 CONT
2 CONT I can't remember if Grandpa ever had a "job" but he had a photography studio and I guress he also taught school after his marriage to Sarah. He owned 8 lots where their house was located and did a lot of gardening, selling some of
2 CONC  the produce.  They spent very little and somehow managed an existence to their liking.  He also owned some acreage near the "river" and gave one lot to the city.  It was an island formed by two channels of the river.  It was name
2 CONC d Anderson Island.
2 CONT
2 CONT I really shed a few tears at the grave of Mother's Mother, here she was dead at  31, lived on farms, homesteaded in a covered wagon, lost three babies before Mother lived.  Left a home in Ord to go to California and die, not to se
2 CONC e her children grow up. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Addendum: Have just been looking over the old photos and find that one of Grandpa's brothers (half brother) was Eldrige G. born 8/11/1843 and no other information.  I had thought I remembered Mother saying it was her uncle Ed in S
2 CONC F.
2 CONT
2 CONT ================
2 CONT
2 CONT Obituary in Traver County 4/7/1888
2 CONT
2 CONT <unreadable> on the 7th Inst.  Mrs. W.A. Anderson, formerly of Ord.  The immediate cause of her death was paralysis of the brain.  She had been afflicted for some months with rheumatism which finally settled in her right arm rende
2 CONC ring it entirely useless.  With the hope of getting relief, she accompanied her husband to California last December.  She was improving in health until April 1, at which time she removed from San Francisco to Traver to meet her hu
2 CONC sband who had purchased some land some seventy-five miles below there and had gone down there a few days before.  Soon after arriving at Traver, paralysis of the brain set in and before Mr. Anderson could get to her she died.  She
2 CONC  leaves behind <unreadable>
