1 OCCU Teacher, Bookkeeper
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Schoolteacher in Joint District #61(Garfield/Valley counties, NE	
2 CONT
2 CONT She and her husband met when she was a boarder at his parents' house.	
2 CONT
2 CONT Part of a long line of Abernethy/Anderson teachers.	
2 CONT
2 CONT Married 14-Oct-1905 at Grand Island, Nebraska.	
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------Mabel writes of her family history<nowiki>--------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT The earliest record of the Anderson family dates back to the year 1745 in the reign of George the Second of England.  At that time the Anderson family espoused the cause of the pretender in a Scottish rebellion and as the Stuart f
2 CONC amily were driven from Scotland.  All those who were with the Stuarts had to leave or be executed.  And so David Anderson and his family took passage for Virginia in America.  On the voyage over, Samuel Anderson was born in 1745 a
2 CONC nd was the head of the present Anderson family [Evelyn writes: the above birth is disputed by later facts]
2 CONT
2 CONT The family settled in Augusta County, VA and there at the time of the French and Indian war were living in VA.  When the war of the revolution began, Samuel Anderson joined the army and under Washington and fought until Independen
2 CONC ce was secured [Evelyn writes: Archives in Washington DC can give us no such record of his service.]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------Her obituary, typed from the newspaper clipping:<nowiki>--------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT Former Ordite Mabel Abernethy Dies Suddenly
2 CONT
2 CONT A long-time resident of the Ord community, MRs. Mabel Abernethy died Dec 23 at her home in Oakland, Calif.
2 CONT
2 CONT Final services for the 83-year-old woman were conducted Saturday at 2 pm in the Hastings Pearson Chapel, with Rev. C. R. Campbell officiating.  Mrs. Shirley Karre, organist, accompanied Mrs. Douglas Pearson who sang "How Great Tho
2 CONC u Art" and "The Lord's Prayer."
2 CONT
2 CONT Pallbearers were Ed Hackel, Arvin Dye, Clarence Larsen, Wlater Noll, Glenn Watts, and Vere Carson, with interment in the Ord City Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT The daughter of William and Elizabeth (Hather) Anderson, Mrs. Abernethy was born Dec 2, 1880, on a homestead northeast of Ord.  She will be remembered by many as their teacher, having taught in rural schools of Valley and Garfield
2 CONC  counties.  She was married to Jason Abernethy Oct. 4, 1905, and they made their home in the Joint neightborhood for over 40 years - until moving to Ord in 1945.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Abernethy also worked as a bookkeeper in the Ord Creamery for a number of years.  She was a life-long member of the Ord Methodist Church and particpated actively in the Sunday school.  She was also a life member of the Women'
2 CONC s Society of Christian Service.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1961 Mrs. Abernethy moved to California to make her home with a daughter, Mrs. George Howard.  After recovering from a broken hip in 1967, she resided in a church-sponsored home near her two daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was preceded in death by her husband, parents, step-mother (Sarah Anderson) a brother (Roy Anderson) and a great-grandson.  Surviving are three children, Raymond Abernethy  of Oklahoma City, Okla., and Mrs. Paul Faira and Mrs.
2 CONC  George Howard of Oakland, Calif.  She also leaves nine grandchildren and 11 great-grandchildren.
