1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  John William Haggard: John Haggard and his brother William moved from Virginia to Davidson Co., NC. (now Nashboro, Tennessee) in 1787 where John was a private in the militia. He had jury duty in 1787, 1788 and 178
2 CONC 9.  JOHN HAGGARD, son of Gray Haggard, was born in Virginia about1750 and died in an Indian raid on his home in Davidson Co., Tenn. 29 Dec. 1792. He was married in 1773 to Jane (Mayfield) Gibson who was killed by Indians during th
2 CONC e summer of 1792. According to THE ANNALS OF TENNESSEE, in July 1792 Jane was in a resupply group, returning from the settlement (Nashboro) when they were ambushed by Indians. She was pregnant at the time of her death. John and th
2 CONC e four children were at home at the time. "They left five small children in poverty and wretchedness." --Annals of Tenn.  The Indians had been very hostile and had murdered many families, but at this time it was thought a permanen
2 CONC t peace had been established. A fort called Brown's Station, not far from where Nashboro now stands, had been built as a protection against the indians.  The children had gone to the pasture to drive up the cows, when the Indians 
2 CONC suddenly attacked their home on September 1792. John was shot twelve times and scalped. The indians set fire to their home and burned all their books and records. His wife had been killed the previous July so his children were lef
2 CONC t "in poverty and wrechedness."  Some neighbors fleeing the indians met the children and conducted them to safety in the fort. They went to live with their Uncle William Haggard, lster of Montgomery County, TN.  Only four of John 
2 CONC and Jane (Gibson) Haggard's children have been identified: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for Jane Gibson: JANE GIBSON, believed to have been a daughter of Isaac Mayfield (re. DAR Directory, Isaac Mayfield notes his daughter (unnamed) married John Haggard and was killed by indians). David Dawson Haggard's book li
2 CONC sts her as Jane Gibson. The Anals of Tennessee lists her only as Mrs. Haggard. If she was a Marfield, John Haggard could have been her second husband. Sutherland Mayfield had a station in 1788 that was attacked by the indians and 
2 CONC he was killed. The indians took Sutherland Mayfield's son George a prisoner and he lived with them for about twelve years.  The Anals of Tennessee notes: During July 1792, Mrs. Haggard was pregnant and riding with an escort of men
2 CONC  from Brown's Station to Nashboro to purchase material to make baby clothes when the party was ambushed by Creek indians and all were killed except Mrs. Haggard and a man who suffered a broken leg and managed to hide in a hollow l
2 CONC og. The indians tied Mrs. Haggard by a thether to a post around which she was driven all night. At daybreak she was scalped and her stomach was cut open and her baby was removed, and she died. The man who had hidden in the hollow 
2 CONC log managed to get back safely and reported what had happened. It was in this condition that she was found by her children and friends. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of John Haggard and Jane Gibson are:  +	73	i.	 	Edmund6 Haggard, born 1773 in Albemarle Co. VA; died 1865 in Virden, Macoupin Co., IL. +	74	ii.	 	Elizabeth Haggard, born 1775 in Virginia. +	75	iii.	 	John William Haggard,
2 CONC  born 1782 in Kentucky territory, VA; died May 04, 1867 in Plymouth, Hancock, IL. +	76	iv.	 	Samuel Haggard, born December 15, 1784 in Kentucky territory, VA; died May 04, 1867 in Plymouth, Hancock, IL.
2 CONT
