1 NOTE In the town records of Woodstock, Conn., appear the following entries:
2 CONT "Daniel Underwood and Experience Kingsley, both of Woodstock were married Jan. 24, 1769, by Abiel Leonard, Pastor (This man was Chaplain of General Putnam's 3rd Conn. Reg. in 1775)." Daniel Underwoods Ear Mark for cattle is a Half
2 CONC  penny the under side the right ear formerly Nathl Perins. Entered Sept. 18, 1778.  Daniel Underwood is said to have been given to forming rhymes only one of which has been handed down by tradition and is remembered only by its fi
2 CONC rst couplet commencing:
2 CONT "Marvel Daniel, my second son
2 CONT Now that my race is nearly run."
2 CONT
2 CONT His second son was born soon after the father's recovery from a severe illness and as he  regarded the recovery marvelous he named his son by the rather unusual name, Marvel. His death was occasioned by a peculiar disease of the l
2 CONC ower limbs traditionally believed by some of his descendants and by some of the descendants of his brother, Nehemiah, to be hereditary. In the early days it was mentioned familiarly in the family as the "Underwood disease." The di
2 CONC sease so far as recorded has reappeared among his descendants in only one instance.
2 CONT Daniel Underwood died intestate and Experience Underwood was made administratrix. His estate is inventoried in the Pomfret Probate and included house and shop and about seventy acres of land valued at L174 11s. together with perso
2 CONC nal property.
