1 NOTE The name Keziah Streeter, twice given by Marvel Underwood to his daughters, is said to have been the name of a sweetheart whom he was expecting to marry, but who died in early life. This family tradition has not been verified.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marvel Underwood was a farmer and carpenter at New Woodstock, N.Y., where he rem. with a number of others from Woodstock, Conn., about 1800. He was one of the founders of the Baptist Church at that place and was the first clerk of
2 CONC  the church, an office which he held for nine years. He was an ardent mason and master of a lodge. At the time of the Morgan tragedy in Western New York his two sons, Marcus and John were intending to join the order but public sen
2 CONC timent became too strong and neither ever joined the order. So bitter was the feeling that the father could no longer attend the lodge in his home town, but made several trips to Eastern Connecticut in order to join his brethren i
2 CONC n fraternal relations. Like his brother Kingsley, Marvel underwood inherited from his father a tendency to break from ordinary prose into rhymes. Very few have been handed down; one will suffice for and illustration. At the raisin
2 CONC g of a barn for a neighbor whose parsimony was well known, he was the principal carpenter. After the building was raised he mounted the plate and bluntly characterized his neighbor as follows:
2 CONT "Penney------- is his name
2 CONT He's the owner of this frame
2 CONT He'll wring and twist and split a hair
2 CONT And cheat his neighbor if he dare."
2 CONT Marvel Underwood was a good singer and for a good many years was the teacher of a singing school in New Woodstock. Marvel Underwood was a captain in the War of 1812 serving at Sackett's Harbor for a short period. He died at New Wo
2 CONC odstock and is buried in the village cemetery by the side of his wife, Betsey Lyon Underwood. His two unmarried daughters are buried in the same lot and the family of John L. Underwood, his youngest son, is buried in the adjoining
2 CONC  lot where a simple granite monument has been erected over their ashes.
