1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Republican, March 22, 1928, page 6
2 CONT
2 CONT MRS. MARGARET HOLMAN
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Barnes Holman, daughter of James and Sally Barnes, was born Aug. 12, 1835, and departed this life March 1, 1928, age 92 years, 6 months, 20 days, being the oldest native born citizen of Parke county and a life long reside
2 CONC nt within its borders.
2 CONT
2 CONT The home of her birth was a pioneer log house located south of Jessup, where the old house of Oscar Feuquay now stands.  Here with her parents, three brothers, three sisters and two half-brothers, she resided until her 20th birthd
2 CONC ay.  Aug. 12, 1855, when she was united in marriage with John F. Holman with whom she traveled 60 years of her lef's journey in happy companionship until he was called to his reward in September, 1915.  To this union were born eig
2 CONC ht children, four of whom died in infancy;  Edward, 25 years ago in young manhood and Milton, eight years ago in the prime of life.  Two daughters survive, Mrs. Rufus Reed of Rosedale, with whom she made her home, and Mrs. Ella St
2 CONC one of near Clinton, both of whom have been untiring in their efforts to make her declining years happy and cheerful.
2 CONT
2 CONT Besides rearing her own children, "Aunt Meg," as she was most familiarly known, took into her home Bert Gates, the son of a niece who was early left motherless and whom she reared to manhood, loving and caring for him as her own a
2 CONC nd who in turn loved her as his own mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was converted to the Christian faith in January, 1851, at the early age of 16 years and united with the Liberty Baptist church, where she was one of its most loyal and faithful members for 77 years.  She was always known as a 
2 CONC woman of high christian faith and character and noted for the deep interest which she took in matters of religion.  Altho of a quiet and retiring nature, one of the most abiding and fundamental characteristics of her life was her 
2 CONC religous interest and outlook.  She loved to go to the house of worship and to join in quiet communion with God and in christian fellowship with others, and as long as her health permitted, which was until the last few months, she
2 CONC  was always found in her pew quiet and unassuming.
2 CONT
2 CONT She knew the hardships of the early pioneers of the county and these struggles seemed to strengthen her for the battles of a long life, for she possessed a remarkable constitution, and retained all her faculties until the end, bei
2 CONC ng especially albert mentally.  She was happiest when busy and was always active and helpful in the homes of her daughters until the last few weeks.  Numerous articles of her needlework, neatly fashioned by her aged, yet nimble fi
2 CONC ngers, since her 90th year, are highly treasured not only in these homes, but in the homes of many of her friends.  She enjoyed knitting for the soldiers of the World War as well as she did for those in the Civil war.
2 CONT
2 CONT She witnessed alost a century of wonders.  How wonderful the panarama that passed before her view and in which she had a part!  What changes have taken place.  The family loom, carding machine and spinning wheel have passed into t
2 CONC he distance being replaced by wonderful electrical inventions for manufacturing.  The messages once carried by horseback rides now transmitted thru the air by wireless.  The heavy rumbling wagon as a mode of family conveyance over
2 CONC  the rough mud roads, supplanted by the auto, smoothly gliding over hard surfaced roads and the aeroplane winging its way above.  We feel that as such advancements take place in the next century, just so will the christian and exe
2 CONC mplary life of the deceased yield its good influence over all with whom she came in contact.  She has finished her part in the great drama of human life--a part no other could play.
2 CONT
2 CONT Besides the daughters she leaves one brother, James Thomas Barnes, age 82, of Washington, Ia.;  six grandchildren, three great-grandchildren and a large number of nieces and nephews.  The community has lost a long revered and resp
2 CONC ected citizen, the church a faithful member and the family a loving, devoted sister, moth and grandmother.  Words are inadequate to pay proper tribute to one who for more than three-quarters of a century was so constant in her fai
2 CONC th.  To family and friends her memory will remain a blessed benediction thru life.  "Blessed are the dead who die in the Lord.  They rest from their labors and their works do follow them."
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services were held at Liberty Baptist church, near Jessup, Sunday, March 4, Rev. Albert Abston, pastor, officiating.  Interment in Forks of the Creek cemetery.
2 CONT * Reference: [https://old.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&amp;GRid=30243680 Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Mar 16 2018, 22:19:37 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
