2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 21 AUG 1619
2 PLAC London, Middlesex, England
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Edited from WikiTree contributors, "William Feake (abt.1538-bef.1595)," WikiTree: The Free Family Tree, (https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Feake-9 : accessed 24 July 2025).
2 CONT
2 CONT William Feake was the fifth son of James Feake of Wighton, Norfolk by his wife Agnes. His birth is estimated between 1538-1539. He was apprenticed to a London goldsmith at an early age but to whom he was apprenticed was not ascert
2 CONC ained by Clarence Torrey or Sir Ambrose Heal in his list of goldsmith's prior to 1800. He lived on Lombard Street, St Edmund the King parish, London.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT 12 Nov 1564 he married Mary Wetherell (Wetherall) at St Mary Woolnoth, London.[2] She is sometimes seen as "Margaret Mary" which is a genealogical fiction as these given names appear nowhere in contemporaneous records.
2 CONT
2 CONT William's will was dated 7 May 1595, probated 19 May 1595[3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT # Mary b ca 1565 m Thomas Barneham
2 CONT # William b ca 1566 m Mary __________ by 7 May 1595
2 CONT # James b ca 1567 d ca 1625
2 CONT # Thomas b after 1574, matriculated Trinity, Cambridge Easter 1589, no record of marriage.
2 CONT # John
2 CONT # Edward
2 CONT # Sarah m William Smyth, London mercer, by 1619
2 CONT # Rebecca m William bournford, London grocer, by 1619
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: the gap between James and Thomas suggests other children who did not survive.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Records  
2 CONT
2 CONT * Marriage: Husband: William Feake
2 CONT * Wife: Margaret Mary Wetherall [NOTE: the marriage record simply reads Mary and not Margaret Mary]
2 CONT * Marriage Date: 12 Nov 1564
2 CONT * Marriage Place: Saint Mary Woolnoth, London, England
2 CONT * Marriage on 12 NOV 1564 in London, Middlesex, England [1][2][3]
2 CONT * Children James Feake 
2 CONT * Death 22 AUG 1619 in London, Middlesex, England[4]
2 CONT * Burial Cemetery: St Edmund, King and Martyr Churchyard, London, England
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Will
2 CONT
2 CONT WikiTree contributors, "Mary (Wetherell) Feake (1539-1619)," WikiTree: The Free Family Tree, (https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Wetherell-119 : accessed 24 July 2025).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary, widow of William Feake, died on 21 August 1619. She had made her will on 09 March 1618/19 and it was probated 23 August 1619 (P.C.C.), an abstract of which follows: Burial is directed in the "church of St. Edmund the King ne
2 CONC ar the place where my husband lieth buried." To every of my sons and daughters and their wives and husbands and every of their children. To Sarah Bullocke, my servant. To poor prisoners of eight prisons, to the hospitals, to other
2 CONC  poor and needy people. To the parish of Wighton in Norfolk where my husband was born. To the Company of Goldsmiths. To the Governors of Bridewell.
2 CONT
2 CONT To son James Feake, to son Edward Feake, 20 acres in Home, Surrey, called the Moores, which I lately bought of on Nicholas Hurling. To son John Feake a messuage by the Sign of Noah and the two tenements in three in St. Swynthens L
2 CONC ane which I bought of my son Thomas Feake. To son John for life certain properties in Godstone als Walcomstead, Surrey, a messuage called Maynards, bought of my son-in-law William Smyth of London, mercer. After John's decease, the
2 CONC n to grandson Samuel Feake, son of my son John, remainder to Judith Feake, daughter of said John, lastly to right heirs of John.
2 CONT
2 CONT To son James two messuages in Lumbard Street now in tenure of Anthony Bradshaw and Robert Davies, goldsmiths. To every of the children of my son John 20 pounds a piece. To daughter Rebecca Bournford 600 pounds and certain goods of
2 CONC  her late husband sold unto me by the late Sheriffes of London by force of an extent. Said Rebecca to occupy the house in Bow Lane which I hold to the Company of Goldsmiths for life, then to Samuel and Henry Bournford, her childre
2 CONC n.
2 CONT
2 CONT To Alice, Feake, daughter of son James, 100 pounds. To Son-in-law William Smith, 300 pounds on condition he pay son William Feake 20 pounds per annum for life. To Katherine Smith, daughter of said William and Sarah his wife, 100 p
2 CONC ounds. To daughter-in-law Mary Feake, wife of William. To daughter Barnham. Mention is made also of the lunacy of Mary Barnham and of the dwelling house of son James in Lumbard Street. James Feake and Robert Feake, my grandchildre
2 CONC n, to either of them, 100 pounds, etc. Son James Feake is named sole executor.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
