1 NOTE Mary Wetherell Feake was buried with her husband in the church of St. Edmund the King. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1623 pedigree gives her father's occupation at aurifaber, or goldsmith, but does not reveal his first name.  Dr. McCraken mentioned the possibility that he might have been Thomas Wetherall, citizen and goldsmith of London, who
2 CONC  was buried at St. Mary Woolnoth and whose widow Margaret married William Ynchorem (Ingrahan?).  The baptisms of children of Thomas and Margaret were recorded at St. Mary Woolnoth from 1540 but Mary might have been baptized earlie
2 CONC r.  There was a John Wetherhill, goldsmith of St. Mary Woolnoth, whose will, dated May 17, 1578, was proved June 18, 1578, but apparantly his wife Elizabeth was the one of the same parish whose own will, proved in 1585, mentioned 
2 CONC a daughter Mary obviously not married.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her will mentioned property in Surrey which she had purchased, three messuages, and a considerable sum of money.
1 NOTE Mary Wetherell Feake was buried with her husband in the church of St. Edmund the King. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1623 pedigree gives her father's occupation at aurifaber, or goldsmith, but does not reveal his first name.  Dr. McCraken mentioned the possibility that he might have been Thomas Wetherall, citizen and goldsmith of London, who
2 CONC  was buried at St. Mary Woolnoth and whose widow Margaret married William Ynchorem (Ingrahan?).  The baptisms of children of Thomas and Margaret were recorded at St. Mary Woolnoth from 1540 but Mary might have been baptized earlie
2 CONC r.  There was a John Wetherhill, goldsmith of St. Mary Woolnoth, whose will, dated May 17, 1578, was proved June 18, 1578, but apparantly his wife Elizabeth was the one of the same parish whose own will, proved in 1585, mentioned 
2 CONC a daughter Mary obviously not married.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her will mentioned property in Surrey which she had purchased, three messuages, and a considerable sum of money.
1 NOTE
2 CONT
2 CONT The father of Mary Weatherall was a "aurifaber" or goldsmith,
2 CONT and was most
2 CONT likely Thomas Weatherall, citizen and goldsmith of London.  His
2 CONT will
2 CONT mentioned his wife, Margaret.
2 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
