1 NOTE William Feake's desired burial in the parish church of St. Edmund the King, on Lumbard Street, London, where he was a parishioner.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to General Delafield, William presented to the Goldsmith's Company a silver-gilt cup of which an illustration appears in Delafield (2:491).  the cup was melted down in 1663, its cover in 1665, and the whole then remade w
2 CONC ith the Feake Coat of Arms and those of his wife's family, the Wetheralls.  On this cup are said to be annulets which indicate that William was a fifth son.  
2 CONT
2 CONT At an early age, he removed to London where he must have apprenticed to some goldsmith.  Neither the list of apprentices obtained by Mr. Torrey nor in Sir Ambrose Heal's list of goldsmiths before 1800 do we find the name of the go
2 CONC ldsmith to whom he was apprenticed.
