1 OCCU citizen and draper of London
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Summary
2 CONT * https://www.geni.com/people/Robert-Thomas-citizen-and-draper-of-London/6000000003649229224, of St. Nicholas Acorns Parish, was born about 1547, and died 8 Jun 1610.
2 CONT *The names of his parents are not known.
2 CONT * His brothers were https://www.geni.com/people/William-Thomas/6000000027431164945?through=6000000220184276925 (d. Aft 1613)  and https://www.geni.com/people/Simon-Thomas/6000000027421037529?through=6000000003649229224 (d. betw 16
2 CONC 10-1613)
2 CONT * His uncles were William Thomas (d. 1610) and https://www.geni.com/people/Robert-Thomas/6000000027420585724?through=6000000220184276925 (d. betw 1557-1566) of St. Katherine by the Tower, London.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT Retrieved from  WikiTree contributors, "Robert Thomas (1530-1610)," WikiTree: The Free Family Tree, (https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Thomas-17374 : accessed 23 July 2025). and then edited
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Thomas died on 8 Jun 1610 and was buried on 26 June 1610, at St. Nicholas Acon, London, Middlesex, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1559, Robert was apprenticed as a draper to William Carow until 12 Apr 1568. [This suggests a birth date of say 1547.]
2 CONT
2 CONT He married first say 1565, at London, Middlesex, ENG, Judith FISHER; children (surname THOMAS):
2 CONT
2 CONT # William - b. c1565; d. bef 28 Aug 1639; m. Martha BENETT
2 CONT # Humphrey - b. c1567; d. bef 12 Feb 1615; m. Jane COTTON
2 CONT # John - b. 1568; wpr. 15 Sep 1613
2 CONT # Judith - b. c1572; d. 21 Dec 1625; m. James FEAKE, goldsmith
2 CONT # Edward - b. c1575; d. bef 27 Dec 1602
2 CONT # Sarah - b. c1578; d. 8 Sep 1613; m. George SOUTHCOTT, Kt. of Dartmouth
2 CONT
2 CONT On 26 Jan 1589, at St. Pancras, Soper Lane, London, Middlesex, ENG, Robert m2. Ellen MUFFETT Lynaker. She was probably the daughter of William Muffett mentioned in Robert's will and a widow since the marriage entry called her "Ell
2 CONC en Lynaker of this par."
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert's will dated 14 Feb 1609/10 at St. Nicholas Acons Church, London, Middlesex, ENG, and proved 12 Oct 1610, mentioned "my present wife Ellen Thomas" (to have £2000 bound by Robert to William Muffett late of Chipping Barnett),
2 CONC  eldest son William Thomas, uncle William Thomas late of St Katherines, Sir George Southcott, son Humphrey Thomas, Humphrey's son Robert, son John Thomas, brother Symon Thomas and his two daughters, daughter Judith Feake (to have 
2 CONC £1000) and her four children James, Robert, Alice, and Judith, son-in-law James Feake (to loose wife's legacy if he sues for his portion), daughter Lady Sara Southcott and her husband, her son Thomas Southcott, brother William's d
2 CONC aughter Sara Thomas, and maid servant Mary Handlye. Executors were Sir George Southcott Knight and Humphrey Thomas; overseers were brother William Thomas, son-in-law James Feake, and Clement Bucke.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Will
2 CONT
2 CONT WILL OF ROBERT THOMAS, St. Nicholas Acons.  
2 CONT Dated 14 Feb. 1609-10. Proved 18 Oct. 1610. 
2 CONT * Robert Thomas, citizen and draper of London, of the parish of St. Nicholas Acon, London.
2 CONT * My body to the earth.
2 CONT * I stand bound to one William Muffett, late of Chippinge Barnett, Co. Hertford, gent. deceased and to his Exors and Administrators to leave to '''my present wife Ellen Thomas''' the sum of £2000 at the time of my death. 
2 CONT * To Ellen Thomas ... and all such plate as she brought with her now being in my house, so the property is not altered. To her my house in which I live. She shall care for my children.
2 CONT * I have given to my eldest son William Thomas £500 to set up his trade and £300, which I bestowed on copyhold lands for him, which I bought of my uncle William Thomas, late of St. Katherines. And also in consideration of a marria
2 CONC ge with Mrs. Benette's daughter have assured him and his heirs males my lands called Spenbye in Lincoln, being to the value of  £3000. Yet nevertheless I bequeath to Sir George Southcott, Knight and Humfrey Thomas my exors.  £800 
2 CONC to allow him a yearly portion of  £60 only for his maintenance. An Indenture tripartite made between his father-in-law Mr. Benet, himself and myself touching the settling of my land Spendye.
2 CONT * Upon my son Humfrey's marriage I assured and made over to him the house he now lives in and  £500 stock, which house and stock cost me  £1000 and did also enter into bond with one Mr. Sillyard to leave my son £1000 more.
2 CONT * Also to his (Humfrey's) son Robert  £100. 
2 CONT *And also further to him all my lands in Essex called Dagman with 12 acres of Marsh by the Thames side, which one John Hardwood now holds. 
2 CONT *To my son John Thomas £1500 provided he makes a general release of all actions and demands, the said legacies excepted. My shop in Candleweekestreete and all the rest of those houses that are upon the same lease to son John.
2 CONT * Lands in Hartfordshire with my copyhold there called Beech Hide to my son Humfrey Thomas and to his heirs.
2 CONT * To my brother Symon Thomas, his two daughters  £70 apiece to be paid to them the day of their marriages.
2 CONT * To my daughter Judith Feake  £1000 in full satisfaction of her marriage money and Child's part. To her four children James, Robert, Alice and Judith  £100 apiece.
2 CONT * James Feake; my son-in-law 'hathe given out threatening wordes that he would go to law with me for his portion.' If the said James refuses to release my exors. from all Actions and demands, within a quarter of a year after my de
2 CONC cease, then this my said, legacy to his wife with the legacies to his children are cancelled, and I leave him to be relieved by the law.
2 CONT * To my daughter the Lady Sara Southcott and to her husband £1000. 
2 CONT *To her son Thomas Southcott £100 to be bestowed in land to the use of him and his mother. 
2 CONT *To Sara Thomas, my brother William's daughter £10 at the day of her marriage.
2 CONT * To my maid servant Mary Handlye £10.
2 CONT * To the relief of the poor children in Christ's Hospital in London £10. 
2 CONT * To the Company of Drapers whereof I am free £20 for a dinner to the Livery of the same Company, who go to my funeral.
2 CONT * Executors: Sir George Southcott, Knight, Humfrey Thomas. 
2 CONT * Overseers: My brother William Thomas, and I give to him a black gown, and to his wife a black gown, my son-in-law James Feake, and Clement Bucke, and to each of them and their wives black gowns.
2 CONT * Witnesses: Humfrey Clarke, Hugh Farye.
2 CONT * Memorandum. Alterations were made 6. June 1610 in the presence of Humfrey Were; Clement Burke, John Curwen, and Thomas Astley.
2 CONT * Probate: 12th Oct. 1610 to George Southcott, Knight and Humfrey Thomas, Executors.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Researcher notes 
2 CONT 1. "The American Genealogist," 1940:95-101: The Thomas Family of London, England (Maternal Ancestry of Lieutenant Robert Feake)," by Clarence Almon Torrey, of Dorchester, Mass.:
2 CONT
2 CONT *"It has long been known that the mother of Lieut. Robert Feake, who came in the Winthrop Fleet in 1630, was Judith Thomas, daughter of Robert Thomas, draper, of London, England. A search of English records made in the interest of
2 CONC  the writer resulted in securing additional information about the Thomas family. It was learned that Robert Thomas, draper, was a man of very great wealth for the time in which he lived and that his first wife, Judith's mother, wa
2 CONC s Judith Fisher, daughter of William Fisher. The marriage date of James Feake and Judith Thomas is given, and the names of the members of the Thomas family closely related to Robert Thomas are mentioned.
2 CONT * The records here given include the following items:
2 CONT ** 1. The Thomas pedigree at the College of Heraldry, London.
2 CONT ** 2. Abstracts of London parish records.
2 CONT ** 3. Allegations for Marriage License of Robert Thomas and Margaret Thomas.
2 CONT ** 4. Abstracts of Thomas wills and administrations.
2 CONT ** 5. Abstract of the Inquisition Post Mortem of Robert Thomas.
2 CONT ** 6. Data from the Roll of the Drapers' Company of London. (From the records of the Drapers' Company it was learned that Robert Thomas secured his freedom Apr. 12, 1568, '''from which it appears that he was born about 1547'''.)
2 CONT *The Thomas Pedigree at the College of Heraldry, London. The information supplied in pedigree chart form is here condensed as follows:
2 CONT ** Robert Thomas, citizen and Draper of London. Died 8 June 1610, buried 26 same month. Judith Fisher, daughter of William Fisher, 1st wife. Ellen Muffett, 2nd wife. Children by first wife:
2 CONT ** William Thomas, married Martha, daughter of William Benett of London.
2 CONT ** Humphrey Thomas, second son, married Jane, daughter of ___ Cotton. Child: Robert Thomas.
2 CONT ** John Thomas.
2 CONT ** '''Judith, married to ffeake of London, goldsmith; had issue.''' [emigrants to America] 
2 CONT ** Sara, married to George Southcott, Kt. of Dartmouth, Co. Devon. Child: Thomas Southcott.
2 CONT * Extracts from London Pariah Records [''not copied here''] 
2 CONT * From the English records in this paper we have learned that Robert Thomas, wealthy draper of London, had brothers William and Simon Thomas, and uncles William Thomas and Robert Thomas of St. Katherine by the Tower, London. Altho
2 CONC ugh we have the names of two uncles of Robert Thomas, draper, we lack the names of his parents.
2 CONT * His niece, Margaret Thomas, daughter of his brother Simon Thomas, married in 1613, by licence, Robert Thomas of St. Botolph, Aldgate. The groom and bride were probably relatives but the relationship has not been learned. The Joh
2 CONC n Thomas mentioned in the register of St. Nicholas Acons was probably a relative of Robert Thomas, draper.
2 CONT * ''The baptisms of Robert's children have not been found in any of the printed records of London churches. The records of some of the churches, including those of St. Botolph, Aldgate, have not been published.''
2 CONT * The will of his uncle William Thomas mentions a cousin [?nephew] William Thomas of East Smithfield, co. Middlesex, and relatives in Lincolnshire. Perhaps Robert's father and uncles were born in Lincolnshire.
2 CONT * If the earlier history of this branch of the Thomas family is secured, the information will be offered to "The American Genealogist" for publication."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT # Find a Grave, database and images (https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/134354844/robert-thomas: accessed July 23, 2025), memorial page for Robert Thomas (unknown–26 May 1610), Find a Grave Memorial ID 134354844, citing St. Nicho
2 CONC las Acons Churchyard, London, City of London, Greater London, England; Maintained by The Pathfinder (contributor 48133744).
2 CONT # https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Nicholas_Acons St. Nicholas Acons was apparently a local parish church in London which was destroyed in the Great Fire of London in 1666 and not rebuilt. It stood on the West side of what is now 
2 CONC called Nicholas Lane in Langbourne Ward of London.
2 CONT # Clarence Almon Torrey, "The Thomas Family of London, England," The American Genealogist, (AmericanAncestors.org, NEHGS, 2009-), 1939, 16:96-101.
2 CONT # WikiTree contributors, "Robert Thomas (1530-1610)," WikiTree: The Free Family Tree, (https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Thomas-17374 : accessed 23 July 2025).
2 CONT #https://www.werelate.org/wiki/Person:Robert_Thomas_(21)
2 CONT # https://myfamilysearch.net/getperson.php?personID=I4796&tree=2005217a
