1 NOTE In 1559 Robert Thomas was bound as an apprentice to William Carow, and was free April 12, 1568.  He was of record as freeing his apprentices Jervise Eyre in 1583, and Edward Handen in 1590.  He served in the Company of Drapers as 
2 CONC warden in 1593, 1599 and 1601, and as Master in 1606.  He lent 20 pounds to Queen Elizabeth in 1598.  He had arms granted him three times in February 1606, once being identified as of Spanby Hall, Lincs.  Apparently, however, ther
2 CONC e was no Thomas wills for Spanby.
2 CONT
2 CONT His will mentioned his present wife Ellen, his children, his late uncle William Thomas of St. Katherines, his brother Symon, daughter Judith Feake and her children James, Robert, Alice and Judith.  It also mentions lands in Lincol
2 CONC nshire called Spenbye, in Essex called Dagman, a shop in Candleweekestreete, and Beech Hide in Hertfordshire.  The inquisition post mortom of Robert Thomas, Gent. was held in Brentwood, Essex, June 28, 1613, and mentioned his will
2 CONC , holdings in Dagenham, Essex, a messuage called "Pittitts" near Edristreet, and Dagman.
1 NOTE
2 CONT If interested in more generations, contact CalOwen@attbi.com and I'll send you what you want
