1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT Not the wife of https://www.geni.com/people/Richard-Knyvette/6000000226817644868?through=6000000003392287548
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne Browne married Adam Winthrop, of Groton, named executor under this will. Supra, p. 5.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Browne-612 accessed 2 Sept 2025 and then edited 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne Browne was the daughter of Henry Browne of Edwardston; her mother's name was Agnes. In her husband's diary, he gave her age as 41 years when their daughter Lucy was born on 09 January 1600[/1] supplying an estimated birth yea
2 CONC r for Ann of 1560. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne married, 20 Feb 1579, as his second wife, Adam Winthrop, son of Adam Winthrop and Agnes Sharpe. Adam was born 10 Aug 1548 at London, England.[2][3] Adam Winthrop was a man of good education and high social standing, lord and 
2 CONC patron of the manor of Groton.[3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne’s father was Henry Browne who lived from 1520 to 1596 in Edwardstone, Suffolk. He married Agnes (1520-90). He was a clothier and a yeoman. Henry’s parents were John and Elizabeth Browne. John was born in 1504 in Witham, Essex
2 CONC , England. John died about 1562 in Edwardstone.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Adam was “bred to the law.”[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Adam and Anne had four children. Their eldest was Anna. Her brother John Winthrop was a pious puritan like his mother and eventually became the first Governor of New England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Adam Winthrop and Anne Browne
2 CONT
2 CONT Adam and Anne had at least five children.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT # Anne, b. 5 Jan 1580-1, d. 20 Jan 1580-1
2 CONT # Anne, b. 16 Jan 1585-6; d. 16 May 1618; m. 25 Feb 1604-5, Thomas Fones
2 CONT # John, b. 12 Jan 1587, the governor of Massachusetts
2 CONT # Jane, bapt 17 Jun 1592; m. 5 Jan 1612, Thomas Gostling
2 CONT # Lucy, b. 9 Jan 1600-1, m. 10 Apr 1622, Emanuel Downing
2 CONT
2 CONT Adam Winthrop was buried 29 Mar 1623 at the age of 75, and Anne 19 Apr 1629, aged 85, at Groton, Suffolk, England.[5][6][3][4] They are buried at St. Bartholomew's Churchyard, Groton, Suffolk, England.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Research Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT A previous version gave her birth as 13 Jan 1544 at Edwardstone, Suffolk, England.[5][6] However, this date was disproven by her husband's diary which gave her age at the time of daughter Lucy's birth.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.genealogy.com/forum/surnames/topics/winthrop/244/
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.Anne4 Browne (Henry3, John2, John1)6,7 was born 1558 in Edwardston, Suffolk, England, and died April 16, 1629 in Groton, Suffolk, England.She married Adam Winthrop, Jr. February 20, 1578/79 in Suffolk, England, son of Adam Winth
2 CONC rop and Agnes Sharpe.He was born August 10, 1548 in London, St. Peter's Parish, house on Fracechurch Street, and died March 29, 1623 in Groton Manor, Suffolk Co, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for Anne Browne:
2 CONT Anne was well educated for the time, fluent in a number of languages, and deeply interested in religious matters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Adam and Anne lived in the house of her father, Henry, who had moved to his lands in Prittewell Essex.Adam and Anne lost three children to death while infants. The first, Anne, died at three weeks.Anne Winthrop was an industrious 
2 CONC and devoted wife, a tender mother, an intelligent writer, as her letters show, and a good French scholar.She wrote charming, affectionate letters to her husband when he was away from home.There is a touch of piety in them and the 
2 CONC suggestion that she was a thrifty housewife.Near the end of her life, she wrote from the bottom of her heart to her grown-up son, John, who was then in London recovering from a serious illness.
2 CONT
2 CONT My Dear and OInoy Son, My MOst Loving Son,--
2 CONT I am sorry for thy sickness and pray to God night and day for thy good recovery which I desire with the most entire affection of my heart, and wish myself presen with thee.I have no balm to send thee, but I will offer up to {the} 
2 CONC Lord the prayers of a true mournful mother's heart til I see thy face with comfort, which God in his rich mercy grant me, poor soul.I pary, Son, remember me to your brother and sister.
2 CONT
2 CONT Your mournful mother,
2 CONT A.W.
2 CONT
2 CONT It has been surmised that the grandson's intense Puritanism came to him from the Browne side rather than from the Winthrops.{From "The Winthrop Family in England"]
2 CONT
2 CONT More About Anne Browne:
2 CONT Burial: 1629, St. Bartholomew's Church, Groton, Suffolk, Engalnd8
2 CONT Fact3: 10th great grandmother of Charlotte Carpenter Johnson
2 CONT Fact 1: married 14 months after death of 1st wife8
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
