1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.genealogy.com/forum/surnames/topics/winthrop/244/
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for Adam Winthrop, Jr.:
2 CONT
2 CONT Adam Winthrop (1548-1623) was the father of John Winthrop (1588-1649), the 1st governor of the Massachusetts Bay Colony. He was the youngest son of Adam Winthrop (1498-1562), Master of the Cloth workers. Born in London in 1548, he
2 CONC  probably spent some of his youth at Groton, in Suffolk, his father having purchased the former monastic manor in 1544 and taken up residence there in around 1552 . The elder Adam Winthrop died in 1562 and his widow, young Adam's 
2 CONC mother, married William Mildmay. Young Adam inherited little land from his father. At the time he was enrolled in a private grammar school run by John Dawes in Ipswich. In 1567 he matriculated fellow commoner at Magdalene, Cambrid
2 CONC ge where he stayed for a few years, making friendships with John Still, John Knewstub, and Henry Sandes among others.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1574 Adam married Alice Still and around then presumably began legal studies at the Inns of Court. In 1575 the fellows of St John's College, where John Still was Master, appointed Adam steward of the college's Kentish manors. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1577 his wife Alice died and tlater he married Anne Browne, son of Henry Browne, former clergyman of Groton.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1584 Adam was called to the outer bar of the Inner Temple following a recommendation to the benchers from Robert Dudley, the earl of Leicester. He was one of the signatories of a declaration of loyalty to the Queen signed by th
2 CONC e barristers of the Inner Temple in 1585. He was already active in the Groton area as a minor landowner and estate manager for his brother John, who had inherited Groton Manor. He also performed legal services for local landowners
2 CONC , including presiding over manor courts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Anne Browne and Adam Winthrop are:
2 CONT *        12       i.       Anne5 Winthrop9, born January 05, 1580/81 in Groton Manor County Suffolk, England; died January 20, 1580/81.
2 CONT *        13       ii.       Anne Winthrop9, born January 16, 1585/86; died May 16, 1618.She married Thomas Fones10 February 25, 1604/05 in St. Peters, London, England; born March 1572/73 in London, England; died April 15, 1629 in 
2 CONC Groton, Suffolk, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Adam Winthrop's Library
2 CONT
2 CONT draft: 30 January 96; Not to be quoted or cited
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Adam Winthrop (1548-1623) was the father of John Winthrop (1588-1649), the first governor of the Massachusetts Bay Colony. He was the youngest son of Adam Winthrop (1498-1562), Master of the Clothworkers. Born in London in 1548, h
2 CONC e probably spent some of his youth at Groton, in Suffolk, his father having purchased the former monastic manor in 1544 and taken up residence there in around 1552 . The elder Adam Winthrop died in 1562 and his widow, young Adam's
2 CONC  mother, married William Mildmay. Young Adam inherited little land from his father. At the time he was enrolled in a private grammar school run by John Dawes in Ipswich. In 1567 he matriculated fellow commoner at Magdalene, Cambri
2 CONC dge where he stayed for a few years, making friendships with John Still, John Knewstub, and Henry Sandes among others. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1574 Adam married Alice Still and around then presumably began legal studies at the Inns of Court. In 1575 the fellows of St John's College, where John Still was Master, appointed Adam steward of the college's Kentish manors. I
2 CONC n 1577 his wife Alice died and two years later he married Anne Browne, daughter of Henry Browne, former clergyman of Groton. In 1584 Adam was called to the outer bar of the Inner Temple following a recommendation to the benchers f
2 CONC rom Robert Dudley, the earl of Leicester. He was one of the signatories of a declaration of loyalty to the Queen signed by the barristers of the Inner Temple in 1585. He was already active in the Groton area as a minor landowner a
2 CONC nd estate manager for his brother John, who had inherited Groton Manor. He also performed legal services for local landowners, including presiding over manor courts. Adam was one of the supporters of a lectureship at Boxford held 
2 CONC by his friend Henry Sandes and was in contact with other members of the Dedham classis and also with John Knewstub, now at Cockfield and leader of a Suffolk classis. In 1592 he was appointed auditor of Trinity college and began an
2 CONC nual journeys to Cambridge to check the college accounts. This appointment was undoubtedly due to the influence of John Still, who was Master of Trinity. Still was also archdeacon of Sudbury, presiding over the ecclesiastical juri
2 CONC sdiction that included Groton and Boxford. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Following his brother John's migration to the Munster Plantation in 1595, Adam assumed a larger role in local affairs. He would hold local offices such as overseer of the poor, coroner, and searcher for cloth, and served on commis
2 CONC sions. Over time he had contacts with many of the leading families of the Stour Valley region and his son, John (born in 1588) would marry into the Clopton, Forth, and Tyndal families. Adam was also in contact with his nephew, Wil
2 CONC liam Alabaster, during the period of that individual's flirtations with the Church of Rome. Adam's own religious views were those of a reformer, in sympathy with the views of local clergymen like Knewstub, Sandes, and Richard Roge
2 CONC rs. In this he followed the lead of his older brother William (1529 - 1582 ), a Londoner who was a friend of John Foxe, a churchwarden of St Michael's Cornhill, and who had extensive involvements with the stranger churches in Lond
2 CONC on. Adam acquired numerous volumes of theology and practical divinity and shared them with clerical kin and friends. He continued to correspond with John Still after the latter became Bishop of Bath and Wells. Indeed, Adam briefly
2 CONC  held an appointment as receiver for that diocese but does not appear to have taken up the responsibilities of the post. In 1610 his brother John sold Groton Manor to Adam's son John with Adam handling much of the detail and subse
2 CONC quently assisting his son in management of the estate. In 1616 both Adam and his son John were included on a patent roll listing of the Suffolk Commission of the Peace. This is the only such listing that includes Adam Winthrop. He
2 CONC  died at Groton on 28 March 1623. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Library of Adam Winthrop has been reconstructed from references to books that he made in his diary and from an examination of collections that passed down in the family. Of these collections the largest are those in the posses
2 CONC sion of the Massachusetts Historical Society, the New York Society, and Allegheny College. Examination of the actual volumes has made it possible to identify by signature, annotation and other means books that belonged to Adam Win
2 CONC throp. The books and the annotation provide an insight into the reading and ideas of a gentleman and country barrister who was a supporter of Puritan reform in the Elizabethan and Jacobean periods and well connected with lay and c
2 CONC lerical leaders of the movement in East Anglia. This compilation does not include the various almanacs that Adam Winthrop owned and annotated, many of which are in the Winthrop Papers at the Massachusetts Historical Society. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Adam Winthrop's Library
2 CONT
2 CONT Key: 
2 CONT
2 CONT WPI -- references to books in Diary of Adam Winthrop; titles often incomplete
2 CONT
2 CONT MHS: WP -- volumes in Winthrop Papers Manuscript Collection
2 CONT
2 CONT MHS: WL -- volumes in Massachusetts Historical Society "Winthrop Library"
2 CONT
2 CONT MHS: SL -- volumes in Massachusetts Historical Society "Special Library"
2 CONT
2 CONT MHS: Marrrs -- volume in Massachusetts Historical Society Marrs Collection
2 CONT
2 CONT MHS: Waterstone -- volume in Massachusetts Historical Society Waterstone Collection
2 CONT
2 CONT NYS -- volumes in New York Society Library
2 CONT
2 CONT AC -- volumes in James Winthrop Collection of Allegheny College
2 CONT
2 CONT HU -- Harvard University Libraries, including list of books given now lost
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT A: Books with clear evidence of being Adam Winthrop's
2 CONT
2 CONT Bound together The Book of Common Prayer (1615); John Speed's The Genealoggies 66of the Scriptures (1611); The Bible (OT 1614; NT 1615), Two Concordances by R. F. Herry [?] (1619) & The Psalms set to music by Sternhold & Hopkins (
2 CONC 1618)
2 CONT
2 CONT -- handwritten notes by AW
2 CONT
2 CONT MHS: WL
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Alanus de Insulis, Anticlaudiani Singulari (Basel, 1536)
2 CONT
2 CONT -- AW identification of author on title page & some notes
2 CONT
2 CONT MHS:WL
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Apuleius, Lucius, Opera Quae quidem extant (Basel, 1560)
2 CONT
2 CONT -- on fly leaf: "underpinning the dwelling house/ the wall of the leanto/ underpinning the salthouse/ Making back for the chimney stacke high and lathing and dobing the chimney/ lathing and dobing a partition"
2 CONT
2 CONT MHS: WL
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT [Aristotle], Aristotelis ad Nicomachum filium, de Moribus, quae Ethica Nominatur, Libi Decem (Paris, 1572)
2 CONT
2 CONT -- extensive marginal notes in what appears to be italic hand of AW, most notes simply summarizing or highlighting popints in text
2 CONT
2 CONT AC
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Bede, Axiomata Philosophicae (Cologne, 1605)
2 CONT
2 CONT -- there is extensive writing in an old hand that is not AW's on the front and back fly leaves. At the top of the fly leaf in front of the title page is "Spes [ ] Christus" in AW's hand.
2 CONT
2 CONT NYS: 34
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Blundeville, Thomas,The Foure Chiefest Offices belonging to Horsemanship (1580)
2 CONT
2 CONT -- Th Blundevill of Newton Flotman in Norfolke,; dedicated to Lord Robert Dudley, Earl of Leicester; no notes except loose mss frgament stuck in back which is two lines of a medical recipe of some sort, evidently in hand of AW
2 CONT
2 CONT NYS: 39
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Bright, Timothy, Hygieira, id est De Sanitate tuenda (1581)
2 CONT
2 CONT WPI: 72 
2 CONT
2 CONT [Brinkelow, Henry], The Complaint of Roderick Mors, sometime a Gray Fryre, unto the Parliamnet House of England for the dredresse of certain wicked laws, evill customs and cruel decrees (Geneve in Savoye, [1545])
2 CONT
2 CONT -- a note in AW's hand on title page states "Mr Bale maketh mention of the author of this booke in the ende of his Centuries. Clarvisse [ait] anno dmi 1540. varia cendens."; a few marginal notes
2 CONT
2 CONT NYS: 169
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Bullinger, Henry, A Hundred Sermons upon the Apocalips of Jesu Christe (1561), translated by John Daus
2 CONT
2 CONT -- writings by AW, including on one fly leaf "A true narration of the lyfe and deathe of Mr. John Daus, the translator of this booke, written by a Scholler of his. I cannot shewe where or when he was borne, or of what parentage he
2 CONC  came, neither under what schoolemrs he was trained up in learninge, but this I have heard him saye, that he was brought up for divers yeres together in St Johns College in Cambridge & prosceded there to the Bachelors and Mrs acte
2 CONC s. He was very well learned in the latin and greeke tounges and also in the true knowledge of the holy scriptures. In Queene Maries time he was in service wth the L Wentworth deputie of Calice untill it was loste, where he also lo
2 CONC ste all his goodes & one of his sonnes. From thence he wente into Germanye to keepe his conscience free from Idolatrie, wch then was sett up in England, and lived poorely there untill Q. Mary died. After whose death he retourned h
2 CONC ome, and dwelt at Ipswch in Suff many yeres where he taught first a private gramer schoole & afterwards was chosen Mr of the free schoole wthin the said towne, & continued in that office, untill one Mr Jermye, a very religious gen
2 CONC tleman (who had bin sometimes his scholler) did bestowe freely uppon him the benefice of Sutton in Suff where he spent the rest of his life, in preaching the worde of god & teachign of his flocke. And there he died, beinge about f
2 CONC owrescore yeres olde. Anno dm 15.: Besides these C. Sermons of Mr Bullengers, uppon the Apocalips, he translated the Commentaryes of Mr Sleyden into Englishe & the Institutions of Mr Calvin in the largest volume, though Mr Norton 
2 CONC afterwardes reviewed the same & nowe beareth the name of the translator thereof. And he had translated (as I heard him saie) the Ecclesiasticall storie of Eusebius out of greeke into Englishe, wch amongst other thinges he lost at 
