2 CONT
2 CONT John Winthrop, son of Adam Winthrop de Groton in Suffolk, and his wife Anne, was born at Edwardstone, near Groton, Suffolk, England, on 02 January 1587[/8].  He was baptised at Groton on 16 January following.
2 CONT
2 CONT He studied at Trinity College, Cambridge, came into a family fortune, and became a government administrator with strong Puritan leanings.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is best known as one of the leading figures in the founding of the Massachusetts Bay Colony, the first major settlement in New England after Plymouth Colony. He departed England on the flagship Arbella, leader of The Winthrop F
2 CONC leet of 1630; the largest fleet of Englishmen ever to depart for the New World.
2 CONT
2 CONT Winthrop was a deeply religious man, and he believed that the Anglican Church needed to rid itself of Catholic ceremonies. He and his followers decided to leave England because they thought that God would punish their country for 
2 CONC this heresy, and they thought they would be safe in the New World.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was elected governor of the colony on 20 October 1629, before their departure in 1630, and he was re-elected several times after they had arrived in the New World. As governor, he tried to keep the number of executions for here
2 CONC sy to a minimum, and he opposed the veiling of women, which many colonists supported.
2 CONT
2 CONT Winthrop served as governor for 12 of the colony's first 20 years of existence. His writings and vision of the colony as a Puritan "city upon a hill" dominated New England colonial development, influencing the government and relig
2 CONC ion of neighboring colonies.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia's extensively well-documented biography:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Winthrop, John, ... He arrived (1630) in the ship Arbella at Salem and shortly founded on Shawmut peninsula the settlement that became Boston. He was - with the possible exception of John Cotton - the most distinguished citizen o
2 CONC f Massachusetts Bay colony, serving as governor some 12 times. He helped to shape the theocratic policy of the colony and opposed broad democracy. It was while he was deputy governor and Sir Henry Vane (1613–62) was governor that 
2 CONC Winthrop bitterly and successfully opposed the Antinomian beliefs of Anne Hutchinson and her followers, who were supported by Vane. The force of his influence on the history of Massachusetts was enormous. Winthrop's journal, which
2 CONC  was edited by J. K. Hosmer and published in 1908 as The History of New England from 1630 to 1649 is one of the most valuable of American historical sources." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages and Children
2 CONT John married first, at Great Stambridge, Essex, England, 16 April 1605, Mary Forth (buried 26 June 1615). They had three sons and three daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT John Winthrop, Governor of Connecticut
2 CONT Henry Winthrop
2 CONT Forth Winthrop (son)
2 CONT Mary Winthrop, wife of Samuel Dudley
2 CONT Anne Winthrop
2 CONT Anne Winthrop (second child named Anne)
2 CONT
2 CONT John married second, at Groton, Suffolk, England, 6 Dec 1615, Thomasine Clopton (baptized 18 Feb 1583; buried 11 Dec 1616) and they had one daughter who died as an infant.
2 CONT
2 CONT John married third, Margaret Tyndall at Great Maplestead, Essex, England on 29 April 1618. They had six sons and two daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen Winthrop
2 CONT Adam Winthrop, abt 27 years old at his father's death (granted administration of father's estate with Martha, Gov Winthrop's widow)
2 CONT Deane Winthrop
2 CONT Nathanial Winthrop
2 CONT Samuel Winthrop, Deputy Governor of Antigua
2 CONT William Winthrop
2 CONT Anne Winthrop
2 CONT Sarah Winthrop 
2 CONT
2 CONT John's wife Margaret died at Boston, Massachusetts 14 June 1647. He married fourth, soon after 20 Dec 1647, Martha Rainsborough, widow of Thomas Coytemore and daughter of William Rainsborough. They had one son:
2 CONT
2 CONT Joshua Winthrop
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial
2 CONT King's Chapel Burying Ground, Tremont St., Boston, Massachusetts.
