1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A history of the Underhill family compiled in the 1930s states that remains from the Underhill Burial Ground were removed to the cemetery at St. Paul’s Church in Mount Vernon, where some Underhill family members wo
2 CONC rshipped in the 18th century. However, local historians and preservations believe that, although tombstones from the site were moved to St. Paul’s ca. 1920, the graves are still in the parcel at Adee and Colden avenues. - https://
2 CONC nycemetery.wordpress.com/2018/08/31/underhill-family-burial-ground/
2 CONT NOTE: Nathaniel's remains were too decomposed to be removed along with his gravestone to St. Paul's. Please see the article reference at the end of this profile for further information. 
2 CONT
2 CONT FATHER: Nathaniel Underhill
2 CONT MOTHER: Mary Ferris
2 CONT
2 CONT WIFE: Mary Hunt
2 CONT m. 19 Apr 1711, Westchester County, New York
2 CONT
2 CONT CHILDREN:
2 CONT  Phebe Underhill, 1713 – 1714
2 CONT  Sarah Underhill, 1714 – 1805
2 CONT  John Underhill
2 CONT  Mary Underhill, 1719 – 1729
2 CONT  Nathaniel Underhill, 1723 – 1784
2 CONT  Bartow Underhill 
2 CONT  William Underhill, 1727 – 1784
2 CONT  Helena Underhill, 1728 – 1764
2 CONT  Israel Underhill
2 CONT  Elizabeth Underhill
2 CONT
2 CONT FURTHER INFORMATION:
2 CONT 1. Burial Information
2 CONT A.  The Underhill Society has evidence Nathaniel Underhill was originally buried in the Israel Underhill Family Burying Ground in Williamsbridge, NY. The following information describes why his remains were reinterred to this ceme
2 CONC tery. 
2 CONT
2 CONT " The Thirtieth and Thirty-First Annual Reports of the Underhill Society make the following statement:
2 CONT
2 CONT "The Israel Underhill Family Burying Ground in Williamsbridge [New York], through which a street is to be cut, was incorporated through the efforts of Willard Underhill Taylor, counsel and member of the Underhill Society of Americ
2 CONC a, and the ground placed on the city map as cemetery. The city bought the property and paid for it. The remains were taken up and removed to St. Paul's Churchyard in Eastchester [Mount Vernon]."
2 CONT SOURCE: Underhill Genealogy Descendants of Capt. John Underhill, Volume 2, Edited by Josephine C. Frost, published privately for the Underhill Society of America, 1932
2 CONT
2 CONT B.  "2133 Nathaniel Underhill, II. Aug. 1690 - Nov. 1775 (Buried originally on his farm in Westchester. Son of Nathaniel Underhill & Mary Ferris." 
2 CONT Ancestry.com. St. Paul's Church, Eastchester N.Y. [database on-line]. Provo, UT: The Generations Network, Inc., 2004.
2 CONT
