2 NICK Cox
1 NAME James Cock
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 4 APR 1674
2 PLAC Killingworth upon Matinecock, Queens County, New York
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 24 MAY 1728
2 PLAC Buckram, Queens County, Long Island, New York
2 ADDR
1 OCCU Gentleman Farmer
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} James Cock, born Killingworth upon Matinecock, Long Island, 4/2 mo (April) 1674; d. Buckram, now Locust Valley, LI 26/3 mo (May) 1728; married at Matinecock 1/10 mo (December) 1698 Hannah Feke, b. there 6/8 mo (Oct
2 CONC ober) 1695; died Buckram 28/2 mo (April) 1750, noted on the Westbury Monthly Meeting record of her death as an "innocent (pure) woman", daughter of John & Elizabeth (Priar) Feke of Killingworth. The marriage certificate was in the
2 CONC  possession of George W. Cocks of the seventh generation (1912). The noted minister Thomas Chalkley, who signed this certificate as a witness, writes in his journal at this time. "We had a meeting at a place called Matinecock, whe
2 CONC re I met with some of the people called "Ranters", who disturbed our meeting. I may say as the apostle Paul (only altering Ephesus to Matinecock), I fought with beasts there"; referring to the "Ranters" who gave Friends much troub
2 CONC le at this time. James was given by his father's will "all my land lying on ye South side of ye Musketa Cove Path" comprising about 200 acres, bought by his father James Cock and Benjamin Birdsall of the Indians in 1684/5, later k
2 CONC nown as the "Piping Rock Farm"; resided there until his purchase of the Samuel Birdsall homestead on west side of road to Matinecock, late in the occupation of Joshua Cock of the Seventh generation. Here he died, as per Will, befo
2 CONC re 10/4 mo (June) 1728. On a commission to re-establish a Boundary Line "Wee Coll. Isaac Hicks, William Willis, & Thomas Williams with the consent and voat of ye Town of Hempstead together with Capt. Jarwise Mudge, Jeames Cock " S
2 CONC amuel Underhill, chosen at a Town Meeting in Oysterbay the second of November 1723--did on the second and third days of December 1723 Perambulate and Remark ye old marked trees from ye East side of Hempstead Harbour Rightover agai
2 CONC nst the East end of ye bar of beach to a walnut tree at Cantiage point and remarked the trees with a splice down ye north west and south east side of ye old marked trees & three Chops on the South west & North east sides of sd tre
2 CONC es." 
2 CONT
2 CONT The info comes from the "Cock-Cocks-Cox" genealogy by I think George Cox. It covers the Virginia Cox family and the LI Cock/Cockses. 
