1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Hickson Woolman Field'''
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/161159488/hickson-woolman-field Find A Grave Memorial ID # 161159488]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hickson Woolman Field, the thirteenth child of John and Lydia, was born October 17th, 1788, married Eleanor, daughter of William de Forrest, by whom he had Hickson Woolman Field, who married Mary Elizabeth, daughter of John Maunse
2 CONC ll Bradhurst and Eleanor Kingsland, who married Hon. John Jay. Hixson W. Field, the father, married secondly, Catharine, daughter of Samuel Bradhurst and died at Rome, on February 12th, 1873.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Field went to New York City at about the age of eighteen, and entered the counting room of Bradhurst & Field, of which firm his brother [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/63809021/moses-field Moses Field] was a partner. Afte
2 CONC r acquiring some commercial experience, he made a voyage to Guadeloupe, and embarked on his return on the ship Washington, which was captured by the British sloop of war Hippomenes, and taken to Antigua, where she was released aft
2 CONC er fifty days' detention, as no French goods were found on board. Mr. Field spent the following winter at Charleston and was present later at the inauguration of President Madison on March 4, 1809. Not long after his return to New
2 CONC  York, he sailed from there for Amsterdam, and passed the British fleet In the Scheldt, which was attempting to intercept the French. Hie vessel. the Dean, passed unnoticed between two lines of battleships, and anchored in the Tex
2 CONC el roads; but was driven ashore in a violent gale and condemned. Her cargo of sugar and coffee was seized by the government of Holland, whose throne was then occupied by Louis Bonaparte.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1831 Mr. Field recovered damages from France for this seizure, under the treaty made with that country by the United States during Jackson's presidency. From Amsterdam, Mr. Field visited successively London, Cadiz, Palermo and 
2 CONC New York. Ho returned to London from the last place and opened a commission home there, which he gave up shortly after, on the passing of the Non-importation act. and left England for Holland, accompanied by Mr. George Astor, a ne
2 CONC phew of [https://www.geni.com/people/John-Jacob-Astor/6000000000889288243 John Jacob], the founder of the New York family of that name. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They landed at the mouth of the Elbe, on a smuggling boat in defiance of a French prohibition, and by doing 10 incurred the penalty of death. On the approach of a sentinel, they had to tie down to avoid detection, and after severa
2 CONC l narrow escapes, they reached Oldenburg, where their passports were "vises" for Bremen. They sent them to the commissary of justice on their arrival at Neustadt and were summoned by him to attend in person. Mr. Field. who spoke G
2 CONC erman, underwent a long examination, during which he stated that he had corresponded with Meyer & Company, of Bremen. So, Mr. Meyer was sent for who not only identified Mr. Field, but also became his bail. After a visit to Copenha
2 CONC gen, Mr. Field returned to America, where he arrived just before war broke out with Great Britain, whereupon be joined a well-known New York company called the 'Iron Greys' and served with this corps during hostilities. On their c
2 CONC aution, be established a commercial house, from which be retired many years before bis death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Eleanor Kingsland in 1818, daughter of William de Forest. His wife died at an early age after giving birth to three children, one of whom died in infancy. He married secondly, Catherine, daughter of Samuel Bradhurst and
2 CONC  the widow of John McKesson. She died at Nice in 1868, leaving no issue. Mr. Field died at Rome, February 12, 1873.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his first wife be left a son, Hickson Woolman Field II, who married Mary Elizabeth, the daughter of John M. Bradhurst and a daughter, Eleanor Kingsland, wife of the Hon. John Jay. Residence New York, New York. 
