1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Isaac Horner
2 CONT
2 CONT 	The first clue as to the origin of the Horner's in Tyler County was found in the Revolutionary War records filed under Isaac Horner from New Jersey.  A letter of application for his widow's pension was contained therein.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	"On this 13th day of August, 1849 before me, James Wise, a Justice of the Peace in and for the County of Monongahela, Virginia, personally appeared on oath that she makes the following declaration in order to obtain the benefits 
2 CONC of the provisions of the Acts of Congress passed June 17, 1832 on her husband's account up to his death, February 4, 1849, aged 94, and Act of February 2, 1848 granting pension to widows of persons who served during the Revolution
2 CONC ary War, that she is the widow of Isaac Horner who served as drummer and blacksmith for the State of New Jersey; that he resided near New Market, New Jersey, that the said Isaac Horner served as a militiaman under Captain Phillip 
2 CONC Snooks, a term of eighteen months at two different times, that he was in several skirmishes and at the Battles of Monmouth and Princeton, that she has no documentary evidence to substantiate her claim or any other evidence than he
2 CONC r own knowledge and traitions together with what her husband has told her and others that knew him in the service, that she was married to the said Isaac Horner at Pennytown (probably Pennington) N.J. in April 1786, that she has h
2 CONC ad eleven (11) children, 9 now living born between 1786 - 1809, that she lived with her husband about 63 1/2 years, that she resides near the line of Mononhalhela and Wetzel and that she is not able to attend court on account of t
2 CONC he distance of 30 miles to (New) Martinsville, that it is more convenient for her to have the business done in Monongahela County, that she is carried in a chair 1/4 miles over the line to meet the Justice, that she desires her na
2 CONC me to be entered on the pension rolls, that she has resided where she now resides about 39 years, and near the waters of Dunkard about 50 years, that she is now a widow as it will appear by the annexed proof."
2 CONT
2 CONT 	S/Zilpha (her mark) Horner
2 CONT
2 CONT 	After the marriage of Isaac and Zilpha Horner they migrated westward through Pennsylvania to finally settle in Tyler County, Virginia where Isaac located 200 acres of land on Fish Creek by virtue of a Treasure Warrent.  This land
2 CONC  grant listed on the Tyler County Entry Books and in Sim's Index to Land Grants was a grant made by Lord Fairfax prior to the creation of the Virginia Land Office by the Commonwealth of Virginia of lands now embracing the State of
2 CONC  West Virginia under it's first constitution.  The property was located in that part of Tyler County, which later became Wetzel County in 1846.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	We do not know the exact date of Isaac's entry as that part of the page is torn off the Entry Book.  It was recorded in Sim's Index as 1821.  However, it was earlier as he was listed on the 1820 Tyler County Census with eight of 
2 CONC their children, one already married.  Within several years of Isaac's entry of land, sons John, Charles, Isaac Jr, William, and Nathan located on or near their father's property on Fish Creek.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Zilpha Horner was not awarded the pension for Isaac Horner's military service.  She failed to return her original papers and could not substantiate her claim.  She was living in the household of Joseph Horner and his family when 
2 CONC the 1850 Wetzel County Census was taken.  Her age was listed as 84 which would make her birth date 1766.  According to information in her pension file, she was still living in District 4 of Wetzel County in 1854.  I find no record
2 CONC  of her death and do not know the burial spots for Isaac or Zilpha.
