1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/Elizabeth-Dusenbury/6000000035043954143
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~wltravis/genealogy/Travis3.html
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT William Travis, born abt. 1742 in Westchester County, New York and died January 1779 in the The garrison, New York. He was married to Elizabeth Fowler who was born in New York to Jeremiah Fowler and Sarah Dusenbury. She died in St
2 CONC . John, New Brunswick. They were married in 1762 in New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had 8 children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Nathaniel Travis, born June 20, 1763 in New York - was married to Deborah Newcomb
2 CONT # Jeremiah Travis born August 5, 1766 in New York; died 1847/8 in East Gwillimbury, Ontario, married Elizabeth Dunham (?) born abt. 1768
2 CONT # Daniel Travis born abt. 1767/8 in New York, died before June 1857, York County, Ontario, m. Grace Pegg died abt. 1848 in East Gwillimbury twp., York Co., Ontario born 1782
2 CONT # William Travis, born Mary 1769 died October 20, 1845 in York County, Ontario and was married to Mary.
2 CONT # Sarah Travis born April 1765 in Courtland Manor, Westchester County, New York died in Nova Scotia
2 CONT # Anna Travis born 1772 in New York
2 CONT # Whitney Travis born 1774 in New York, died 1811 in St. John New Brunswick, Canada. He married Ann (unknown) who was born about 1775 and died December 30, 1836 in New Brunswick.
2 CONT # Elizabeth Travis born May 15, 1773 in New York and was married to Thomas Dunham born 1763 and died 1850.  Unconfirmed records state that Elizabeth married Thomas Dunham, who with his family went to Markham Twp, York County, Onta
2 CONC rio with the brothers of Elizabeth, Daniel Taviss, single and William Traviss, married with family. These three families later settled in Concession 3, E. Gwillimbury, York County, Ontario
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From: "Descendants of Garrett Travis of Westchester County, New York" by Maxine Phelps, Lines, (Main Author) The film # is 1035740 Item 40 US/CAN film Family History Library in Salt Lake City, Utah.
2 CONT
2 CONT In claims papers for losses of Williams's estate, Elizabeth did not give birth years for the last four children. They were 'infants'.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT Letter from Elizabeth Travis after the death of husband asking for compensation from the British government.
2 CONT
2 CONT To the Commissioners appointed by UcA of Parliament for inquiring into the losses and services of the American Loyalists. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Memorial of Elizabeth Travis for herself and eight children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Humbly Showeth, 
2 CONT
2 CONT That your Memorialist husband whilst living was a staunch and loyal friend to his Majesty’s Government and sincerely attached to the contributions and People of England, which made him obnoxious to the rebels, who persecuted him a
2 CONC nd finally compelled him to take refuge in the City of New York, at an early period of the War.
2 CONT
2 CONT That the State of New York sometime after seized and confiscated his estate real and personal. That your Memorialist husband died a Loyalist in the Garrison of New York and left her with eight children to wit, five sons named Nath
2 CONC an, Jeremiah, Daniel, William and Whitney and three daughters to wit Anna, Sarah and Elizabeth in great poverty and distress.
2 CONT
2 CONT That Your Memorialist with great industry supported herself and smallest of her children until the month of September 1783 at New York and then came with all her children to this country where she is now endeavoring to procure a f
2 CONC urther support. 
2 CONT
2 CONT That your Memorialist husband was _____ of the property specified in the ____________schedule, compensation for which she now bets leave to claims for herself and her said eight children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Your Memorialist therefore prays that her safe and the safe of her said children may be taken into your consideration in order that she and they may be enabled under your report to receive such aid or relief as her and their ____ 
2 CONC and services may be found to deserve. 
2 CONT
2 CONT March 13, 1786        	Elizabeth __X____  Travis 					       Her mark
2 CONT
2 CONT SCHEDULE & ESTIMATE OF THE ESTATE REAL AND PERSONAL OF  WILLIAM TRAVIS, deceased, seized, confiscated and sold by the State of New York, in consequence of his loyalty to His Majesty and Machinist  					
2 CONT
2 CONT 81 ½ acres of land near Cortland Manor in the State of New York, the whole (excepting 2 acres) cleared, wherein was erected a dwelling house, barn and other outhouses. 					450 A working horse and a Mare						  41 Two yearling col
2 CONC ts								  10 Five Cows	 @ 5 pounds							  25 Six Steers upwards of 1 year old	@ 4 pounds			    8 Four calves 	@ 1 pound							    4 Forty Five sheep @ 12 shillings						   27 Ten hogs  @ 12 shillings							     6 Farming utensi
2 CONC ls									   10 Fifty bushels of wheat @ 6 shillings					   15 Twelve bushels of Indian Corn @ 2 shillings				     1     10 Eight bushels buckwheat	@ 2 shillings					  16 Outstanding Debts and Notes 						 276       2   10	 					
2 CONC 						_____________ 					New York Currency			 896       8   10
2 CONT
2 CONT Amounting in the whole to Four Hundred and Fifty Pounds, Four Shillings and Eleven Pence Half Penny Sterling (money of Great Britain)
2 CONT
2 CONT March 13, 1786				Elizabeth ___X___ Travis  							       Her mark	
