1 NOTE {geni:about_me} CHRONOLOGY OF RICHARD WOOLSEY & SARAH FOWLER FAMILY
2 CONT
2 CONT 1697 JAMAICA, QUEENS,LONG ISLAND, NEW YORK;
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Woolsey B: 1697 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1700 FLUSHING, QUEENS, LONG ISLAND, NEW YORK: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah Fowler B: about 1700 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah is dau. Of William Fowler & Mary Thorne; See Chronology for her parents) 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1718 JAMACIA, QUEENS, LONG ISLAND, NEW YORK:
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Woolsey & Sarah Fowler M: 24 Jan. 1718:
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard & Sarah removed from Jamaica, Queens, New York to Westchester Co. New York about 1719 with Richard’s parents Thomas & Ruth's family. I believe all of Richard & Sarah’s children were born in Westchester Co. New York. They, 
2 CONC Richard & Sarah, lived the remainder of their lives in Westchester.
2 CONT
2 CONT 18 Sept. 1738: Land transaction:
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Woolsey & Jacheriah Mills witnessed the transaction between John Holmes & Joseph Holmes; All parties involved in transaction are of Bedford, Westchester, N.Y.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1754 BEDFORD, WESTCHESTER, NEW YORK:
2 CONT
2 CONT “In 1754 Richard Woolsey bought eight hundred acres of land from Daniel and Abraham Gomez, on the west side of the Hudson River in the Precinct of Highland, Ulster County.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1760 BEDFORD, WESTCHESTER, NEW YORK:
2 CONT
2 CONT “In 1760 Richard Woolsey conveyed land to his sons, Benjamin & John, which John left to his son Henry.”
2 CONT
2 CONT 1763 BEDFORD, WESTCHESTER, NEW YORK:
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Woolsey & William Woolsey’s name appears on a list of Freeholders in Westchester Co in 1763
2 CONT
2 CONT “In 1763 Richard was on the list of those subscribing to the Presbyterian Society and Church at Marlborough.”
2 CONT
2 CONT April 1775: He signed the pledge to stand by the orders and resolutions of the Continental and Provincial Congress, sent to all precincts from the convention at New Platz, Ulster County. Before this time he had been appoinged Comm
2 CONC issionere of Highways
2 CONT
2 CONT 1770 BEDFORD, WESTCHESTER, NEW YORK:
2 CONT
2 CONT 28 Apr 1770: Richard Woolsey of Bedford in Co. of Westchester & providence of New York, yeoman of the first part, sells to Thomas Knowlton in precienct of New Boro, Ulster, N.Y.; A parcel of land lying & being in precinct of New B
2 CONC urough, Ulster, Co; Beginning at Hudson River--Northeast Corner of Richard Hercourts farm; 211 acres; Witness: Richard Woolsey, Joseph Woolsey;
2 CONT
2 CONT 1774 MARLBOROUGH, ULSTER, NEW YORK:
2 CONT
2 CONT “While residing at Marlborough, in 1774, Richard was road commissioner. At the time of the first settlement of this community, it was the custom of branding cattle, so that each owner might readily distinquish his property, and th
2 CONC ose of Richard were identified by a “crop ye left ear and two slits in the right ear.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: W_W@emailaddress.com (omitted).
2 CONT
2 CONT 1779 BEDFORD, WESTCHESTER, NEW YORK;
2 CONT
2 CONT 31 March 1779: A Richard Woolsey appears on the tax list for Bedford, N.Y. I can't determine if this is the father or the son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Will: Dated: 21 Apr 1779
2 CONT
2 CONT 1782 BEDFORD, WESTCHESTER, NEW YORK:
2 CONT
2 CONT 12 Dec 1782: Will of Richard Woolsey was Probated. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (The will reads as follows)
2 CONT
2 CONT “In the Name of God,amen. I Richard Wolsey of Bedford in Westminister County and State of New Yor, being Weakly in Body But of perfect Mind and Memory Thanks be given unto God, calling to mind the Mortality of my body and Knowing 
2 CONC that it is appointed to all Mence once to Dei, Do make and ordain this My Last Will and Testament That is to Say Principally and first of all I give and Recommend My Soul into the Hand of Almighty God that gave it and My Body I Re
2 CONC commend to the Eaarth to be buried in Decent Christian Mannere at the Discretion of My Edecutors Nothing Doubting but at the general Resureciton I shall Receive the Same again by the Mighty Power of Gnd, and as touching Such World
2 CONC ly Estate Wherewith it has Pleased God to Bless me in this Life I give demise and Dispose of the same in the following Manner and Form: First I make Daniel Wolsey of New Palze and Jacob Griffin of Fish Kills the Sole Executors of 
2 CONC this My Last Will and Testament. I give to Sarah My well beloved Wife all My Estate During her life and after her Decease to be Divided in the following Manner: First I give to my Eldest Son Thomas ten pounds New York Money the Re
2 CONC st of my Estate I give to my three Daughters that is Ruth, Sarah and Hannah to be divided Equally Beteween the three, and I do hereby Utterly disallow Revoke and Disanul all and every other former Testament Wills Legacies Bequests
2 CONC  and Executions, by me in any ways Before Named Willed and Bequeathed Ratifying and Comfirming this and no othere to be My Last Will and Testament in Witness whereof I have hereunto Set My hand and Seal this twenty first Day of Ap
2 CONC ril in the year of our Lord one thousand Seven hundred and Seventy Nine. Signed Sealed Published Pronounced and Declared by the Said Richard Woley as his Last Will and Testament in the Presence of us sibscribed our names.
2 CONT
2 CONT Justes Harris
2 CONT
2 CONT James Clark Richard Wooley
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard is buried in Union Cemetery in Bedford, Westchester, N.Y.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah (Fowler) Woolsey died about & shortly after 1782;
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/18575908/richard-woolsey
