0 @N6000000005077104747@ NOTE [Weeks_4_24_09.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT When Robert Feake departed from Greenwich, in 1647, he left his overseer William Hallett in charge of his properties, along with Robert's wife, Elizabeth. The story of the dissolution of Elizabeth's marriage to Feake, and her subs
1 CONC equent marriage to Hallett, has fascinated many historians and genealogists. There is a wealth of primary data available, though certain key facts have always remained obscure. (See McCracken, vol. 86, pp. 214-20)
1 CONT Whatever transpired, in 1648 Elizabeth bore Hallett a son. The were forced to leave Connecticut, but with help from John Winthrop, Jr., they found refuge under the Dutch on Long Island, where Elizabeth spent the rest of her days.
1 CONT (Those wishing to delve further into Elizabeth's life should read the relevant correspondence in "The Winthrop Papers".)
1 CONT Elizabeth is also, of course, the heroine of Anya Seton's novel, "The Winthrop Woman", which recaptures much of the flavor of the times. In retelling the controversies which filled their lives, Miss Seton portrayed Elizabeth in a 
1 CONC most favorable light, but chose to treat Captain John Underhill less kindly.
1 CONT William married three more times after Elizabeth's death.
