0 @N6000000002625917162@ NOTE Source: Hedger Binder C-228,246 C-228 "Perrins History of Kentucky"
1 CONT p.22,23 Reuben grew up on the farm of his parents and continued that way of
1 CONT life for himself. The 1850 census lists him twice, once in the household
1 CONT of his father, and also at the nearby farm of Absalom Faulconer, son of
1 CONT Richard Faulconer. There he is listed as a 17 year old "Laborer." Reuben
1 CONT and Ann sold her portion of inherited land from her father, to Mary
1 CONT E.Sinclair, Ann's sister, for $700 on Sep.5,1868. (D.B.9,p.442) Meanwhile,
1 CONT Reuben bought 44 acres in Harrison County from Cornelius Dunn for $887.50
1 CONT on Mar.23,1865. (D.B.30,p.285) He later sold this same land to Edmund
1 CONT Swinford for $800 on Mar.2,1875. (D.B.37,p.536) Reuben and Ann lived and
1 CONT farmed in Harrison County, and had 9 children. Reuben joined the Masons,
1 CONT Muellen Lodge #296 on January 15,1860. After his wife's death, Reuben
1 CONT continued to farm, and lived his latter years with different members of
1 CONT his family. He was living with Ed Faulconer near Hinton when he died. His
1 CONT obituary, published in The Log Cabin of Cynthiana, notes that he was a
1 CONT member of the M.E.Church of Hinton, and that "he will be missed by many."
1 CONT His gravestone reads "Gone But Not Forgotten."
