1 CONT
1 CONT Robert was named in the will of his grandfather Thomas, in 1610, and in 1619 his grandmother Feake made her will, leaving him £100.Robert came to Massachussetts Bay with Gov. Winthrop before 19 October 1630. The earliest notice of
1 CONC  him occurs in the journal of the Governor, detailing the particulars and incidents of a prospecting expedition made by him (the Gov.) "and some company with him" on 27 January 1631, "when they went up by Charles River about eight
1 CONC  miles above Watertown.......they came to another high pointed rock having a fair ascent on the west side, which they called Mount Feake, for one Robert Feake who had married the Governor's daughter-in-law, Elizabeth, or Bess, Fon
1 CONC es, widow of Henry Winthrop.
1 CONT He was admitted Freeman May 1631, appointed Lieutenant to Capt. Patrick, 1632, held until 1636. He had his "homestall" in Watertown, and was a grantee of a number of plats in the same place; was selectman several years; 1634-6, re
1 CONC presentative to General court from Watertown. He appears to have followed the fortunes of Capt. Patrick and in 1638-40 accompanied him in his removal to Connecticut. In July 1840, they purchased land from the Indians, in-now-Green
1 CONC wich, CT. The Dutch claimed the land, but they continued to hold it for two years more, but on account of strifes between English and Dutch, and threatening troubles with the Indians, they put themselves under the protection of th
1 CONC e Dutch, 9 April 1642, the submission being signed by Patrick alone, as Feake was sick and had commissioned his wife to act in his stead. Patrick was killed by a Dutch soldier in 1644. This was a serious loss to Feake and may have
1 CONC  been the cause of his loss of reason later. In 1647 he sailed for England. What his reason was we do not know. In a letter concerning the management of his estate he said "till he saw how God would deal with him in England". He r
1 CONC eturned to Greenwich prior to 18 September 1649. Some men of Greenwich wrote to the Dutch Governor complaining about their neighbors of Stamford "and now they lay hold upon a new occasion as we apprehend for such an ende, Mr. Feke
1 CONC  being returned from Old England, they make use of his weakness and silliness to wring the land out of Mrs. Hallett's hands; and they stand ready as we think to gain a grant from your honor for such a thing."
1 CONT Whereupon the Gov., judging him unfit to dispose a plantation, gave the land to Mrs. Feke as her own for inheritance to dispose of as she preferred.
1 CONT If Robert Feake of Greenwich be identical with the "Robert Feake" whose name appears in a resolution of the House of Commons, adopted on the 4th of March 1649, approving and directing a pardon to him and others, then he undoubtedl
1 CONC y attained the object of his visit. The succeeding years of his life were to him a blank. He died in the house of Samuel Thatcher of Watertown, February 1662. When he went to England he entrusted his lands and property at Greenwic
1 CONC h to his wife and William Hallett. They appeared to have sold and conveyed parcels of these lands to new settlers; but this region still continued to be the debatable ground between the two rival governments of New Haven and New N
1 CONC etherlands, and the fact that Hallett was found managing the estate in conjunction with Mrs. Feake was made the pretext and occasion of scandalous proceedings against them by the authorities of both powers--the Dutch seizing and a
1 CONC ttempting to confiscate the property as within their jurisdiction, and the magistrates of New Haven pursuing them with the like rigorous orders and enactments, until they were compelled to abandon the settlement, whence they repai
1 CONC red with the children to Nameag (New London) under the protection of her brother-in-law, John Winthrop, Jr. (who was also her cousin. Winthrop wrote to Gov. Stuyvesant, asking that proceeds of Feake's estate at Greenwich be sent t
1 CONC o Mrs. Feake for the maintenance of her and her children, and that Hallett be allowed to go to Greenwich and gather up the scattered estate and improve the land.)
1 CONT The privilege of their return to Greenwich was not accorded them and Hallett, in the early part of the following year (1649) removed to Long Island--probably Flushing--taking with him Mrs. Feake and her children. They probably con
1 CONC tinued to constitute his household; the date of Mrs. Feake's death is unknown.
1 CONT
1 CONT From George Cocks, The Cocks......Genealogy of Long Island:
1 CONT The following is extracted from a very admirable paper prepared by the late John J. Latting and published in the NYG&BR, 1880.
1 CONT Robert Feake came to Massachussetts Bay in the fleet with Governor Winthrop, in the year 1630. He married 1631 or '32, Elizabeth, the young widow of Henry Winthrop (son of the Governor) to whom she had been married in England as r
1 CONC ecently as the month of April, 1629. She was the daughter of Thomas Fones of London, Apothecary, and Anna Winthrop daughter of Adam and Anne Browne Winthrop of the Manor of Groton, County Suffolk, consequently niece of Governor Wi
1 CONC nthrop and own cousin to her husband. When the latter, following his father, sailed from England in the month of April 1630, he left her at Groton to come over subsequently with his stepmother, both then on the verge of maternity.
1 CONC  Henry arrived in the ship TALBOT in the Harbor of Salem, on 1 July 1630, and on the following day, while walking out to view the country, in attempting to swim across the river, was taken with cramps and drowned. The widow with h
1 CONC er little daughter Martha Johanna, b. Groton 9 May 1630, came over to New England in the ship LYON, with Captain Pierce, which arrived 2 November 1631, bringing also the Governor's wife and others of his family. Her marriage with 
1 CONC Robert Feake must have occurred not very long after her arrival. This connection with the Governor's family quickly brought him to notice. He was admitted a freeman of the colony in May, 1631, and on 4 September 1632, he was appoi
1 CONC nted Lieutenant to Captain Patrick, then chief military officer at Watertown and the neighboring settlements, which position he held until the month of March 1636. He had his "homestall" in Watertown, and was grantee and owner of 
1 CONC a number of plots in the same place. he held for several successive years the office of Selectman of the town - one of the persons termed "freemen chosen to order all civill affaires of ye towne." In 1634, '35, and '36, he was rep
1 CONC resentative in the General Court from Watertown. On 3 September 1634, he, with Captain Underhill, Daniel Patrick and others were appointed by the General Court of Boston to fix upon the site for a fort on Castle Island in the Bay.
1 CONC  He appears to have continued to follow the fortunes of Captain Patrick, and in 1639-40 accompanied him on his removal to Connecticut. In the month of July, 1640, they united in purchasing from the native Indian proprietors the la
1 CONC nds which subsequently constituted the town of Greenwich, Connecticut. Included in this tract was a parcel of land, some time called, "Elizabeth Neck" in honor of the wife of Robert Feake, being declared in the Indian deed to be h
1 CONC er "peticaler perchase". Although this settlement was made under the sanction and in the interest of the New Haven Colony, Director-General Kieft of New Amsterdam soon warned them off as intruders on Dutch territory. Patrick and F
1 CONC eake persisted and continued for two or more years in the occupation of these lands, uncertain between the strifes of the English and Dutch, which power to acknowledge; harassed and threatened meanwhile by the treacherous Indians 
1 CONC of the neighborhood until they finally decided to put themselves under the protection of the Dutch. This submission was signed by Captain Patrick alone on 9 April 1642, it appearing Robert Feake was then sick and could not attend;
1 CONC  although it also appears that he had commissioned his wife, Elizabeth Feake, to act instead.
1 CONT In May 1642, Captain Underhill had become a resident of Stamford, adjoining the plantation of Patrick and Feake, and was now in the service of the Dutch in their encounters with the Indians. Patrick, who had been Underhill's early
1 CONC  companion in arms, was ignominiously assassinated by a Dutch soldier at Robert Feake's house in January 1644. His death undoubtedly proved a serious loss to his co-proprietor in the Greenwich lands, and not unlikely precipitated 
1 CONC the malady which a few years afterward terminated in his "loss of reason". In the month of October 1647 we find him in Boston, on the point of setting sail for England. What was the occasion or object of this journey is not appare
1 CONC nt. That it was necessary, perhaps compulsory, may be inferred from some expressions in a letter which he at that time wrote his friends at Stamford in reference to the management and disposition of his estate in his absence, sayi
1 CONC ng, he reserved the whole propriety of his estate till he saw how God would deal with him in England. How long he continued abroad is not known. That he returned to Greenwich some time prior to September 1649, is shown by a letter
1 CONC  from Robert Husted and others to the Dutch Governor, in reference to their rights of property conveyed to them by Mrs. Feake in the absence of her husband. If Robert Feake of Greenwich be identical with the "Robert Feake" whose n
1 CONC ame appears in a resolution of the House of Commons, adopted on the 4th of March, 1649, approving and directing the issuing of a pardon to him and others, then he undoubtedly attained the object of his visit. But what the offence 
1 CONC could be for which such pardon was sought is not stated.
1 CONT The succeeding years of the life of Mr. Feake were to him a blank. The darkness which first overshadowed his mental faculties at Greenwich never passed until death came to his relief. He found an asylum in the house of Samuel That
1 CONC cher of Watertown, Massachussetts, when he died in February 1662. An inventory of his personal effects taken on the 18th of that month, may be seen in Vol. 1 of Wills in the Probate Office at East Cambridge. His interest in the la
1 CONC nd and property at Greenwich had been entrusted by him, prior to his voyage to England in 1647-48 to his wife and William Hallett. They appear to have sold and conveyed parcels of these lands to new settlers; but this region still
1 CONC  continued to be debatable ground between the two rival governments of New Haven and New Netherland, so they were compelled to abandon the settlement, and repaired with the children to New London, under the protection of her broth
1 CONC er-in-law and cousin John Winthrop, Jr. In spite of the vigorous efforts for the restoration of Mrs. Feake to her rights at Greenwich, the privilege of returning was not accorded to them, and Hallett, in the early part of 1649 rem
1 CONC oved to Long Island, probably to Flushing, taking with him Mrs. Feake and her children. It is not unlikely this removal was at the suggestion of John Winthrop, Jr., himself, who at this time entertained intentions of settling near
1 CONC er New Amsterdam. William Hallett in 1652 made a purchase and settlement at Hell Gate, LI, and doubtless Mrs. Feake and her children continued to constitute his household and as conveyances at Greenwich lands were made by William 
1 CONC Hallett and Elizabeth Hallett, we are justified in the belief that they were married by, or before 1649. Here at Flushing and Newtown the family came under the Quaker influence, and at least three of the children became members; a
1 CONC nd Hannah, the wife of John Bowne, having received a gift in the ministry made two (or several) religious visits to Friends in Great Britain, dying on the last one in England.
1 CONT At the time of the preparation of this paper by Mr. Latting, the date of the death of Mrs. Feake does not seem to have been known, but the Annual Report of the New York State Historian for 1897, Vol. 2, p. 182, shows that on 25 Ap
1 CONC ril 1674, William  Hallett had recently married Susannah, widow of William Thorn of Flushing, consequently, it may be assumed that Bess (Fones, Winthrop, Feke) Hallett had deceased some time in the year 1673 at Newtown, LI. Her da
1 CONC ughter Martha Johanna Winthrop, b. Groton, England, 9 May 1630, came to Massachussetts in 1631, married about 1646, Thomas Lyon of Stamford, Connecticut, and died without living issue. (She died 1668. See Fones Lineage.The followi
1 CONC ng article has her bearing a second child who lived named Mary, and shows her children. This article has Martha Johanna dying ca. 1654. )
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT This information comes thanks to Bob Starkins, letter below, from FTM CD # 173; the data was copied from the NYG&BR from two articles: The Feake Family of Norfolk, London, and Colonial America, by George E. McCracken (86: 212-21),
1 CONC  and "That Winthrop Woman Again", by Donald Lines Jacobus. Unfortunately there is no reference to issue number for this article. It comprises pages 428 to 431, inclusively.
1 CONT
1 CONT On Sat, 19 Jun 1999 05:34:37 EDT, you wrote:
1 CONT
1 CONT >Hi Bob,
1 CONT >
1 CONT >Thanks for your reply; I've never seen the articles you mention. The only
1 CONT >thing I've read about her is that some of the family in the 19th century
1 CONT >tried to expunge any record of her from existence. Luckily, it didn't work!
1 CONT >I definitely would like some excerpts, if it's not too much trouble! I
1 CONT >discovered not too long ago she's my 9xggrandmother, with Robert Feake as her
1 CONT >husband.
1 CONT >
1 CONT >Thank you again,
1 CONT >Janice
1 CONT
1 CONT Hi Janice (or should I say Cousin?)
1 CONT
1 CONT I am descended from Elizabeth at least three times. Through Hannah, John,
1 CONT and Elizabeth (Lisbet) Feake. It was a pretty closed society at that time
1 CONT and you may find more connections as you go. My connection is through the
1 CONT Cock's who moved from Long Island to Orange County, NY after the
1 CONT Revolution. Specifically Philadelphia Cock who married Henry Rider in 1815.
1 CONT Possibly we can compare notes when we find our common connection.
1 CONT
1 CONT The CD version I have is in image form and I will have to send the stuff as
1 CONT attachments. The easiest way for me would be as Microsoft Word documents.
1 CONT Can you handle Word attachments? Is there a size limit on your email
1 CONT server? There'll probably be a total of about 1MB.
1 CONT
1 CONT Bob
1 CONT
1 CONT -- Bob Starkins
1 CONT mailto:rstarkins@earthlink.ne
1 CONT
1 CONT (49.)      Lieutenant Robert¹ Feake, second son of James Feake (no. 32) by wife Judith Thomas, is mentioned as such in the wills of his maternal grandfather in 1610 and of his paternal grandmother in 1619, and he was apprenticed t
1 CONC o his father, James Feake, citizen and goldsmith of London, for the term of eight years beginning on 21 September 1615. This would suggest that he was born in 1602, perhaps even on 20 September of that year. It is under his name t
1 CONC hat the Feake arms have recently been registered in the American Roll of Arms (New England Historical and Genealogical Register 107:269).
1 CONT      Colonel Banks (The Winthrop Fleet of 1630, p.69) says he came to America with that fleet, and, if he did not, he must have arrived soon afterwards, for he requested to be made freeman on 19 October 1630, and was accepted 16 M
1 CONC ay 1631. John Winthrop's "Journal" (1:83, under date of 27 January 1631/32) states that a certain hill -- in that part of Watertown afterwards Waltham -- was named for Robert Feak who had married the governor's daughter-in-law.
1 CONT      She was Elizabeth Fones, born 21 January 1609/10 at Groton, England, daughter of the London apothecary, Thomas Fones, by his wife Ann Winthrop who was sister to Governor John¹ Winthrop, and she had married, first, 25 April 16
1 CONC 29, in England, her first cousin, Henry² Winthrop, son of Governor John¹ Winthrop by his wife Mary Forth (Muskett's Suffolk Manorial Families, 1:26, 87). Henry Winthrop did not accompany his father to America in 1629 but followed 
1 CONC him on the "Talbott", arriving at Salem on 2 July 1630. On the same day, seeing a small boat across a bay he attempted to swim over to it but, being seized by cramps, was drowned in full sight of his friends, none of whom was able
1 CONC  to swim. The young widow was still in England, having given birth on 9 May 1630 to a daughter who was christened with a double name, Martha Johanna, unusual for this period. Faced with the prospect of having ultimately to support
1 CONC  his widowed daughter-in-law and her child, the thrifty governor naturally looked about for a suitable candidate to be her second husband, and when his eye lighted upon a young man of pious character, goodly estate, and great prom
1 CONC ise, the future Governor William Coddington, he attempted to interest him in the widow but he married, instead, another. Thus Elizabeth was still a widow when, on 2 November 1631, she arrived in the Bay with her daughter as passen
1 CONC gers on the ship "Lyon". In less than three months, however, she had found her second husband, Robert Feake, and had married him.
1 CONT      Henry Winthrop's daughter Martha Johanna grew up in the Feake household and, though she was not of robust health, about 1647 she married one Thomas Lyon, b. ca. 1621, died at Greenwich, Connecticut, in 1690, on whom see the "
1 CONC Lyon Memorial" (Detroit, 1907, 2:28-37). Lyon's correspondence with the Winthrops is also printed in the "Proceedings of the Massachussetts Historical Society 2:6:1-20, and is highly important for the subsequent history of the Fea
1 CONC kes. Thomas and Martha Johanna (Winthrop) Lyon had two children, the first of whom apparently died very young as we hear no more of it, but the second was a daughter Mary, born shortly before 2 September 1649, aet. 6 months on 23 
1 CONC January 1649/50. Mary married, first, Joseph Stedwell of Rye, and second, John Wilson of Gedford and Rye (D.L. Jacobus, "Families of Old Fairfield 1:394 f.). The Stedwell children were Mary, Joseph, Martha, and Johanna; the Wilson
1 CONC  children: Martha Johanna, Roger, John Winthrop, Susanna, Samuel, and Thomas. Martha Johanna (Winthrop) Lyon died ca. 1654, and her widower married, second, Mary Hoyt, daughter of Simon Hoyt and had a family by her (Jacobus, op.ci
1 CONC t. 1:294, 394).
1 CONT      Robert and Elizabetha Feake and their family may have lived for a time in Boston but soon moved to Watertown. Elizabeth's double relationship to the governor of the colony was naturally sufficient to insure that, if Robert ha
1 CONC d any abilities, he would get a chance to demonstrate them, so we are not surprised to discover that on 4 September 1632 he was made lieutenant under Captain Daniel Patrick of Watertown, of whom we shall hear much at a later point
1 CONC  ("Records of Massachussetts Bay" 1:99), and on 3 September 1634 was on a committee to prepare fortifications (ibid. 1:124). From 1634 to 1636 he was a member of the General Court and in the latter year is first clearly recorded a
1 CONC t Watertown. His land there is listed as totalling 200 acres in nine parcels (Watertown Records 1:4, 7, 8, 11, 17, 19, 71). He was elected selectman of Watertown in 1636 and 1638 but not in 1637. Before the first term was out, he 
1 CONC had apparently moved to Dedham where his name is the first on the Covenant (Dedham Town Records 1:3) and where he was present at four town council meetings between 29 August 1636, and 28 January 1636/37. His house and farm lot at 
1 CONC Dedham are mentioned on 11 August 1637, but he resigned his right to these on 21 September 1638, and to all his Dedham property on 23 November 1638. In 1639 he was back at Watertown, a gentleman, when he signed the power of attorn
1 CONC ey discussed in the sketch of his brother, but at this time he was doubtless preparing to move elsewhere, for his Watertown homestead is recorded as belonging next to Thomas Bright who sold it again to Colonel William Rainsborough
1 CONC  as early as 17 December 1630.
1 CONT      On 18 July 1630, Daniel Patrick and Robert Feake obtained from the Indians a deed conveying the site of Greenwich, Connecticut, and doubtless intended this to be an English settlement, but Greenwich was so close to the Dutch 
1 CONC at New Amsterdam that these two Englishmen -- Patrick, as will be seen, had married a Dutch wife in Holland ten years before -- were forced to submit to the Dutch authority and became patroons of Greenwich. At what date Robert Fea
1 CONC ke first exhibited signs of the insanity which clouded his later years we do not know, but it is perhaps significant that the very act of submission to the Dutch, dated 9 April 1642, was signed by Patrick and, in the absence and i
1 CONC llness of her husband, by Elizabeth Feake (Proceedings of the Massachussetts Historical Society 2:6:1-20); Documents relating to the Colonial History of New York 2:144). It may well be that the illness was mental, even at this ear
1 CONC ly date. Insanity had been present in the Feake family in the preceding generation. Robert's aunt, Mary (Feake) Barnham (no. 30) was a lunatic for at least twenty years, and it may be that Robert had inherited the taint. If so, th
1 CONC ese are the only two instances of mental illness discovered in the family.
1 CONT      Testimony was later given in court by John Bishop, Richard Lawe, and Francis Bell (quoted in extenso by Mr. Latting), that Robert Feake "was a man whose God-fearing heart was so absorbed with spiritual and heavenly things tha
1 CONC t he little thought of the things of this life, and took neither heed nor care of what was tendered to his external property," and so allowed his wife to dominate him. Bishop also added that Feake had lived with him and during thi
1 CONC s period went to Greenwich and there made an agreement contrary to what Bishop and his wife thought reasonable. It may well be that his mental instability was a partial cause of his wife's looking elsewhere for manly protection, o
1 CONC r the fact that his wife did not take her marital vows very seriously may have contributed to his mental downfall: at this late date we cannot be sure which. Certain it is that for many years before his death Robert was unable to 
1 CONC manage his own affairs and that he made some sort of arrangement with William Hallett to care for them, though we have not been able to lay our hands on the instrument which made the arrangement effective. There is, however, on re
1 CONC cord a letter in which John² Winthrop expressed his satisfaction with the way in which Hallett discharged these duties, but it would be too much to say that this letter expresses any approval of the relationship between Hallett an
1 CONC d Elizabeth, as some have tried to maintain that it does.
1 CONT      Secondary sources also claim that during the lifetime of Daniel Patrick, who was assassinated by a soldier at Greenwich in 1644, that worthy had conducted an illicit love affair with Elizabeth Feake. The source usually cited 
1 CONC is Winthrop's "Journal" but my friend, G. Andrews Moriarty, Esq., who is fortunate enough to own a copy of Savage's unexpurgated edition of the Journal, informs me that though Patrick's "folly" is recorded, there is no mention of 
1 CONC Elizabeth Feake as the partner. The old governor would, naturally, have been loath to record any fault of his niece and erstwhile daughter-in-law, though he was quite willing to set down in gross detail the follies of others.
1 CONT      In any case, Robert Feake would appear to have returned to the Boston area and in 1647 he went back to England, for what reason does not appear, though the departure was undertaken suddenly. On 4 March 1649/50, the House of C
1 CONC ommons took action pardoning a Robert Feake for some unstated offense (The Record 11:16), and our Robert Feake is the only man of the name known to us who could be involved. We can only guess that the reason for the pardon was tha
1 CONC t as he was patroon of a Dutch town some question had been raised as to his loyalty to the British crown or to the Commonwealth which had by then usurped the authority of the crown, and that this vote of the House cleared him.
1 CONT      When Thomas Lyon married Martha Johanna Winthrop about this time, he seems to have expected, as one of the delights brought by matrimony, to get the right to serve as manager of the estate of her stepfather and was deeply dis
1 CONC appointed when Hallett supplanted him. Thus, on 14 April 1648, he wrote to his wife's grandfather, Governor John² Winthrop, a remarkable letter (see the correspondence cited above) in which he unburdened himself about the unhappy 
1 CONC situation then existing in the Feake household. He refers to "my father being distracted" (i.e., Feake's mental difficulties), "my father Feake going away sodingly," and boldly accuses his mother-in-law of adultery: "she openly co
1 CONC nfesses that she is married to him (William Hallett) and is with child." However much we may impute to Thomas Lyon unworthy motives in making this accusation, or explain his attitude as only an acute case of the natural antipathy 
1 CONC which some men feel towards their mothers-in-law, it is certain that Elizabeth must have been pregnant at the time, for she gave birth, probably towards the end of 1648/49, to a child thereafter known as William Hallett. The child
1 CONC 's name precludes the possibility that in Elizabeth's defense any one might suggest that this was Robert Feake's child, conceived before Robert's departure from Greenwich at an unknown date. The child was admittedly Hallet's, not 
1 CONC Feake's. Moreover, included in the Lyon correspondence cited above, there is a letter dated 12 February 1649 (i.e., 1648/89), addressed to her cousin and erstwhile brother-in-law, Governor John² Winthrop, which is signed by Elizab
1 CONC eth as Elizabeth Hallett, and also letters from Hallett to the same person whom he calls kinsman, thereby abundantly making it clear that William Hallett and Elizabeth claimed to be husband and wife, this, though her second and al
1 CONC so currently legal husband, Robart Feake, was certainly not dead at the time.
1 CONT      For reasons quite understandable it has been claimed by descendants of William Hallett that a legal divorce had been granted to Elizabeth from Robert Feake and that Elizabeth had then married, as, it would be, her third husba
1 CONC nd, William Hallett. We have searched for primary records to support this claim in the extant archives of the three jurisdictions where the couple resided, namely, New Netherland, New Haven Colony, and Connecticut Colony, but we h
1 CONC ave found only one secondary source which even purports to cite a primary source. The primary source cited is a document still extant at the State Library in Albany, namely, New York Colonial Manuscripts, volume 4, page 365. This 
1 CONC exists in the original Dutch form and also in an English translation made in the nineteenth century by the late Dr. E.B. O'Callaghan, New York Colonial Manuscripts (translation), volume 4, page 365, and is of two pages in the Engl
1 CONC ish version. We have taken the trouble to obtain photostats of both versions. The English translation begins with the following words: "Whereas Elizabeth Feax has for adultery been legally divorced from her former husband Robart F
1 CONC eax, before our arrival, by the preceding Director General and Council....." At first sight these words would appear to prove that a divorce had been granted to Robert Feake from his wife Elizabeth on the grounds of adultery, this
1 CONC  before the departure of Governor William Kieft from New Netherland. Inasmuch, however, as the sentence is taken from a translation made about two centuries after the fact, we must appeal to the original document itself. In this c
1 CONC ase the Dutch original is not only extant but is better preserved than the English translation, and is written in a fine, clear hand which presents no difficulties to an experienced reader. The words translated by "legally divorce
1 CONC d" are "wettelyck gescheyden." The adverb gives no trouble and has been correctly translated but at least it can be said that the participle, if not wrongly rendered by Dr. O'Callaghan, can be rendered differently. We consult K. T
1 CONC en Bruggencate's Engelsch Woordenboek, 12th edition revised by A. Broers (Groningen, etc., preface dated 1932), which contains both an English-Dutch and a Dutch-English section in separate volumes. Part I, page 207, lists the Engl
1 CONC ish word "divorce" and gives the Dutch equivalent of the noun as "(echt)scheiden" and for the verb "scheiden." The latter word, of which we have the participle in the phrase under discussion, really means, as reference to the same
1 CONC  dictionary, Part II, page 685, shows, "separate" in any sense but when used of married people can mean "divorce". That is, it so means in the twentieth century and probably so meant in the nineteenth when O'Callaghan worked. Did 
1 CONC it also mean this in the seventeenth century? Or was it used in this document in the more general and at any rate original sense, i.e., "separate." Note that the scrivener found it necessary to add the word "wettelyck." This court
1 CONC  proceeding is called a legal one. Now all divorces are legal in that a court proceeding must precede dissolution of a marriage. Not all separations, however, are legal. We think it certainly possible that what happened to the Fea
1 CONC kes was a court proceeding whereby they were legally separated but not divorced. Care was taken by the court to protect Feake's property in his own interest and that of his children, but that did not mean that either party to this
1 CONC  marriage was now free to marry again.
1 CONT      If it be argued that we are wrong in our interpretation and that O'Callaghan's rendering of the phrase is perfectly correct, we wish to point out a fact which corroborates our interpretation. Let us assume that a divorce was 
1 CONC really granted and that the marriage was completely dissolved. Then the former wife was in a position to marry again, yet she did not do so, despite the fact that she was twice put under severe persecution for adultery, as we shal
1 CONC l see. If a divorce has been granted, and one of the parties is then accused of adultery, the easiest way out of the difficulty is for the accused person to point to the fact that he or she has been married again, subsequent to th
1 CONC e divorce. Whether William and Elizabeth ever were married at all we do not know, but it is absolutely certain that on both 9 March 1649 (i.e., 1648/49) and on 17 May 1649 they had not yet been married to each other, since documen
1 CONC ts bearing those dates, shortly to be quoted in full, show them as persecuted by, respectively, the Dutch and the Connecticut authorities for continued adultery. (Continued in MSWorks)
0 @N6000000006348478300@ NOTE LIEUTENANT ROBERT FEAKE, was born in England, and died in Watertown, Mass., 1662. He was the son of James and Judith (Thomas) Feake, and the grandson of William and Mary Wetherell Feake, of London (will 1595), and great-grandson o
1 CONC f James Feake of County Wighton, Eng. He came to Massachusetts Bay with Governor Winthrop,16 1630, and subsequently held many public offices of trust. In 1632 he was appointed Lieutenant to Captain Patrick, chief military officer 
1 CONC at Watertown. In 1634, when the first Court of Delegates was held, his name appears fourth on the list, which is given by Winthrop in his History. Again, 1635 and 1636, he was Representative from Watertown, and 1639 to 1640 he uni
1 CONC ted with Captain Patrick in the purchase of land, now the town of Greenwich. He married Elizabeth, the widow of Henry Winthrop (the son of Governor Winthrop) and the daughter of Thomas and Anna (Winthrop) Fones.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source:
1 CONT Ancestral Records and Portraits vol 1
1 CONT Author: no author
1 CONT Call Number: R929.1 C72 v.1
