0 @N6000000005077075319@ NOTE [Weeks_4_24_09.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes for Elizabeth Fones:
1 CONT Elizabeth Fones ("The Winthrop Woman"). She was born at her grandfather Winthrop's manor house, and grew up very close to her mother's family. Only ten days after her father's death she married her first cousin Henry Winthrop, a d
1 CONC ashing young man just returned from Barbados, who the next year sailed to Massachussetts to join his father, Governor John Winthrop, only to drown while trying to swim in the Charles River, on the day of his arrival. Thus, when El
1 CONC izabeth arrived at Boston 2 November 1631, aboard the "Lyon", she was already a widow, with a babe in arms. (The "Lyon", which also brought the Governor's wife, Mrs. Margaret Winthrop, was greeted on arrival by Captain John Underh
1 CONC ill and his militia.)
1 CONT Elizabeth was soon to marry Robert Feake, and he quickly became a prominent citizen, perhaps because his wife was such near kin to the Colony's Governor. He was Lieutenant under Captain Patrick in 1632, member of the General Court
1 CONC  1634-36, and Selectman of Watertown 1636-38, having settled in the latter place.
1 CONT By 18 July 1640 the Feakes had removed from the Bay Colony, as on that date they and Captain Daniel Patrick purchased from the Indians the site of Greenwich, Connecticut, where they settled. When, on 9 April 1642 they accepted Dut
1 CONC ch jurisdiction over Greenwich, Elizabeth Feake signed for her husband, who was ill. There is ample evidence that this was a mental illness, which eventually broke up their marriage. In 1647 Robert returned to Boston and made a tr
1 CONC ip to England. Whether he ever came back to Greenwich is not clear, but he spent most of his remaining years in Watertown, Massachussetts, where he became a public charge. (From F.E.Weeks) It seems that Robert Feake did not live w
1 CONC ith his wife after his return from England in 1649 until his death in 1662. Probably he had lost his mind and had to be taken care of, and his wife was virtually his widow.
1 CONT By her three husbands, she had eight children.
1 CONT 9 March 1648. Order in council enjoining Elizabeth Feaks from alienating any part of her late husband's property; placing her children under the care of curators at Greenwich, where she is permitted to reside, on condition she qui
1 CONC t the bed and board of William Hallett, her paramour, who is sentenced to be banished for adultery.
1 CONT
1 CONT From Robert Coles GED:
1 CONT [Elizabeth] first married Henry Winthrop, her first cousin and son of John Winthrop, in April of 1629. Henry went to America with his father a year later and drowned, leaving Elizabeth a young widow. She left England and sailed to
1 CONC  America on the ship "LYON", arriving on 2 November 1631. Shortly thereafter, she married Robert Feake.
1 CONT Elizabeth, during her husband's "sickness", began an affair with William Hallett, who was married and had children (I'm not sure this is accurate..JWB). She moved with him to New London and then to Flushing, NY. On 9 March 1648, p
1 CONC rior to Robert's return from England, there was a court order issued which enjoined Elizabeth from selling any part of her husband's property [she and Hallett had already begun selling off parcels]; placing her children under the 
1 CONC care of curators at Greenwich, where she is permitted to reside on the condition she quit the bed and board of William hallett, her paramour, who is sentenced to be banished for adultery.
1 CONT
1 CONT In a letter dated Stamford 14 April 1648, Thomas Lyon related to his "loving grandfather" John Winthrop the history of Mr. Feake and Elizabeth (Fones) Winthrop:
1 CONT "...when I married first, I lived in the house with her because my father being distracted I might be a help to her. Whereupon seeing several carriages between the fellow she now hath to be her husband and she the people also took
1 CONC  notice of it which was to her disgrace which grieved me very much...and seeing what condition she were in I spake to her about it privately and after I discovered my dislike I see her carriage alter toward me...Father concerning 
1 CONC the condition she is in and the children and estate my father Feike going away suddenly, having taken no course about the children and estate only desired a friend of his and I in case we see them about making away the estate and 
1 CONC to remove we should stay it...She hath also confessed since she came there openly she is married to him is with child by him and she hath been at New Haven but could have no compfort nor hopes for present to live in the jurisdicti
1 CONC on adn what will become of her I know not." [It seems Mr. Thomas Lyon, Elizabeth's son-in-law, was more concerned with what property wasn't going to be coming to him by way of his marriage to Bess' eldest daughter, Martha Johanna 
1 CONC (Joan)  Winthrop, and he sought to wrest control from Bess and Will through this letter to Gov. Winthrop of Massachussetts. JWB]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1656 William Hallett, sheriff of Flushing, was sentenced for allowing (Baptist) conventicles in his house, contrary to law; to be removed from office; to pay a fine of £50 Flemish and to be banished, meanwhile to be imprisoned 
1 CONC until the same be paid. A petition was presented by his neighbors, asking for the remittal of the sentence, which was done, except the fine.
1 CONT In 1707 William Hallett Jr., of Newtown, his wife and five children were all murdered by an Indian man and a Negro woman, their own slaves. The murderers were executed. (Evidently the order of 1648 concerning William Hallett did n
1 CONC ot disgrace him in the eyes of the inhabitants of Flushing, as he was chosen sheriff.)
1 CONT For a sympathetic portrait of Elizabeth Fones, see "The Winthrop Woman", by Anya Seton.
1 CONT
1 CONT "THAT WINTHROP WOMAN AGAIN" from NYG&BRecord, by Donald Lines Jacobus
1 CONT      Anya Seton's excellent, though romanticized, novel, "THE WINTHROP WOMAN" (1958), accepts the fact that Elizabeth (Fones) (Winthrop) Feake was divorced from her second husband, Robert Feake, and supposes that she was married a
1 CONC t New Amsterdam to her third husband, William Hallett. In 1955 appeared Dr. George E. McCracken's excellent account of the Feake family, wherein he discussed at length (Rec. 86:212-21) the supposed divorce of Mrs. Feake and her al
1 CONC leged marriage to Hallett. Before this was published, the late General John Ross Delafield sought my opinion on these matters and placed in my hands his collection of data on the subject. It was my understanding that he intended t
1 CONC o publish his own views, but so far as I know, he never did so.
1 CONT      Dr. McCracken pointed out that Mrs. Feake's bill of separation is not in existence but is referred to in a Council order in New Netherland dated 9 March 1649, which states that Elizabeth "has for adultery been legally separat
1 CONC ed from her former husband Robert Feax, before our arrival," that is, before May 1647, while Kieft was still Director General. Although Dr. McCracken seems to feel that 1649 is the correct date of this document, the last line, whi
1 CONC ch includes the date, is in a different handwriting from that of the body of the text, and the document is entered between two others of the year 1648. The translation of this decree obtained by Maturin L. Delafield from the Secre
1 CONC tary of State's Office, Albany, in 1873 is dated 9 March 1648. Most important, the letter of Mrs. Feake's son-in-law, Thomas Lyon to old Governor Winthrop, dated 14 April 1648, 'recites the substance of this decree' and also refer
1 CONC s to Mrs. Feake's pregnancy by William Hallett and her claim that she was married to him ("Winthrop Papers", 5-215, footnote, which refers to the Dutch decree and corrects the date). So the date must be 1648, not 1649.
1 CONT      As for the meaning of the earlier separation decree, Dr. McCracken correctly translates the words as "legally separated", but implies that this was not an outright divorce which gave Elizabeth a right to remarry. However, we 
1 CONC are here confronted, not with a matter of linguistics, but with a question of law. Did the Dutch law of that period admit what is today known as a legal separation? There were indeed separations that were not legal, most often due
1 CONC  to desertion on the part of the husband. But could a 'legal' separation at that period mean anything short of a divorce? No such status as that of "legal separation" was then known in the English colonies, and even in the next ce
1 CONC ntury (the 18th) cases are on record in Connecticut of women mistreated by their husbands who had to petition the Legislature for the right to live separately and control their own property, for no such recourse as "legal separati
1 CONC on" was offered them through court action.
1 CONT      Although Dutch law may have differed, it seems likely that this legal separation of 1647 was really a divorce, and Dr. E.B. O"Callaghan so translated it in 1865. It is significant that the 1648 decree which refers to the sepa
1 CONC ration calls Robert Feake in two places Elizabeth's " 'former' husband", whereas he would still be her husband if the separation were short of a divorce.
1 CONT      Then why did not Elizabeth marry Hallett at once? So far as New Netherland is concerned, it is difficult to answer the question. Miss Seton in her novel suggests that it was because Kieft left immediately after the divorce wa
1 CONC s granted and the new Director General, Stuyvesant, was unsympathetic. By Dutch law or custom a widow was supposed to wait a year before remarrying, and quite likely a divorced woman would also be subject to this delay. In New Eng
1 CONC land it would have been difficult for Elizabeth to find a magistrate willing to perform the marriage. Even if he could read Dutch or have her bill of divorcement translated, he would be likely to hesitate as to the validity of a d
1 CONC ivorce granted by a foreign country. Mrs. Feake was a resident of Greenwich, then Dutch territory, and the divorce had been granted by the Dutch authorities. A similar problem might present itself today in the case of an American 
1 CONC who had obtained a divorce in Mexico.
1 CONT      To get the chronology straight: Elizabeth's divorce was granted by May 1647. In March 1648 she was still not married to Hallett and the Dutch government issued its decree, based on the fact that she was still living with Hall
1 CONC ett and with child by him, a decree which permitted her to live at Greenwich but placed the property of herself and her Feake children under curators, and banished Hallett, confiscating any property he might have for the benefit o
1 CONC f the (Hallett) child "begotten on her". From Lyon's letter of a month later, April 1648, it seems clear that Hallett and Elizabeth had visited New Haven, perhaps in an attempt to marry there. It is certain that they did not succe
1 CONC ed because, not long after, Governor Eaton of New Haven had to depend on hearsay and did not know whether the couple were married or not. If we believe all that Lyon wrote, Elizabeth pretended to him that she had married Hallett a
1 CONC nd he did not actually know her marital status.
1 CONT      At least by July of that year, Elizabeth with Hallett sought refuge in Pequot (New London) with her first cousin (and brother of her first husband), the younger John Winthrop, who later became Governor of Connecticut. On 21 J
1 CONC uly 1648, Governor Eaton wrote to John Winthrop (answering Winthrop's inquiry of four days earlier) with regard to Hallett and Mrs. Feake who "are come to your plantation at Nameag". This letter dealt mainly with the matter of Fea
1 CONC ke and Hallett property which had been brought to Stamford, "being from the Dutch in danger of confiscation". So evidently Winthrop's concern in writing to Eaton was to try to safeguard the couple's property rights, Stamford being
1 CONC  just east of Greenwich but in New Haven Colony.
1 CONT      The following year, the trouble-makers were still busy, and on 17 May 1649 the General Court of Connecticut, sitting in Hartford, having been informed that "one Hallitt" was living at Pequot (New London) "with one that was Mr
1 CONC . Pheax his wife" in the foul sin of adultery, ordered their arrest, the case to be heard the following month. But appended to this order, "the Governor is desired to write to Mr. Wenthrope and acquaint him with it." The law could
1 CONC  be no respecter of persons, but, after all, Mrs. Feake was a Winthrop connection. The very next day, Governor Haynes wrote to Winthrop and referred to the Court action with regard to "one that was the wife sometimes of Mr. Feake 
1 CONC and who it seemes did confesse her selfe an Adulteresse (which is uppon record at the Dutch) and now pretends marriage with another man, how trew, or legall is not well knowen." Of course Haynes knew he was writing about Winthrop'
1 CONC s own relative. In modern parlance, he might as well have written, "Look, fellow, it this is on the up-and-up,  okey, otherwise get her out of there."
1 CONT      The Halletts left very hurriedly after this warning. There is a short undated note from Elizabeth written on the vessel of Tobias Feake (her husband's nephew), addressed merely "Sir", telling Winthrop where she had left some 
1 CONC household appliances he had lent her, and thanking him briefly. The tone of this note may well be due to extreme haste and the fact that it had to be left unsealed and hence might be read by others before delivery. However, her ne
1 CONC xt letter, written from Greenwich 6 August 1649, starts emotionally, "Deare Brother, All the love and servis and thankefullnes I am Able to express is next unto god dew unto your selfe as the Instrument of my present well being." 
1 CONC She also refers to "my 'husband' " and the kindness of the (Dutch) governor.
1 CONT      Winthrop was a methodical man and after dealing with letters received he folded them, endorsed them on the outside with the writers' names, and filed them. Up to this time he had endorse Elizabeth's letters, "Sister Feakes" o
1 CONC r the equivalent. This letter, for the first time, he endorsed "Mrs. Hallett". Why? The letter had not announced her marriage. Yet Winthrop knew she was now Mrs. Hallett.
1 CONT      Miss Seton, in her novel, supposes that Winthrop, through the good offices of Mr. Baxter, the Dutch governor's English secretary, had helped to make it possible for Elizabeth to marry Hallett in New Amsterdam. Earlier in the 
1 CONC story she has Elizabeth ask Winthrop himself to marry her to Hallett, which he declines on the ground that his commission has not yet arrived and when it does arrive he will have to arrest her because the warrant for her arrest wi
1 CONC ll arrive at the same time.
1 CONT      The fact is that Winthrop, during his first winter at New London, 1646-1647, held a commission from Massachussetts Bay and could marry people as a magistrate. That is proved by his own deposition years later regarding boundar
1 CONC ies, which tells the famous story of Bride's Brook and his marriage of a couple at the boundary line. Shortly after, it was decided that New London belonged to Connecticut Colony, the General Court of which issued him a commission
1 CONC  on 9 September 1647, and this was renewed 18 May 1648 when "Mr. Wynthrope the younger is to have Commission for to execute the place of a Magistrate at Pequoyt" (Col. Rec. Of Conn. 1:157, 164). There is no reason why Winthrop cou
1 CONC ld not have married Elizabeth to Hallett long before the order for her arrest was passed. That would explain the exuberant gratitude expressed by Elizabeth when she called him the "Instrument" of her well-being. It would also expl
1 CONC ain the change he made in endorsing her subsequent letters as "Mrs. Hallett": for he would know of her marriage without having to be told.
1 CONT      There would have been no moral objection (if there were no legal one) to the marriage of an adulterous person to a former paramour. The New England Puritans set strict standards and punished violators of their sex code with f
1 CONC ines and sometimes corporal punishment, but they were practical. A few years later, at New Haven in 1692, a man was divorced by his wife for adultery and he immediately married, virtually by court order, the young woman for whose 
1 CONC pregnancy he was responsible. And no doubt Winthrop would have preferred to see his relative married to her lover (if it could be legally accomplished) than living with him sinfully under his sheltering roof. Probably the only que
1 CONC stion in Winthrop's mind would be the validity of a Dutch divorce, a divorce decree issued by a foreign government. For the sake of a relative he might have been willing to take that risk, but he would not care to have the validit
1 CONC y of the divorce called into question immediately after he married Elizabeth to Hallett. That of itself would explain why the Halletts were shipped off to New Amsterdam shortly after news of the indictment by the Hartford Court wa
1 CONC s received. Afterward, Winthrop need only state that he knew the Halletts to be legally married and offer to depose to the fact, and, if a questioner pressed him too closely, give him a glassy stare. He was, after all, a Winthrop.
1 CONT      On the other hand, the greatest risk would have been involved if the Halletts had been returned to New Amsterdam still unmarried. Not only had they violated the Dutch decree respecting themselves and their property, but they 
1 CONC would still be living in adultery. But their marriage by Winthrop would have changed their status completely, and carrying Winthrop's letters to Baxter and the Dutch governor, Stuyvesant, would have softened the attitude of the Ne
1 CONC w Amsterdam government towards these erstwhile transgressors. It must be remembered that Winthrop not only was a learned man, interested in science and a practicing physician, but was also one of the prime diplomats of his generat
1 CONC ion. Although this solution cannot be proved, it is offered as answering all the requirements of the problem and as explaining all the records and factors involved.
1 CONT      The present study owes much to the collection of data made by General Delafield, but it cannot be stated that the conclusions set forth would have been his.
1 CONT
1 CONT The following was copied from a website that's since disappeared from it's last known address.
1 CONT This is from a docu-drama book called THE WINTHROP WOMAN, that is very well researched by Anya Seton. On page 250, there is related a cannon-ball pitching contest between Will Hallet and "his opponent - Bigelow, the blacksmith." T
1 CONC he contest was held in the Common of "little Boston", on Election Day 14 May 1634. The book relates the life of Elizabeth, a grand-daughter of Adam Winthrop, and a passenger on one of the ships of the Winthrop Fleet. Elizabeth lat
1 CONC er became the wife of this Will Hallet.
1 CONT It is probable that John Biglow and/or Mary Warren (his wife) came over on one of the ships of the Winthrop Fleet. Mary's parents, John and Margaret Warren were reported on one of the ships:
1 CONT "John Warren came to New England from Nayland, Suffolk, England, with the fleet of Sir Richard Saltonstall. [4/462] The fleet arrived at Salem June 12, 1630. John Warren traveled with Governor Winthrop aboard the "Arabella". [9] F
1 CONC rom Salem, he with the rest of the company went to Charlestown, whence, after a brief stay they moved to Watertown. [2/252] John Warren settled in Watertown, Massachusetts at age 45." From passage taken from Warren 1 page.
1 CONT Elizabeth crossed paths with Bigelows, Warrens, and others in Boston, Watertown, Connecticut, England, and aboard ship. Her motehr was Anne (Winthrop) Fones, and her father was Thomas Fones, an apothecary in London in 1617. Anne F
1 CONC ones' father was Adam Winthrop, who had been a wealthy Suffolk clothier at Lavenham. He had risen to be Master of the Clothesworkers Guild in London, and like many others, celebrated his successes by joining the gentry. This was a
1 CONC ccomplished by means of a coat-of-arms awarded by the Royal College of heralds and a manor grant bought from the King. The Winthrops were henceforth esquires and lords of Groton Manor. The book opens up with Elizabeth, her younger
1 CONC  sister Martha, her mother, adn her father traveling from London to Groton Manor to visit the relatives. John Winthrop, Anne fones brother, was Lord of Groton Manor. Their father was Adam Winthrop, and their mother and his wife Mi
1 CONC stress Winthrop also at Groton Manor. She had been plain Anne Browne of Edwardstone, a yeoman's daughter, before their marriage. It was June 19, 1617, and there was a cruel east wind from the sea that linked their Suffold coast wi
1 CONC th Holland. It was the time of King James and his Queen. The King's children were Charles, the Prince of Wales and Elizabeth, Queen of Bohemia.
1 CONT "John Winthrop was born in Suffolk, England in 1587. He was his parents' only son. His father, Adam Winthrop, was the lord of Groton Manor, a small estate in the English countryside. John grew up on his father's estate, amid gentl
1 CONC y rolling hills, fields of wheat and rye, adn shallow ponds. In his childhood he was educated by a private tutor, adn at the age of fourteen his father enrolled him in Trinity College in Cambridge. He studied there for two years a
1 CONC nd then returned to Groton to begin practical training in running his father's estate. Soon Adam Winthrop saw his son's hidden marriage problem and introduced him to Mary Worth, the daughter of a distinguished Essex nobleman. Thre
1 CONC e weeks later John was married at the age of seventeen. Ten months later, just after his eighteenth birthday, he became a father. John and his wife Mary worked hard and had six children in ten years. Then Mary suddenly died. After
1 CONC  six months John remarried, but on his first wedding anniversary his second wife died. One year later John married his third wife, Margaret. By all accounts, Margaret was one of the most appealing women in all of American history.
1 CONC  She was beautiful and gracious. She was also a woman of faith. John Winthrop treasured her as his greatest possession. When he traveled away from home, he never failed to send her love letters." From Winthrop Papers text.
1 CONT
1 CONT John Winthrop's children were also in Groton Manor upon Elizabeth's visit in 1617. The oldest appears to have been Lucy, age 16, and the two youngest being Forth and Mary. John Winthrop's wife and their mother had died previously 
1 CONC (as stated above).
1 CONT
1 CONT To jump ahead a bit, "It was 6 July, when John Winthrop sat in his private bark wigwam at Charlestown on the Mystic river, writing home-bound letters for which Captain Pierce, Master of the Lyon, was waiting.
1 CONT ........there was a crowd of people milling about as usual outside, desirous of interviews. They were discontented settlers, tired of existing on mussels, wild berries and Indian corn.........There were also four Indians......two 
1 CONC of which were John and James Sagamore.....with the chief of the Neponsetts, Chickatabot." The narrative goes on with this interesting section: "Many of Winthrop's company were sick of the scurvy and flux, the water supply at Charl
1 CONC estown was poor. It was obvious, as the ships straggled in, tht so many people could not exist on this barren peninsula. Already Sir Richard Saltonstall and the minister, George Phillips, had gone up the Charles and found a new lo
1 CONC cation which Sir Richard called Watertown............Each day since arrival at Charlestown the ships had been coming, after touching at Salem for directions. The Mayflower, the Whale, the Hopewell, the Trial, the Success, the Will
1 CONC iam and Francis, and at each landing Winthrop had questioned the passengers about Harry Winthrop", his son.......He had his other two sons with him, Stephen and Adam..........One day Winthrop heard word of the Talbot, and Peter's 
1 CONC possible passage on it. It was long overdue and on the day of arrival: "Er flag also is at half-mast", said Captain Pierce............"When they land send them here", said Winthrop..........Henry had died after diving into the col
1 CONC d water on a very hot day at Salem, four days ago.
1 CONT Elizabeth Fones came over to the New World on the ship "Lyon", also part of the Winthrop Fleet. Her fellow passengers included Will Hallett, mentioned above; Jack, son of Governor John Winthrop, and Martha, Elizabeth's sister. Als
1 CONC o on the ship was Margaret, the Governor's wife and his youngest children.
1 CONT ************************************************************************** ***************************************
1 CONT This information is from : http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~mbfriedlander/hawxhurst.html
1 CONT Fones Family
1 CONT
1 CONT The easiest method of showing the rather complicated relationship of Elizabeth Fones to the Winthrop family is the following self explanatory drop chart. Her story has been told in the historical novel, The Winthrop Woman, by Anya
1 CONC  Seton.
1 CONT
1 CONT                                                         Adam Winthrop married Anne Browne
1 CONT
1 CONT                                                             | |
1 CONT
1 CONT        Mary Forth m. Governor John Winthrop (MA)                     Ann Winthrop m .Thomas Fones
1 CONT
1 CONT                  |                                | |                    |
1 CONT
1 CONT                  |               Governor John Winthrop (CT)          m. Martha Fones         |
1 CONT
1 CONT                  | |
1 CONT
1 CONT        Edward Winthrop                                          married Elizabeth Fones
1 CONT
1 CONT ELIZABETH FONES was born in England about 1610, daughter of Thomas and Ann Fones, and died in Long Island about 1667. She married three times:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1) Her first marriage was to her cousin Henry, second son of the great John Winthrop, in London 25 April 1629. When the Winthrop Fleet sailed in the spring of 1630, Henry was taken along to assist his father, because John, the eld
1 CONC est and most reliable son needed to stay at home to mind the family and business there. Elizabeth also stayed home, because she was pregnant; she was waiting until the child was born and old enough to travel before she joined Henr
1 CONC y in America. The baby was born 9 May 1630 and christened Martha Joanna. Then Henry drowned .A diarist, Hubbard, is quoted in the footnotes to Winthrop's History; "A sprightly and hopeful young gentleman he was, who, though he esc
1 CONC aped the danger of the main sea, was unhappily drowned in a small creek, not long after he came ashore, even the very next day, July the 2nd." He was a strong swimmer, but a. got a cramp and died very close to his friends who were
1 CONC  unable to help him because none of them could swim. .
1 CONT
1 CONT 2) Elizabeth came later, bringing the baby, on the ship Lyon, landing 2 November 1631. Governor Winthrop quickly married her off to Robert Feake, in Watertown, Massachusetts, 2 months later. The Feakes stayed in Watertown for seve
1 CONC ral years, and Robert was appointed lieutenant and elected deputy and selectman. Then his commanding officer, Captain Patrick persuaded them to by some property from the Indians in Greenwich, Connecticut.There are many stories abo
1 CONC ut Elizabeth and Patrick; she was undoubtedly a flirt, undoubtedly bored with Robert, and probably did have an affair with him, and equally probably Robert was well aware of it.. Then Patrick was assassinated in Greenwich in 1644;
1 CONC  and W illiam Hallett was put in charge of the Greenwich property because Robert was ill and then was away.
1 CONT
1 CONT 3) In the meantime Elizabeth and William Hallett had become a pair. Again.it not quite clear what happened. Because of Robert's insanity, Elizabeth obtained some kind of a divorce in New Amsterdam. She had another child, William H
1 CONC allett's, towards the end of 1648..A "legal separation" was supposedly granted by the Dutch, but at least until May of 1649, no marriage had taken place between William and Elizabeth.. The council in Fort Amsterdam on 9 March 1649
1 CONC , banished William from Dutch lands, so they had to flee to New London in Connecticut where her cousin John Winthrop was deputy governor. When it looked as if they would be charged there too for adultery, they fled again, this tim
1 CONC e to Long Island. To quote McCracken:"We have found no evidence of how William Hallett and his "wife" Elizabeth finally made their peace with the authorities." Governor Eaton wrote to John Winthrop Jr., "it is possible that Willia
1 CONC m Hallett and she that was Mr. Feakes his wife, are marryed; though not only the lawfulness and validity of such a marriage but the reality and truth is by some questioned (LIF)." Jacobus studied the documents and said he thought 
1 CONC it was a legitimate marriage.(LIF) Whatever the truth was, Elizabeth had another Hallett baby, Samuel, born sometime around 1652.
1 CONT
1 CONT William Hallett became Sheriff of Flushing, Long Island. While holding that office he permitted William Wickenden, "the famous Baptist evangelist of Rhode Island" to hold services at his house, and this so angered Governor Stuyves
1 CONC ant that he again banished William, but that was somehow smoothed over. Elizabeth died before 1669; after the death of Elizabeth,William married Susanna (Booth) Thorn as her second husband .But that marriage did not work out and a
1 CONC  separation was arranged. A third wife, Katherine, is on record in 1684 and in 1686 a fourth wife, Rebecca, widow of John Bayliss. William Hallett died at Hell Gate, Long Island in 1706.
0 @N6000000000485194701@ NOTE
1 CONT She came over with the Winthrop Fleet;  “The Winthrop Woman”
0 @N6000000005077075347@ NOTE [Weeks_4_24_09.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Bunker, LI Genealogies, Elizabeth appears to have been the daughter of Elizabeth Fones and her first husband, Henry Winthrop, her cousin, to whom she was married about 1629; he was drowned on 3 July 1630, in attemptin
1 CONC g to swim a small river. The young widow came to Massachussetts Bay, and subsequently married Robert Feaks. Elizabeth Underhill is generally supposed to have been his daughter, having grown up with the Feaks children.
1 CONT In the Underhill Genealogies, Deats, compiler, Elizabeth is the daughter of Robert Feake, born about 1633.
1 CONT Elizabeth actually was the daughter of Robert Feake, born supposedly in Watertown, Massachussetts, about 1633. George Cock has her marriage date as 1649.
