1 CONT
1 CONT He resided on part of Captain John Underhill's Killingworth grant, and was the founder of the Feake (Feeks) family of Oyster Bay. Among his descendants was grandson Robert Feke of Newport, Rhode Island, regarded as one of he forem
1 CONC ost portrait painters of the colonial period.
1 CONT From George Cock:
1 CONT With his brother Robert Feeke in 1660 and 1669, he executed conveyances to "our brother John Bowne" of certain land at Flushing in which it would appear that John came to Killingworth between the two dates. He was probably a mecha
1 CONC nic as in 1672 with Samuel Andrews he engaged to build the Meeting House for Friends at Oyster Bay. In 1673 he was a witness to the will of Anthony Wright, and in 1683 on a list of the Estates of Oysterbay Township as valued at £1
1 CONC 30.  In 1684/5 with John Underhill, William Frost, James Cock and twelve others of Killingworth, he effected a final settlement with the Indians for the Rights of Commonage granted by them in 1667 to the Seven Purchasers. In 1694 
1 CONC an Agreement was made between Henry Townsend, Jr., of Oyster Bay and John Feekes of Matinecock, and Samuel Birdsall and William Birdsall, to build a Sawmill, at ye place betwixt ye land of Henry Townsend, and ye meadow of John Fee
1 CONC kes upon ye Crick called Beaver Swamp Crick, with the said Feekes to carry on in labor and charge the ½ of the Mill, Henry Townsend Jr., ¼ and Samuel and William Birdsall ¼.
1 CONT From George Cocks, "birth date not certainly known, died as per Friends' Record, "an Ancient friend of Matinecock" 3 mo. (May) 1724; son of Robert and Elizabeth (Fones--Winthrop) Feake of Watertown, Mass."
1 CONT
1 CONT Following information from : http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~mbfriedlander/hawxhurst.html
1 CONT
1 CONT 2. JOHN2 (Robert1 ) FEAKE was born in Watertown, Massachusetts about 1639, and died in Killingworth, Oyster Bay, Long Island May 1724; he married in Killingworth 15 September 1673 ELIZABETH PRIOR, who was born in England August 16
1 CONC 56 and died in Killingworth, 25 January 1702, daughter of Matthew and Mary Prior. Both John and Elizabeth were Quakers; meetings were held at his house in Killingworth, Long Island. The Quaker book calls John Feake an "ancient Fri
1 CONC end." (Bunker,EAQ, and LIF)
