0 @N6000000000491137782@ NOTE [Himes.Core.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT [143983.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT They moved to Solebury in 1700 and bought land there in 1705.  They were members of the buckingham Monthly meeting. John III had come to America in 1682 with his father.  When the father returned to England, 15 or 17 year old John
1 CONC  was left with a Quaker but reportedly he ran away and lived with the Indians.  He sold the farm in Middleton Twp., in 1700 and bought 510 acres in Solebury twp.  In 1705 trade for the 820 acres ajoinging it near Lahaska Station. 
1 CONC  He was the first white settler in Buckingham-Solebury Valley.  Part of this far was later owned by great, great, grandson Isaac Pearson/Pierson Scarborough.  John and Mary were members of Middletown and Falls Monthly meetings.  O
1 CONC ctober 1724 he was received as a minister.  John was also a member of Bluckingham meeting, Bucks Co., PA earlier as an elder.
1 CONT      It is said that he ran away from the family to which he was apprecticed and lived with the Indians for five years and later served as William Penn's interpreter.  He traded farms twice, in 1705 for an adjoining property in Al
1 CONC aska and i n 1709 for 820 acres with Jacob Holcombe.  He is thought to be the first white settler in the buckingham-Solebury Valley.
1 CONT      John and Mary were members of Middletown then Falls Meetings and became charter members of Buckingham MM when that meeting was created. On 3 Sept 1705 he was condemned by Falls MM for too much celebrating of so-called Christm
1 CONC as the previous year.  John was memorialized by Buckingham Meeting:  ' in his youth somewhat airy...a man of remarkable self-denial and endowed with much mildness..with loving and kind deportment."
1 CONT Source: SOME QUAKER FAMILIES Scarborough/Haworth by Roger Boone, 1991
1 CONT      5) John the younger received all of his father's possessions
1 CONT in America, with the injunction to be kind to the Indians, his
1 CONT father having received many favors from them. It is said that
1 CONT he spent five and ten years with the Indians, learning their
1 CONT language and often serving as an interpreter and negotiator
1 CONT between them and the Engoish settlers. It is also said that he
1 CONT had run away from the family to whom he had been apprenticed
1 CONT and that he was  mistreated and cheated out of his father's
1 CONT holdings. However, land transactions suggest otherwise. About
1 CONT 1700, he sold the tract in Middletown and was a pioneer
1 CONT settler in Solebury Township on a 510 acre tract of limestone
1 CONT land now located between Aquetong and the Lakaska Station. In
1 CONT 1709, retaining 60 acres of this tract, he exchanged the
1 CONT balance for an adjoining 820 acres of limestone land.
1 CONT Buckingham Meeting was established at his request and that of
1 CONT his neighbor, John Bye in 1702. He was a Minister of that
1 CONT Meeting, and had a Certificate to the Meeting of Ministers and
1 CONT Elders. He married (1690) Mary  (Farr) PEARSON (ca. 1666 in
1 CONT Bucks Co. PA - 1 / 23 Jan 1751), a woman of Indian descent.
1 CONT [She married next Phillip TORREY with whom she had no
1 CONT children.]
1 CONT Tradition says that John was interested in the Indians and possibly lived among them, learning their language, and serving them as interpreter at treaties.
1 CONT
1 CONT Their children were William (30 Oct 1691 - Apr 1727) {m. (1)
1 CONT 1712 Alice Longshore (b. 1693), daughter of Robert Longshore
1 CONT and Margaret Cook; m. (2) Mary}; Sarah; Mary (8 Aug 1695 - 14
1 CONT Jan 1787 in Frederick Co. VA) {m. (1) 1 Nov 1712 Samuel
1 CONT Pickering (bapt. 3 Aug 1684 in Salem MA - d. 10 Aug 1727
1 CONT Solebury, Bucks Co. PA), with 9 children; m (2) ca. 1730
1 CONT Joseph Lupton (1686 - 9 Aug 1758), with 3 children -- his
1 CONT first wife had been Mercy TWINING}; Susannah (19 May 1697 -
1 CONT bef. 1727) {m. Richard Brock (b. ca. 1695 - bef. 1747), son of
1 CONT John Brock and Elizabeth Rowden. Seven children}; Elizabeth
1 CONT (b. ca. 1699 - 1742) {m. 29 Oct 1719 John Fisher, who wrote
1 CONT his will 15 Dec 1756 }; John (31 Aug 1704 - 6 May 1769) Quaker
1 CONT minister {m. 2 Apr 1731 Jane Margerum (d. aft. 1783); no
1 CONT children}; Hanna (31 Aug 1704 - 21 Feb 1743) {m. 27 Jun 1728
1 CONT Benjamin Fell (1 Sep 1703 - 12 Sep 1758) Six children.} and
1 CONT Robert (10 Jun 1708 - 19 Jan 1805, prob. Greenbrier Co. VA)
1 CONT {m. (1) ca. 1733 Elizabeth Haworth. Three children; m. (2)
1 CONT ____ Bailey after death of Elizabeth. Three children}.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a Quaker.  A farmer by occupation.  He resided in Solebury, PA. He is buried in the Friends Burying Ground, Buckingham, PA
1 CONT      Buckingham Monthly meeting memorialized him:  "in his youth somewhat airy--a man of remarkable self-denial and endured with much mildness--with loving and kind deportment.
1 CONT His will was dated 13 March 1726-27 and confirmed 2 Oct 1727 by the original witnesses at Doylestown, Bucks Co., PA
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources:  History of Bucks County, Pennsylvania Vol III, pp. 176-178
1 CONT Memoirs of the Historical Society of Pennsylvania Vol. I p. 296
1 CONT Pennsylvania Archives  Vol IX
0 @N6000000006788784791@ NOTE JOHN7 SCARBOROUGH (JOHN 6, JOHN 5, WILLIAM 4, ISAAC 3, WILLIAM 2, JOHN 1)
1 CONT
1 CONT 5-2937
1 CONT 5-1294
1 CONT
1 CONT A Minister and landowner: Born in London, England, He came to Pennsylvania at the age of 15 with his father.  His father, John Scarborough, Sr. went back to London to get his wife, Sarah Ashby, but she refused to come to America. 
1 CONC The son was left in the care of friends, but he rebelled against their harshness. He ran away and for ten years lived with the Indians and learned their language. Often he served as interpreter and negotiator between them and the 
1 CONC English settlers. John was an unusually competent man and much respected. Many of his descendants are
1 CONT still living in Pennsylvania. 
1 CONT       John Scarborough's parents never came back to America. His wife was Mary Peirson. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Another telling of the tale, about John, Sr.:
1 CONT The progenitor of the Scarboroughs of America was John Scaraborough, Sr. of 
1 CONT London, England, a member of the Peel Meeting of Friends, located at 65 
1 CONT St. Johns Street, London, not far from the Bank of England. He resided on 
1 CONT Hosier Lane. John and his only son, John Jr. age 13 came to America about 
1 CONT October 1682 and settled on 250 acres of land in Middletown Township, 
1 CONT Bucks County, PA near what is now the Borough of Langhorne. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1684 he returned to London for his wife, leaving his son, John Jr., in 
1 CONT the care of friends. His wife not being a Quaker, refused to come to 
1 CONT Pennsylvania with him and the persecution of the Quakers in England having 
1 CONT ceased at that time, he remained in London where he died April 21, 1706. 
1 CONT He is buried in Burnhill Fields Burying Ground. There is a William 
1 CONT Scarborough (born 1598) also buried there who is probably the grandfather of 
1 CONT John, Sr.
1 CONT
1 CONT Among the descendants of John Scarborough, Jr. are many distinguished persons 
1 CONT the most illustrious of these is the former President of the United 
1 CONT States, Herbert Hoover, who descends through Sarah, a daughter of John and 
1 CONT Mary Scarborough, who married George Haworth. 
