1 CONT From a post at GenWeb:
1 CONT A Judith Feake married Lt. William Palmer; they had at least seven children. William died after 9 March 1660. His estate in Middleboro was evaluated 29 November 1661. Judith married second, Jeffrey Ferris (his third wife) and live
1 CONC d in Stamford. He died 31 May 1666. Judith then married John Bowers (his second wife). Judith died 1668 and Bowers in 1695, leaving land to her daughter Judith or Judah Reynolds.
0 @N6000000005077075333@ NOTE [Weeks_4_24_09.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry and Elizabeth were first cousins; shortly after their marriage, Henry sailed from England in April 1630, leaving his wife at Groton, to come over subsequently with his mother. Henry arrived on the ship Talbot, in the harbor 
1 CONC of Salem, on the 1st of July 1630, and, as the ancient family record states the circumstance, on the following day he went on shore with the principal officers of the ship, and "walking out to a place now called by the Salemites, 
1 CONC Northfield, to view the Indian wig-wams they saw on the other side of the river a small canoe. He would have had one of the company swim over and fetch it, rather than walk several miles on foot, it being very hot weather; but non
1 CONC e of the party could swim but himself; and so he plunged in, and, as he was swimming over, was taken with the cramp a few rods from the shore, and drowned."
1 CONT In Deats, the River is given as the Charles, at Boston.
1 CONT His widow, Bessie, without a suitor, a formerly interested Mr. Coddington (the future governor of Rhode Island) having found a wife, came over to New England in the ship Lyon, which arrived on the 2nd of November 1631, bringing al
1 CONC so the Governor's wife and others of his family. Her marriage with Robert Feake must have occurred not very long after her arrival.
1 CONT According to LDS Ancestral File, Harry was christened 20 January 1608 at Groton, Suffolk, England.
1 CONT From the same records, his mother might have been Thomasine Clopton, daughter of William Clopton, Esq., and Margery Waldegrave.
0 @N6000000005077104715@ NOTE [Weeks_4_24_09.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT Martha was named after Elizabeth's sister Martha, and her cousin and double brother-in-law John Winthrop, who was later to be Governor of Connecticut. Martha was commonly known as Joan.
1 CONT Her death might have occurred earlier, 1653, in Greenwich, Connecticut.
0 @N6000000005077104758@ NOTE [Weeks_4_24_09.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT This descendant from Wm. Jr. to Stanhewicz comes from Janet Jackson Stanhewicz, posted 4 March 1999.
1 CONT
1 CONT Abstracts of Wills Vol 1 1665-1707, NYHistorical Society Collections, 1892, abstracts of wills on file at the Surrogates Office, City of New York, 1665 to 1701.
1 CONT Page 492.--An inventory and appraisement of the personal estate of WILLIAM HALLETT, JR. late of Newtown in Queens County, Gentleman, deceased, "who together with his wife and 5 children was barbarously murdered on ye 24th day of J
1 CONC anuary, 1707, by two of his own slaves." 2 horses, £10;
1 CONT 2 mares, £5; 2 colts, £3; 7 cows, 19.5s.; 29 sheep, £8.15; 1 silver Tankard, £7; 7 spoons, £16.6s.; 57 ½ bushels of wheat at 4s., £11.10s. No total given, but list shows very extensive farm and farming products and utensils. Exhib
1 CONC ited as a true inventory by William Hallet, Sr., administrator, June 1
1 CONT
