0 @N6000000005077075504@ NOTE [Weeks_4_24_09.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT The Fones surname was an old one in Worcestershire, originally Fowne or Fawne. There is a long pedigree usually attributed to this family, but after considerable research Delafield could only prove the line back to Elizabeth's gra
1 CONC ndfather, William Fones; by an unidentified wife, he had a son Thomas, probably born after 1573. He was probably born at Bromsgrove, Worcs., died 15 April 1629, in London. He married first, 25 February 1604/05 at Groton, Suffolk, 
1 CONC Anne Winthrop, born 1586, died 1618; daughter of Adam and Anne Browne Winthrop. Thomas married second Mrs. Priscilla (Burgess) Shearman.
1 CONT Thomas Fones was apprenticed to a London grocer in 1595 and made a freeman of the Company of Grocers in 1602, but for most of his life he was an apothecary. He lived at the sign of the Three Fawns in Old Bailey, London, and appear
1 CONC s to have been a man of some learning. His eldest surviving child by Anne was Elizabeth.
1 CONT The pedigree given here is from F.E.Weeks, in Genealogy of Francis Weeks.
1 CONT From Genealogy of the Weeks Family........F.E.Weeks
1 CONT Citizen and apothecary of London, 1628. He married 1st Ann, daughter of Adam Winthrop, Esq., of Groton, by whom he had Elizabeth (Bess, who married Henry Winthrop), Martha and Samuel, his heir aged 12 in 1628. By his second wife P
1 CONC riscilla (Shearman), daughter of John Burgis (Burgess), D.D., he had Mary and Priscilla.
1 CONT Thomas wrote Ann some letters before their marriage, two of which are copied here.
1 CONT "Thomas Fones to Mrs. Ann Winthrop of Groton, about 1603. (He writes her name "Mrs." instead of Miss, probably a custom in those days.) "Long looked for comes at last, so sayth our english Prouerb, which might wel be verified in m
1 CONC e. I tooke accompt of the dayes the Neights the howres the Minutes may lesser Moments expecting Thursday and when yt came be off toward Suffolk long in vayne walkt the exchange nether to buy or sell; yt Proffited nought Coming hom
1 CONC e and sadly Musing what occasion barde mee my hope I at last perceiue the foot of good Tydinges at doore, which largely recompense my travell. Yea such effects workes the true effectes of thy hart and mind if they do even dissolue
1 CONC  me with emulation and desire to exceed thee in loue for why should that loue that could admit no equall: be now second to any: Ah how Impatient is loue of delayes if the sight of a poore letter bring such Joy how far wold yt exce
1 CONC ed to touch that hand that wrote yt to behold and Imbrace that fayre frame of Nature that Contaynes that loving hart that guydes that truly Medicinable touch from whence flowes the soveraine balmes that cures loue sick minkes then
1 CONC  since yow know how Precious is your Presence to me as I haue sayd before so still I say, festina, I Hope to see yow before xii dayes yf there come no letter in the Meane tyme wryte yow forget to satisfy me for the vse of your Phy
1 CONC sick whethr yow had begon but I hope yow will. I am now so full of business that I wryte not in that Methode so otherwise I might haue sent an Answere at your brothers letter which I Received not before Monday I haue write to my M
1 CONC other. I haue sent my brother Lutestringes My Mother and yourself washing half Fingerd gloues to work with hard wax for your letters. Many such loving wishes for thy kindness, harty vows of constancy and perseverance to my self ha
1 CONC rt wringing sighs for thy absence, for yt fares with me as with the sick of the dropsy who the more they drink the more they thirst for mine eyes and eares are Insatiable who Gredily and Glutonously devoure these Mourning teares t
1 CONC hy pen sheds. They tell me thy sighs for my sake is hartravishing Musick that is more Pleasing to my Parchedhot hart then the sweet breath of cooling Zephyrus: but like Phylomely by the hand of a cruell Lowler (a variant of Lollar
1 CONC d, used here as a general term of approbation) out of in the Medos of hir wales so am I by the Messenger who Gapeth for an end of this sad Letter but none but he that takes all shall ever end my loue.
1 CONT Wealth is my want
1 CONT my store is Need
1 CONT such Imperfections
1 CONT loue doth breed
1 CONT in                            Th. Fones
1 CONT
1 CONT Commend vs to Groton Hall your friendes do as Much to yow at the Prese thank my Mother for our Capones."
1 CONT      ***************************************************************
1 CONT "To my best beloued friend Mrs. Anne Winthrop at Groton."
1 CONT "Farest of my hopes, chiefest of my desires, my Earths Happiness, the Pleasant objict of my mind, the subiect ("i" instead of "j", and sometimes the reverse, as xij for xii, in old writings) that reason and all my sences Guyded by
1 CONC  Loue Contemplate what shalt thow expect in this Paper the shadow of my mind but broken and vnperfect sentences interupted by Cloudy and Misty foggs of Melancholy, tost and shaken, with blustring and stormy Passions ract and disio
1 CONC ynted with wishes desires earnist and Impatient Longinges which rayse such forts and bulwarkes of griefe and discontent that reason, Judgment: invention, fantasy, and memory assisted with all the ayd of councel and Pollicy were vn
1 CONC able to hold out: were they not relieued by a stedfast perswasion of they fayth loue and fLoyalty, which is sufficient to calm al Tempests with the sunshine of his beames to chase away those Traytrous Impes of Fears and Doubtes an
1 CONC d to cast the Anchor of firme hope to make me ryde safe were yt on the Billowes of raging dispayre. Then vouchsafe they fayre hand, directed by Truth Constancy and Persaverance to giue some ease to a distressed mind with the preco
1 CONC ius Balm of Comfort vntill Tyme shall giue a full Period and Conclusion to theas my discontents and place me in the height of Happiness by manifesting to the world the Inviolable and Imutable League begon, confirmed and already se
1 CONC aled in our hartes to our endless Joy and Comfort: farewel, and liue happy to make me more happy which without thy happyness is not or ever shall be a Happy.........
1 CONT                          ...........Thomas Fones.
1 CONT Elisabeth, or "Bess" Fones was married to Henry Winthrop in April 1629. Henry, as will be seen in Winthrop genealogy, was her first cousin. Henry followed his father, Governor John Winthrop, and sailed from England in April 1630, 
1 CONC leaving the wife at Groton, to come over subsequently with his mother. Henry arrived on the ship Talbot, in the harbor of Salem, on the 1st of July 1630, and, as the ancient family record states the circumstances, on the following
1 CONC  day he went on shore with the principal officers of he ship, and "walking out to a place now called by the Salemites, Northfield, to view the Indian wigwams they saw on the other side of the river a small canoe. He would have had
1 CONC  one of the company swim over and fetch it, rather than walk several miles on foot, it being very hot weather; but none of the party could swim but himself; and so he plunged in, and, as he was swimming over, was taken with the cr
1 CONC amp a few rods from the shore, and drowned." His young wife, thus suddenly widowed, appears to have been highly esteemed by the Winthrop family, and was the object of their solicitude and continued interest in her welfare.
1 CONT The Governor (John of Massachussetts), writing to his son John by the same vessel in which William Coddington sailed, adds this postscript to his letter: "I hope the Lord hath provided a good husband for your sister Winthrop. Mr. 
1 CONC Coddington is well affected to her. If he proceed, I wish you to further it; for he is a godly man and of good estate."
1 CONT On his arrival in England he repaired to Groton, and Margaret Winthrop, the Governor's wife, writing to her son John Winthrop, Jr., then in London, says: "I rejoice much to hear that Mr. Coddington bears such good affections to my
1 CONC  daughter, &c." The aspirations for this "mach" were not realized, Mr. Coddington, the future Governor of Rhode Island, quickly found another wife and Bessie Winthrop the young widow, without a suitor, came over to New England in 
1 CONC the ship Lyon, which arrived on the 2nd of November 1631, bringing also the Governor's wife and others of his family. Her marriage with Robert Feake must have occurred not very long after her arrival.
1 CONT For data on the Fones family, see "Suffolk Manorial Families", by Joseph James Muskett.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas' will was dated 14 April 1629, proved at Doctors Commons, 29 April, named wife and John Winthrop executors. Son Samuel and daughters Elizabeth and Martha to be brought up by John Winthrop and (???) White Esq. of the Temple,
1 CONC  and daughter Mary by her mother Priscilla. All the children were minors.
0 @N6000000005077075518@ NOTE [Weeks_4_24_09.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT Anne was the sister of Governor John Winthrop, of Massachussetts. She was probably born at the family home at Groton; she is buried at St. Sepulchre's Church, London, England.
1 CONT On 22 July 1597, she returned to her father's from a visit to her Uncle Mildmay's; on 10 May 1602, she "had a new gown brought from London"; on 23 February 1604/05, Thomas Fones came to her father's house; on 25th they were marrie
1 CONC d; on 27th they departed toward London; on 2 September 1604 she was at Ipswich at the marriage of the maid of her father's cousin Sparrow. On 8 May 1605, she visited her father, and departed for home 23 May, having been there at t
1 CONC he marriage feast of her brother John, 9 May. On 24 October 1605, their first child was born, and on 2 November she was christened Dorothy. Sir Robert Crane and his Lady were present, with Mrs. Sampson and Mrs. Bronde, and Adam Wi
1 CONC nthrop, the child's grandfather.
