0 @N6000000003016387566@ NOTE Note: He changed the spelling of his surname from 'Peirson' to 'Pearson' after coming to America. Six children were born to Edward and Sarah (Burgis) Peirson/Pearson before they left England on their long voyage to America. The fi
1 CONC rst child and the sixth child died in childhood and are buried in the Friends' Burying Ground near Mobberley, England. Sarah was expecting their seventh child when they sailed for America. It must have been a long, tedious voyage 
1 CONC (the youngest only four) and expecting another. They landed in Pennsylvania on the 29th of the 7th month (Sept.) 1687, and Edward and his family settled near Darby, Pennsylvania, where they presented their Removal Certificate to t
1 CONC he Monthly Meeting. In 1688 they moved from the Darby Monthly Meeting to within the limits of the Falls Monthly Meeting in Bucks County, Pennslyvania. (The Pearson Family Tree From England To America) 1
0 @N6000000006788784846@ NOTE He and his wife were Quaker, he was a stonemason.  His father was also a Quaker and stonemason.
0 @N6000000003016387592@ NOTE Note: She came to America with her parents when she was about twelve years old. An item on page 1021 of Falls Monthly Meeting records states,"1703/4, 1, 2 Deborah Gilbert (late Pearson) reported married out of unity." Whether that
1 CONC  record refers to this Deborah Pearson is not known. (The Pearson Family Tree) 1
