0 @N6000000001014895050@ NOTE George the Emigrant emigrated to America in 1699
1 CONT
1 CONT As far as now known the Haworth family of America sprang from one ancestor, George Haworth, called George The Emigrant, who came to America from Lancashire, England, in 1699, with William Penn on his second voyage.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1702 or 1703 George purchased 2 tracts of land the first 200 acres was for the consideration of twelve pounds. The second of 250 acres joined the first tract and cost twenty two pounds. George is listed in the deeds as a labore
1 CONC r in Bristol Township.  This land was primeval forest and was located 4 miles northeast of Doylestown in Buckingham Township of Bucks County, Pennsylvania. As there is little reference in Bucks County to the principal points of th
1 CONC e compass, the land can be described as lying in a Southwest to Northeast direction for its greater length of about two miles. Bounding it on its Northeast side is the present Carversville Road which meets the Durham road at a ang
1 CONC le. Within this angle and lying North and East of the intersection is the site of the Haworth farm.  George and Sarah did not live on this farm until 1722 which was shortly before his death.
0 @N6000000004978502178@ NOTE Came to America in 1699
1 CONT
1 CONT George Haworth  came to America in 1699 aboard the Britannia, the sickship from Liverpool in which 56 of the 140 passengers died enroute.  Hisfamily lived in a depressed area of England, a section where sheep wereraised, so his fa
1 CONC mily were sheep raisers and weavers.  He became askilled weaver of fine linens so came to America as a free man.  Hebought 450 acres near Doylestown, Bucks Co. PA.
0 @N6000000006788784811@ NOTE From:  http://www.familytreemaker.com/users/m/a/r/Kirk-E-Mardock
1 CONT
1 CONT George arrived in America 1699, in the "Britannia". 
1 CONT
1 CONT LAST WILL AND TESTAMENT OF GEORGE HAYWORTH 
1 CONT "Memorandum that I George Hayworth of Buckingham in the County of Bucks and Province of Pennsylvania being sick of body but of a
1 CONT perfect mind and memory thanks be to God for the same I calling to mind the mortality of my body & knowing that it is appointed once for all
1 CONT men to Dye I do Make and ordain this my last will & testament Recommending my Soul unto God (who) Gave it me & my body to be buried
1 CONT Decently at the Discretion of my executors hereafter name & I dispose of my worldly estate as followeth 
1 CONT "First I will that my funeral expenses & my Just Debts be paid & Discharged 
1 CONT "Item I will that my loving Wife Sarah Hayworth have & enjoy my plantation with all my wood land and personal estate after my Just Debts be
1 CONT paid until she shall Marry or Dye 
1 CONT "Item I will that when it shall happen that my said loving wife Sarah Shall either Marry or Dye that then my plantation with all my woodland &
1 CONT personal estate shall be praized by four substantial men & all my children that are living Shall Receive equal Shares thereof equally Divided
1 CONT among them 
1 CONT "Always provided that if my loving wife Sarah Shall happen to Marry that then she shall have two pound a year paid to her during her natural
1 CONT life out of my said estate Secured to her by all my Children that are then living share & share alike 
1 CONT "Item I leave my said loving wife in full power of all my estate both real and personal until she either marry or Dye for bring up of my Children
1 CONT in the best way and manner that she is capable of 
1 CONT "Item I will that if my loving wife Sarah shall live a widow till my oldest Son Come to the age of 21 years that then he shall have liberty to build
1 CONT & plant at some convenient place on my land if he please 
1 CONT "Item I will that if my loving Wife Sarah either Marry or Dye before my Children be at full age & their be a Division of my estate as above
1 CONT Directed and after that any of my Children shall Dye in minority that then all their shares or any of them so Dyeing shall be equally Divided
1 CONT among the rest of them living 
1 CONT "Item I will that my executors sell as much of my woodland as shall pay and Discharge my Just Debts at what Side or end of my tact as they
1 CONT shall think fit & hereby empower them to sell & make good title to the buyer of so much land as they shall sell for the use aforesaid 
1 CONT "Item I hereby Constitute and appoint My Loving Wife Sarah and my Brother in law John Fisher my sole & whole executors of this my last will
1 CONT & testament and of all my whole estate both real & personal & of all my Chattells & goods movable & unmovable 
1 CONT "Item I will that the said John Fisher be executor in Trust & that he be paid reasonable charges for all that he Doth in the execution of his trust
1 CONT in helping my loving Wife & Children in the managing of what is herein expressed 
1 CONT "And I hereby Disannull all my former wills and testaments by me made whether by word or writing and Declare this above written to be my
1 CONT last will and testament. 
1 CONT "Item I will that what I have before expressed to be put in execution as my last will & testament and I hereby publish it to be my last will 
1 CONT "In witness whereof I have hereunto set my hand and seal this twenty-seventh Day of the 11th month Called January in the year of our Lord
1 CONT one thousand Seven hundred & twenty four or five 1724/5 
1 CONT "Signed Sealed and published in the presence of us John Scarborough 
1 CONT Ambrose Bancroft 
1 CONT Lawrence Pearson 
1 CONT Joseph Sidell George Haworth 
1 CONT
1 CONT THE BRITANNIA: ARRIVING 1699 
1 CONT       The Britannia had been chartered on behalf of the Lancaster Quaker Meeting out of Liverpool for Philadelphia. Many from nearby
1 CONT meetings, including Marsden, sold their estates and embarked with their families for Pennsylvania. In spite of various Toleration Acts in
1 CONT England which permitted nonconformist religious meetings, the system of compulsory tithes was still enforced. 
1 CONT       The dates of the sailing or arrival in America cannot be determined, except that arrival was previous to August 26, 1699, which was the date
1 CONT of George's first letter home. 
1 CONT       The Britannia was a large ship, carried 140 passengers and was a full sailor. The season was reported as hot and dry, even at sea. George in
1 CONT his letter, described the hazards and suffering of the journey. "We were about fourteen weeks at sea--were thronged in the ship--many died at
1 CONT sea, about fifty six and at shore there dies about twenty--many distempers among us as fevers flux and jaundice--having salt beef we were
1 CONT much athirst--for the seamen stowed the hold so full of goods that they had not room enough for water and beer." 
1 CONT       George debarked somewhere near the Delaware Capes and as he had been 
1 CONT "very weekly at sea" found his sister Mary Myers and spent a week with her before continuing to Philadelphia. 
1 CONT       In the records of Marsden Meeting we noted, that on August 20, 1698, Thomas Pearson had stated his intentions of removing to
1 CONT Pennsylvania in America with his family. Thomas Pearson and wife Grace were among those who died on the Britannia leaving two young
1 CONT daughters. Most numerous victims of the journey were men, next women, and least affected were children and young persons. 
1 CONT       In the summer of 1699, there was an epidemic in Philadelphia and vicinity presumed to have been yellow fever. In ten weeks about 200 died
1 CONT among Quakers. This was coincidental with the arrival of the Britannia. The Monthly Meeting of the area took care of the numerous orphans,
1 CONT among them the two Pearson daughters. 
1 CONT       In the Quarterly Meeting records of February 4, 1700, we saw the following: "that all Friends who are concerned in transporting people into
1 CONT foreign parts take care not to crowd them together in ships to prejudice their health or endanger their lives." For some years thereafter no
1 CONT Friends from Lancashire again attempted the crossing. 
1 CONT       In none of the Haworth records had we found any information about the ship that brought George to America. It seemed that this narrative
1 CONT should at least include its name. Letters to Liverpool and Falmouth brought no result. Finally in published letters of some Philadelphia
1 CONT Quakers to friends in Lancashire in 1699 we found the name of the ship they termed "that sick ship from Liverpool". It was the Britannia. 
1 CONT       When George migrated, he must have been about twenty years of age. At his marriage in 1710 he was about thirty or thirty-one and at his
1 CONT death in 1724/25, near forty five. Some records give the date of his death as the date of his will, which is unlikely. It is best to say that the exact
1 CONT date is unknown. 
1 CONT ************************************************************************ 
1 CONT
1 CONT The Haworth Farm: 
1 CONT In 1702 or 1703 George purchased two tracts of land the first of 200 acres was for the consideration of twelve pounds. The second of 250
1 CONT acres, joined the first tract and cost twenty two pounds. George is listed in the deeds as a laborer in Bristol Township. 
1 CONT This land was largely primeval forest and was located four miles northeast of Doylestown in Buckingham Township of Bucks County
1 CONT Pennsylvania. As there is little reference in Bucks County to the principal points of the compass, the land can be described as lying in a
1 CONT Southwest to Northeast direction for its greater length of about two miles. Bounding it on its Northeast side is the present Carversville Road
1 CONT which meets the Durham road at a right angle. Within this angle and lying North and east of the intersection is the site of the Haworth farm. 
1 CONT George and Sarah did not live on this farm until 1722 which was shortly before his death. 
1 CONT ************************************************************************ 
1 CONT
1 CONT On the Courtship of George and Sarah: 
1 CONT
1 CONT It was on this branch trail over the hill that George Haworth and Samuel Pickering courted John Scarborough's two eldest daughters during
1 CONT long walks and talks. This was an ancient Indian trail and made an ideal lovers lane. There were big chestnut trees, huckleberry and wild
1 CONT honeysuckle bushes on both sides. This trail was near the John Scarborough farm in Solebury Township, the present site of the borough of
1 CONT Lahaska. (This quotation is from Place Names in Bucks County by George MacReynolds.) 
1 CONT On Sept. 28, 1710 at Falls Quaker Meeting in Buckingham Township, Bucks County, PA, George Haworth of Makefield Township married
1 CONT Sarah Scarborough of Solebury Township according to the manner of Friends. Sarah was the daughter of John and Mary Scarborough. The
1 CONT certificate is signed by thirty seven witnesses. 
1 CONT After their marriage, George and Sarah lived on the John Scarborough farm in Solebury Township near the site of Lahaska. A log cabin was
1 CONT built for them. It is said that a peach tree now grows on the site which is near a stone house still standing. All of George the Emigrant's
1 CONT children were born on the Scarborough farm, where the family lived until they moved to the 450 acre Haworth farm. This was in 1722, shortly
1 CONT before the death of the Emigrant. Parents and children were members of Falls Monthly Meeting until the Buckingham Meeting was established
1 CONT about 1720. 
1 CONT Came to America with William Penn in 1699. He Married Sarah on September 28, 1710 where he farmed in Bucks County Pennsylvania until his
1 CONT death. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Herbert Hoover is a descendant, through their son John.
