0 @N6000000005077075455@ NOTE [Weeks_4_24_09.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT William was a prosperous goldsmith of Lombard St.,  London, England, when he made his will, 7 May 1595, proven 19 May 1595. He desired burial in the parish church of St. Edmund the King, in Lumbard Street, London, where he was a p
1 CONC arishioner. He mentions his wife Mary and his children, as William, James and Mary.
1 CONT See McCracken for details from the wills of William and Mary, and suggestions as to her origins.
1 CONT From George Cocks, The Cocks.....Genealogy of Long Island:
1 CONT To Henry F. Water's "Genealogical Gleanings in England" we are indebted for information concerning the ancestry of ROBERT FEAKE who came to Massachussetts Bay in the fleet with Governor Winthrop in 1630. First appears the will of 
1 CONC William Feake the elder, citizen and goldsmith of London, dated 7 May 1595, proved 19 May 1595, "To be buried in the parish church of St. Edmund the King in Lumbard Street, London, where I am a parishioner, at my pew door." Mentio
1 CONC ns wife Mary (?Wetherell?); his birth place, Wighton, County Norfolk; children, Thomas, John, Edward, Sarah m. William Smithe, Rebecca Bourneford (or Boumeford), James m. Judith Thomas, William m. Mary ??, Mary m. Thomas Barneham;
1 CONC  brothers James deceased, Edmond and Simon Feake, sister Jygg, and brothers (?in-law?) John and William Angell.
1 CONT The will of the widow repeats the names of the children and gives to "Alice Feake daughter of my son James one hundred pounds, and to my grandson James Feake and Robert Feake each one hundred pounds." Mr. Water adds note, "There c
1 CONC an be but little question that to the above family, belonged Lieutenant Robert Feke of Watertown, Massachussetts, who with Sergeant Palmer of Yarmouth, New England, and Judith his wife, and Tobias Feke (aged 17) son and daughter o
1 CONC f James Feke late of London, goldsmith deceased, made a letter of attorney to Tobias Dixon, citizen and mercer of London to sell one tenement or house and shop in Lumbard Street, London held by the company of Goldsmiths in London.
1 CONC  (See Thomas Letchford's Note Book pp. 228-9). And I have no doubt that he was akin to the John Feke of London goldsmith, whose pedigree is given in the Visitation of London (1633-4) published by the Harleian Society, Vol 1, p. 26
1 CONC 8. (Henry F. Waters)."
