0 @N6000000005077034551@ NOTE [Weeks_4_24_09.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT James Cock, born Killingworth upon Matinecock, Long Island, 4/2 mo (April) 1674; d. Buckram, now Locust Valley, LI 26/3 mo (May) 1728; married at Matinecock 1/10 mo (December) 1698 Hannah Feke, b. there 6/8 mo (October) 1695; died
1 CONC  Buckram 28/2 mo (April) 1750, noted on the Westbury Monthly Meeting record of her death as an "innocent (pure) woman", daughter of John & Elizabeth (Priar) Feke of Killingworth. The marriage certificate was in the possession of G
1 CONC eorge W. Cocks of the seventh generation (1912). The noted minister Thomas Chalkley, who signed this certificate as a witness, writes in his journal at this time. "We had a meeting at a place called Matinecock, where I met with so
1 CONC me of the people called "Ranters", who disturbed our meeting. I may say as the apostle Paul (only altering Ephesus to Matinecock), I fought with beasts there"; referring to the "Ranters" who gave Friends much trouble at this time.
1 CONC  James was given by his father's will "all my land lying on ye South side of ye Musketa Cove Path" comprising about 200 acres, bought by his father James Cock and Benjamin Birdsall of the Indians in 1684/5, later known as the "Pip
1 CONC ing Rock Farm"; resided there until his purchase of the Samuel Birdsall homestead on west side of road to Matinecock, late in the occupation of Joshua Cock of the Seventh generation. Here he died, as per Will, before 10/4 mo (June
1 CONC ) 1728. On a commission to re-establish a Boundary Line "Wee Coll. Isaac Hicks, William Willis, & Thomas Williams with the consent and voat of ye Town of Hempstead together with Capt. Jarwise Mudge, Jeames Cock " Samuel Underhill,
1 CONC  chosen at a Town Meeting in Oysterbay the second of November 1723--did on the second and third days of December 1723 Perambulate and Remark ye old marked trees from ye East side of Hempstead Harbour Rightover against the East end
1 CONC  of ye bar of beach to a walnut tree at Cantiage point and remarked the trees with a splice down ye north west and south east side of ye old marked trees & three Chops on the South west & North east sides of sd trees."
0 @N6000000005077034271@ NOTE [Weeks_4_24_09.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT Born 20/5 mo (July) 1702, on the Piping Rock Farm; died 20/7 mo. (September) 1741, at the house probably built by Jeremiah Smith, on the little "Gore" bought of Samuel Birdsall, situate on the north side of the road to Musketa Cov
1 CONC e and on the west side of the highway to Matinecock, at Buckram, now Locust Valley; married 1729, probably by Elder Feke, Martha Alling, died 1742, daughter of Abraham Alling of Oyster Bay and Meribah Harcourt his wife, widow of G
1 CONC eorge Townsend. Samuel was a weaver and a farmer. They were married before 26/1 mo (March) 1729, at which date he "condemns his outrunning in marriage contrary to advice and to one not of our Society", vide Westbury Monthly Meetin
1 CONC g Records. The identity of Samuel's wife was discovered by George Cocks in the quaint settlement of a part of her father Abraham Alling's Estate, accrueing to Samuel as her husband. Samuel bought a 100 acre farm and a home lot nea
1 CONC r Susco's wigwam, Beaver Swamp Hollow, about 1731, but as he soon sold it, lived there but about one year.
1 CONT His will is dated 14 September 1741, proved 18 November 1741.
1 CONT Abstracts of Wills Vol III 1730-1744, page 347:
1 CONT Page 153.--"This 14th day of September, in the year 1741, I, SAMUEL COCK, of
1 CONT Oyster bay, in Queens County," being sickly and weak, I leave to my wife
1 CONT Martha the use of all my lands and housing, so long as she remains my widow,
1 CONT except 14 acres, which my executors are to sell, "and it is to be taken
1 CONT anywhere off my land which lyes south of the highway that leads from Muskeeto
1 CONT Cove to Oyster bay town;" And the price of 10 acres is to be put out to use
1 CONT for my son Clark Cock, until he is of age, and the price of the other 4 acres
1 CONT is to pay debts. I leave to my wife Martha, 1/3 of all movables, and the rest
1 CONT to my daughters Hannah, Anne, and Penelope, when of age. My executors are to
1 CONT sell all my rights of marsh and creek in Simmons creek, so-called, and all my
1 CONT rights of Plain land on the Great Plains, and the money is to be put at use
1 CONT for my three daughters. "Although I have given to my wife Martha the use of
1 CONT all my estate, yet having considered I give to my son Samuel 1/2 my estate
1 CONT when he is of age." If my mother, Hannah Cock, should have need of any
1 CONT benefit of my estate, my executors are to help her. I make my two brothers,
1 CONT Joshua and Josiah Cock, and my cousin, John Cock, executors.
1 CONT
1 CONT Witnesses, Thomas Youngs, George Frost, Samuel Cook. Proved before Adam
1 CONT Lawrence, Esq., November 18, 1741.
0 @N6000000005077034654@ NOTE [Weeks_4_24_09.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT From the Cox Family in America:
1 CONT Joshua died at his house on property situate on east side of highway to Matinecock and on south side of "the narrow Lane leading Eastward to George Frost's Mill".  Joshua never married, but, as acknowledged by his cousin (nephew) 
1 CONC Samuel Cock, Jr., "did as Executor of his brother Samuel, take the houses and lands into his care and management, and therewith by his prudence and care bring up and support the family and children of the deceased brother Samuel C
1 CONC ock in their minority"; willed legacies to Sister Sarah; cousin (nephew) Stephen Lines, so called, son of sister Penelope, wife of John Hawxhurst; to kinsmen, Samuel and Clark Cock; to brother Robert Cock; to Joshua Titus, son of 
1 CONC James Titus of Westbury, when he comes of age; to my Executors in trust for Hannah Coles wife of Joseph Coles; to cousin (niece) Elizabeth, daughter of brother Josiah; to Executors in trust £50 for use of the poor in Westbury Meet
1 CONC ing and £50 for the same purpose in the Meeting at Harrison's Purchase; to the children of sisters Martha Frost and Mary Frost, and of brother Josiah Cock; to brother Robert one of my cows, and all the remainder of my personal est
1 CONC ate to the family of my brother Samuel.
