1 CONT DovetalesOK <DovetalesOK@msn.com>
1 CONT
1 CONT Hi Chuck,
1 CONT Always happy to correspondent and share information with other family members working on their genealogy.   I worked on this project from 1974 to 1982.  I wasn't big on getting documentation at the time.  As long as I knew where i
1 CONC t was, I could always go back for it when I needed it.  I came by all my research during that period the hard way via talking and visiting relatives, trips to the genealogical libraries in both NJ and in OK.  Bob and I moved here 
1 CONC in 1976 with two small children.  Our third child was born in Norman, OK.  I'll bring you up to speed on our family soon enough.   I put the project on hold while I raised my children.  An entire generation has grown up since then
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT I picked the project back up again this past January; and have made progress by leaps and bounds.  No more having to beat the pavement or go cemetery hopping as I always called it.  With the age of computers and access to document
1 CONC s online through www.ancestry.com and other genealogical websites.  I found there are now three other Watson cousins who are working on their respective lines and our collective lines.  It's really neat to be able to share informa
1 CONC tion with each other over the internet.  Also, now I'm into collecting documentation whenever I locate it.
1 CONT
1 CONT I'm attaching family group sheets that I have for Phillip and Naomi Watson Calloway, Jesse & Sara Ware Watson, Peter & Catherine Backley Watson, and Benjamin & Martha Roberts Evans Watson.  This will give you a clearer picture of 
1 CONC the lines.  I recently was able to link another person's tree to ours through Martha Roberts Evans parents.   I'm not so sure I agree with Martha being the age they show on their tree; but I'm sure it's her line, which takes her s
1 CONC ide back into the 1500s.  I'll look for it tomorrow and send it to you.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although there is some conflict as to who actually started it, the Watsontown, New Jersey that you referred to in your e-mail was started by our Watsons.   I'm attaching a copy of the Watson Plat Map.  Part of that land is still i
1 CONC n the hands of family.  I spoke with two of the three little old daughters of Isaac Watson & Margaret Magill.  One said it was her father; and the other said it was Peter Watson & Catherine Backley who started it.   It that isn't 
1 CONC bad enough, in "Clementon: A Historical Outline" it says it was Aaron C. Watson, a storekeeper in the town, copy attached.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yes, according to Patty DiFelice, a lot of the Watson men worked at a big box factory right outside of Watsontown in a little hamlet called Brownsville, now incorporated in what is now Lindenwold, NJ.   On one of the census record
1 CONC s, I can't recall which one at the moment, it said they were candle box makers.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thanks for the information you sent regarding your ancestors.  This is great!  I have lots to share with you; but I can't keep my eyes open any longer.  Bob is in Florida on business this week; and I must get up at 6 a.m. to take 
1 CONC our middle son to work.
1 CONT
1 CONT Looking forward to getting to know you.  We live in Broken Arrow, OK, a suburb of south Tulsa, OK.
1 CONT
